window.INCIDENTS = [{"protocol": "Parity Multisig Wallet", "date": "2017-07", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Parity multisig Wallet exposed a public `initWallet(address[] _owners, uint256 _required, uint256 _daylimit)` with no access control and no initialized-guard. The attacker called `initWallet([attacker], 0, walletBalance)` on a live wallet, making himself the sole owner with a 0-of-N signature requirement and a daylimit equal to the full balance; then `execute(attacker, walletBalance, \"\")` drained the wallet in one tx (153,037 ETH). The PoC replays: assert not-owner → initWallet → isOwner true → execute → victim balance 0, attacker +stolen amount.", "lesson": "Every initializer / constructor-equivalent function must be guarded (only-once modifier or ownership check) — an unprotected init on a deployed wallet hands over the entire balance.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2017-2018.md"}, {"protocol": "Parity WalletLibrary", "date": "2017-11", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (uninitialized shared library + selfdestruct)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The shared `WalletLibrary` that all Parity multisigs delegatecall'd into had the same unprotected public `initWallet`. The attacker called `initWallet` directly on the library itself, becoming its owner, then called `kill()` which selfdestructed the library. With the delegatecall target destroyed, every wallet routing through it (~513k ETH across user wallets) became permanently frozen and unusable.", "lesson": "Delegatecall target libraries must be non-ownable, immutable, and incapable of selfdestruct — a shared library is a single point of failure for every contract that delegates to it.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2017-2018.md"}, {"protocol": "BEC (BeautyChain)", "date": "2018-04", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "integer-overflow (infinite-mint)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`batchTransfer(address[] _receivers, uint256 _value)` computed `amount = _value * cnt` in unchecked arithmetic. With `_value = 2^255`, `_value * 2` wrapped to 0, so the sender-balance check (`balances[msg.sender] >= amount`) passed against amount = 0 while each receiver was credited the full `_value` via SafeMath.add. The two attacker accounts each received 2^255 BEC minted from nothing; the PoC replays with `type(uint256).max / 2 + 1` and two receivers.", "lesson": "Check every multiplication/sum of user-supplied transfer amounts (SafeMath / checked arithmetic) — an unchecked `value * count` in a batch transfer mints unbounded tokens.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2017-2018.md"}, {"protocol": "SmartMesh", "date": "2018-04", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "integer-overflow (infinite-mint)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`transferProxy(_from, _to, _value, _feeSmt, v, r, s)` enforced `balances[_from] >= _feeSmt + _value`; with `_value = 0x8fff...ff` and `_feeSmt = 0x7000...01`, the addition wrapped to a tiny number so a near-zero balance passed the check. The recipient was then credited the huge `_value` (mint) while only a negligible fee was subtracted from `_from`. The PoC transfers from/to the same address using a replayed valid signature.", "lesson": "Add user-controlled amounts (value + fee) with checked arithmetic inside transfer logic — an overflow in the balance comparison turns a fee-charging transfer into an infinite mint.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2017-2018.md"}, {"protocol": "SpankChain", "date": "2018-10", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "reentrancy", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The payment-channel `createChannel` pulled the ERC20 deposit via `token.transferFrom` before marking the channel open; the attacker passed a malicious token whose `transferFrom` re-entered `LCOpenTimeout`. The attacker also set `_confirmTime = type(uint256).max - block.timestamp + 1`, overflowing the channel's open-timeout to 0 so the \"timeout passed\" check succeeded instantly. Each of 32 reentrant calls refunded the recorded 5 ETH party balance without clearing it, draining other users' pooled channel ETH (~165 ETH) from the contract.", "lesson": "Apply checks-effects-interactions and clear balances on refund before any external token call, and use checked arithmetic on timeout math — an overflowing timeout plus a reentrant token drains pooled channel funds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2017-2018.md"}, {"protocol": "Lendf.Me", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Lendf.Me's MoneyMarket did not guard against ERC777 hook reentrancy. The attacker registered itself as an ERC777TokensSender implementer, supplied imBTC, then withdrew; the tokensToSend callback (triggered by the contract's own token transfer during supply) called withdraw(type(uint).max) before internal accounting was updated, draining the pool. The PoC supplies all but 1 wei, then supplies 1 wei to trigger the hook which reenters withdraw, so the attacker walks away with both the borrowed balance and the pool's imBTC.", "lesson": "Any token with transfer hooks (ERC777, ERC1155 batch, TUSD) can reenter your logic mid-accounting — treat token callbacks as untrusted code and apply reentrancy guards/CEI even where no ETH is involved.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2020.md"}, {"protocol": "Uniswap V1 — uniswap-erc777.sol", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Uniswap V1's tokenToEthSwapInput computed the sell price from stale reserves and updated balances only after the token transfer. Using imBTC (ERC777), the attacker's tokensToSend callback re-entered tokenToEthSwapInput a second time before reserves were updated, selling the same 823,084 imBTC twice at nearly the same price and draining ~34 ETH from the pool. The PoC first swaps 1 ETH for imBTC to set up the balance, then approves and triggers the double-sell.", "lesson": "Never rely on post-transfer state updates for pricing — token callbacks (ERC777) let an attacker re-execute a swap before reserves/balances reflect the first trade.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2020.md"}, {"protocol": "Balancer", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The STA token burns 1% on every transfer, so Balancer's recorded pool balance diverged from the actual balance. After a dYdX flash loan of WETH, the attacker repeatedly swapped WETH→STA and STA→WETH while calling gulp() to resync the STA balance, driving the recorded STA balance down to ~1 wei, which made the spot price of STA astronomically high. The attacker then sold 1 STA per swap for WETH, gulping after each trade to keep the price high across 20 swaps, repaying the flash loan with profit.", "lesson": "When a pool holds a fee-on-transfer or deflationary token, its internal balance bookkeeping drifts from reality and becomes manipulable — verify received amounts and never let attackers force resyncs (gulp) that amplify the divergence.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2020.md"}, {"protocol": "Bancor", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Newly deployed Bancor contracts exposed safeTransferFrom as a public function. Since any user who had previously approved those contracts could have their tokens pulled, the attacker simply called safeTransferFrom(token, victim, attacker, victim's full balance) and drained the victim's BNT pool tokens. The PoC demonstrates the theft by calling the public function directly with the victim's allowance.", "lesson": "Any public function that moves tokens on behalf of a third party using their allowance is a transfer-from-anyone primitive — audit every public surface for allowlist/msg.sender checks.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2020.md"}, {"protocol": "Opyn", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Opyn's exercise() accepted a vault array and moved ETH collateral to an attacker-designated address without tracking which oTokens had been exercised, allowing the same put options to be exercised twice. The attacker minted oTokens with a small amount of USDC collateral, then called exercise with the same msg.value and a two-element vault array, delivering ETH to two vaults from one payment, and pulled the underlying via removeUnderlying() for a ~$370k profit.", "lesson": "When a function processes a batch of objects against one payment/asset, check for duplicate entries and per-item consumption — missing double-spend accounting in exercise paths is a classic options-protocol killer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2020.md"}, {"protocol": "bZx", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "bZx's loan token transfer logic did not handle self-transfers correctly: transferring tokens to yourself credited the balance without zeroing the sender's balance first (or vice versa), so each self-transfer effectively doubled the balance. The attacker minted iETH with 200 ETH, looped self-transfers 4 times to inflate the balance ~16x, then called burnToEther to redeem far more ETH than deposited, repaying the simulated flash loan and profiting.", "lesson": "Self-transfers (from == to) are a cheap fuzz target in any token/accounting system — credit/debit ordering that works for distinct parties can double-count when sender and receiver coincide.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2020.md"}, {"protocol": "Harvest Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loaned USDC and USDT from Uniswap V2 pairs, then swapped back and forth through the Curve y pool (exchange_underlying) while depositing into and withdrawing from Harvest's fUSDC vault, exploiting the fact that Harvest priced its vault shares using the manipulable Curve pool price. Repeating this 6 times skewed the Curve USDC/USDT price, letting the attacker withdraw more USDC/USDT from the vault than deposited. The PoC repays both flash loans with ~0.3% fee and keeps the profit.", "lesson": "Vault share pricing or withdrawal value derived from a spot AMM price the attacker can swing with flash loans is oracle manipulation — price deposits/withdrawals from a manipulation-resistant source.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2020.md"}, {"protocol": "Pickle Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Pickle's Controller.swapExactJarForJar accepted attacker-supplied jar contracts without validating their token()/transfer() behavior. The attacker passed two FakeJar contracts whose token() returns cDAI, and through CurveLogic.add_liquidity chained arbitrary calls: withdrawAll() on the pDAI strategy, earn() on pDAI (which routed funds through the fake jar's deposit() that transfers to tx.origin), and withdraw(cDAI). The attacker-controlled fake token/jar implementations funneled ~20M DAI out of the pDAI jar to the attacker.", "lesson": "Any function accepting a contract address as a parameter (jar, curve, adapter) must validate what it points to — unvalidated external calls plus attacker-supplied contracts equals arbitrary execution.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2020.md"}, {"protocol": "Cover Protocol", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Cover/Blacksmith reward contract miscalculated the COVER rewards a user could claim — depositing BPT (a Balancer pool token) then calling claimRewards minted rewards without properly tracking the deposit snapshot, letting an attacker mint COVER far beyond the legitimate amount. The PoC deposits ~15,255 BPT and claims, showing a COVER balance gain after claimRewards.", "lesson": "Reward/mining contracts must snapshot user state and recompute accrued rewards against time and staked amount on every deposit/claim — missing accrual updates let a single deposit claim repeatedly at inflated rates.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2020.md"}, {"protocol": "SushiSwap (SushiMaker) / Badger DAO DIGG", "date": "2021-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "misconfiguration (unconfigured bridge in fee maker)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SushiMaker.convert() is permissionless and burns protocol LP fee tokens; the new Onsen DIGG/WBTC pair was added without a bridge configuration in the maker. The PoC mints a fresh DIGG<>WETH pair with dust liquidity (DIGG had no real WETH market), calls sushiMaker.convert(WBTC, DIGG) so the maker swaps through the manipulated route, then removes the liquidity and swaps DIGG→WBTC→WETH at the inflated rate, taking value out of the maker's fee accumulator.", "lesson": "Permissionless keeper/convert functions that route protocol-owned fees through spot pairs must verify pair registration and price sanity — an unconfigured bridge on a new listing exposes the fee maker.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Yearn Finance (yvDAI)", "date": "2021-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "rounding", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Attacker first skews Curve 3pool by add_liquidity with only DAI/USDC, then loops 5 times: remove_liquidity_imbalance to pull USDT, deposit a huge DAI amount into yvDAI, call earn(), re-add the USDT, and withdrawAll(). A precision loss in the vault's share/withdrawal accounting lets each cycle return slightly more than deposited; the final remove_liquidity_imbalance converts the accumulated excess into ~11M USD of 3CRV/USDT profit.", "lesson": "Vault share-price rounding around deposit/withdraw must never leak value to the withdrawer — a small per-cycle precision loss compounds when the share price can be pushed by a skewed underlying pool.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "PAID Network", "date": "2021-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (compromised mint key)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The token's privileged mint key was compromised; the PoC pranks the attacker EOA and calls PAID.mint() to create ~59.47M PAID with no cap or timelock. The minted tokens were then market-dumped. (Postmortem root cause: key compromised or rugged.)", "lesson": "A single-EOA unlimited mint with no timelock is a rug vector no contract audit can fix — flag it in ownership and cap review.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "DODO (DVM pool)", "date": "2021-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (unprotected init", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The DVM pool's init() had no caller restriction and no initialized guard. During the flashLoan callback, the attacker calls dvm.init() with himself as maintainer and attacker-chosen k, resetting the pool state; he then transfers the borrowed wCRES and USDT out, and the reset balances let him walk away without a real repayment.", "lesson": "Initializers need auth and a one-shot guard — a public init() on a live pool is a re-takeover and fund-drain vector.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Uranium Finance", "date": "2021-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "math error (invariant constant mismatch)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The pair's balance adjustment used fee magic value 10000 while the K check still compared against 1000**2, making the invariant ~100x looser than intended. The attacker transfers 1 WBNB into the WBNB/BUSD pair, then calls pair.swap to pull 99% of the pair's BUSD out; he repeats the same trick in the opposite direction, stealing both reserves.", "lesson": "When auditing a fork, diff every fee/constant against the original — one unchanged magic number silently broke the entire K invariant.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "BurgerSwap (Demax)", "date": "2021-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "reentrancy (malicious token hook)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Demax router computed a multi-hop swap's intermediate amounts once from stale reserves. The attacker deploys a FAKE token whose transferFrom re-enters the router mid-swap, executing an inner BURGER→WBNB swap at the pre-update price before the outer swap commits state, so the same BURGER stack is effectively sold twice. A 6047 WBNB flashloan first pumps the WBNB→BURGER pair to size the profit.", "lesson": "Token hooks inside multi-hop swap loops are reentrancy points — verify the router updates reserves/amounts before any external token call, or add a reentrancy lock.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "JulSwap / JustLiquidity (JULb)", "date": "2021-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "pricing flaw (reserve desync via addBNB)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swaps 70k JULb from the BSCswap pair; in the callback he swaps JULb→BNB, calls JulProtocolV2.addBNB() to inject 515 BNB into the pool's real balance without touching its pricing reserves, then uses swapBNBForExactTokens to buy back ~70.31M JULb at a price computed from the stale reserves. He repays 70.21M JULb to the pair and keeps the remainder.", "lesson": "Any path that changes a pool's actual token balance without syncing its pricing reserves lets an attacker buy tokens at a phantom discount.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "PancakeBunny", "date": "2021-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "flashloan + oracle manipulation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After zapping 1 WBNB into the WBNB-USDT VaultFlipToFlip and letting the keeper harvest, the attacker flashloans WBNB from 6 PancakeSwap pairs plus USDT from Fortube, then dumps all WBNB into the WBNB-USDT v1 pair to crash its spot price. flip.getReward() then mints BUNNY by pricing the attacker's LP deposit against the manipulated reserves (BunnyMinter._zapAssetsToBunnyBNB), minting a huge reward that he sells for WBNB.", "lesson": "Reward minters must not value LP deposits from a single spot pair's reserves at harvest time — use TWAP or multi-source pricing.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Rari Capital (ETH pool)", "date": "2021-05", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "reentrancy (arbitrary worker", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The ETH pool's vault.work() accepted an attacker-supplied goblin contract and token. The attacker donates ~1031 ETH to his fake token, then calls vault.work() with crafted data; the flow triggers the fake token's callback mid-work, re-entering the vault while balances are inflated, so the attacker withdraws more ETH than the deposit accounting allows.", "lesson": "Vaults accepting arbitrary workers/tokens must treat their callbacks as reentrancy boundaries and recompute balance deltas after every external call.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Spartan Protocol", "date": "2021-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "liquidity-accounting flaw (spot balance vs reserves)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Spartan pool's removeLiquidity priced withdrawals from live token balances (balanceOf) without syncing them into reserves. With a 100k WBNB flashloan, the attacker swaps WBNB→SPARTA to skew the price, calls addLiquidity() to mint LP, donates WBNB+SPARTA directly to the pool, then removeLiquidity pays out based on the inflated spot balances; re-adding liquidity recovers the donations, and the cycle repeats ~8x to drain the pool.", "lesson": "LP share math must be based on synced reserves, never on spot balances that a direct transfer can inflate right before an exit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Value DeFi (vSafe WBNB / Alpaca vault)", "date": "2021-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (untrusted calldata)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The vault's work() trusted a bankController address embedded in caller-supplied data without validation. The attacker calls vault.work() with data pointing the bankController at his own contract, redirecting the vault's borrowed WBNB flow to himself while minting him vSafe shares, turning the loan into direct profit.", "lesson": "Vault/worker entry points must whitelist or verify controller and worker addresses — raw calldata is not a trust boundary.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "bEarn Fi (bVault BUSD Alpaca strategy)", "date": "2021-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "accounting flaw (share pricing on strategy balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "bVault.deposit() minted shares based on the strategy contract's token balance, which included pending rewards, while emergencyWithdraw() redeemed at full value. With a Cream flashloan of all available BUSD, the attacker loops deposit(13)→emergencyWithdraw(13) ten times; each cycle captures the gap between the inflated balance and the share value, and the loan is repaid with the fee.", "lesson": "Deposit share pricing must use committed principal, not a strategy balance that includes unharvested rewards — always compare deposit vs emergency-exit math for asymmetry.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "88mph (MPH NFT)", "date": "2021-06", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (unprotected init)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The NFT contract's init() was protected only by an initialized flag, not by caller restriction. The attacker calls mphNFT.init(address(this),...) to transfer ownership to himself, then uses owner privileges to burn token #1 and mint a replacement #1 to his own address.", "lesson": "A boolean-only initializer guard is a takeover, not protection — init must be auth-gated and one-shot.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Eleven Finance (NeverSell vault)", "date": "2021-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public emergencyBurn)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The vault's emergencyBurn() was callable by anyone and did not destroy the economic value backing the caller's shares. The attacker flashloans BUSD, swaps to NRV, mints NRV/BUSD LP, depositAll() into the vault, calls emergencyBurn(), then withdrawAll() and removes liquidity — exiting with the full underlying, funded by other depositors.", "lesson": "Emergency functions must be owner-gated and must actually destroy the share's value — a public burn plus full withdraw is a free exit from someone else's principal.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "PancakeHunny", "date": "2021-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "accounting flaw (balanceOf instead of deposit delta)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "HunnyMinter.mintFor() converted the minter contract's entire CAKE balance (balanceOf(address(this)), including fees accumulated from all stakers) instead of the delta actually deposited. The attacker swaps 5.752 BNB to CAKE, transfers CAKE directly to the minter contract, and triggers getReward() on the CAKE-BNB hive — the mint pays rewards as if he deposited the minter's whole CAKE stack, and he sells the inflated HUNNY for WBNB.", "lesson": "Mint/reward accounting must track per-user deltas, never the contract's aggregate balanceOf — \"everything the contract holds\" is not \"what you just deposited\".", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "SafeDollar", "date": "2021-06", "chain": "Polygon", "class": "oracle manipulation (spot-priced reward pool)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SDO reward pool valued staked PLX via Polydex pair spot reserves. The attacker deposits PLX into pool pid 9, then flash-swaps PLX out of the WETH-PLX pair to skew the price; inside the callback he repeatedly deposits/withdraws PLX to the reward pool to inflate his shares at the manipulated price, then warps time and harvests the inflated SDO rewards, selling them for USDC.", "lesson": "Reward pools that price staked assets from thin spot pairs are flashloan targets — enforce TWAP or oracle-based reward valuation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "xWin Finance", "date": "2021-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "slippage-control flaw + flashloan", "loss": "", "mechanics": "xWin's Subscribe/Redeem accepted priceImpactTolerance 10000 — effectively no slippage bound — so the fund would execute at any price. With a 76k BNB Fortube flashloan, the attacker subscribes, sells the received XWIN for BNB, and redeems in a 20-round loop; each round mints excess XWIN rewards because the fund prices his deposits/redeems at the self-manipulated spot price, and he sells the XWIN harvest before repaying the loan.", "lesson": "A \"slippage check\" that allows 100% deviation is no check — verify tolerance math actually bounds price movement, and don't let subscribe/redeem price from spot.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Chainswap — Chainswap_exp1.sol", "date": "2021-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The deployed `receive()` on Chainswap's bridge proxy checked ECDSA signature validity and signer quota but, unlike the intended source, did not enforce `require(signatory == signatures[i].signatory)` — i.e. the recovered address was never compared against the claimed signatory. The attacker passed 3 real signer addresses with arbitrary (v,r,s) values as the signatories and withdrew 19,392,277,118,050,930,170,440 units of the bridged token with no valid signature. The PoC replays exactly that call against the proxy at block 12,751,487.", "lesson": "When auditing signature-gated bridges, verify the recovered signer is checked against the claimed signatory on the deployed bytecode, not just in the source repo.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Chainswap — Chainswap_exp2.sol", "date": "2021-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same missing-signer-check flaw in a second Chainswap deployment (proxy 0x0891...3466). A single Signature struct naming signatory 0xF1790A... and arbitrary (v,r,s) is accepted because the contract never proves the recovered address equals the named signatory; the attacker mints 500,000,000,000,000,000,000,000 tokens to himself via `receive()`.", "lesson": "A signature-verification bug cloned across deployments multiplies losses — hunt for the same vulnerable pattern on every chain a protocol deployed to.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Levyathan", "date": "2021-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Levyathan team left the deployer's private key (owner of the Timelock controlling the MasterChef) in a public GitHub repo. Using it, the attacker scheduled `transferOwnership(attacker)` through the Timelock, waited the 172,800s delay, executed it, called `recoverLevOwnership()`, and minted 1e26 LEV tokens. Legitimate stakers could no longer withdraw (only `emergencyWithdraw` still worked for some).", "lesson": "Grep a protocol's public repos and CI for private keys/hardhat configs — an exposed privileged key turns any timelock into a self-service treasury drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Cream", "date": "2021-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "crETH's borrow path transferred ETH via an ERC777-style `tokensReceived` callback registered through the ERC1820 registry. The attacker flash-swapped 500 WETH from the Uni pair, minted crETH, and inside the borrow flow re-entered crETH.borrow during the callback (line 74) and stacked crAMP borrows: accrueInterest then a 19,480,000,000,000,000,000,000,000 crAMP borrow while the collateral check was bypassed mid-callback, converting the AMP to WETH and repaying the flash swap.", "lesson": "Any token that invokes a caller-registered callback during transfer breaks the checks-effects-interactions assumption — audit lending protocols for reentrancy through ERC777/ERC1820 hooks, not just plain ETH callbacks.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "PolyNetwork", "date": "2021-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`EthCrossChainManager.verifyHeaderAndExecuteTx` let anyone submit a cross-chain transaction and executed it against any contract, passing `onlyOwner` checks because msg.sender was the manager itself. The attacker pointed `toContract` at `EthCrossChainData` with method `putCurEpochConPubKeyBytes` (encoded as f1121318093), replacing the consensus public keys with attacker-controlled ones; with those keys he then forged headers that made the manager call `unlock` on the AssetProxy, releasing 2,857 ETH (and later the rest of the drained assets).", "lesson": "Cross-chain message executors must whitelist which target contracts and method selectors a message may invoke — the privileged caller's own identity must never be the only access control.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Popsicle", "date": "2021-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Popsicle's SorbettoFragola vaults tracked fee entitlement via per-share-paid markers that were not correctly reset when LP shares moved. The attacker Aave-flash-loaned six assets, deposited into eight vaults, shuffled the LP tokens between two helper TokenVaults calling `collectFees(0,0)` at each hop, then claimed `collectFees` for each holder — collecting the same accrued protocol fees multiple times from each vault before selling the assets back.", "lesson": "When shares change hands, reward/fee debt must be settled on BOTH sides of the transfer — a missing `updateFees` on transfer is an invitation to multi-claim.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "WaultFinance", "date": "2021-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swapped nearly the whole WUSD reserve from the WUSD/BUSD pair, redeemed WUSD for USDT and WEX through WUSD Master, flash-swapped USDT from the WBNB/USDT pair, then converted USDT→WEX and staked WEX into the Master 68 times — each stake pumped the WEX price the protocol used, inflating the attacker's WEX holdings. Selling the inflated WEX back for USDT repaid both flash swaps with a large BUSD profit left over.", "lesson": "When a protocol derives a token's price from its own stake/redeem state, a flash-loan can push that price arbitrarily — never price collateral from a manipulable in-protocol mechanism.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "XSURGE", "date": "2021-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Surge token's `sell()` sent BNB to the seller before updating the token supply/price state. The attacker flash-loaned 10,000 WBNB, bought Surge with the BNB, then called `sell()` seven times; each sell's BNB payout triggered the attacker's `receive()` which re-bought Surge mid-execution (6 times), so every subsequent sell computed its payout on stale state and yielded more BNB than the tokens were worth. He repaid 10,030 WBNB and banked the difference.", "lesson": "Token contracts that pay out before updating state are reentrancy-ripe: check every `buy`/`sell` for effects ordered after external value transfers.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "DaoMaker", "date": "2021-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The vesting contract's `init()` lacked an initialized/onlyOwner guard, so anyone could re-initialize the already-deployed contract and set themselves as owner with a vesting schedule (100% released). The attacker called `init()` with himself as beneficiary and then `emergencyExit()`, draining DERC and, in variants, CAPS/CPD/SHO from four vesting pools (~$7M).", "lesson": "Always check `initializer` modifiers on deployed contracts — \"initialized once at deployment\" is an invariant, not a given.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Nimbus", "date": "2021-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Nimbus swap pair computed `balanceAdjusted = balance*10000 - amountIn*15` but compared the product against `reserve0*reserve1*1000**2`, a 100x laxer K-check than intended. Flash-swapping 99% of the pair's USDT and repaying only 1/10 of it (via the `NimbusCall` callback) still satisfied the invariant, so the attacker walked away with ~90% of the borrowed USDT.", "lesson": "When reviewing forks of UniswapV2, diff every constant and operator in the K-check against the original — off-by-one-zero constants are instantly exploitable.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "NowSwap", "date": "2021-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "NowSwap's unverified NWETH/NBU pair carried the same 10000-vs-1000 inconsistency: the fee-adjusted product check demanded 100x less than it should. The attacker flash-swapped 99% of the pair's NBU, and in the fallback repaid only 10% of his NBU balance; the lax invariant accepted it, netting ~90% of the pool's NBU.", "lesson": "Unverified-but-used pair contracts are prime targets — a constant-consistency diff of the swap invariant is a 5-minute check that finds 7-figure bugs.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Sushimiso", "date": "2021-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MISO's DutchAuction `batch()` executed an array of calls while each sub-call received the single `msg.value` sent to the batch. The attacker sent 100 ETH with five `commitEth` calls packed into the batch, so the auction credited 500 ETH of commitments for 100 ETH sent, and after the auction he withdrew most of the ETH pool via refunds.", "lesson": "Batch/delegatecall helpers must either forward value exactly once or reject value-bearing sub-calls — track where msg.value lands in every multi-call primitive.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "ZABU", "date": "2021-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "ZabuFarm's `withdraw` did not verify that the requested amount matched the caller's staked position — the attacker's helper deposited a little SPORE then called `withdraw(38, SPORE.balanceOf(farm))` to pull the farm's ENTIRE SPORE balance. Wrapping this in flash-swaps on two Pangolin SPORE pairs and repeated deposit/withdraw loops, he drained the farm's SPORE, farmed ZABU rewards, and sold ZABU for WAVAX profit.", "lesson": "Any farm `withdraw` that reads the pool balance instead of the user's staked mapping is a full-drain waiting to happen — check the withdrawal amount source.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Cream_2", "date": "2021-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "CREAM valued crYUSD collateral via the yUSD vault's `pricePerShare`. The attacker Maker-flash-loaned 500M DAI into yUSD collateral and Aave-flash-loaned 524K ETH into crETH collateral, then cycled borrow/mint of yUSD→crYUSD between two contracts to inflate the recorded collateral. In the final step he withdrew yUSD and donated the underlying yDAI/yUSDC/yUSDT/yTUSD back to the vault (`transfer(yUSD, totalAssets)`), roughly doubling `pricePerShare` — doubling his collateral value — then borrowed every crToken market dry (~$130M).", "lesson": "Any lending market whose oracle reads a vault's `pricePerShare` is vulnerable to donation attacks — price must be derived from externally verifiable sources, never from manipulable internal state.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "IndexedFinance", "date": "2021-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The DEFI5 index pool priced spot swaps from denormalized weights that were out of sync with the pool's actual token set. The attacker flash-loaned UNI/AAVE/COMP/CRV/MKR/SNX, called `reindexPool`, then swapped each loaned token for the pool's cheapest token (tokenOut) at the stale-weight prices, minted DEFI5 pool tokens with that cheap token, flash-borrowed 220K SUSHI and donated it to the pool followed by `gulp(SUSHI)` — raising the pool's recorded value — and finally `exitPool`'d his DEFI5 for a proportional slice of the now-inflated pool.", "lesson": "Weight-based pools must recompute weights and total supply atomically with every state change — stale weights plus a balance-syncing `gulp` gives attackers a mint-and-exit arbitrage.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Mono", "date": "2021-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MonoX's `swapExactTokenForToken` allowed tokenIn == tokenOut and updated the input token's reserve before computing the output price, so swapping MONO→MONO raised the MONO price on each iteration. The attacker removed other users' MONO/USDC liquidity, added a little of his own MONO, swapped MONO for MONO 55 times to pump its price ~2^55 times, then swapped the inflated MONO for ~4B USDC from the pool.", "lesson": "Same-token swaps must be rejected outright — any AMM that credits the input before pricing the output turns tokenIn==tokenOut into a free price pump.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Ploutoz", "date": "2021-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Ploutoz's lending market valued DOP collateral at the live DEX spot price. The attacker Pancake-flash-loaned 1,000,400 BUSD, used it to pump the DOP price on both TwindexSwap and PancakeSwap, then borrowed CAKE/DOLLY/WETH/BTCB/USDT/BUSD loans against a few overvalued DOP tokens; he sold part of the DOP back to repay the flash loan and kept the borrowed assets (~$365K).", "lesson": "A lending protocol that appraises collateral with an uncapped spot price of its own reward token is one flash loan away from liquidation-free borrowing.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Grim", "date": "2021-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "GrimBoostVault's `depositFor` transferred the LP tokens from the user (triggering the attacker contract's `transferFrom`) BEFORE recording the user's shares. The attacker's fake `transferFrom` re-entered `depositFor` six times passing his own contract as the token (fake transfers that succeed trivially), each minting shares for the same underlying LP deposit; on the last step he deposited the real Spirit LP. `withdrawAll` then redeemed all seven share positions for real LPs, and he removed the flash-loaned BTC/WFTM liquidity with a large profit.", "lesson": "Vault share minting must be the first state change before any token pull — a transfer hook before share accounting lets one deposit mint N shares.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "NerveBridge", "date": "2021-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loaned 50,000 BUSD from ForTube, swapped to fUSD on Ellipsis, then ran seven cycles of: metaSwapPool.swap(fUSD→Nerve 3-LP), nerve3pool.removeLiquidityOneToken(→BUSD), and metaSwapPool.swapUnderlying(BUSD→fUSD). The metapool's `swapUnderlying` priced BUSD using internal recorded balances that were never synced with the BUSD removed from the underlying pool, so each cycle returned more fUSD than it consumed; the final fUSD→BUSD swap repaid the loan and kept the profit.", "lesson": "Meta-pools wrapping base pools must reconcile their internal balances with the underlying pool after every LP add/remove — a desync between recorded and real balances is a free arbitrage loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Visor", "date": "2021-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2021-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "RewardsHypervisor's `deposit` pulled the deposit from a caller-supplied `from` address by calling its `owner()` and `delegatedTransferERC20`, trusting the result. The attacker passed his own contract as `from` with `owner()` returning himself and `delegatedTransferERC20` doing nothing, so the hypervisor credited a 1e26-token vVISR position without receiving any tokens — minting value from nothing.", "lesson": "Never trust external `owner()`/transfer callbacks on a user-supplied address — verify token balances actually moved (before/after) when crediting positions.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2021-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Anyswap (Multichain)", "date": "2022-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-01 · Ethereum · access-control", "loss": "", "mechanics": "anySwapOutUnderlyingWithPermit on the AnyswapV4 router took caller-supplied from/token/amount plus an unvalidated permit signature (v=0, r=s=0x0). The router called permit() on the underlying (WETH has no EIP-2612 permit, so it was a silent no-op), ignored the result, then ran safeTransferFrom(underlying, from, token, amount) against any pre-existing allowance and credited anyTokens via the token's depositVault. In the PoC the attacker's fake token self-reports underlying()=WETH, the router pulls the victim's WETH into it, and the attacker transfers it out; real-world loss ~$3M across WETH/AVAX/MATIC/WBNB pools.", "lesson": "A router that runs permit-then-transferFrom without verifying the permit actually raised the allowance lets anyone spend every stale infinite approval — compare allowance before/after the permit call.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Qubit Finance", "date": "2022-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-01 · Ethereum→BSC · bridge", "loss": "", "mechanics": "QBridgeHandler registered the zero address for the ETH resourceID and contractWhitelist[address(0)] was true. The attacker called QBridge.deposit with data encoding (option, amount, to) for ~190 ETH worth of xETH and no ETH sent; the handler passed the zero-address \"token\" through the generic path, skipped any asset transfer, and only emitted a Deposit event, which the relayer trusted to mint xETH on BSC. The minted xETH was then used as collateral to drain ~$80M.", "lesson": "Sentinel addresses like 0x0 must never be whitelisted tokens, and a Deposit event must never fire without on-chain proof the asset was actually locked.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Build Finance", "date": "2022-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-02 · Ethereum · governance (hostile takeover)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker acquired BUILD, proposed that the governor approve() their address for the DAO's entire BUILD treasury, voted it through with cheaply bought voting power, and after the timelock executed it and swept the treasury with transferFrom. (~$470K)", "lesson": "A DAO whose voting token is freely purchasable and whose treasury sits inside the governor with a generic execute() is one cheap token purchase away from takeover — voting power must be hard to buy instantly.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Meter.io", "date": "2022-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-02 · Moonriver · bridge", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Meter's ChainBridge-fork handler trusted the amount in calldata for burn-listed wrapped assets (WETH/WBNB): calling deposit with an arbitrarily large amount and no msg.value burned nothing from the attacker yet instructed the destination to release the full amount. The PoC then swaps the freshly minted 2000 WETH for WMOVR via SushiSwap. Loss ~$4.4M.", "lesson": "In bridge handlers, wrapped/native assets must be validated against msg.value or a real transferFrom — the amount in calldata is attacker input, never a receipt.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Sandbox", "date": "2022-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-02 · Ethereum · access-control (public burn)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The LAND NFT contract exposed its internal-style _burn(from, to, id) publicly with no authorization check, so any caller could burn any holder's LAND. The PoC burns a whale's tokenId 3738 in a loop, destroying 100 NFTs (~$500K+).", "lesson": "Grep NFT collections for public _burn/_transfer-style functions — an exposed destructor is a griefing weapon against any holder.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "TecraSpace", "date": "2022-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-02 · Ethereum · access-control (permissionless burnFrom) + LP manipulation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "TCR's burnFrom was callable by anyone. The attacker bought TCR, burned nearly the entire TCR balance of its Uniswap pair, called sync() so reserves matched the depleted balance, then sold their remaining TCR back at the artificially inflated price, extracting the USDT side. Loss 639,222 USDT.", "lesson": "A token with a permissionless burnFrom lets anyone rug its own LP pool — check burn authorization before treating any burnable token as an asset.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Agave", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Gnosis · reentrancy (ERC677 token hook in liquidation)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Aave-v2 fork: the attacker built a position with health factor just above 1, warped time so accrued interest pushed it below 1, then called liquidationCall on themselves. Because the aToken transfer during liquidation triggered the Gnosis onTokenTransfer hook before liquidation state updates completed, the attacker re-entered and ran deposit/borrow loops, draining WETH and the other markets' liquidity. (~$1.5M per KeyInfo)", "lesson": "Lending-fork liquidation paths that hand control to ERC677/ERC777 token hooks mid-callback are reentrancy targets — finalize all state before any external call, liquidations included.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Auctus", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Ethereum · access-control", "loss": "", "mechanics": "ACOWriter.write accepted an arbitrary acoToken, strikeAsset and data blob and executed the caller-supplied data against the strike asset with the writer's own authority. The attacker passed a fake ACO token plus a transferFrom(victim-with-standing-approval, attacker, balance) on USDC, draining funds the writer was authorized to move. (~$1M)", "lesson": "Routers that execute arbitrary calldata on user-chosen assets inherit every standing approval granted to the router — constrain data to a whitelisted action set.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Bacon Protocol", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Ethereum · reentrancy (ERC777 hook)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "bacon.lend updated its exchange-rate accounting after the ERC777 HOME token transfer; the tokensReceived hook re-entered lend twice more, minting bHOME at the pre-update rate, and redeem then converted the inflated balance back to USDC, repaying the Uniswap flashswap with profit. (~$1M)", "lesson": "ERC777/ERC677 receiver hooks let borrowers re-enter before share-price updates — apply CEI and reentrancy guards to every mint/redeem pair.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Compound (cTUSD)", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Ethereum · business logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After TUSD's legacy→new migration, the cTUSD market's cash sat in the legacy contract. sweepToken only rejected the current underlying address, so sweeping the legacy TUSD — economically 1:1 with new TUSD — moved the market's entire liquidity out; the market was drained (later patched via governance).", "lesson": "Address-equality checks for \"the underlying\" break across token migrations — block economically equivalent legacy tokens too, and keep sweep functions admin-gated.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Fantasm", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Fantom · rounding", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The xFTM pool's mint() mis-normalized FSM's decimals when computing _xftmOut, so depositing 100 FSM minted far more xFTM than the pool ratio justified; the attacker then called collect() to realize the inflated yield. (~$2.6M)", "lesson": "Cross-token decimal normalization in mint/redemption math is a one-line bug with nine-figure impact — unit-test share math against non-18-decimal tokens.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Hundred Finance", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Gnosis · reentrancy (ERC677 hook)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "A Compound fork whose hxdai borrow transfers xDAI through an ERC677-compatible token fired onTokenTransfer before the comptroller recorded the new borrow. The attacker flashswapped USDC, deposited, borrowed, and re-entered on the token hook to borrow more xDAI against collateral not yet accounted for, converting it to USDC via Curve.", "lesson": "Forks that substitute ERC677/ERC777 tokens into Compound-style borrow paths inherit a callback window — re-audit every external-call ordering in the fork diff.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "LiFi", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Ethereum · access-control", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The pre-bridge swap feature let callers pass an array of arbitrary callTo + callData (any contract, any method); the contract only verified the ending balance covered the bridge amount. The attacker chained one legitimate swap with ~37 transferFrom calls on USDT/USDC/DAI/MATIC executed from the LiFi contract's address, sweeping every wallet that had granted it infinite approval. (~$600K)", "lesson": "Flexible swap adapters are arbitrary-call executors running under the contract's identity — standing approvals become a honeypot; allowlist callTo and never let user calldata reach token transferFrom.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "OneRing", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Fantom · rounding", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The vault's share price used 18-decimal math for USDC (6 decimals), so depositSafe of an 80M USDC flashloan minted wildly inflated shares; withdrawing the full share balance paid out more USDC than was deposited. (~$1.45M)", "lesson": "Share-price math must derive decimals from the token, not hardcode 1e18 — a flashloan deposit/withdraw round-trip is the fastest way to find this bug.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Paraluni", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · BSC · flashloan + untrusted-token accounting (MasterChef LP share mint)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MasterChef's depositByAddLiquidity trusted the caller-supplied token pair: the attacker's EvilToken contracts returned max allowance and fake balances, and their transferFrom approved the chef for the attacker's real USDT/BUSD instead of moving anything. The chef minted LP shares for a near-zero deposit; withdrawAndRemoveLiquidity then drained the USDT/BUSD pool. (~$1.7M)", "lesson": "Farm contracts must not trust caller-supplied token contracts' allowance()/balanceOf()/transferFrom() — fake tokens mint LP shares for free.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Redacted Cartel", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Ethereum · access-control", "loss": "", "mechanics": "wxBTRFLY's custom transferFrom called _approve(sender, msg.sender, allowance(sender, recipient) - amount) — it re-keyed the allowance from the recipient's allowance instead of msg.sender's. Bob calls transferFrom(Alice, AliceContract, 0): no funds move, but Bob receives an allowance equal to Alice's allowance to AliceContract, then sweeps Alice's full balance.", "lesson": "A hand-rolled transferFrom that reads the wrong allowance key grants spenders permissions they were never given — fuzz allowance transitions, including transferFrom with amount=0.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Revest Finance", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Ethereum · reentrancy", "loss": "", "mechanics": "withdrawFNFT transferred ERC1155 FNFTs to the caller before finalizing the FNFT value bookkeeping; via onERC1155Received the attacker re-entered depositAdditionalToFNFT on the adjacent FNFT id, inflating its per-unit value to 1e18 RENA mid-withdraw, so the 360,001 withdrawn units paid out far more RENA than was deposited. (~$2M)", "lesson": "Vesting/NFT-lock withdraws must finalize mappings before transferring the NFT — the receive hook is a free re-entry point.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Ronin Bridge", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Ethereum", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The bridge's signature-threshold loop inverted its validator check (counting non-validator signers toward the threshold), so five signatures from any arbitrary key satisfied the \"5-of-9\" requirement; the attacker submitted withdrawERC20For for 173,600 ETH and 25.5M USDC with forged signatures. $625M — the largest DeFi theft to date.", "lesson": "Multisig verification loops are where a single inverted ! or misplaced increment silently deletes the whole threshold — write exhaustive signer-set permutation tests.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "TreasureDAO", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Arbitrum · missing validation (zero-quantity purchase)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The marketplace's buyItem computed total price as pricePerItem × quantity and never checked quantity > 0, so a purchase with quantity=0 transferred the SmolBrain NFT for 0 MAGIC; the attacker repeated this to sweep 100+ NFTs. (~$1.4M)", "lesson": "Price-by-multiplication without a quantity>0 check turns any marketplace into a free-mint — audit every payable transfer for paths where the computed price can be zero.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Umbrella Network", "date": "2022-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-03 · Ethereum · arithmetic underflow (missing balance check in withdraw)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "StakingRewards.withdraw only required amount != 0, then executed _balances[user] -= amount (underflow) and transferred amount LP tokens to the caller. With no prior stake, the attacker withdrew the pool's entire UniLP balance. (~$700K)", "lesson": "Withdraw functions that transfer amount before (or without) checking balance >= amount are infinite-withdraw bugs regardless of the \"safe math\" comments above them.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Akutar", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · Ethereum · DoS + logic error (refund loop + inverted require)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Two flaws: (1) processRefunds iterated bidders pushing ETH refunds, so a contract bidder whose fallback reverts bricks the entire refund loop, trapping honest bidders' funds; (2) claimProjectFunds used an inverted require condition, permanently locking the ~$34M of project funds (11,539 ETH).", "lesson": "Refund loops must be pull-based (or skip reverting recipients), and one inverted require can lock nine figures — test failure branches, not just the happy path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Beanstalk", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · Ethereum · governance + flashloan", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Depositing BEAN3CRV LP into the Silo granted instant BIP voting power and emergencyCommit executed a proposal with >=2/3 support immediately. The attacker flashloaned ~$1B (DAI/USDC/USDT) from Aave, added Curve liquidity, deposited the LP for votes, and passed BIP-18, which donated the protocol's BEAN3CRV LP to the attacker's contract; liquidity was removed and the flashloan repaid. $181M.", "lesson": "Governance that weights votes on instantly deposit-able (flashloanable) liquidity plus immediate emergency execution is directly purchasable — votes need lock-ups and proposals need time-locks.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Elephant Money", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · BSC · oracle manipulation (spot-price treasury) + flashloan", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The treasury's mint/redeem valued TRUNK off the manipulable PancakeSwap ELEPHANT price. The attacker flashloaned 100k WBNB, pumped ELEPHANT, minted 90M TRUNK against the inflated treasury value, redeemed for BUSD, then dumped ELEPHANT back and repaid. (~$11M)", "lesson": "Treasury tokens minted/redeemed at live DEX spot prices are flashloan-pumpable — value mints against TWAP or capped backing, never spot.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Gym Network (migration)", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · BSC · flashloan + migration ratio manipulation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The liquidity-migration contract converted old-pair LP to new-pair LP at a ratio derived from manipulable pool balances. The attacker flashswapped WBNB, added liquidity to the old GYM pair at an artificial ratio (using the migrator's own GYMNET holdings as the amount), migrated the LP, removed liquidity from the GYMNET pair, and swapped back for profit.", "lesson": "Token-migration ratios computed from live pool balances are manipulable in one transaction — migrations must use fixed, on-chain-verified conversion rates.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Rari Capital (Fuse)", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · Ethereum · reentrancy (comptroller exitMarket during borrow)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "fETH.borrow transferred ETH before the borrow was recorded; in the receive() hook the attacker called comptroller.exitMarket on their collateral market, exploiting stale collateral/borrow accounting so the 1,977 ETH borrow passed against collateral that was then redeemed out, leaving the loan uncollateralized. (Part of the ~$80M Fuse hack)", "lesson": "Compound-style forks where borrow pays out before recording the debt are reentrancy-open — trace the receive() hook reachable during the ETH transfer against every comptroller state update.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Rikkei Finance", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · BSC · access-control (permissionless oracle update)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SimplePriceOracle.setOracleData had no access control, so the attacker pointed the rBNB oracle at a malicious feed returning the Chainlink BNB price x1e10; a dust rBNB deposit was then valued astronomically and the attacker borrowed the market's entire USDC cash. (~$1.1M)", "lesson": "An oracle setter callable by anyone is a full bank takeover — audit every setOracleData/setPrice admin function for access control before anything else.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Saddle Finance", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · Ethereum · math error (metapool virtual price) + flashloan", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The sUSD metapool's swap math mispriced the SaddleUSDV2 LP token (virtual-price accounting), so round-tripping sUSD -> LP token -> sUSD with a 15M USDC flashloan returned more sUSD than it consumed; the attacker repeated the round trip and cashed out via Curve. (~$10M)", "lesson": "Metapool swaps involving the pool's own LP token are prone to virtual-price bugs — property-test round-trip swaps (A->LP->A must never gain) for every meta pool.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Wdoge", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · BSC · flashloan + DEX accounting manipulation (skim", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashswapped 2,900 WBNB and donated it straight to the WDoge pair, then alternated swaps, direct token donations, skim() and sync() to harvest the balance-vs-reserve drift, walking away with the pair's WBNB after repaying the flashswap.", "lesson": "Pairs with externally donatable balances and public skim/sync are accounting-manipulation targets — watch any token whose transfers into a pair do not route through swap().", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Zeed", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · BSC · flashloan + DEX accounting manipulation (skim cycles)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashswapped the USDT-YEED pair's entire YEED reserve, donated YEED directly to the pair, then cycled skim() across three linked pairs (USDT-YEED -> HO-YEED -> ZEED-YEED) to funnel the excess into their own address, repaid the flashswap and dumped the accumulated YEED for USDT. (~$1M)", "lesson": "Multi-pair skim() chains turn token donations into arbitrage — pairs sharing a token must distrust balanceOf drift caused by fee-on-transfer or reflection tokens.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "CF Token", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · BSC · access-control (public internal function)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The CF token left _transfer() callable by anyone (visibility defaulting to public in the old compiler), so the attacker simply invoked _transfer(pair, attacker, 1e21) to move the pair's CF balance out and dump it (930 CF in the PoC).", "lesson": "In pre-0.5 Solidity a missing visibility modifier on an \"internal\" function makes it public — grep tokens for _transfer/_mint/_burn without an explicit internal keyword.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "DEUS Finance", "date": "2022-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-04 · Fantom · signature validation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "DeiLenderSolidex.borrow verified the MuSig-Schnorr signature against the public key embedded in the attacker-supplied report ID (repID) instead of a registered signer, so one self-made signature unlocked a ~17.2M DEI loan against LP collateral; the attacker dumped DEI for USDC and repaid the flash-minted USDC. (~$13M)", "lesson": "Multi-signature schemes that derive the signer key from attacker-controlled data validate nothing — the key must come from immutable storage and the threshold must be real.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "ApeCoin airdrop / NFTX", "date": "2022-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-05 · Ethereum · flashloan + airdrop-claim (transient ownership)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-borrowed BAYC vault tokens from NFTX, redeemed specific BAYC NFTs, claimed the APE airdrop for each (the airdrop only checked current ownership), then re-minted the NFTs into the vault and repaid the flashloan — 60,564 APE for zero capital.", "lesson": "Airdrops that check only the instantaneous owner are farmable by anyone who can borrow the NFT — claims must be tied to historical snapshots or non-borrowable ownership.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Fortress Loans", "date": "2022-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-05 · BSC · governance + oracle manipulation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker passed a GovernorAlpha proposal (with cheaply bought MAHA votes) raising the fFTS collateral factor to 0.7, then submitted forged price data (FTS-USD = 4e34) to the Umbrella-style Chain oracle whose submit() signature check accepted it, so ~$2 of FTS collateral was valued astronomically and all 13 lending markets were drained. (~$3M per KeyInfo)", "lesson": "Governance-set collateral factors plus a permissionless/forgable price oracle is a two-step vault key — model \"attacker passes CF proposal\" and \"attacker submits price\" as one exploit chain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "HackDao", "date": "2022-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-05 · BSC · flashloan + skim", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker bought HackDao with a DODO flashloan, donated it to the WBNB pair, used skim() to shuttle the excess into a second pair and back (pair-to-pair skim cycling) so the first pair's token balance exceeded its reserves, then swapped WBNB out at the manipulated ratio before repaying the flashloan.", "lesson": "Any pair whose token balance can be pushed above reserves via direct transfer plus skim is a price-oracle-free arbitrage target — the same donation/skim pattern as Zeed and Wdoge.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Novo", "date": "2022-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-05 · BSC · access-control (broken transferFrom) + price manipulation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "NOVO's transferFrom did not validate allowances, so the attacker pulled 113.9M NOVO directly out of the NOVO/WBNB pair, called sync() to collapse the pair's NOVO reserves, then sold their flash-bought NOVO back at the manipulated price and repaid the 17.2 WBNB flashswap.", "lesson": "A token whose transferFrom skips the allowance check is a portable \"drain any pair\" weapon — verify allowance math on every new token before touching its pools.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Discover", "date": "2022-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-06 · BSC · access-control (permissionless credit)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "ETHpledge.pledgein credited the caller-specified \"father address\" with DIS value for a caller-specified amount without taking the corresponding payment; the attacker used a BUSD flashswap to satisfy input requirements and walked away with credited DIS to dump (the PoC ends before the dump; the final router swap is expected to fail).", "lesson": "Pledge/referral functions that credit value to an arbitrary address must pull or burn the equivalent input — check that every credit has a matching debit in the same call.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Gym Network (single pool)", "date": "2022-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-06 · BSC · accounting flaw (deposit without payment)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "GymSinglePool.depositFromOtherContract credited the caller's shares for 8,000 GYMNET without transferring the tokens in (the amount was never pulled from the caller), and after the deposit window the attacker's withdraw paid out real GYMNET from the pool.", "lesson": "A deposit function that credits shares without a corresponding transferFrom is a free-money faucet — verify the debit exists for every credit in staking pools.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Harmony Horizon Bridge", "date": "2022-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-06 · Ethereum · multisig key compromise (opsec)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Horizon bridge's 2-of-5 multisig only needed two signatures and the attacker had compromised two of the five keys; the PoC replays the exact submitTransaction + confirmTransaction pair that unlocked 9.98M USDT to the attacker. $100M.", "lesson": "Smart contracts cannot save a 2-of-N multisig whose keys live in one place — hunting includes opsec: threshold size, key custody diversity, and whether confirmations are truly independent.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Optimism (Wintermute)", "date": "2022-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-06 · Optimism · deployment gap", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Wintermute's multisig existed on Ethereum but was never deployed on Optimism, leaving a funded EOA; the attacker brute-forced Gnosis Safe ProxyFactory createProxy salts until CREATE2 landed at Wintermute's address, then controlled the 20M OP sitting there. The PoC loops createProxy until childcontract == 0x4f3a120E72C76c22ae802D129F599BFDbc31cb81.", "lesson": "Addresses holding funds on an L2 without deployed code are claimable by anyone via CREATE2 factory grinding — check for funded-but-codeless accounts and replay deployments before funding.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Inverse Finance", "date": "2022-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-06 · Ethereum · oracle manipulation (spot price via Curve pool) + flashloan", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The yvCurve3Crypto price feed read the Curve 3crypto pool's live price; the attacker flashloaned 27,000 WBTC, added liquidity and swapped to pump the pool's price, re-valuing their anYvCrv3Crypto collateral so they could borrow ~10.1M DOLA, then unwound via USDT/WBTC swaps and kept the margin. (~$1.26M)", "lesson": "LP-token collateral priced off a spot pool is flashloan-pumpable — LP oracles need TWAP or manipulation resistance, and single-block mega-swaps should be sanitized.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Snood", "date": "2022-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-06 · Ethereum · access-control (broken transferFrom) + price manipulation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SNOOD's transferFrom ignored approvals, so the attacker drained the Uniswap pair's entire SNOOD balance, called sync() to shrink the reserves, returned the tokens, and swapped WETH out at the manipulated price.", "lesson": "Same pattern as Novo — a no-allowance-check transferFrom plus sync() is a two-call pair drain; verify allowance enforcement before trusting any pool with a new token.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "XCarnival", "date": "2022-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-06 · Ethereum · access-control", "loss": "", "mechanics": "pledgeAndBorrow trusted the caller-supplied xToken address; the attacker pledged BAYC #5110 with a fake xToken whose borrow() did nothing (no debt recorded), withdrew the NFT, then called the real XToken.borrow on the same orderId — withdrawNFT never invalidated the order — so each of 33 payload contracts borrowed 36 ETH against the same already-withdrawn NFT. 3,087 ETH lost (~$3.87M).", "lesson": "NFT-lending flows must invalidate orders on withdrawal and validate the xToken parameter — draw the pledge/withdraw/borrow state machine first when auditing.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Audius", "date": "2022-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The governance proxy's `initialize()` was callable by anyone (no init guard), so the attacker re-initialized it with votingPeriod=3 blocks, executionDelay=0 and themselves as guardian. They then submitted a proposal to `transfer` 99% of the governance treasury's AUDIO to their contract, self-delegated stake via `delegateStake` to satisfy quorum, voted Yes, executed, and swapped the AUDIO for ~704 ETH.", "lesson": "Unguarded initializers on governance proxies are full treasury takeovers — always require `initialized` state checks on every config entry point.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "FlippazOne", "date": "2022-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The NFT bidding game's `ownerWithdrawAllTo()` lacked an `onlyOwner` modifier, so any caller could drain the contract. The PoC bids 2 ETH and then calls `ownerWithdrawAllTo(attacker)` to sweep the entire ETH balance.", "lesson": "Grep every withdraw/rescue function for a missing access-control modifier — the cheapest bugs are plain missing `onlyOwner`.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "LPC", "date": "2022-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swaps the PancakeSwap pair's entire LPC reserve, then self-transfers the balance 10 times in a loop; each self-transfer triggers the token's flawed transfer logic that credits the sender extra tokens. They repay the flash loan (with a 10% fee) and dump the inflated LPC for ~178 BNB.", "lesson": "Self-transfers on fee-on-transfer/reward tokens must be a no-op; test transfer(own address, x) in isolation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Omni", "date": "2022-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Lib helper supplies 3 Doodles as collateral and borrows WETH from the Omni pool; when the pool's `withdrawERC721` calls `safeTransferFrom`, the attacker's `onERC721Received` hook re-enters to liquidate and re-supply the same NFTs (with `useAsCollateral=true`) while the health-factor check still sees stale state. They repeat borrows against the same 20 NFTs, redeem the vault's WETH and unwind via Balancer/Doodles flash loans.", "lesson": "ERC721 `onERC721Received` is a reentrancy vector — health checks and collateral accounting must be re-entrancy-safe around NFT transfers.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Quixotic", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`fillSellOrder` only validated the seller's signature (`_validateSellerSignature`) and not the buyer's authorization, so the attacker could pass an arbitrary `buyer` address and have the marketplace pull that buyer's tokens to settle their own sell order.", "lesson": "NFT marketplace fills must verify both sides — seller signature AND buyer consent/approval — before moving funds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "SpaceGodzilla", "date": "2022-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With ~2.95M USDT flashloaned from 16 pools, the attacker calls the token's permissionless `swapTokensForOther` and `swapAndLiquifyStepv1`, donates tokens to the LP pair and executes large swaps that skew the pair reserves, then sells back the pumped SpaceGodzilla for USDT (25,378 BUSD profit).", "lesson": "Public \"swapAndLiquify\" tokenomics entry points let anyone re-shape pool reserves — never price anything off a token's auto-liquify loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "ANCH", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan 50k USDT from DODO, buy ANCH, then donate the ANCH to its pair and call `Pair.skim(address(Pair))` 60 times; each skim transfer triggers ANCH's fee-on-transfer logic that inflates the pair's ANCH balance. A final `skim(attacker)` extracts the excess and the attacker sells ANCH back for more USDT than borrowed.", "lesson": "`skim`/`sync` are permissionless — combined with fee-on-transfer tokens they become price-manipulation primitives.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Circle (USDC vault) — Circle_exp1.sol", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "A Maker Vault owner had previously granted the attacker's contract CDP authority (`cdpAllow`). The attacker flashloans DAI, joins it to the vault urn, then `frob`s with negative dink/dart (1/4 of the debt/collateral) using that delegated authority, `flux`es the UNI-V2 LP collateral out, burns the LP for DAI/USDC and sells the USDC.", "lesson": "Token/CDP allowances granted to third-party contracts are live attack surface — track and revoke them like approvals.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Circle (USDC vault) — Circle_exp2.sol", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same incident, second tx: the attacker flashloans a larger DAI amount and this time zeroes out the entire urn (`dink = -urn.ink`, `dart = -urn.art`), fluxing all LP collateral out, burning it and selling the USDC for ~$151K.", "lesson": "A compromised CDP allowance enables complete vault liquidation — monitor unusual `frob`/`flux` activity on vaults.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "DDC", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The token's `handleDeductFee(actionType, feeAmount, from, user)` was public and let anyone pull \"fees\" from an arbitrary `from` address. The attacker buys DDC, calls `handleDeductFee(0, pairReserve-1, TargetPair, attacker)` to move the pair's DDC to themselves, then `sync()`s and sells into USDT.", "lesson": "Fee-deduction handlers must restrict `from` to legitimate sources — a public \"deduct from anyone\" is a drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "EGD Finance", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After staking 100 USDT, the attacker flashloans 2000 USDT and flash-swaps 99.99999925% of the EGD/USDT pair's USDT, crashing the `getEGDPrice()` used for reward claims. `claimAllReward()` then pays out EGD priced at the manipulated (inflated) EGD/USDT rate, and the attacker dumps the EGD to repay.", "lesson": "Staking reward claims priced from live pair reserves can be pumped and farmed within one flashloan tx.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "ETN Product", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan 9400 BUSD; the attacker swaps to WBNB, mints an ETN NFT, self-invites into the Etnshop (commId 11), mints a shop and creates a product, then donates 600k U to its pair and burns LP — each step interacts with reward logic that overpays, and finally `Market.saleU` converts the inflated U into profit before repaying the loan.", "lesson": "Invite/mint/product reward flows with attacker-chosen IDs and pair donations are farmable — trace every reward math path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Lucky Tiger", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The NFT mint's `getRandom()` uses `keccak256(abi.encodePacked(block.difficulty, block.timestamp)) % 2`, which the attacker computes off-chain and re-creates by warping to the winning timestamp, minting 10 NFTs at 0.01 ETH each whenever the parity check favors them.", "lesson": "Block-derived randomness is predictable by anyone in the mempool — use Chainlink VRF for lottery logic.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Nomad Bridge", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After Nomad's Replica was mistakenly initialized with a trusted root that is provable without a real merkle proof, any attacker can replay a legitimate cross-chain transfer message (100 WBTC from Moonbeam) with the recipient field swapped to their own address, and `Replica.process` accepts it.", "lesson": "Verify initialization state of cross-chain verifiers — a mis-set trusted root turns the bridge into an open faucet.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Qixi", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swaps essentially all WBNB out of the QIXI pair, then in the callback transfers ~1e15 QIXI to the pair, which triggers QIXI's transfer-fee/dividend logic that credits the attacker during the reserve imbalance, and repays the flash swap with profit.", "lesson": "Dividend-distribution code inside `transfer` executes in pair context during flash swaps — model it as state-changing, not a view.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Reaper Farm", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`ReaperVaultV2.redeem(shares, receiver, owner)` never checks that `msg.sender` is the owner or has an allowance; the attacker simply calls `redeem(victim's shares, attacker, victim)` and receives the victim's entire USDC share of the vault (~$1.7M).", "lesson": "ERC-4626-style `redeem(owner)` must enforce `owner == msg.sender || allowance` — test cross-account redemption.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "XST", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flash-swap WETH from the WETH/USDT pair, swap into XST on the WETH/XST pair, donate XST back and spam `skim()` 15 times so each skim transfer triggers XST's fee-on-transfer burn on the pair, skewing its reserves, then swap XST back to WETH at the inflated rate and refund the flash swap.", "lesson": "Repeated `skim` calls on fee-on-transfer tokens burn/redistribute pair balances — audit fee logic for transfer-frequency side effects.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "XST — XST_exp2.sol", "date": "2022-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Cleaner rewrite of the same XST exploit: flash-swap 2× the pair's WETH, sell WETH→XST, donate XST/8 to the pair, loop 15 `skim(pair)` calls, final `skim(attacker)`, then sell all XST back to WETH at the inflated price and repay with a 0.3% fee.", "lesson": "Same transferable lesson as XST_exp.sol: skim loops on fee-on-transfer tokens are a manipulation primitive.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BNB48 MEV Bot", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The MEV bot's public `pancakeCall` accepts attacker-crafted `data` whose first bytes encode the recipient the bot should pay; the attacker calls it directly four times (once per token) with themselves encoded as recipient and drains the bot's USDT, WBNB, BUSD and USDC.", "lesson": "Flash-loan/callback entry points must verify `msg.sender` is the pool and validate every field of `data` — bots are prime targets.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BXH", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 3.17M USDT, swaps most into BXH to pump its pair, donates 805k USDT to the staking contract, and calls `deposit(0, 0)` through the delegate caller path — the zero-amount deposit records a stake based on the contract's USDT balance, then they drain the staking contract's USDT and dump BXH back.", "lesson": "`deposit(0)` paths that read `balanceOf(this)` instead of the argument let attackers donate-and-stake; amount must be the source of truth.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Bad Guys by RPF", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`WhiteListMint(merkleProof, chosenAmount)` validated only the merkle proof for whitelist membership and never checked the amount against the leaf data; the attacker supplied a valid proof and minted 400 NFTs in one call.", "lesson": "Whitelist mints must bind the allowed quantity into the merkle leaf and track claimed counts.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "DPC", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker buys DPC, calls the permissionless `tokenAirdrop(100)` to mint airdrop tokens, adds LP and stakes it, warps 24h, then calls `claimStakeLp` nine times and `claimDpcAirdrop` — the claim functions don't consume the accounting state, so the same stake pays repeatedly before being dumped for WBNB.", "lesson": "Reward claims must decrement/mark claimed state — test whether the same claim can be replayed in a loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "badc0de MEV Bot", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Using dYdX Solo Margin's `operate` with an `ActionType.Call` whose target is the bot, the attacker coaxes the bot into executing crafted calldata that approves WETH to the attacker's contract; they then `transferFrom` the bot's entire WETH balance.", "lesson": "Bots holding assets must never grant infinite approvals — arbitrary-call actions (dYdX Call) can force them.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Nereus NXUSD", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With Aave-flashloaned USDC the attacker mints the USDC/WAVAX LP token used as CauldronV2 collateral, then swaps USDC→WAVAX to pump the LP token's value used in `updateExchangeRate`, cooks a borrow of ~998k NXUSD against the inflated collateral, and swaps it out via Curve before repaying.", "lesson": "Lending markets pricing LP-token collateral off its own underlying pair reserves are manipulable in one block.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "New Free DAO", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The reward contract's claim (0x6811e3b9) pays out based on time since the caller's `owner_d` timestamp, which defaults in a way that fresh addresses get a large first payout; the attacker spawns 50 new contracts, sends each the full NFD balance, and every one claims a full reward, minting NFD repeatedly, then dumps for WBNB (4481 BNB total).", "lesson": "Per-address first-claim defaults that reset for fresh addresses are farmable via contract factories — snapshot total minted, not per-address time.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "RADT", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker sends 1 USDT to the pair, then calls `wrap.withdraw(0x68Db…, pair, pairRADT * 100/9)` — the wrap contract pulls RADT out of an arbitrary `from` address and deposits it to the pair, triggering RADT's 9%-burn-on-transfer, after which `sync()` raises the price and the attacker sells RADT for USDT at the inflated rate.", "lesson": "Wrap/withdraw helpers that accept arbitrary `from` addresses let attackers burn others' tokens to skew reserves.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "ROI (Ragnarok)", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`transferOwnership` lacked a check, so the attacker becomes owner, zeroes fees, excludes all holders from rewards, donates ROI to the pair, sets a 99% tax, flashloans ROI from the pair (the 99% tax burns the pair's tokens), repays, then calls `includeInReward(self)` which resets `_tOwned` to 0 while `_rOwned` remains, letting them sell tokens they no longer \"own\".", "lesson": "Reflection tokens — audit `includeInReward`/`excludeFromReward` transitions for rOwned/tOwned mismatches and always guard ownership transfer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Shadowfi", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`SDF.burn(address, uint256)` is public; the attacker buys SDF, calls `burn(TargetPair, pairBalance - 1)` to torch the pair's SDF reserve, calls `sync()`, and sells their SDF at the now-inflated price for WBNB.", "lesson": "Any public `burn(from, amount)` lets attackers burn the LP pair's tokens to pump their own holdings.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Thunder Brawl", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`shoot`/`claimReward` derive winners from a sha256 of attacker-supplied `_x/name/_add` values (precomputable), and `claimReward` is re-entered from `onERC721Received` while the house wallet still holds ≥2× the amount, paying the reward twice per game.", "lesson": "Game payouts must be non-reentrant and randomness must come from VRF — never from attacker-supplied inputs.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "YYDS", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flash-swap nearly all USDT from the YYDS/USDT pair, then call `claim` plus the three `withdrawReturnAmountBy*` functions which pay out YYDS based on reserve-derived values; the attacker transfers the YYDS back to the pair and repays a computed USDT amount, keeping the spread.", "lesson": "Merchant/consumer refund functions that read live pool reserves are flash-loanable payout faucets.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Zoom Pro", "date": "2022-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan 3M USDT and buy Zoom via the protocol's swap contract; mint 1M \"fake USDT\" through `batchToken` and donate it to the fakeUSDT/Zoom pair, `sync()` the pair, then `sell` the Zoom back through the swap which prices Zoom off the manipulated pair.", "lesson": "Protocols pricing assets off any pair they don't own must validate the pair — fake-token donations are free manipulation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "ATK", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The closed-source staking contract's `claimToken1()` prices rewards via the ATK token's `getPrice()` derived from the ATK/BUSDT pair; the attacker flash-swaps the pair's BUSDT, prank-calls the real exploit contract's `claimToken1()` while the price is pumped, then repays the flash swap (~127k BUSDT).", "lesson": "Claim functions that read live pool reserves are manipulable with a single flash swap — require TWAP or fixed-price oracles.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BEGO", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "BEGO's `mint()` verified an ECDSA signature array, but passing empty `r/s/v` arrays skips the verification loop entirely (zero iterations pass), so anyone mints 1e15 BEGO and dumps it for ~12 WBNB.", "lesson": "Multi-signature verification with arrays must require non-empty arrays — the empty-array edge case bypasses ECDSA.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BabySwap", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The router's `swapExactTokensForTokens` accepts a `factories` array; the attacker deploys a `FakeFactory` whose `getPair` returns itself with fake reserves and no real liquidity, performs two swaps \"through\" it, then calls `SwapMining.takerWithdraw()` to claim swap-mining rewards for fake volume and sells the BABY rewards.", "lesson": "Reward/volume accounting must resolve pairs via the canonical factory — never trust caller-supplied factory addresses.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Carrot", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`transReward(bytes)` forwards arbitrary calls to the Pool contract without auth; the attacker uses it to change the Pool's owner to themselves. The token's `_beforeTransfer` then adds the (now attacker-owned) pool caller to `_isExcludedFromFee`, and `transferFrom` skips allowance deduction for excluded senders — so they drain any holder's CARROT and dump it.", "lesson": "\"Action-check\" allowance patterns plus permissionless admin forwarding is a drain — never skip allowance checks based on mutable state.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "EFLever Vault", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The vault prices shares off `address(this).balance`; the attacker deposits 0.1 ETH, then takes a Balancer flash loan with the *vault* as recipient so 1000 ETH temporarily sits in the vault, inflating the share price, and withdraws their shares at the pumped rate (~750 ETH profit).", "lesson": "Share prices derived from raw token balance are inflated by donation/flashloan — use internal accounting.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "HEALTH", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 40 WBNB and buys HEALTH, then calls `HEALTH.transfer(self, 0)` 1000 times; the token's `_transfer` burns HEALTH from the pair on every call even for amount 0, shrinking the pair's reserve and pumping the price, and the attacker sells back for profit.", "lesson": "Transfer logic must early-return on zero amounts — zero-value transfers should never mutate balances or fees.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "HPAY", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`BONUS.setToken(address)` was public; the attacker deploys a worthless self-minted SHITCOIN, stakes it, rolls 1000 blocks, then switches the reward token to HPAY and withdraws rewards computed for 30M HPAY, dumping for ~115 BNB.", "lesson": "Reward-token configuration setters must be owner-only — swapping the reward asset is how stake contracts get drained.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "INUKO", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker adds INUKO/USDT liquidity to get LP tokens, chains flashloans across 8 DODO pools and Venus markets to pump the INUKO/USDT pair reserve, then `buyBond` prices the bond off those reserves and sells/claims INUKO after the price reverts.", "lesson": "Bond purchase prices read from spot reserves are flash-loanable — require TWAP or fixed pricing.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MEV Bot 0xa47b", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The bot receives a Balancer flash loan whose attacker-controlled `userData` encodes a swap path; the bot's callback executes the embedded swap with the attacker's address as recipient (the PoC rewrites that field), and the attacker swaps the drained USDC to WETH — 187.75 WETH total.", "lesson": "Flash-loan callbacks must validate/ignore `userData` destinations — never let embedded calldata pick the recipient.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Market.xyz (MAI)", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With Aave/Balancer flashloans of WMATIC/stMATIC, the attacker adds liquidity to the stMATIC/WMATIC Curve pool, deposits LP into Beefy and mints collateral in Market.xyz, then `remove_liquidity` triggers `receive()` which borrows 250k miMATIC against the now-pumped collateral price; a second contract self-liquidates the position with that miMATIC, capturing the collateral.", "lesson": "Curve's `remove_liquidity` ETH refund callback re-enters price reads mid-tx — protect against read-only reentrancy around oracle-dependent borrows.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Multicall (unnamed)", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The contract's `multicallWithoutCheck` executes arbitrary `(target, callData, value)` with no whitelist; since the contract holds USDT, the attacker multicalls `USDT.transfer(attacker, balance)` and drains it entirely.", "lesson": "Multicall implementations without a target whitelist or auth are self-service drains for anyone.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "N00d", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "n00d is an ERC777 token; the xN00d bar's `enter()` triggers `tokensToSend`/`tokensReceived` hooks, which the attacker uses to re-enter `enter()` while the flash-swapped n00d balance is still on the books, minting xN00d shares against stale accounting. After four flash-swap rounds they `leave()` for more n00d than deposited and sell for WETH.", "lesson": "ERC777-style transfer hooks make staking `enter/leave` reentrant — add reentrancy guards and post-transfer accounting.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Olympus DAO", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`BondFixedExpiryTeller.redeem(token_, amount_)` trusts the supplied token's `underlying()`/`expiry()` without checking it's a registered bond; the attacker deploys a FakeToken claiming `underlying == OHM`, then redeems the teller's entire OHM balance (30,437 OHM).", "lesson": "Redeem/claim functions must verify the asset against a registry — never trust metadata from the token being redeemed.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "PLTD", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan 660k USDT across two DODO pools, buy PLTD, donate 2× the pair's PLTD back to the pair, `skim(attacker)` to extract the excess the fee logic credited, send 1 PLTD to `tx.origin`, then sell PLTD at the skewed price and repay.", "lesson": "Donation + skim on fee-on-transfer tokens inflates extractable balances — audit token fee paths that credit the pair.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "RES", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan ~10M USDT; the attacker performs six flash-swaps USDT→RES into a victim wallet, calls the public `RES.thisAToB()` which converts/mints token states and inflates holdings, then (via pre-approved allowances) pulls the victim's RES and ALL tokens and dumps both pairs for USDT (~290k USDT).", "lesson": "Public token-conversion functions that alter global accounting are manipulation levers — require auth and price-proofs.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "RES — RES_exp2.sol", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Alternative PoC for the same RES incident: flashloans from two DODO pools, buys RES into a create2-deployed receive contract (to pass `isContract` checks), calls `thisAToB()`, then sells the victim's RES and ALL tokens directly against the pairs with manually computed amounts.", "lesson": "Same lesson as RES_exp.sol — conversion entry points and contract-address checks are bypassable with create2 factories.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "RL", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker pre-deploys 100 `AirDropRewardContract`s via create2, flashloans 450k USDT, buys RL and adds LP, then calls `distributeAirdrop(each fresh contract)` — the airdrop pays every address with no eligibility snapshot — and removes liquidity to repay.", "lesson": "Airdrops without a snapshot or eligibility bound are farmed by contract factories — payout must be bounded per identity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Rabby Wallet Swap Router", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`swap()` lets the caller pass arbitrary `dexRouter`, `dexSpender` and callback data; the attacker enumerates 29 EOA victims who had approved the router and, for each with allowance ≥ balance, passes `transferFrom(victim → attacker)` as the callback, draining their USDC (~$200k).", "lesson": "Aggregator routers must whitelist the dexRouter they call — attacker-chosen routers + user allowances = allowance drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Team Finance", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The token-lock contract's `migrate()` trusted caller-supplied `MigrateParams`: `liquidityToMigrate` (set to the lock contract's *entire* LP balance, not the caller's deposit), attacker-chosen token0/token1 and a caller-chosen `sqrtPriceX96` (~price 1). The attacker locks worthless self-made tokens, extends lock durations, then migrates other users' locked LP at the manipulated price and pockets the refunds (~$15.8M).", "lesson": "Migration functions must validate amounts against the caller's own locked deposit and never accept caller-chosen prices.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "TempleDAO", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`StaxLPStaking.migrateStake(oldStaking, amount)` was public with no ownership check; the attacker passes their own contract as `oldStaking` and mints themselves the staking contract's entire xFraxTempleLP balance, then `withdrawAll(false)` (~$2.3M).", "lesson": "Migration entry points must verify the source staking position belongs to the caller or is whitelisted.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "TransitSwap", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The swap router's calldata allowed specifying an arbitrary \"owner\" that the claimTokens helper (0xed1a…) used for `transferFrom`; the attacker crafts calldata naming an innocent user who had pre-approved the helper and pulls that user's BUSD to themselves ($21M+ across chains).", "lesson": "Claim/withdraw helpers must derive `from` from msg.sender or a verified signature — caller-supplied owner fields are drains.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "ULME", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`ULME.buyMiner(user, usdt)` pulls USDT from `user` via their pre-existing allowance to the ULME contract with no restriction on the caller; after flashloaning ~1M USDT and pumping the ULME price, the attacker calls buyMiner for 101 victims, draining their USDT allowances, and dumps ULME (~250k lost, ~50k profit).", "lesson": "Functions that spend another user's allowance must be callable only by that user — public buy-from-victim is a drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "ULME — ULME_exp2.sol", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same ULME incident with 7 chained DODO flashloans: each callback borrows from the next pool, then the attacker swaps USDT→ULME, calls `buyMiner(victim, allowance*10/11)` for all 101 victims, swaps back and repays the chain of loans.", "lesson": "Same lesson as ULME_exp.sol — third-party allowance spenders need caller auth.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "UERII", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`UERII.mint()` has no access control, so the attacker mints a large amount and swaps it for USDC then WETH via Uniswap V3 (~$2.5k).", "lesson": "Check every mint function for `onlyOwner` — an unguarded mint is instant value extraction.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "VTF", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deploys 400 `claimReward` contracts via create2, flashloans 100k USDT, buys VTF and transfers it to the first contract, then chains `claim()` calls through all 400 — each fresh address's `updateUserBalance` grants a full first-time reward, multiplying the payout, which is finally sold back for USDT.", "lesson": "Fresh-address reward resets are farmable in bulk with factories — bound rewards by total stake, not per-address timestamps.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Xave Finance", "date": "2022-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SafeNap DAO module lets anyone `addProposal` and then answer the Reality.eth question themselves (`submitAnswer` with a 1-wei bond, no arbiter); the attacker proposes a mint of 100T RNBW plus `transferOwnership` of RNBW/LPOP/PrimaryBridge and executes all four tx hashes.", "lesson": "DAO modules where the proposer can also answer the oracle are governance bypasses — separate proposer, oracle and executor roles.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "AUR", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`changeNodePrice` and `changeRewardPerNode` on the AurumNodePool are public; the attacker sets both to astronomical values, creates a node, rolls/warps time forward and claims the inflated node reward, then dumps AUR for BNB.", "lesson": "Node-reward rate and price setters must be owner-only — public setters turn rewards into faucets.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Annex", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deploys a worthless token, adds its LP with 8 flashloaned WBNB, then flash-swaps WBNB into the Annex `Liquidator` contract, whose logic (triggered by the swap) grants the attacker a transferFrom; the attacker pulls the liquidator's WBNB balance and removes liquidity.", "lesson": "Liquidation helpers that react to attacker-supplied pair swaps can be coerced into transferring out their own funds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BDEX", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deposits 34 WBNB into the BDEX pair and manually swaps out BDEX, then calls the vault strategy's `convertDustToEarned()` which harvests against the skewed reserves, and sells the BDEX back for more WBNB than spent.", "lesson": "Public harvest/convert functions that read live pool reserves can be triggered at attacker-chosen skew moments.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Brahma (BrahmaTOPGE)", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`Zapper.zapIn` executes caller-supplied `swapTarget`/`callData`; the attacker sets swapTarget = USDC and callData = `transferFrom(victim → attacker)` for a victim who had approved the zapper, draining their USDC.", "lesson": "Zap-in functions must whitelist swap targets — arbitrary callData on user-approved spenders is an allowance drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "DFX Finance", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker calls `dfx.flash()` with 99.5% of the pool's XIDR/USDC and, inside `flashCallback`, calls `dfx.deposit(200k)` while the flash accounting is in-flight — the deposit is priced against pre-flash state, minting LP too cheaply; after the flash they `withdraw` the LP and sell the proceeds.", "lesson": "Flash-loan callbacks that permit deposit/withdraw re-entry against in-flight state are exploitable — lock state during flash.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Kashi (Abracadabra)", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan 450k xSUSHI from BentoBox, deposit it as Cauldron collateral, borrow 800k MIM, call `updateExchangeRate()` to reprice, then liquidate their own position — taking the collateral while leaving bad debt — and repay the flashloan with profit.", "lesson": "Self-liquidation combined with in-tx exchange-rate updates can walk off with collateral — check for bad-debt invariants on liquidation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MBC & ZZSH", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan USDT, buy MBC, call `MBC.swapAndLiquifyStepv1()` (the token's auto-tax-swap) to skew the pair reserves, then donate 1001 USDT + all MBC to the pair (bypassing the `_isAddLiquidityV1` gate) and sell MBC at the inflated price; the same sequence is repeated on ZZSH.", "lesson": "Public auto-liquify functions plus donation gates are a repeatable reserve-manipulation combo — audit both together.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MEV Bot 0x0ad8", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Calling the bot's function `0x090f88ca` with attacker-crafted calldata makes it execute `transferFrom(victim → attacker)` for a victim's full USDC balance, using the bot's pre-existing allowances.", "lesson": "MEV bots with standing approvals and unauthenticated entry points are free transferFrom machines — validate callers.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Beefy mooCAKE+CTX", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker pre-funds the SmartChef with CTK, flashloans 400k BUSD, mints vBUSD as Venus collateral, borrows 50k CAKE, deposits into the Beefy vault, triggers the strategy `harvest()` (via a fresh contract) to realize inflated rewards, withdraws and repays — profiting from the harvest-induced rebalancing.", "lesson": "Strategy harvest functions callable by anyone during a flashloan let attackers time reward realization — restrict harvest to the keeper.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Numbers Protocol", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Multichain's `anySwapOutUnderlyingWithPermit` accepts `(from, to, v, r, s)`; the attacker passes a victim as `from`, dummy signature values, and themselves as `to` — the router pulls the victim's NUM via their pre-approved allowance and \"bridges\" it to the attacker.", "lesson": "Bridge permit functions must verify `from == msg.sender` or validate the signature — never trust caller-supplied from/sig tuples.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Polynomial", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`swapAndDeposit(user=victim, token=ETH, depositToken=attacker, swapTarget=USDC, swapData=transferFrom(victim→attacker))` — the zap executes the attacker's swapData against the attacker-chosen target, draining five victims' USDC allowances.", "lesson": "Zap functions must whitelist swap targets and ignore attacker-controlled calldata destinations.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "SDAO", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan 500 USDT, buy SDAO, add LP and stake half; transferring SDAO to the pair raises `totalStakeReward` above `lastTotalStakeReward`, and `withdrawTeam(pair)` further skews the accounting — so `getReward()` pays out inflated rewards that the attacker dumps for USDT.", "lesson": "Reward accumulators driven by pair transfers (totalStakeReward) are manipulable by donations — snapshot rewards at deposit time.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "SEAMAN", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan 800k USDT, buy SEAMAN and GVC; the attacker loops 20 transfers of 1 SEAMAN to the pair, and each transfer triggers SEAMAN's fee logic which pays dividends in GVC to holders — the attacker then sells the farmed GVC for USDT and repays.", "lesson": "Dividend-on-transfer tokens pay out during attacker-triggered transfers — model transfer fees as a farming loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Sheep Farm", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker calls `register(neighbor)` 200 times in a loop, adds gems, upgrades villages, sells the village, and withdraws 20,000 wool — each register call credits team/referral rewards to the attacker without any anti-spam bound.", "lesson": "Referral registration must be idempotent or bounded — unbounded register loops farm team rewards.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Sheep Farm", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same Sheep Farm bug exploited with 4 fresh contracts that each register the neighbor 402 times, add gems, upgrade, sell and withdraw 156,000 wool, then selfdestruct the funds back to the attacker.", "lesson": "Same lesson as SheepFarm_exp.sol — fresh contracts multiply the referral spam until limits are enforced.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "UEarn Pool", "date": "2022-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "22 fresh contracts are bound in a referral chain; the attacker flashloans 2.42M USDT and the last contract stakes 2.4M USDT to reach the top team tier, then each contract stakes 20k and claims the tiered team reward (up to 162k USDT total per level), withdrawing to the attacker and repaying the loan.", "lesson": "Tiered referral rewards are farmable with flashloaned stake and fresh-address chains — require locked, time-bound team amounts.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "AES", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan 100k USDT, buy AES, donate half to the pair, then `skim(pair)` 37 times to trigger AES's transfer-fee side effects on the pair balance, call `distributeFee()`, sync, and sell AES back at the inflated price.", "lesson": "Skim loops + fee-distribution functions on fee-on-transfer tokens are a repeatable manipulation recipe.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "APC", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan 500k USDT, pump the APC price on PancakeSwap, then `transSwap.swap(APC → MUSD)` prices the swap off the pumped pair and hands out excess MUSD; the attacker dumps APC, swaps MUSD back to APC at normal price and sells again.", "lesson": "In-house swap contracts reading live pair reserves are arbitrage engines for flashloan pump-and-dump sequences.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BBOX", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan WBNB, buy BBOX through a fresh `TransferBBOXHelp` contract to dodge the token's sell-time limits, have the helper transfer the BBOX back, then sell 90% for WBNB — profiting from the fee/limit asymmetry.", "lesson": "Sell limits keyed per address are bypassed with fresh contracts — enforce limits at the pair/tokenomics level.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BGLD", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan WBNB; the attacker donates to and skims the oldBGLD pair to pump its price, calls `Proxy.migrate()` converting oldBGLD→newBGLD at the manipulated rate, flashloans DEBT to repeat the skim trick on the oldBGLD/DEBT pair, and finally sells newBGLD/DEBT into USDT.", "lesson": "Token migration functions must not use manipulable spot prices — snapshots or TWAPs only.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "DFS", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flash-swap the pair's USDT, then flash-swap half the DFS reserve; donate DFS back (98% then 95%) and loop 12 `skim(pair)` calls plus a final `skim(attacker)`, each triggering DFS's fee-on-transfer on the pair, then swap USDT out at the skewed price and repay with 0.5% fee.", "lesson": "Fee-on-transfer + skim loops skew reserves in the attacker's favor — audit fee logic for per-transfer burns.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Defrost Finance", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flash-swap USDC, then take the LSWUSDC vault's own flashloan and `deposit` the borrowed USDC straight back into the vault — minting shares while the vault balance is flashloan-inflated — then `redeem` those shares at the pumped rate and repay.", "lesson": "Vault share minting during a flashloan inflates the exchange rate — block deposit/redeem inside flash callbacks.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "ElasticSwap", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flash-swap TIC; the attacker repeatedly calls `addLiquidity(1e9, 0)` with tiny amounts and `removeLiquidity`, exploiting the gap between the AMM's `internalBalances` and actual token balances (donation/decay accounting) to withdraw more than deposited, plus a `swapQuoteTokenForBaseToken` with a 100× reserve amount — draining ~$850k.", "lesson": "Custom AMMs must enforce x*y=k against actual balances after every op — internal-vs-actual balance drift is a drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "FPR", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`setAdmin(address)` on the four reward contracts was public; the attacker sets themselves as admin, calls `remaining()` to sweep each contract's FPR (and the LP pair's tokens via the fourth), and sells/removes liquidity.", "lesson": "`setAdmin` without auth is a giveaway — verify ownership on every privileged setter.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "JayPeggers", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`buyJay` accepts an attacker-supplied NFT address and calls `transferFrom` on it during purchase; the attacker's `transferFrom` re-enters `JAY.sell()` while the buy accounting is incomplete, repeatedly selling shares at an inflated balance, and they keep the ETH difference (~15.32 ETH).", "lesson": "Bonding curves calling arbitrary token contracts are reentrant via transfer hooks — guard or use reentrancy-safe token handling.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Lodestar", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With flashloans from Aave/Radiant/UniswapV3, the attacker mints GLP via GMX, `donate()`s sGLP to the plvGLP depositor to pump the plvGLP exchange rate, then loops 16× `borrow(lplvGLP)` → `mint(lplvGLP)` to compound against the inflated collateral price, finally borrowing out every Lodestar market (~4500 ETH).", "lesson": "Exchange rates updated by donations + uncapped borrow-mint loops = collateral amplification — cap borrows and use TWAP pricing.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MEV Bot 0x28d9", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker loops DODO `flashLoan` calls with the bot as `assetTo`, so the bot repeatedly receives flashloaned USDT and its flawed callback returns its own USDT to the pool — each round drains the bot's balance until it's empty (~$1300).", "lesson": "Contracts receiving flashloans must validate the initiator — attacker-invoked flashloans drain bot balances.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MUMUG", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flash-swap the MU/MUG pair's MU, sell MU→USDC.e to pump the USDC price in MU terms, then call `mu_bond`/`mu_gold_bond` which price bonds off the manipulated pair, swap back and repay.", "lesson": "Bond issuance priced off spot pair reserves is flash-loanable — use TWAPs or fixed prices.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Nimbus Platform", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan WBNB plus a flash-swap of the NBU_WBNB/NIMB pair; swapping NBU_WBNB→NIMB pumps the NIMB reward-token price used by three staking contracts, the attacker calls `getReward()` on three pre-staked fresh user contracts and dumps NIMB/GNIMB for WBNB.", "lesson": "Staking reward payouts valued via a live pair price are manipulable — fix or TWAP the reward valuation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Nova Exchange", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`rewardHolders(amount)` was public and accepted a caller-chosen amount, minting/distributing NOVA to holders; the attacker called it with 1e28 and received a corresponding NOVA balance.", "lesson": "Reward functions that accept caller-supplied amounts are free mints — amounts must come from internal accounting.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Overnight", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloans of USDC.e/USDC are used to mint qiUSDCn collateral and borrow USDC.e from Benqi, then the attacker repeatedly calls `addLiquidity` with only USDC.e and `removeLiquidityImbalance` on the nUSD pool to skew it, buys USD+ cheaply through the imbalanced pool, and redeems USD+ 1:1 for USDC — six rounds of profit.", "lesson": "Multi-token stable pools allowing imbalanced add/remove let attackers arbitrage the mint price against 1:1 redemption.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "RFB", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flashloan 20 BNB; the attacker loops 50 BNB→RFB→BNB round trips with decreasing amounts, exploiting RFB's asymmetric buy/sell fees to profit on each round, then repays the loan.", "lesson": "Tax-asymmetric tokens are round-trip arbitrage machines — model buy+sell fees together when auditing tokenomics.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Rubic", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`routerCallNative` called the `router` field (attacker-set to the USDC token) with attacker data, executing `transferFrom(victim → attacker)` for 26 victims who had approved the Rubic proxies, draining their USDC.", "lesson": "Cross-chain router params must be validated against a whitelist — attacker-chosen routers turn approved allowances into drains.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "TIFI Finance", "date": "2022-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2022-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flash-swap 5 WBNB + 500 BUSD, deposit BUSD to TIFI, swap WBNB→BUSD on the protocol router to skew the WBNB/BUSD pair, then `borrow(TIFIToken)` — priced off that pair's `getReserves` — hands out excess TIFI which is dumped for WBNB before repayment.", "lesson": "Lending borrow limits read from live pair reserves are manipulable within one flash swap.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2022-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BEVO", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "BEVO is a reflective token whose deliver() burns the caller's reflected balance, inflating every other holder's token balance including the LP pair's. Attacker flash-borrows WBNB, swaps it for BEVO, calls deliver() twice and skim() on the BEVO-WBNB pair, then swaps the pair's excess BEVO for WBNB and repays the loan (144 BNB profit). The pair's reserves never matched its real balance, so the k-invariant check was satisfied by stale accounting.", "lesson": "Reflective/rebasing tokens whose deliver()/skim() can desync an LP pair's real balance from its reserves are an instant drain pattern — hunt for deliver()+skim() pairs.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "BRA", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "BRA's _transfer() charges a double tax when sender or recipient is the LP pair but never calls sync(), so the pair's reserves stay larger than its actual balance. The attacker flash-loans WBNB, buys BRA, transfers it all to the pair, and repeatedly calls skim() (101x) to harvest the tax-inflated excess, then sells BRA back to USDT/WBNB.", "lesson": "Any custom token fee/tax logic that mutates balances on LP-pair interactions without a following sync() leaves the excess skimmable — check tax tokens for missing sync().", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "GDS", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "GDS's ClaimReward flow let a deposit of LP tokens trigger claim logic keyed per contract with no lock or eligibility check. The attacker adds liquidity with flash-loaned USDT, splits LP+GDS across 100 freshly deployed helper contracts, and each helper's transferToken()/withdraw() burns a dust GDS amount and forwards the LP balance (plus swap proceeds) back to the attacker, then removes liquidity and repays.", "lesson": "Reward/vault logic that credits on deposit and can be split across unlimited fresh accounts with no time-weighting or lock is farmable — check that rewards are accrued per-user over time, not per-contract on demand.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Midas Capital", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Attacker deposits stMATIC as collateral and mints the WMATIC-STMATIC cToken, then triggers Curve's remove_liquidity on the MATIC/stMATIC pool, which re-enters via the ETH receive() callback while the pool (used by the oracle for the cToken price) is mid-update. During the callback the collateral price is inflated, so the attacker borrows all four Jarvis fx tokens (FJCHF/FJEUR/FJGBP/FAGEUR), then liquidates their own position and redeems the stMATIC.", "lesson": "Pricing collateral from a live pool that can be distorted mid-call via a token/ETH callback is a read-only-reentrancy oracle attack — verify oracle sources are reentrancy-safe or use TWAP.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "OmniEstate", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The staking pool's invest() accepted arbitrary parameters — the attacker invests with qty_ort=1 and end_date=0, then immediately calls withdrawAndClaim() on the created lock. The reward/lock math with an uninitialized end date granted a huge ORT payout relative to the 1-token stake, which the attacker swaps back to WBNB.", "lesson": "Audit reward and lock-time math for uninitialized or zero parameters (end_date=0, stake=1) that can grant outsized rewards.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "QTN", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "QTN charges a buy-time limit and taxes transfers to the LP pair, crediting the pair's balance without updating reserves. After buying QTN, the attacker transfers QTN to the pair 40 times and skims the tax-inflated excess into 40 helper contracts, waits out the _timeLimitFromLastBuy, then pulls the tokens back and swaps for WETH.", "lesson": "Same family as BEVO/BRA: tokens that credit LP-pair balances via tax/reflection without sync() are skimmable; check custom _transfer logic for pair-address branches.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Roe Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Using a victim's credit delegation (approveDelegation), the attacker deposits USDC and borrows the protocol's WBTC/USDC LP token, then loops deposit→borrow 49 times to stack the LP position without the health check re-validating capacity. The attacker then burns the LP on Uniswap to extract both WBTC and USDC, syncs the pair, and borrows USDC to repay the Balancer flash loan.", "lesson": "Borrow capacity/health must be re-checked on every deposit/borrow iteration, especially when the collateral itself is an LP token that can be burned for both sides.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "SHOCO", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SHOCO's deliver() burns the caller's share of reflected supply, raising the pair's token balance above its recorded reserves. The attacker buys SHOCO with WETH, delivers 99.999% of the balance to shrink supply, then swaps the pair's now-excess SHOCO back for more WETH than the purchase cost.", "lesson": "Reflection accounting (rTotal/rOwned rate) that lets a caller shrink supply while the LP pair holds rOwned shares is exploitable — verify deliver() cannot be called to game the pair.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Thoreum Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Thoreum's transfer implementation mis-accounted self-transfers: sending your full balance to yourself 15 times repeatedly grew the balance (each self-transfer credited fees/reflections on top of the amount). The attacker then swaps the inflated Thoreum for WBNB in chunks.", "lesson": "Test custom ERC20s for self-transfer (from == to) behavior — a transfer that credits the recipient without debiting the sender doubles balances.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "TINU (Tom Inu)", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flash-borrowed WETH is swapped to TINU (charging the pair fees), then the attacker calls deliver() on their whole balance, which inflates the pair's TINU balance relative to reserves; skim() harvests the excess; deliver+swap is repeated, and finally the pair's WETH side is drained via a direct pair swap.", "lesson": "Canonical deliver()+skim() reflection exploit — when auditing reflective tokens, check whether pair accounting can be driven apart and harvested in one transaction.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "UFDAO", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker buys the public offer of UF with USDC, then calls UFT.burn(amount, tokens, adapters, pools) which returns the underlying USDC (and more, due to the reward/fee accounting), and repeats buyPublicOffer→burn with a second size. The buy fee vs. burn payout asymmetry let the attacker walk away with more USDC than spent.", "lesson": "Audit buy→burn/claim loops for economic asymmetry — if the sum of payouts exceeds the input after fees, the loop is profitable and will be farmed.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Upswing", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "UPS prices trades via a custom \"pressure\" state that changes on every transfer. The attacker buys UPS, then transfers their whole balance to the LP pair and skims it back 8 times, pumping their own pressure counter (fake volume), then sells the same balance back for more WETH than it cost.", "lesson": "Tokens whose price/pressure state is driven by raw transfer events rather than reserves can be gamed with self-made transfer volume — check for off-reserve state influencing swaps.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "EFVault", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "ENF.redeem(shares, receiver) let any caller burn an arbitrary share amount and route the underlying USDC to any receiver, without checking the caller's share balance. The PoC holds a single 1e18 ENF and redeems 676,562 shares straight to the attacker, draining the vault's USDC.", "lesson": "Redeem/withdraw paths must validate the caller's share balance against the amount — a public unauthenticated redeem is a full drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Sperax USDs", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "USDs caches each address's balance and recalculates on first transfer from an address. The attacker mints 11 USDs, sends them to a not-yet-deployed address, then deploys code there (Gnosis Safe) and triggers a 1-wei transfer back — the balance-recalculation path for the fresh address credited the wrong amount, effectively duplicating the balance.", "lesson": "Rebase/balance-cache tokens must handle first-transfer-from-new-address edge cases; test the recalc path against addresses that received tokens before deployment.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "CowSwap (SwapGuard)", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SwapGuard.envelope() was meant to enforce solvency by checking token prices and balance changes, but it trusted caller-supplied prices (0) and an allowedLoss of max. The attacker encoded DAI.transferFrom(GPv2Settlement → attacker) as an \"interaction\" and the guard executed it with the settlement contract's allowance, draining its DAI.", "lesson": "Solvency guards must derive prices independently (not from caller input) and must never execute arbitrary calldata with third-party allowances.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Sheep", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SHEEP is a deflationary token with a public burn(). After a flash loan and a WBNB→SHEEP swap, the attacker repeatedly burns their whole SHEEP balance while the pair still holds SHEEP, then calls sync() so reserves match the shrunken supply picture, and swaps the pair's SHEEP for WBNB at a favorable rate before repaying.", "lesson": "Public burn() on deflationary tokens is a liquidity-drain primitive: burning shrinks supply and re-prices the pair in the attacker's favor — check for burn + sync combinations.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Starlink", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With flash loans from three DODO pools, the attacker swaps WBNB to Starlink directly on the pair (taking 51% of the pair's Starlink), then loops: transfer the pair's whole Starlink balance back to the pair (taxing it), skim() the excess, sync() — repeating until the pair is drained, then sells Starlink for WBNB.", "lesson": "Transfer-to-pair + skim + sync loops are the fingerprint of tax/reflection token drains; flag any protocol listing such tokens or holding their liquidity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Dexible", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Dexible.selfSwap() executed attacker-supplied \"router\" calldata with the contract's own privileges. The attacker set spender=Dexible and routerData=TRU.transferFrom(victim → attacker); because victims had approved Dexible, the swap contract's execution of that calldata moved the victims' TRU to the attacker (a 15 USDC fee paid for the privilege).", "lesson": "Aggregators that execute arbitrary user-supplied swap data while holding user approvals are allowance-theft vectors — the router/spender/calldata trio must be constrained.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "SwapX", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified SwapX aggregator's swap function (selector 0x4f1f05bc) let the caller pass an arbitrary victim address as the funds source. For each of 16 victims who had approved SwapX, the attacker routed BUSD→WBNB→DND swaps charged to the victim and received the DND, then sold the DND for WBNB.", "lesson": "Swap aggregators must hard-code msg.sender as the funds source — a caller-chosen \"from\" turns every existing approval into free money.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Revert Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "V3Utils.swap() accepted an arbitrary swapData executed against the tokenOut. The attacker passed a fake ERC20 whose transferFrom returns true without moving funds and whose balanceOf lies (1 then 0), and swapData = USDC.transferFrom(victim → attacker). The victim had approved V3Utils, so the contract's own allowance was spent to pay the swap while the fake-token balance check passed.", "lesson": "If a swap utility lets callers supply both the swap target and the token, attackers will pair lying tokens with real approvals — validate tokens and swap targets against whitelists.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "FDP", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After a DODO flash loan and a WBNB→FDP swap, the attacker calls FDP.deliver() on a huge amount, which redistributes reflected supply so the pair's real FDP balance exceeds its reserve accounting. A direct pair swap then drains nearly all the pair's WBNB before repaying the loan.", "lesson": "deliver()-based reflection tokens + LP pairs = drain; the pair must be excluded from reflection redistribution or the token excluded from pools.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "DYNA", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The staking contract paid rewards on deposit regardless of staking duration. The attacker seeds 200 helper contracts with deposits, then flash-borrows the pair's DYNA and loops: send DYNA to a helper, deposit, immediately redeem — each helper's withdraw() forwards its whole DYNA balance to the attacker, farming the per-deposit reward across all helpers before repaying the pair.", "lesson": "Rewards must accrue per-user per-second, and flash-loaned/instant deposits must never qualify — check whether deposit→redeem in one block yields profit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Platypus Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deposits USDC, stakes the LP-USDC in MasterPlatypus, borrows USP up to the borrow limit, then calls emergencyWithdraw() which removes the staked collateral WITHOUT repaying or re-checking the outstanding debt. The freed USDC is withdrawn and the borrowed USP is swapped to USDC/USDT/BUSD/DAI-E — a classic collateral-with-debt-out flow.", "lesson": "Every withdrawal path — including emergency/escape-hatch functions — must re-check solvency; emergencyWithdraw that ignores debt is a free borrow.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "LaunchZone", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same SwapX-style hole: the unverified swapX implementation executed a payload that swept LZ tokens from the deployer via the deployer's standing approval, crashing LZ's price on Biswap. The attacker then bought LZ with 50 BUSD on the crashed pool and sold on Pancake for ~88.9k BUSD.", "lesson": "Unverified aggregator contracts holding user allowances are prime targets — the exploit is \"call with victim as from, dump the tokens, arbitrage the resulting price gap\".", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Orion Protocol", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker lists an attacker-controlled token (ATK) whose transfer() re-enters Orion's depositAsset() whenever the attacker holds USDT. During swapThroughOrionPool(USDC→ATK→USDT), the pool's internal USDT accounting is updated after the external ATK transfer, so the reentrant deposit double-credits USDT, which the attacker withdraws and swaps to WETH.", "lesson": "Swap/deposit functions that touch attacker-controlled tokens must follow checks-effects-interactions — any external call before state update is a reentrancy window.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "dForce", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deposits wstETHCRV-gauge as collateral and borrows USX, then calls Curve's remove_liquidity on the wstETH/wstETHCRV pool. Curve's ETH callback fires while the pool's balances are mid-update, and in that window the price of the vWSTETHCRVGAUGE collateral (priced from the Curve pool) collapses, letting the attacker liquidate their own and a victim's borrow positions at the distorted price and redeem the gauge collateral.", "lesson": "Read-only reentrancy through Curve/Balancer callbacks while a lending market reads the manipulated pool price is a repeatable pattern (dForce, Midas, Sentiment, Sturdy) — check oracle reads inside token/ETH callbacks.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "BonqDAO", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The TellorFlex oracle for wALBT accepted fresh submissions with only a 10 TRB stake and no dispute window. Tx1: the attacker reports wALBT at an astronomically high price, opens a trove with 0.1 wALBT, and borrows 100M BEUR. Tx2: they report the price near zero and liquidate every other wALBT trove, seizing their collateral, then repay their own trove.", "lesson": "Oracles that let a staker submit an arbitrary price with a stake smaller than the borrowable profit are exploitable in both directions (mint high, liquidate low) — require TWAP/VWAP or a dispute window via getDataBefore.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "BIGFI", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans USDT, swaps to BIGFI, and computes the exact burn amount so the pair's BIGFI balance drops to ~1 while reserves stay recorded. After burning and calling sync(), the attacker swaps their BIGFI back to USDT at the old reserve ratio and repays the loan.", "lesson": "Burn/supply-reduction functions on tokens with LP pairs let attackers shrink the pair's redeemable balance while reserves remain — check burn arithmetic against pair reserves.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "DKP", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The closed-source DKP exchange priced USDT→DKP using the live ratio of the USDT-DKP pair balances. The attacker flash-borrows the pair's USDT (crashing the ratio), and in the callback a fresh contract exchanges 100 USDT for a massive amount of DKP at the distorted rate, then sells DKP back through Pancake for profit.", "lesson": "Spot-pair-balance ratios as exchange prices are one flash loan away from free money — require TWAP or a proper oracle.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Thena", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "ThenaRewardPool.unstake() accepted arbitrary token/pool parameters without verifying the caller had actually staked. The attacker's helper calls unstake(BUSD, 0, attackerPool, true) and immediately forwards the BUSD the reward pool pays out — claiming rewards for a non-existent staking position.", "lesson": "Reward pools must verify a real staked position for the (token, pool, user) tuple before paying; arbitrary params in claim/unstake are free-reward bugs.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Phoenix", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "PhxProxy.delegateCallSwap() executed arbitrary calldata via delegatecall with no access control. The attacker first calls buyLeverage() (which leaves WETH inside the proxy), then passes a Router swap that trades the proxy's own WETH balance into the attacker's token — because the delegatecall runs in the proxy's storage/context, the attacker spends the proxy's funds.", "lesson": "Never expose a delegatecall with caller-supplied data; if one exists, it spends the contract's own balances — this is an instant drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Euler Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Using eDAI as self-referential collateral, the attacker mints 200M eDAI and 200M dDAI debt, repays 10M, mints again, then donates 100M eDAI to reserves — an operation the health-check math did not account for. The donation made the violator account liquidatable, and the attacker liquidates their own position capturing the liquidation yield, then withdraws the remaining DAI (38.9M).", "lesson": "Health-factor and liquidation math must be invariant under mint/donate/repay sequences — audit token-accounting edge cases (donations, self-collateral) that break the collateral≥debt invariant.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "SafeMoon", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Two separate bugs in one contract: mint() was callable by anyone, so the attacker minted the bridge-burn address's entire balance of SFM; separately burn() was public, so the attacker burned the LP pair's SFM and the token's own balance, then synced and swapped the pair's WBNB out (27.4k WBNB).", "lesson": "mint/burn with public visibility is an infinite-mint/drain primitive — every mint/burn must be owner- or module-gated, and pair addresses must be excluded.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "DBW", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "DBW's static-income dividends are based on the percentage of LP a user holds at claim time, ignoring transfers and duration. The attacker flash-mints LP by donating USDT to the pair and calling Pair.mint, splits the LP across 18 mini-proxy clones, and each clone pledges the LP, claims getStaticIncome(), and redeems — farming dividends repeatedly per account before repaying.", "lesson": "Dividend systems must be time-weighted and per-deposit tracked; \"share of LP at claim time\" rewards can be farmed with flash-minted LP and account splitting.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "ParaSpace", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans wstETH, supplies it, and borrows cAPE; slave contracts then re-supply the borrowed cAPE as fresh collateral and borrow more cAPE, looping 8x to stack collateral on top of borrowed funds without the protocol re-validating solvency between steps. The final APE deposit into APE staking and 3 borrows (wstETH/USDC/WETH) leave the protocol under-collateralized.", "lesson": "Borrowed assets must never be immediately re-suppliable as their own collateral; add timelocks or per-step health checks to break circular collateral loops.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "ParaSpace — Paraspace_exp_2.sol", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "A cleaner reconstruction of the same attack: Aave flash loan of 47.35k wstETH, then 8 slave contracts each supply wstETH and borrow cAPE (the borrowed cAPE is re-supplied by the attacker as more collateral each round), APE is deposited into APE staking to pump the borrowable base, and finally wstETH/USDC/WETH are borrowed and swapped to repay the flash loan. ParaSpace's fix was a withdrawal-and-borrow timelock.", "lesson": "The same circular-collateral loop is visible twice in the PoC folder — when a protocol lets you borrow X against Y and instantly re-supply X as Y's collateral, it is leverage without limit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Poolz Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "CreateMassPools accepted a StartAmount of type(uint256).max - poolBalance + 2, which overflowed in the pool's accounting so the recorded pool amount wrapped while the protocol's real balance stayed intact. WithdrawToken() then paid out the entire pool balance — repeated for MNZ, SIP, WOD and ECIO, each funded by a 1 BNB purchase before selling back.", "lesson": "Pool-creation/withdraw amount math must be overflow-checked (Solidity ≥0.8 or SafeMath) — a wrap in \"amount owed\" is a whole-balance withdrawal.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "0vix", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "0vix's vGHST oracle price comes from vGHST.convertVGHST(), which reflects the GHST/vGHST backing ratio. After building a leveraged USDC position, the attacker donates 1.656M GHST straight to the vGHST contract, tripling the share price, then liquidates the leveraged position and redeems USDC collateral at the inflated valuation.", "lesson": "Share-price oracles derived from a token's own backing ratio (convertToAssets style) are manipulable by direct donation — treat balance-derived prices as attacker-controlled.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Allbridge", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The bridge's BUSD/USDT pools keep a vUsd balance vs. token balance used for swap pricing. The attacker flash-loans 7.5M BUSD, deposits 5M into the BUSD pool and 2M into the USDT pool, then bridge-swaps USDT→BUSD (breaking the pools' internal ratio), withdraws LP to amplify the imbalance, and finally swaps 40k BUSD for ~790k USDT at the corrupted rate.", "lesson": "Bridge liquidity pools whose internal vUsd/token rate can be driven apart by a deposit→swap→withdraw sequence pay out at a corrupt rate — check pool rebalancing invariants.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Allbridge — Allbridge_exp2.sol", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "An alternate PoC of the same incident that logs the corrupted rate explicitly: flash-borrow 7.5M BUSD, swap 2M+496.7k BUSD→USDT, deposit 5M BUSD and 2M USDT into the bridge pools, bridge-swap all USDT to BUSD, then withdraw 4.83B LP from the BUSD pool — after which the pool's vUsd/tokenBalance ratio shows ~3.2 vUSD per BUSD — so a 40k BUSD bridge-swap extracts ~790k USDT.", "lesson": "The second PoC makes the invariant visible: print the pool's vUsdBalance/tokenBalance ratio before and after each step when auditing bridge pools — an imbalanced ratio is the exploit state.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Axioma", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Axioma's presale buyToken() had no per-address cap or vesting. The attacker flash-loans 32.5 BNB, buys AXT at the fixed presale price, and immediately sells the whole allocation on Pancake for more than the purchase cost, repaying the loan.", "lesson": "Presales need per-address caps, whitelists and vesting — an uncapped fixed-price buy that can be flipped on a DEX in the same block is free arbitrage.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Hundred Finance (2nd hack)", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The hWBTC market was nearly empty, so the attacker mints a tiny WBTC share and donates 500 WBTC to the market, inflating the exchange rate so their dust share is worth the whole market. That overvalued share is then used as collateral to liquidate borrowers in ETH/SNX/USDC/DAI/USDT/sUSD/FRAX markets, seizing their collateral for a trivial repayment.", "lesson": "Donations to near-empty cToken markets inflate exchangeRateStored and mint attacker collateral from nothing — check market TVL before trusting exchange rates in liquidation math.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "MetaPoint", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Each MetaPoint user had a personal vault contract with a public approve() function that approved msg.sender to spend the vault's POT tokens. The attacker calls approve() on 11 victim vaults and then transferFrom() each vault's entire POT balance, swapping to USDT/WBNB.", "lesson": "Any function that grants an allowance must be owner-gated — a public approve() on vault contracts is a direct drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "OLIFE", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "OLIFE's reflection rate = rSupply/tSupply. The attacker flash-loans WBNB, buys OLIFE, self-transfers the balance 19 times (each taxed transfer shrinks tSupply), then calls deliver() to shrink further — the pair's reflected balance rises far above its recorded reserve, so a direct pair swap returns more WBNB than the purchase cost.", "lesson": "Self-transfer loops plus deliver() are the standard way to crush a reflection token's rate — check whether transfers to self or the pair mutate the supply accounting.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Paribus", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Paribus forked an old CompoundV2 with the known redeem reentrancy. After minting pETH and pUSDT collateral, the attacker calls pETH.redeem(); the ETH payout triggers receive(), where — before collateral accounting is finalized — they borrow all available USDT and WBTC. Two accounts are used to stack borrows during the reentrant window.", "lesson": "CompoundV2 forks predating the reentrancy fix are exploitable as-is — grep forks for redeem() without a reentrancy guard and for the borrow-after-redeem sequence.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Sentiment", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker opens a Sentiment account, deposits WETH, joins the Balancer wstETH/WETH pool via the account's exec(), then exits the pool — Balancer's exit triggers a callback while the pool balances are not yet updated, and the protocol's weighted-Balancer-LP oracle reads the inflated price. Borrowing against the overpriced LP collateral yields ~1M USDC-equivalent profit.", "lesson": "Balancer join/exit callbacks let an attacker query a stale inflated pool price inside the protocol's borrow path — require oracles to be safe against read-only reentrancy (or use manipulation-resistant pricing).", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Silo Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With the WETH silo nearly empty, the attacker deposits dust WETH and donates 1 WETH directly, so the accruing interest rate (computed on totalDeposits that include the donation) explodes; a second account deposits LINK as collateral-only and borrows WETH. After interest accrues, the attacker borrows the entire XAI reserve — the inflated accounting lets debt exceed true collateral value.", "lesson": "Interest accrual over attacker-donated balances plus cross-asset borrow limits = undercollateralized lending; donations must not enter the interest/base-rate calculation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "SushiSwap RouteProcessor2", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "processRoute() did not validate the pool address or the callback data. The attacker passes their own contract as the \"pool\"; when the router calls pool.swap(), the fake pool calls back into uniswapV3SwapCallback with data encoding (WETH, victim), and the router pays the callback by transferFrom the victim — who had approved the router — draining their WETH.", "lesson": "Routers must whitelist/validate pools and never trust callback-encoded payer addresses; a fake pool + real approval is a complete theft vector.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Swapos", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SWAPOS/WETH pair's swap() accepted trades violating the k-invariant. After depositing 3 ETH worth of WETH and sending 10 wei to the pair, the attacker calls swap(142e21 SWAPOS out, 0 WETH) — the pair lets a huge token0 amount leave against a dust donation, giving the attacker the pair's SWAPOS balance for free.", "lesson": "Verify pairs enforce the constant-product check on every swap — fee-on-transfer or stale-reserve flaws that skip the invariant check allow near-free withdrawals.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Yearn (yUSDT mispricing)", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker repays Aave v1 users' USDT debts to inflate the USDT APR, deposits 900k USDT into yUSDT, then mints bZx iUSDC against yUSDT's balanceAave and transfers the minted iUSDC into the yUSDT vault — inflating getPricePerFullShare(). Withdrawing at the inflated share price returns more USDT than deposited, which is swapped to DAI/USDC and kept.", "lesson": "Vault price-per-share that counts donated/loaned tokens into the strategy value is manipulable — audit what enters the share-price numerator (balance + balanceAave) for donation paths.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Melo", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MEL.mint(account, amount, txId) had no access control. The attacker mints 50x the LP pair's MEL balance to themselves and swaps it all for USDT on Pancake, leaving the pair worthless.", "lesson": "Public mint() = infinite mint; every mint must be gated and ideally behind a role with a supply cap.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "landNFT", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Miner contract's mint(to[], value[]) lacked any permission check. The attacker calls it with themselves as recipient and 200 as amount, minting 200 land NFTs (worth ~149.6k BUSD in XQJ) for free.", "lesson": "NFT minter contracts are the same bug class as token mints — check the miner/faucet contracts that call the NFT's mint for missing access control.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "DEI", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "DEI's burnFrom(pair, 0) could be called by anyone and, with the flawed burn/rebasing logic, let the caller then transferFrom the LP pair's entire DEI balance to themselves without holding or being approved by the pair. After the drain the attacker calls pair.sync() and swaps the stolen DEI for USDC.", "lesson": "burnFrom implementations must enforce allowance and rebase correctly — a burn entry point that also moves rebase value to msg.sender is a drain of any pair holding the token.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "FAPEN", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "FAPEN's unstake() checked the wrong account's balance (the token contract's own holdings rather than the caller's staked balance), so the attacker calls unstake(FAPEN.balanceOf(FAPEN)) while holding zero FAPEN and receives the contract's treasury FAPEN, which is sold for BNB.", "lesson": "Withdraw/unstake functions must validate the caller's staked amount, not the contract's own token balance — a swapped balance check is a treasury drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Bitpaidio", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The staking contract stored one lock timestamp per plan. The attacker first locks 0 BTP in plan 1, warps 6 months so the plan matures, then flash-borrows BTP, locks it all into the same plan and immediately withdraws — the second lock inherits the matured timestamp, so the attacker collects matured-plan rewards on flash-loaned funds.", "lesson": "Lock timestamps must be stored per-position and updated on every new lock — per-plan timestamps let new locks inherit maturity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "CS", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "CS computed its burn amount from a global sellAmount variable that was not updated correctly across trades. The attacker performs 99 small BUSD→CS buys, then sells CS in 3000-CS chunks interleaved with self-transfers, exploiting the stale sellAmount so the tax/burn charged is far lower than intended, netting BUSD.", "lesson": "Fee/burn state derived from global trade counters must be updated atomically per trade — stale globals let attackers under-pay tax while the pair over-pays out.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "ERC20TokenBank", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The bank's doExchange() priced USDC/USDT off the live Curve pool ratio. The attacker flash-borrows 120k USDC from a Uniswap V3 pool, skews the Curve pool by swapping USDC→USDT, calls doExchange() to buy USDT at the skewed rate, then swaps back and repays, pocketing the difference.", "lesson": "Exchange/token-bank functions that use a live pool's current ratio as the price are one flash swap away from being drained — enforce oracle-based pricing with slippage bounds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "NeverFall", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "NeverFall's buy() and sell() use a contract-internal price formula that diverges from the DEX price. The attacker buys 200k USDT worth via buy(), pumps the Pancake price with a 1.4M USDT swap, then sells 75.5M tokens back via sell() — the contract pays out more USDT than the tokens are worth, draining its reserves.", "lesson": "Protocol-native buy/sell pricing must be arbitrage-proof against the DEX price — a fixed internal rate plus an external pump is a money printer for the attacker.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "NOON", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "NOON's _transfer() was public instead of internal, so the attacker simply calls NO._transfer(pair, attacker, pairBalance-1) to move the pair's NOON to themselves, syncs the pair, transfers the NOON back, and swaps out the pair's WETH.", "lesson": "_transfer must be internal; a public _transfer lets anyone move any holder's tokens and desync pair accounting in one call.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "LFI", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker stakes 86k LFI on behalf of a claimer contract, then chains 200 \"delegate\" hops: each hop calls vLFI.claimRewards() and transfers the vLFI position to a fresh claimer. Because claiming does not reset the accrued reward counter, every hop re-claims the same accrued rewards, multiplying the payout.", "lesson": "claimRewards must zero out (or debit) the accrued amount on claim — a claim that leaves the accumulator intact can be replayed across any number of accounts.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "LocalTrader", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Two functions on the proxy — 0xb5863c10 (change owner) and 0x925d400c (set token price) — had no access control. The attacker sets themselves as owner, sets the LCT price to 1 wei, buys the exchange's entire LCT balance for dust via buyTokens(), and dumps it on Pancake for BNB.", "lesson": "Owner-transfer and price-setter functions must be onlyOwner — raw-selector calls against an unverified proxy are a quick way to probe for unguarded setters.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "LocalTrader", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "A storage-verifying reconstruction of the same hack: the PoC reads slot 0 (owner) and slot 3 (token price) with vm.load before and after the two calls, asserting the attacker's owner address and price=1 land in storage, then buys the exchange's LCT for dust and swaps to WBNB.", "lesson": "For unverified proxies, storage-slot diffs before/after each selector are a precise way to prove which setter does what — use this technique to confirm access-control bugs.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "GPT", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "GPT's transferFrom to the pair charges a fee that inflates the pair's GPT balance. The attacker flash-loans BUSD from five DODO pools, buys GPT, then loops 50 times: transferFrom(self → pair, 0.5 GPT) and skim() — harvesting the fee-inflated excess each round — then sells GPT back for BUSD.", "lesson": "Fee-on-transfer tokens that credit the pair on each transfer are skimmable in a loop — any token with pair-directed fees plus skim is the same exploit shape as the reflection family.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "LW", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The LW contract auto-swapped USDT for LW in its receive() at getTokenPrice(), a price the attacker can move. The attacker flash-borrows USDT, buys LW, then repeatedly transfers LW to the LP pair, calls thanPrice() and skim(), and sends 1 wei to trigger the receive-swap — each trigger buys LW at the stale cheap price and drains the contract's 3000-USDT market pot.", "lesson": "Contracts that auto-swap on receive() using a manipulable price are drained by \"manipulate → trigger receive → collect\" loops — check receive/fallback swap triggers.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "SellToken", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The shorting contract priced SELLC with the pair's getAmountOut spot quote. The attacker flash-loans WBNB, pumps SELLC with 99% of the loan, opens a short (ShortStart) at the inflated price, dumps SELLC to crash it, then closes the short (withdraw) — profiting from the difference between the inflated entry and the crashed exit, twice.", "lesson": "Short/long settlement must use TWAP or an independent oracle; spot getAmountOut pricing lets attackers enter at a pumped price and exit at a dumped one.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "BabyDogeCoin", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "FarmZAP.buyTokensAndDepositOnBehalf accepted an arbitrary farm contract and moved BABYDOGE/WBNB between Pancake and the farm using the ZAP's own balances/allowances. The attacker passes a fake farm (itself) whose stakeToken() lies (returns WBNB on the 3rd call), uses the ZAP to pump and drain the pair, and finally transferFrom's the ZAP's BABYDOGE and WBNB to itself (7.5M USD).", "lesson": "ZAP/helper contracts must not trust caller-supplied farm addresses or leave approvals on themselves — \"deposit-on-behalf\" with arbitrary farms turns the ZAP into the attacker's wallet.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "SELLC (QIQI staking)", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The staking contract's stake/claim accepted arbitrary token addresses without a whitelist. The attacker stakes USDT across 10 fresh referral-chain accounts, adds QIQI/TOKENA liquidity (TOKENA is an attacker-minted token) to the same factory, and each account's claim() prices the reward against the attacker-created pair — the contract pays out its QIQI stash at the inflated valuation.", "lesson": "Staking claim/stake functions must whitelist their token and pair parameters; caller-supplied tokens in the reward valuation are an oracle-in-your-pocket bug.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Level Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Level's referral controller claimMultiple() did not check for duplicate epochs in the array. After wash-trading with a flash loan to generate referral points (self-referrals), the attacker claims with an array of 2000 copies of the same epoch — the reward is paid once per array element, multiplying the payout ~2000x, repeated 11 times.", "lesson": "Batch claim functions must deduplicate array elements; a missing uniqueness check on epochs/IDs is a straight reward multiplier.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "SELLC", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The staking contract's addLiquidity/sell functions accepted any token addresses. The attacker registers their own contract and a minted SHITCOIN as a \"token\" via addLiquidity(), creates a pair of their contract vs. the protocol's SellQILP LP token, then loops sell(attackerToken, SellQILP, amount) — selling self-minted worthless tokens to the contract for real LP tokens.", "lesson": "Any sell/addLiquidity that accepts arbitrary tokens lets attackers sell fake tokens for the contract's real assets — token whitelists are mandatory.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "MultiChainCapital", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MCC's reflection rate is driven by rTotal/tSupply and transfers to an excluded-from-fee address. The attacker flash-loans WETH, buys MCC, then alternates swaps with deliver()/skim() (and transferFrom to the excluded address) across dozens of iterations — each iteration re-shrinks supply and harvests the pair's inflated MCC — before swapping out WETH.", "lesson": "The deliver()+skim()+excluded-address dance is the extended reflection exploit; when you see isExcludedFromFee/deliver on a token with an LP pair, assume it's drainable.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "HODLCapital", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same template as MultiChainCapital on HODL: flash loan WETH, buy HODL, loop pair-swaps with deliver() and skim() to an excluded-from-fee address (each loop shrinking supply and siphoning the pair's HODL), then swap the harvested HODL back to WETH for ~2.3 ETH profit.", "lesson": "Identical code shape across protocols is a fingerprint — once you know one reflection drain, grep new tokens for deliver/isExcluded/skim to find the next.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "SNK", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SNK minter computed rewards as the parent pool's rewardPerToken multiplied by the balance of all children bound to it. The attacker stakes 100 SNK in 10 parent templates, waits 20 days, then binds fresh child templates and stakes a flash-borrowed 80k SNK — each parent's rewardPerToken is applied to the new children's balances, paying out far more SNK than earned.", "lesson": "Reward math must accumulate per-user (userRewardPerTokenPaid + share × rate delta), not rate × current balance — the classic staking-reward double-count.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Jimbo Protocol", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 10k WETH and adds single-sided JIMBO liquidity to the highest bin of the Trader Joe-style LB pair, then swaps to push activeId above triggerBin and calls controller.shift(), which moves the protocol's liquidity/price into a favorable position. Buying JIMBO at the shifted price, shifting back, and selling drains the pair's WETH.", "lesson": "Liquidity-rebalancing controllers whose shift()/reset() logic trusts the current bin distribution can be steered by the attacker — model the rebalancing state machine, especially \"inverted shift\" edge cases.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "UnverifiedContract 0x9ad32", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified contract's deposit (0xe2bbb158) and withdraw (0xc3490263) pair let the attacker approve it BUSD, deposit, then withdraw to themselves — the withdraw path paid out the attacker's specified amount without a balance check, and a follow-up transferFrom drained the contract's BUSD (~6k).", "lesson": "Unverified contracts with deposit/withdraw selectors are a classic audit target — test whether withdraw pays arbitrary amounts/recipients without validating balances.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "BUNN", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "During a pair flash-swap callback, the attacker calls BUNN.deliver() which shrinks the pair's real BUNN balance below its recorded reserve; the attacker's swap then takes out 44 + 8 BNB of WBNB against the stale reserves.", "lesson": "deliver() inside a pair-swap callback is the minimal reflection drain — any reflective token pair is exploitable in exactly this shape.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Contract 0x7657 (sniper bot)", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The trading bot's sell flow (selector 0x0a8fe064) transferred USDT to a caller-supplied address based on caller-supplied parameters, and called back into the attacker's Sell() which performed USDT.transferFrom(bot → attacker). The attacker invoked it with a victim's balance as the amount and drained the bot's USDT (~1300).", "lesson": "Trading bots' sell/withdraw entry points must check msg.sender; a bot that pays out to arbitrary addresses is a public piggy bank.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "STRAC", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Contract 0x1f90's function (0x4a75084c) transferred its entire STRAC balance to a caller-supplied address with no access control; the attacker's fake ERC20 transferFrom returns true so the contract's internal check passes, and the attacker sells the STRAC for ETH.", "lesson": "Functions that transfer the contract's token balance to a parameter address must be owner-gated — combined with a lying token they are one call drains.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "DEPUSDT / LEVUSDC", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Both proxies expose approveToken(token, spender, amount) with no access control. The attacker calls it to approve themselves max USDT/USDC on each proxy, then transferFrom the proxies' full balances (~36k + ~69k USD).", "lesson": "approveToken-style helpers are self-approval machines when public — the audit question is simply \"who can call approve, and to whom?\".", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "NST (Milktech)", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The swapper's buyNST/sellNST pair maintained a fixed USDT↔NST ratio but mishandled allowances. The attacker flash-borrows 40k USDT, buys NST, sells it back, and then USDT.transferFrom(swapper → attacker, 31.5M) succeeds — the swapper's leftover approval of the attacker exposes its USDT balance, which is drained.", "lesson": "Swap contracts must revoke/reset allowances after each swap and never approve msg.sender of their own funds — leftover approvals are the drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "UN", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans BUSD, buys UN from the pair, then transfers UN back to the pair three times at 93%/90%/80% of the balance — each transfer's fee inflates the pair's UN balance — and skims the excess each time, then sells the accumulated UN for BUSD.", "lesson": "The transfer-to-pair + skim pattern works on any fee-on-transfer token; check whether the token's pair transfers are taxed without a sync.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "MyAi", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MultiSender.batchTokenTransfer took a caller-supplied _from and performed transferFrom(_from, ...) with the contract's allowance, without checking msg.sender == _from. The attacker passes a victim as _from and themselves as all 100 recipients, sweeping the victim's MyAi, then sells it in 100 swaps for WBNB.", "lesson": "Batch-send helpers must force _from = msg.sender or check the caller's authority over _from — an arbitrary _from spends every allowance the sender contract holds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "SELLC (miner)", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After registering with setBNB(), the attacker flash-loans WBNB, pumps SELLC's price, adds liquidity, then calls miner.sendMiner() — the miner pays out mining rewards valued at the current (pumped) SELLC spot price, so the attacker collects oversized rewards and unwinds.", "lesson": "Mining/reward payouts priced off current spot are flash-pumpable — reward valuation needs TWAP or fixed emission rates.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "VINU", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "VINU.addLiquidityETH(router, lpr, dev) was public and trusted the passed router/recipient. The attacker calls it 4 times with a fake router (whose addLiquidityETH is a no-op) and themselves as lpr — the token function's internal logic mints/credits VINU to the chosen address, which the attacker then dumps on Uniswap.", "lesson": "Token \"addLiquidity\" helpers that accept arbitrary router and recipient parameters mint to the caller — gate them or remove the parameters entirely.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Shido", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "ShidoLock converted SHIDOInu (9 decimals) into SHIDO (18 decimals) on demand via lockTokens()+claimTokens(). The attacker flash-loans 40 WBNB, buys SHIDOInu cheap on Pancake, adds fee-free liquidity to distort the pair, then locks and immediately claims — the conversion rate is misaligned with the market pools, and the minted SHIDO is sold back for WBNB.", "lesson": "Lock/claim conversion contracts that pay out at a fixed rate while the input token trades at a different market price are arbitraged instantly — reconcile conversion rates with real market prices.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Shido — SHIDO_exp2.sol", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "An annotated step-by-step reconstruction of the same 977-WBNB exploit: flash loan 40 WBNB → buy SHIDOInu on Pancake → add fee-free liquidity → lockTokens() then claimTokens() to convert to standard SHIDO → sell all SHIDO for WBNB, profiting from the price difference between the SHIDOInu and SHIDO pools.", "lesson": "Two PoCs for one incident is a reminder to audit the whole conversion pipeline (input pool, lock contract rate, output pool) — the profit comes from whichever leg is mispriced.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "ARA", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The ARA swap contract (0x135b43e9) executed swaps at the current spot price, paying to a fixed \"approved address\". The attacker first sells ARA to it for USDT, pumps ARA's price with a 1.2M USDT flash loan, then swaps USDT→ARA through it at the pumped price, and finally dumps the ARA — profiting from the contract's spot-priced fills.", "lesson": "Swap contracts that price fills at manipulable spot rates and honor third-party recipient addresses can be traded against both directions — check pricing and recipient validation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Abracadabra MIM/Spell", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "ZeroXStargateLPSwapper.swap() accepted arbitrary swapData and executed it through a 0x proxy while the swapper itself held USDT. The attacker deposits 3 USDT worth of sUSDT shares, then passes sellToLiquidityProvider calldata that sells the swapper's whole USDT balance on Curve for MIM with the attacker as recipient (~17k).", "lesson": "Swapper contracts that execute caller-supplied calldata against their own token balances let attackers spend the contract's funds as their own — validate the swap target and recipient.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "CFC", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After flash-borrowing USDT from five DODO pools, the attacker swaps to CFC on the pair, then loops 18 times: CFC.transfer(pair, pairBalance-1) (fee inflates the pair's balance) and skim() — harvesting the excess each round — then sells the CFC for SAFE and swaps to USDT.", "lesson": "The transfer-pair-balance + skim loop is the universal fee-on-transfer drain; grep for tokens whose _transfer taxes transfers into the pair without sync.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Compounder Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans DAI, deposits into cDAI and yDAI, and swaps yDAI to yUSDC/yUSDT/yTUSD on Curve; then withdraws cDAI and immediately calls StrategyDAICurve.deposit(), which re-deposits the DAI in a way that distorts the strategy's DAI/yDAI accounting. Reversing the Curve swaps and withdrawing yDAI returns more DAI than deposited (~30k profit).", "lesson": "Vault strategies whose deposit() re-invests withdrawn funds into the same accounting can be manipulated mid-transaction — audit the deposit/withdraw + strategy-deposit interaction for rate distortion.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "DDCoin", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The marketplace's listItem/sellItem allowed the attacker to list orders and then repeatedly sell DD bought on Pancake back to the marketplace at the stale listed price, and the marketplace's leftover allowance to the attacker let them transferFrom its BUSDT balance. The loop (buy DD → sellItem → drain allowance) runs 100 times for ~300k.", "lesson": "Marketplace order books must settle at current prices and never leave allowances on traders — \"list high, buy cheap, sell into your own listing\" is the drain shape.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Cellframe", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "LpMigration.migrate(amountLP) minted the new-pair LP without properly consuming the old CELL9 LP. The attacker flash-borrows newCELL, buys oldCELL and provides old-pair liquidity, then calls migrate() 8 times — each call converts the old LP to the new pair while the old LP remains, so burning the new Pancake LP returns both tokens and the old position is still redeemable.", "lesson": "Migration contracts must burn/consume the source LP in the same call they mint the destination LP — a mint-without-burn migration is a double-claim.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Midas Capital XYZ", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swaps ANKR out of the ankrBNB/ANKR V2 and V3 pools, pumping the ankrBNB price that the SimplePriceOracle reports. With HAY/BUSDT LP and ANKR as collateral in Midas's Compound-fork, they mint/borrow all available ankrBNB and HAY, unwind the swaps, and repay — profiting from the inflated collateral valuation (~600k).", "lesson": "Lending markets whose oracle reads a live LP-pair price for collateral are flash-pumpable in one transaction — require TWAP or multi-source oracles for any pool-backed collateral.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "BabyDogeCoin (2nd)", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "BABYDOGE automatically sells tokens and adds liquidity when its balance crosses numTokensSellToAddToLiquidity, without slippage protection. The attacker borrows BNB from Venus against flash-loaned USDT/BUSD, pushes the token over the threshold, lets the auto-swap fire, and sandwiches it with opposing trades through the FeeFreeRouter, capturing the price impact.", "lesson": "Token contracts with automatic swap-and-liquify triggers are sandwichable — the auto-swap executes at whatever price the attacker sets, so audit the threshold logic and slippage of auto swaps.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Biswap", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "V3Migrator.migrate() burned the LP of a caller-supplied pair and used caller-supplied token0/token1 without verifying they match the pair. The attacker points the migrator at a victim's pair but supplies fake tokens, so the migrator burns the victim's LP and transfers the real underlying tokens to itself; a second migrate() with a fake pair (whose burn returns the migrator's real balances) withdraws the stolen tokens.", "lesson": "Migrators must derive token0/token1 from the pair itself and verify LP ownership — caller-supplied pair/token parameters let an attacker burn other people's LP and cash out.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Pawnfi", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 200k APE, mints sAPE→isAPE as collateral in Pawnfi's Compound-fork, borrows PBAYC (a BAYC-backed token), and converts it to a real BAYC NFT via randomTrade. With ApeStaking1.setCollectRate(1e18) callable by anyone, they crank the collect rate, stake the borrowed NFT, and loop deposit/withdraw 20x to harvest APE staking rewards, also borrowing ETH against the collateral (~820k).", "lesson": "setCollectRate-type configuration setters must be owner-gated, and NFT-backed lending must prevent borrowed NFTs from farming external staking rewards.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Themis", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Themis (Aave v2 fork) priced wstETH via an on-chain feed reading the wstETH/WETH Balancer pool. The attacker supplies 220 WETH as collateral, then pumps the Balancer pool by swapping ~39.7k WETH into wstETH, reads the inflated aggregator price, and borrows the entire WETH (plus DAI/USDC/USDT/ARB/WBTC) reserves against the overpriced collateral (~370k).", "lesson": "A lending market whose collateral price comes from a pool you can imbalance with one swap is fully borrowable — audit which pool the oracle aggregates and its manipulability.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "Sturdy Finance", "date": "", "chain": "", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans wstETH/WETH, joins the Balancer B-stETH-STABLE pool and deposits 1000 steCRV + 233 BPT as Sturdy collateral, then borrows 513 WETH. During Balancer's exitPool, the ETH receive() callback fires while pool balances are stale — the BPT price reads ~3x higher — and the attacker un-collateralizes the steCRV, withdraws it, and self-liquidates the BPT position for more than its worth (~800k).", "lesson": "Balancer exitPool's token callbacks give a window where collateral prices read inflated — lending markets must price LP collateral manipulation-resistant (TWAP/queries) or block reentrant price reads.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h1.md"}, {"protocol": "ApeDAO", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Attacker chains five DODO flash loans of BUSDT, swaps BUSDT for APEDAO, then repeatedly transfers APEDAO into the Pancake pair and calls skim() — the token's reward logic credits the pair on transfer, so the pair balance grows beyond its reserves and each skim withdraws the excess. Calling goDead() then burns the pair's APEDAO, spiking the price, after which the attacker swaps APEDAO back to BUSDT via a low-level Pair.swap at the inflated price.", "lesson": "When a token credits fees/rewards to arbitrary receivers on transfer, an attacker can aim those credits at an AMM pair and skim the surplus to skew reserves.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Arcadia Finance", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Funded by Aave V3 flash loans of WETH and USDC, the attacker creates a fresh vault via the factory with a self-chosen salt, opens a margin account, deposits collateral and borrows via doActionWithLeverage. vaultManagementAction then hands the vault's assets to an attacker helper contract whose rekt() first drains them and then calls liquidateVault — re-entering the pool while the vault's accounting is mid-flight because vault management lacked a reentrancy lock.", "lesson": "Vault-management callbacks that can re-enter liquidation or accounting functions must be protected by a reentrancy lock.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "AzukiDAO (Bean token)", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Bean token's claim() did not invalidate already-used signatures; the attacker replayed one valid claim signature 200 times, each call minting 31,250 BEAN (total ~6.25M BEAN) by reusing the same r/s/v and claim parameters.", "lesson": "Signature-based claim functions must mark signatures as consumed so a valid signature cannot be replayed for repeated mints.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BNO (BNO NFT staking pool)", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "In the Pancake pair's pancakeCall (BNO flash swap), the attacker loops 100 times: stake two NFTs, pledge all held BNO, call emergencyWithdraw() — which refunds the pledge but leaves the NFTs registered as staked — and then unstakeNft() to reclaim the NFTs anyway. Each cycle returns more BNO than it consumes, draining the pool; the attacker finally repays only part of the flash-swapped BNO and keeps the difference.", "lesson": "Emergency/withdraw paths must clear all staking state, otherwise a refund-without-unstake flow becomes a free reward printer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Bamboo", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After buying up most of the pair's bamboo with WBNB, the attacker loops 10,000 transfers of ~1.34e18 bamboo into the pair and calls skim() each time; the bamboo token credits the pair on every transfer, so the pair's real balance keeps inflating above its reserves and skim harvests the surplus. The inflated reserves then let the attacker sell the accumulated bamboo back for far more WBNB.", "lesson": "Tokens that credit balances to recipients on transfer let attackers inflate a pair's balance and harvest it with skim — never trust balanceOf-reserve equality on such pairs.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Bao Finance", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Using Aave flash-loaned USDC and DAI, the attacker joins the bSTBL pool, mints 5 bdbSTBL with a tiny seed, redeems 3, and donates ~34.8M bSTBL to the bdbSTBL contract to inflate its exchange rate. Against the inflated rate he borrows 41.3 WETH from bdbaoETH, redeems almost all underlying, and exits the pool, repaying the flash loan with profit.", "lesson": "Exchange-rate-based lending must not let donated underlying inflate the rate — price borrows off real, non-donatable collateral value.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "CIVNFT", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "CIVNFT's 0x7ca06d68 position-adding function accepts an arbitrary pool address and calls its mint(); the attacker supplies a fake \"Uniswap V3 pool\" whose mint() triggers CIVNFT's own uniswapV3MintCallback (0xd3487997) with attacker-chosen calldata. The callback transfers the victim's entire CIV balance — approved to CIVNFT — to the attacker.", "lesson": "Mint callbacks must verify msg.sender is the genuine pool, and protocol-held user approvals must be scoped to trusted pools only.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Carson", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With BUSDT chained through five DODO flash loans, the attacker buys Carson and then runs 50 small sells of 5,000 Carson back to BUSDT through the project's custom router; the custom pair's flawed fee/reward economics return slightly more per trade than they take, so the repeated sells accumulate BUSDT before a final dump of the remaining Carson.", "lesson": "Custom AMM pairs with non-standard fee math are per-trade profit machines — test repeated small swaps for net-positive returns instead of auditing the pair's headline pricing alone.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Civfund (CivTrade)", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Two CivTrade functions — 0x5ffe72b7 (adds LP with an attacker-supplied pool) and uniswapV3MintCallback — have no access control. The attacker calls the first 31 times; each call mints through the fake pool, whose callback invokes uniswapV3MintCallback(0, victimBalance, data) to sweep tokens (USDT, BONE, WOOF, LEASH, SANI, ONE, CELL, USDC, SHIB) that 31 victims had approved to the contract.", "lesson": "Any callback that moves approved funds is an attack surface; mint/pool callbacks must be restricted to the real pool and the amounts must match the caller's own deposit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Conic Finance (crvUSD Omnipool)", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Funded by a Balancer flash loan (USDC, WETH, USDT), the attacker opens a crvUSD loan with WETH collateral, swaps crvUSD to USDC/USDT to push crvUSD's price down, deposits into the Conic omnipool (which re-adds liquidity and pushes price down further), then swaps back into crvUSD at the depressed price. Withdrawing cncCRVUSD and repeating the deposit/withdraw sandwich three times accumulates crvUSD faster than it is spent, and the loan is repaid with the remainder kept.", "lesson": "Omnipool share prices read from live curve pools during a deposit/withdraw cycle can be sandwiched within one transaction — price shares with a manipulation-resistant oracle.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Conic Finance (ETH Omnipool, variant A)", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "During a Balancer flash loan, the attacker repeatedly deposits WETH into the Conic ETH omnipool and swaps cbETH/rETH, then removes liquidity from the Lido stETH, cbETH/ETH and rETH/ETH Curve pools; each remove_liquidity sends ETH into his receive() mid-call, where he reads the distorted LP price via the oracle and calls handleDepeggedCurvePool() to flag pools as depegged. With weights skewed, he withdraws 6,292 cncETH at the manipulated share price and keeps the difference.", "lesson": "State-changing pool-config calls (like handleDepeggedCurvePool) reachable from a reentrant callback while prices are mid-update let attackers rewrite oracle weights — treat such callbacks as read-only-reentrancy surface.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Conic Finance (ETH Omnipool, variant B) — Conic_exp2.sol", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same incident as Conic_exp.sol, replayed with raw Vyper selectors: during remove-liquidity ETH callbacks from the Lido and rETH/cbETH Curve pools, receive() calls handleDepeggedCurvePool for two pools and finally withdraws 6,292 cncETH from the Conic pool at the price distorted by the in-flight liquidity removal.", "lesson": "Two independent PoCs of the same hack confirm the pattern — protect oracle reads from reentrancy during remove_liquidity ETH callbacks.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Curve Finance (pETH pool, Vyper lock bug) — Curve_exp01.sol", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 80,000 WETH from Balancer, the attacker adds 40,000 ETH liquidity to the pETH/WETH Curve pool and then calls remove_liquidity; the pool's ETH refund re-enters his receive(), which calls add_liquidity again while the pool's totals are already updated — the re-added liquidity is accounted against stale balances. The attacker then removes the extra LP and swaps out pETH for a profit, repaying the flash loan.", "lesson": "Reentrancy protection must cover the entire balance-update window, and compiler-level lock bugs (Vyper 0.2.15/0.2.16) mean deployed bytecode must be re-verified, not just source.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Curve Finance (CRV/ETH pool, Vyper lock bug) — Curve_exp02.sol", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 10,000 WETH from Balancer, the attacker loops 20 times: add_liquidity(400 ETH), remove_liquidity — whose ETH callback re-enters add_liquidity and an ETH→CRV exchange with the broken reentrancy lock — then remove_liquidity_one_coin and CRV→ETH swap. Each iteration nets extra ETH from the pool's stale accounting until the flash loan is repaid with profit left over.", "lesson": "The same Vyper lock bug was exploitable across multiple pool shapes; when auditing Vyper pools from that era, verify the compiled bytecode's locks rather than trusting the source.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "FFIST", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "FFIST's airdrop-on-transfer credits a \"random\" receiver computed from lastAirdropAddress, block.number and the pair address; the attacker computes that receiver deterministically, triggers a zero-value transfer so the pair's balance inflates, and calls sync() to align reserves with the inflated balance. He then sells FFIST back through the router at the boosted price for more WBNB than he bought it with.", "lesson": "Pseudo-random airdrop recipients derived from public state can be aimed at AMM pairs to inflate reserves — sync/skim against such tokens is a price-manipulation primitive.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "GYMNET", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker adds liquidity for a GYMNET/fakeUSDT pair he controls, then for 18 victims calls GymRouter.swapExactTokensForTokensSupportingFeeOnTransferTokens with amountIn set to each victim's GYMNET balance and the victim as recipient — the custom router spends the victims' approved GYMNET and delivers worthless fakeUSDT. He then removes his liquidity, keeping the real value.", "lesson": "Custom router functions must verify the spender actually authorized the amount — never let a caller spend tokens using another address's balance/approval.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "LUSD (loan mint via spot price)", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The loan contract priced BTCB collateral with the BTCB–BSC-USD pair's getAmountsOut; the attacker flash-swaps 1.25M BSC-USD from the pair, supplies BTCB as collateral so the loan mints an inflated amount of LUSD based on the manipulated quote, and withdraws the pool's LUSD. Repaying the swap with only ~800K BSC-USD leaves the inflated LUSD as profit.", "lesson": "Collateral priced by a live AMM getAmountsOut quote is manipulable in one transaction — use time-weighted prices for loan issuance.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Libertify", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Libertify's deposit() forwards arbitrary data to a 1inch executor whose callBytes invokes the attacker's fallback mid-deposit; the fallback re-enters deposit() while the first call's share accounting is in flight. Both deposits compute shares against the same stale totalSupply, over-minting shares, which exit() then redeems for most of the vault's USDT and WETH (funded by two 5M USDT Aave flash loans).", "lesson": "Share-minting entry points that trigger arbitrary external calls must be nonReentrant, or the mint math can be doubled against a stale totalSupply.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Minto Finance", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The referral crowdsale's buyTokens() accepts a caller-chosen paymentToken; the attacker passes his own ERC20 whose transferFrom() returns true without moving any funds. The sale contract mints 12,100 BTCMT for free, which the attacker dumps for BUSD via Pancake V3.", "lesson": "Never trust transferFrom return values as proof of payment — measure the balance delta of a whitelisted payment token instead.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "NewFi", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After flash-swapping BUSD from three Pancake V3 pairs, the attacker calls StakedV3.Invest(), which prices and rebalances its position against the pairs whose liquidity he just removed mid-swap; the price distortion lets him invest BUSD, have the contract swap BUSD→USDT at a favorable rate, then swap USDT back to BUSD for a profit before repaying the flash.", "lesson": "Staking/investment functions that price assets against the same pool being flash-swapped are one-transaction price-manipulation targets.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Palmswap", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 3M BUSDT from Radiant, the attacker purchases PLP at the 1:1 USDP rate, transfers 2M BUSDT to the vault and mints USDP via buyUSDP; redemption via unstakeAndRedeemPlp, however, pays USDP at a 1.9x rate, and selling that USDP back through sellUSDP realizes the difference. The mint and redeem paths priced the same assets at different exchange rates.", "lesson": "Mint and redeem sides of a stable-token system must share one exchange-rate source or the spread becomes free arbitrage for attackers.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Platypus Finance", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With an 85K USDC Aave flash loan, the attacker deposits USDC to mint LP priced at the USDC-LP ratio, then calls withdrawFromOtherAsset to receive USDT.e — but the withdrawal amount is computed against the USDT.e-LP ratio, which is slightly different from the USDC-LP ratio. Swapping the withdrawn USDT.e back to USDC realizes the valuation gap.", "lesson": "Cross-asset LP accounting must use a single consistent valuation per pool, or the delta between ratios is exploitable arbitrage.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Rodeo Finance", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker sandwiched the Camelot V2 ETH-unshETH pool across blocks to skew its TWAP, which averages only the last four price observations updated every 45 minutes. Rodeo's Investor.earn trusted that oracle for LP pricing, so the attacker bypassed the health-factor check, opened a position with borrowed USDC, forced large USDC→WETH→unshETH swaps, and then traded back against the price difference for profit.", "lesson": "Sparse short-window TWAPs (few samples, long update intervals) can be manipulated across blocks — check sample counts and update cadence, not just \"TWAP\" in the name.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "SUT", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SUT token sale's tokenPrice() did not track the live market price; with a 10 WBNB DODO flash loan the attacker bought out the sale's entire SUT balance at the stale price (6.85 BNB) and immediately sold the SUT into the Pancake V3 pool at the higher market price, repaying the loan with profit.", "lesson": "Token-sale pricing must be anchored to a manipulation-resistant market price, or the sale becomes a cheap-source-to-dump arbitrage.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "USDTStakingContract28", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The staking contract's tokenAllowAll(asset, allowee) is public and permissionless, so the attacker called tokenAllowAll(USDT, attackContract) to make himself an approved spender of the contract's entire USDT balance, then transferFrom'd it all out.", "lesson": "Functions that grant token allowances over protocol funds need owner-only or role-based access control.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Utopia", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same family as FFIST: the attacker computes Utopia's deterministic airdrop-recipient seed from lastAirdropAddress/block.number/pair, skims the pair's surplus into the computed address, and syncs reserves so the pair's Utopia valuation is skewed. Tiny transfers into the pair plus a 32-token sell then extract WBNB at the manipulated price.", "lesson": "When airdrop recipients are computable from on-chain state, skim+sync turns them into a reserve-manipulation lever against the token's own pair.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "WGPT", "date": "2023-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker pairs WGPT against his own ExpToken and funds the attack with chained DODO/Pancake V3 flash loans. Because WGPT burns 20% on transfers, he loops transferFrom(WGPT→pair) + skim() to shrink the pair's supply and inflate WGPT's price, then swaps his remaining WGPT for BUSDT at the boosted price, repaying the flash loans and keeping the spread.", "lesson": "Burn-on-transfer tokens allow supply destruction inside pairs to be harvested via skim — when a token pair includes such mechanics, reserve-based pricing is attacker-controllable.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BTC20", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Funded by a 300 ETH Balancer flash loan and flash-borrows from the SDEX/BTC20 and BTC20/WETH Uni V3 pools, the attacker buys BTC20 and then calls PresaleV4.buyWithEthDynamic() for maxTokensToSell() - directTotalTokensSold() — the presale's remaining-allocation counter did not track all previously sold tokens, so the attacker bought the leftover allocation with the borrowed ETH at a stale price. He then dumps the BTC20 for WETH and repays the loans.", "lesson": "A presale's \"remaining tokens\" must be computed from one consistent sold-token counter, or the delta can be bought out cheaply in a single transaction.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Balancer (boosted pools)", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With a 300K USDC Aave flash loan, the attacker drains all aUSDC from the bb-a-USDC pool, then in one batchSwap borrows almost the entire bb-a-USDC virtual supply and swaps a dust amount back out — the dust swap computes zero USDC output due to precision loss but inflates the bb-a-USDC share price. Using the inflated rate he swaps bb-a-USDC for bb-a-DAI and bb-a-USDT in the bb-a-USD pool, then resets the rate to 1 and repays the batch-swap borrow.", "lesson": "Rate = assets/virtualSupply math can be inflated by sub-unit swaps when virtual supply is nearly zero — rounding must always favor the protocol.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Curve Finance (CurveBurner sandwich)", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "CurveBurner.execute() is a permissionless function that adds only-USDT liquidity to 3pool and swaps USDT for DAI/USDC with no slippage protection. The attacker front-runs it with remove_liquidity_imbalance to strip DAI/USDC, lets execute() swap USDT into the skewed pool, then back-runs by adding DAI/USDC and removing USDT, profiting from the imbalance (funded by Balancer/Aave/Compound borrows).", "lesson": "Keeper-style swap functions without minimum-return parameters are free sandwich targets — require slippage bounds even for internal maintenance flows.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "EAC", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With a 300K USDT DODO flash loan the attacker buys EAC, then calls the proxy's 0xe6a24c3f buy function passing the proxy's own USDT balance as the \"paid\" amount — the function credited him EAC for USDT he never transferred. He then sells the EAC back to USDT, repays the loan and keeps the difference.", "lesson": "Purchase/bond functions must pull payment from msg.sender and compute the amount from the actual balance delta, never from a caller-supplied value.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "EHIVE", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "EHIVE's stake() added stakeAmount to 'staked' BEFORE computing 'earned', so a staker with a 0-value position could wait, then stake any amount and be credited rewards for the whole elapsed period as if the new stake had been earning all along. The attacker opened 28 fresh staker contracts with stake(0), waited 38 days, then chained stake→unstake through all of them to multiply the retroactive rewards and dumped the EHIVE for WETH (Aave flash loan).", "lesson": "Always accrue rewards against the pre-update staked amount — updating state before calculating earned turns time into a free mint.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Earning Farm (ENF_ETHLEV)", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The vault's withdraw() sends ETH (via the Controller) before burning the corresponding shares; during that ETH transfer the attacker's receive() moves his shares to a helper contract, so the burn destroys only the dust left behind while the helper still holds shares for the withdrawn assets. The helper then withdraws those assets too, and the loop repeats against Uni V3 flash-loaned WETH until the vault is nearly drained.", "lesson": "In vault withdraw flows, burn shares before any external value transfer, or a callback can smuggle the shares away and double-withdraw.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Exactly Protocol", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "DebtManager.leverage() accepted a caller-supplied Permit struct and set its global _msgSender to the permit's account without verifying the permit originated from that account, letting the attacker impersonate 8 victims through 16 attacker-deployed fake markets and invoke crossDeleverage to drain their USDC into fake tokens. The attacker then deposited into exaUSDC and borrowed/ repaid at six maturities to burn the pool's accrued earnings (backupEarnings), lowering the share price until each victim's collateral fell below their debt, after which he liquidated all 8 positions.", "lesson": "Periphery contracts that set a \"global msg.sender\" from a permit must cryptographically verify it, and liquidation thresholds must not depend on share prices that borrow/repay cycles can deflate.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "GSS", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 30K USDT from DODO, the attacker buys GSS, transfers a huge amount into the GSS/USDT pair (the token credits the pair on transfer), skims the pair's excess into the GSS/GSSDAO pool, syncs the pair, and then skims the GSSDAO pool's own excess to himself. He finally sells his remaining GSS at the inflated price for more USDT than the flash loan.", "lesson": "When a token's transfer mechanics credit recipients, skim() between a pair and a reward pool lets an attacker double-harvest and inflate reserves.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "LeetSwap", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "LeetSwap's pair exposed _transferFeesSupportingTaxTokens(), which moves the pair's own axlUSDC to the fee recipient and was callable by anyone. After buying axlUSDC with 0.001 ETH, the attacker called it with the pair's nearly entire axlUSDC balance, then sync()ed the pair and sold his axlUSDC back at the drastically inflated WETH price.", "lesson": "Underscore-prefixed helper names do not make functions private — audit visibility modifiers on every function that moves protocol funds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Neutra Finance", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With a 1,000 WETH Balancer flash loan, the attacker buys NEU and adds liquidity to the NEU/WETH Camelot pair, then swaps 849 WETH for NEU to skew the pair's reserves. Calling convert() prices his NEU LP against the manipulated reserve ratio and hands him NEU1 LP; burning that LP and selling NEU1 for WETH nets more than the flash loan.", "lesson": "Conversion functions that price tokens from live pool reserves in the same transaction are manipulable — anchor conversion rates to a TWAP or external oracle.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "SVT", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SVT pool's buy() did not reprice between consecutive purchases, so the attacker's second buy() got the same low price as the first. Selling the second batch and 62% of the rest back at the current higher price repaid the DODO flash loan with profit, leaving the attacker 38% of the SVT for free.", "lesson": "Buy/sell contracts must update their price state after every fill — stale pricing between calls is free arbitrage.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Uwerx", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After buying WERX with 20,000 WETH, the attacker transfers ~4.4M WERX into the pair (whose fee mechanics credit the pair on transfer), skims the surplus to 0x01 (burn), and syncs — shrinking the pair's WERX side and inflating its price. He then sells his WERX back at the boosted price, repaying the mocked flash loan with ~176 ETH profit.", "lesson": "Fee-on-transfer tokens that credit pairs on transfer make skim/sync a reserve-shrinking price pump — treat such pairs as attacker-controllable.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Zunami (UZD)", "date": "2023-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "UZD's balanceOf values its staker's pending SDT rewards through a Sushi getAmountsOut spot quote, so the attacker donates SDT to MIMCurveStakeDao, pumps the SDT price by swapping 10,000 WETH into it, and calls cacheAssetPrice() to rebase — inflating his own UZD balance. He then swaps UZD back into crvFRAX/crvUSD, removes liquidity, and exits through the USDC pools, funded by Uni V3 and Balancer flash loans.", "lesson": "Rebasing tokens that price staked rewards via spot router quotes are one-transaction donation+pump targets — use TWAPs and exclude donations from reward valuation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "0x0 DEX (privacy pool)", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The privacy pool's hand-rolled ring-signature check accepted a forged withdrawal signature (public key set to the curve's generator with matching s values), letting the attacker withdraw arbitrary deposits. He first force-sent ETH so the pool balance became a multiple of 10 (the denomination the math rounds on), deposited 10 ETH and withdrew in Swap mode, then looped small 0.1 ETH deposits with further withdrawals until the pool's ETH was drained.", "lesson": "Hand-rolled ZK/ring-signature verification is a minefield — verify the math against a reference implementation and fuzz denomination rounding and key-image reuse.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "APIG", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "APIG's transfer() credited the recipient a reflection reward computed off the sender's balance, so transferring your entire balance to yourself minted you an equal amount — doubling each iteration. The attacker looped self-transfers until his balance exceeded the threshold, then dumped the exponentially grown APIG into the APIG/BUSD and APIG/BETH pairs, repaying the flash swap with profit.", "lesson": "Reflection logic must exclude self-transfers (or the sender's own balance) from the reward base — self-transfer doubling is a classic mint-until-huge bug.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BFC", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 400K BUSDT flash-swapped from the BUSDT/WBNB pair, the attacker buys BFC and loops up to 100 times: transfer BFC into the pair (whose balance the token credits on transfer), skim the surplus, then a zero transfer to re-trigger the credit. After inflating reserves he sells BFC back for more BUSDT than borrowed.", "lesson": "The transfer-to-pair-then-skim loop keeps recurring on BSC — before trusting a pair's reserves, check whether the token credits recipients on transfer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "CEXISWAP", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "CEXISWAP's initialize() was never locked after deployment, so the attacker called it to make his own contract the admin and treasury. He then called upgradeToAndCall() to upgrade the proxy to an implementation whose function delegatecalls in proxy context and transfers the proxy's entire USDT balance to him.", "lesson": "Proxies must be initialized atomically with deployment or lock the initializer — a forgotten public initializer hands over admin and upgrade rights.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "DAppSocial", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After depositing 2M USDT/USDC, the attacker's helper locks tokens to an \"alt account\" with length 0, the attacker pulls 1M out via withdrawTokensWithAlt referencing the helper, and the helper then calls withdrawTokens(token, balanceOf(entire contract)) — the withdrawal path never validated the caller's own locked balance and paid out the contract's full holdings.", "lesson": "Withdraw functions must settle against the caller's tracked balance, and alt-account paths must verify ownership — caller-supplied amounts are never a substitute for state.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "DEXRouter", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim's functionCallWithValue(target, data, value) was permissionless, so the attacker pointed it at his own contract with value equal to the router's whole BNB balance — the router forwarded its entire balance to the attacker's payable function. A companion update() also let him set the callback target to his own address.", "lesson": "Arbitrary call/value-forwarding helpers must be owner-only; letting anyone choose target and value against protocol funds is an instant drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Firebird", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker alternated WMATIC↔HOPE swaps to move the FireBird pair's reserves, then triggered the reserve fund's collectFeeFromProtocol()/sellTokensToUsdc(), which sold WMATIC for USDC at the manipulated price with no slippage protection. Three cycles against Balancer flash-loaned WMATIC extracted the value before repaying.", "lesson": "Permissionless protocol-reserve sells need minimum-return bounds — without them every fee collection is a sandwich opportunity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "FloorDAO", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With floor flash-borrowed from its Uni V3 pool, the attacker loops stake→unstake with _trigger=true (forcing a rebase) up to 17 times while attacker+staking balances exceeded sFloor's tracked circulating supply; each cycle's rebase minted him more floor than it should. He then dumped the surplus floor back into the pool and kept the difference.", "lesson": "Rebase/unstake flows must reconcile against a circulating-supply invariant — a stake/unstake loop whose balance grows every iteration is broken accounting.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "HCT", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 2.2 WBNB flash-loaned, the attacker buys HCT, then repeatedly burns 80% of his remaining HCT to shrink total supply, syncs the pair, and sells the remaining HCT at the deflated-supply price for more WBNB than borrowed.", "lesson": "A public burn on a small-cap token is a price-pump primitive — pairs with tokens that allow unrestricted self-burn can have their price engineered upward.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Heavens Gate", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swaps HATE from its ETH pair and loops stake→unstake(_rebase=true) as many times as the calldata byte instructs; each iteration grows the HATE balance because the rebasing stake/unstake path rounds in the user's favor. The enlarged balance is then sold for ETH.", "lesson": "Olympus-style rebase staking where unstake-with-rebase returns more than staked is a free-token loop — verify stake/unstake rounding direction per cycle.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "JumpFarm", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 15 WETH from Balancer, the attacker buys JUMP and loops 40 stake→unstake(_rebase=true) cycles, each growing his JUMP balance via the same rebase rounding bug, then sells everything back to WETH and repays the flash loan.", "lesson": "Same rebase-loop primitive as Heavens Gate/QuantumWN — when auditing forked rebase-staking contracts, test whether repeated stake/unstake cycles monotonically increase the balance.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Kub Split", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Split.setPair() was callable by anyone, so the attacker repointed the token's reward pair to a fakeUSDC/Split pair he seeded himself, fixing Split's price; zero-amount self-transfers minted him Split, and skims from the BUSDT/Split and KUB/Split pairs grew his holdings. He then repeatedly invoked the staking-reward contract's sell() to convert the inflated Split/KUB into real BUSDT from the reward pools, funded by chained DODO flash loans.", "lesson": "A permissionless setter that repoints the pair a reward token prices against is an oracle takeover — make such config owner-only and validate the new pair.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "QuantumWN", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Balancer-flash-loaned WETH is swapped to Fumog, then 40 stake→unstake(_rebase=true) cycles grow the Fumog balance via rebase rounding before the attacker sells back to WETH and repays.", "lesson": "The identical rebase-stake loop hit three Olympus-style forks in one month — treat any stake/unstake-rebase pair that can be repeated in a loop as a red flag.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "XSD (XSDWETHpool)", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The XSD router's swapXSDForETH executed TransferHelper.safeTransferETH before finalizing the pool state (burnpoolXSD ran after the transfer), so the attacker's fallback re-entered mid-swap: it pushed WBNB into the pool, swapped out more WBNB at the stale price, and ran PIDController.systemCalculations() against the inconsistent state to extract extra value.", "lesson": "Sending ETH before finishing state updates is a reentrancy doorway — complete burn/pool accounting before any value transfer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Unicly (PointFarm)", "date": "2023-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "PointFarm's withdraw() sent the ERC1155 position token before updating accrued points, so the attacker's onERC1155Received hook re-entered deposit(0,0) and credited points against the stale pre-withdrawal balance, inflating his point total. The inflated points then redeemed the Realm NFT (id 4689) from PointShop.", "lesson": "Reward/point accrual must update before any token transfer in a farm — a receive hook that can re-enter point accounting inflates rewards for free.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Astrid", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Astrid's withdraw(_restakedTokenAddress, amount) trusted the supplied token contract's scaledBalanceToBalance() when computing how much real collateral to pay out. The attacker deployed a fake \"restaked token\" for each of stETH, rETH and cbETH that reported the vault's entire balance as his scaled balance, then called withdraw() with the vault's real balance and claim() to drain all three LSDs, swapping them to ETH.", "lesson": "When a staking vault queries a token address for balances/rates to size payouts, that address must be whitelisted — untrusted views must never decide withdrawals.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BH", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified BH contract had a public Upgrade(_lpToken) function that reset its LP accounting; the attacker called it 12 times, added 3M BUSDT of liquidity, swapped 22M BUSDT to BH to skew the removal ratio, and then removed 55% of the LP ten times — each removal paid out more than the deposited share because the removal math used the manipulated state.", "lesson": "Public \"upgrade\"/reset functions on LP contracts are a backdoor to removal-ratio math — gate them and test the add/remove-liquidity invariant after any state reset.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Beluga Dex", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Against Balancer flash loans of USDT and USDCe, the attacker deposits USDT and one third as much USDCe, swaps USDCe→USDT to distort the pool's cash levels, withdraws the USDT LP at the inflated quote, swaps USDT→USDCe, and withdraws the USDCe LP — ten cycles, each pricing against the manipulated cash/liability state.", "lesson": "Single-sided deposit/withdraw pricing against mutable pool cash is arbitrageable within one transaction — assert the pool's value invariant across deposit/swap/withdraw sequences.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "DePay Router", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The DePay payment router executed a caller-supplied plugin route with no validation of the liquidity source; the attacker flash-swapped USDC from the USDC/WETH pair, seeded a brand-new pair of his own token against 1 USDC, and routed the payment through that pair twice so the router swapped USDC into his worthless token. Removing his liquidity then recovered the pair's USDC, paying back the flash swap with profit.", "lesson": "Payment routers must restrict routes to vetted liquidity sources — otherwise the attacker mints his own pair and turns the payment into his exit liquidity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "HopeLend", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deposits 2,000 WBTC, donates another 2,000 WBTC to the hToken contract to inflate the liquidity index, and withdraws down to a 1-share totalSupply; 60 flash loans then accrue fees into the index so that a deposit/withdraw pair mints 1 share but withdraws 1.5x its asset value through rounding. Repeating that cycle thousands of times drains the WBTC, and the inflated collateral lets him borrow every other asset (WETH, USDT, USDC, HOPE, stHOPE) before cashing out.", "lesson": "Aave-fork liquidity indexes are donation-manipulable when totalSupply collapses toward zero — virtual shares/balances in the rate math are the fix.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "LaEeb", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 8.6 WBNB flash-loaned, the attacker buys LaEeb and loops ten times: transfer ~3.25e12 LaEeb into the pair and skim() the surplus, exploiting the token crediting the pair on transfer. The inflated reserves let him sell LaEeb back for more WBNB than borrowed.", "lesson": "The transfer-to-pair/skim loop appears on every chain with reward-on-transfer tokens — verify whether transfers credit the recipient before trusting reserve ratios.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Maestro Router 2", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Users had approved the Maestro bot router to spend their MOG tokens; the router's 0x9239127f function executed attacker-supplied transferFrom calldata, moving each victim's full MOG balance to the attacker. Seven victims were drained across many transactions (~280 ETH), and the tokens were sold through Uniswap.", "lesson": "Bot routers that execute user-supplied calldata against protocol-held allowances must validate targets and spender identity — otherwise every user who approved the router is a standing drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MicDao", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The swap contract's swap(amount, originToken) priced payouts using the caller-supplied originToken; the attacker flash-loaned BUSDT, bought MicDao, then 80 times deployed a helper holding 2,000 BUSDT that called swap(2000e18, owner) — pricing MicDao against the attacker's own address and returning MicDao at a manipulated rate. The accumulated MicDao was then dumped back to BUSDT.", "lesson": "Any swap function where the caller chooses the pricing token/path is an arbitrage machine — the pricing asset must be fixed by the protocol.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "OpenLeverage", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "RewardVaultDelegator's initialize() was never locked, so the attacker called it to install himself as admin, then setImplementation() to his own contract; calling a() delegatecalled his code, which transferFrom'd six victims' approved tokens (RACA, BUSDT, FLOKI, OLE, CSIX, BABY) and swapped them to BNB.", "lesson": "The uninitialized-proxy pattern repeats across chains — initializers must be one-shot and called atomically with deployment, or admin rights are up for grabs.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Platypus Finance (3rd)", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With Aave flash loans of 1.05M WAVAX and 950K SAVAX, the attacker deposits both, swaps SAVAX→WAVAX to manipulate the LP_AVAX pool's cash and liabilities, withdraws WAVAX LP at the inflated price, swaps WAVAX→SAVAX, and withdraws the SAVAX LP — the same cash-ratio pricing flaw as the earlier Platypus exploits.", "lesson": "When the same pool design is exploited three times, the lesson is structural: pricing deposits/withdrawals off mutable cash ratios without an invariant check will be attacked again.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Stars Arena", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "buyShares() (selector 0xe9ccf3a3) transferred AVAX and fees without a reentrancy lock, so during the purchase the attacker's receive() re-entered the fee-parameters setter (0x5632b2e4) with attacker-chosen values, corrupting the share price math mid-purchase; selling the share afterwards realized the inflated price for ~3M profit.", "lesson": "Friend-tech-style share markets must lock reentrancy across buy/sell and any fee/param setters — the price depends on state that a callback can rewrite.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "UniBot", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The UniBot router's 0xb2bd16ab function accepted a path and attacker-supplied transferFrom calldata, which it executed to pull the approved UniBot balances of 17 victims to the attacker (~$84K).", "lesson": "Identical to the Maestro drain — routers executing user-supplied calldata against standing approvals are drains; validate calldata targets against a whitelist.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Wise Lending", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "c0ffeebabe.eth deposited 1 WBTC into two positions, donated ~50 WBTC to inflate the pool share price, and borrowed the maximum of every asset (wstETH, WETH, aTokens, DAI, sDAI, USDC, USDT) against the inflated collateral; a precision-loss loop then withdrew the donated WBTC while burning near-zero shares.", "lesson": "Donation-based share-price inflation plus rounding in withdraw math equals full drain — add virtual balances to share pricing and audit rounding direction.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "ZS", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After priming the pair in a first transaction, the attacker computes the BUSDT needed to buy all but the burnable ZS in the pair, flash-loans it, and buys the ZS; calling the public destory_pair_amount() then burns the pair's ZS balance, after which he sells his ZS back at the reserve-shrunken price.", "lesson": "Any unvetted function that can destroy a pair's token balance is a price-pump switch — pair reserves must only be mutable through the AMM's own swap/add/remove paths.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "KLend (kTAF)", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 4,000 DAI flash-loaned, the attacker liquidates a borrower's kTAF position in slices, then uses a helper to donate TAF to the kTAF market — inflating its exchange rate — before borrowing DAI against the inflated kTAF collateral and redeeming. The loop drains DAI from kDAI while seizing kTAF at favorable rates.", "lesson": "Compound-fork markets whose underlying can be donated without virtual-share protection have manipulable exchange rates — audit mint/redeem against donation inflation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "pSeudoEth", "date": "2023-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans WETH, buys pEth, transfers the entire balance into the pEth/WETH pair (the token credits the pair on transfer), and loops skim(pair) to re-trigger the credit before selling pEth back at the inflated price.", "lesson": "The transfer-credit/skim primitive isn't BSC-only — check reward-on-transfer behavior on Ethereum pairs too.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "3913", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Funded by five chained DODO flash loans of BUSD, the attacker buys 3913, moves it through a helper contract, and loops transfer-to-pair + skim while calling the permissionless burnPairs() — which burns the pair's own token balance. The reserve-shrunken pair then pays out far more BUSD per 3913, and the same trick is repeated on the 3913/9419 pair before repaying.", "lesson": "A public function that burns an AMM pair's balance is a price-pump switch — pair balances must only change through the pair's own swap path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "AIS", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 3M USDT, buys AIS, and loops transferring 90% of his AIS to the pair with skims to harvest the token's transfer credits. He then calls the permissionless harvestMarket(), seizes admin of a companion contract via its uninitialized setAdmin(), drains that contract's AIS, calls setSwapPairs() to repoint the swap pair to his own address, and dumps the AIS for USDT.", "lesson": "Public setters for swap pairs plus an uninitialized admin on a companion contract handed the attacker token pricing and treasury in one transaction.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BRAND", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified vulnerable contract's public buyToken() purchased BRAND using the contract's own funds and credited the caller; the attacker called it 100 times with a 300 WBNB flash loan, converting the treasury into his BRAND balance, then sold the BRAND back to WBNB.", "lesson": "Permissionless buy functions that pay with contract funds and credit msg.sender are treasury faucets — purchases must pull payment from the caller.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BurntBubba (FarmingLPToken)", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker adds liquidity to Sushi pairs and deposits the LP into the farm, then transfers nearly all his farm shares away — keeping only a dust share — and calls emergencyWithdraw(). The farm's payout accounting credited the dust share with the full underlying position, returning the Sushi LP which he burns back to the underlying tokens.", "lesson": "Emergency-withdraw paths must compute payouts from the caller's remaining shares — transferring shares away first must not inflate what the leftover share can claim.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "CAROL Protocol", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With WETH chained through four flash-loan providers, the attacker buys CAROL and repeatedly calls sell(); sell() internally routes a swapExactTokensForETH whose ETH callback re-enters the attacker, who swaps more CAROL for WETH — shifting the contract's ethReserved mid-sell. Each sell then pays out against the corrupted reserve value, and the loop runs until the profit is extracted.", "lesson": "Sell/exit functions that swap through an external router and read reserve state after the external call are reentrancy-prone — settle accounting before the swap or lock the function.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "EEE", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After a 750K USDT flash swap, the attacker transfers the USDT into the EEE LP contract to surge its supply side, swaps EEE out, and then runs eight swaps through an attack router that prices EEE against the manipulated state before transferring EEE back and draining USDT.", "lesson": "Custom swap routers with permissive pricing can be drained in one transaction — assert the pricing invariant against manipulated reserves.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "EHX", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans WBNB, buys EHX, and loops 2,000 transfers of EHX into the pair with skims — the token credits the pair on each transfer, so the harvested surplus inflates his stack — then sells the EHX back for more WBNB than borrowed.", "lesson": "The transfer-to-pair/skim loop again — token mechanics that credit recipients make pair reserves attacker-controlled.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "FiberRouter", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "A victim had approved the FiberRouter for USDC; the router's swapAndCrossOneInch executed the attacker-supplied transferFrom calldata against that approval, moving the victim's entire USDC balance to the attacker.", "lesson": "Cross-chain swap routers must not execute arbitrary calldata against user approvals — validate every target the calldata touches.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "KR", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker first burns his BUSD to the dead address, then calls sellKr() with 94% of another address's KR balance — the sell function paid out BUSD from the contract based on the caller-supplied amount without verifying the caller owned the KR being sold.", "lesson": "Sell functions must validate that the seller actually holds the amount being sold; caller-supplied amounts are not proof of ownership.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "KyberSwap Elastic — KyberSwap_exp.eth.1.sol", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With an Aave flash loan, the attacker swaps the pool into a tick range with zero liquidity, mints an LP position with a manipulated nearestCurrentTick, then removes liquidity — the removal math returns more tokens than the position actually holds because the pool's tick data double-counted reinvestment liquidity. Back-and-forth swaps then extract the imbalance from the pool and the flash loan is repaid.", "lesson": "LP removal must reconcile tick liquidity exactly; fuzz mint→remove roundtrips against tick invariants, since the $46M KyberSwap loss was a removal-time double count.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "LinkDao", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The PoC flash-swaps a large BUSD amount out of a pair and, in its fallback, nudges 1e15 BUSD to the vulnerable LinkDao contract — the transfer triggers the contract's reward function, which pays the sender back an outsized amount from its balance.", "lesson": "Transfer-triggered functions that credit the sender proportionally to the contract's own balance are faucets — payments must come from the sender's stake, not the vault.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MEV bot 0x8c2d", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim's asset-harvesting contract exposed an unauthenticated designateRole (0xac3994ec); the attacker called it to grant himself the privileged role, then invoked harvestAssets (0x1270d364) to transfer the victim bot's entire BUSDT balance to himself, funding the whole thing with a flash swap.", "lesson": "Role-designation functions on bot/keeper contracts must be owner-gated — an open designateRole is an instant takeover of every asset the bot manages.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MEV bot 0xa247", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Across 24 victim bot contracts, the attacker calls the unauthenticated 0xe7d25975 to install himself as admin/recipient, then 0x4abe11b4/0xd547557b/0x90fb9dca to withdraw each bot's token holdings (RAIL, USDT, BUMP, HOPR and 20 more) and swap them to WETH.", "lesson": "The same lesson at scale — unguarded setters on automated bot contracts let one attacker sweep dozens of bots' balances.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MahaLend", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker supplies USDC, donates the flash-loaned USDC to the mUSDC contract to inflate the liquidity index, and withdraws down to a 1-share totalSupply; 55 flash loans accrue fees into the index, after which each supply/withdraw pair mints a share with ~50% of the asset but withdraws it back fully — repeating until the donated funds are drained, then borrowing all ARTH against the inflated collateral.", "lesson": "Identical to HopeLend — Aave-fork index math without virtual balances is donation-manipulable when totalSupply collapses to dust.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MetaLend", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The helper mints 1 ETH into the Compound-fork mETH market, redeems all but 2 shares, and force-sends ~100 ETH via a selfdestructing Donator — inflating mETH's exchange rate with no receive() guard able to stop it. Against the inflated collateral it then borrows nearly all WBTC cash from mWBTC and redeems the remaining ETH, swapping WBTC to WETH to repay Spark.", "lesson": "selfdestruct ETH donations bypass receive() guards — Compound forks need virtual-share accounting or their exchange rate is a donation target.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "OKC", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 2.5e24 USDT from Pancake V3, swaps to OKC, and stages two helper contracts at precomputed addresses whose constructors push funds into the pair so LP is minted to the main contract. Calling the public minerPool.processLPReward() then distributes OKC rewards to LP holders — the freshly minted position included — and the attacker removes the liquidity and dumps the OKC.", "lesson": "Public processReward functions that pay current LP holders are faucets when an attacker can flash-mint an LP position in the same transaction.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Onyx Protocol", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Using the classic Compound-fork precision-loss sequence, an intermediate contract mints 1e18 PEPE, redeems down to 2 shares, and re-donates the PEPE to oPEPE to inflate its exchange rate. Against the inflated oPEPE collateral the attacker borrows the entire cash of oETHER, oUSDC, oUSDT, oPAXG, oDAI, oBTC and oLINK, self-liquidates the intermediate to seize the oPEPE, and redeems PEPE back — repeating per market.", "lesson": "The Hundred/Onxy/MetaLend family all share one root cause: empty-market exchange-rate manipulation in Compound forks — virtual reserves are the standard fix.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "RBalancer (Stone/Rock staking)", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deposits 8,600 WETH into the staking contract (0xd0e30db0), triggers its internal operation (0x5069fb57), and then withdraws via 0xb18f2e91 — the deposit→internal-op→withdraw roundtrip credits him ~17 ETH more than he deposited because the withdrawal math over-values his shares after the internal step.", "lesson": "Test deposit/withdraw roundtrips after every internal harvest/swap step — any path where the roundtrip nets positive is a drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Raft", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker donates cbETH to the PRM, liquidates a position to snapshot the collateral index, and then calls managePosition 60+ times with 1 wei of collateral each time — precision loss mints him a full unit of rcbETH_c per wei, accumulating a huge collateral position. He redeems the donated cbETH back, borrows R against the inflated collateral, and swaps R to cbETH.", "lesson": "One-wei deposits must never mint full-value shares — index math has to round against the attacker at every denomination.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "SHIBA", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With a 20 BNB flash loan the attacker buys SHIBA through the ICO, then calls batchTransferLockToken to airdrop a huge SHIBA amount directly to the trading pair. The credited pair balance lets him swap out most of the pair's BUSD, convert to BNB, repay the loan, and keep ~101 BNB.", "lesson": "Batch airdrop functions that let anyone credit arbitrary addresses — including AMM pairs — are free liquidity pumps; restrict recipients and validate amounts.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Swamp Finance", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker mints vUSDT with flash-loaned BUSD, borrows 500 BNB from Venus, deposits WBNB into beltBNB, stakes the belt shares in NativeFarm, triggers StrategyBeltToken.earn(), and withdraws with _wantAmt=type(uint256).max — the farm's share→amount conversion across the rebasing beltBNB vault returns more than was staked, which he unwinds for profit.", "lesson": "Farms wrapping rebasing or fee-on-transfer vault tokens must not trust share↔amount conversions across deposit/withdraw cycles.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "TheNFTV2", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The NFT's burn() does not permanently clear ownership — after burning, the token becomes claimable from the dead address, so the attacker loops approve+burn+transferFrom(dead) on the same NFT id, minting TheDAO reward tokens each cycle until he has enough to drain the TheDAO/WETH pair via a flash swap.", "lesson": "Burn functions must permanently delete token state — burn-and-reclaim loops turn reward claims into infinite mints.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "The Standard (SmartVault V2)", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With WBTC flash-borrowed, the attacker mints 290,000 EURO from a new smart vault and calls SmartVaultV2.swap(WBTC→PAXG), which routes through a WBTC/PAXG Uni V3 pool where the attacker was the only liquidity provider — the vault overpays massively for PAXG. Decreasing and collecting his position realizes the vault's WBTC.", "lesson": "Vault swap functions must constrain routes to vetted pools — a pool seeded solely by the attacker becomes a siphon for minted assets.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Token 8633/9419", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After a 1.1e24 BUSD flash loan, the attacker feeds BUSD to the token's helper and calls autoAddLp() 130 times — each call adds liquidity with attacker-seeded funds and mints LP to the attacker — then routes swaps through a custom router and runs autoSwapAndAddToMarketing() 900 times to print tokens before dumping 8633 for BUSD.", "lesson": "Permissionless autoAddLp/autoSwap functions must only use protocol-owned balances; if they reuse attacker-supplied funds they become token printers.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "TrustPad", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The staking contract computes lock/reward timing through overridable hooks (depositLockStart/isLocked) that the attacker's helper fakes — claiming a lock that started a full lockPeriod ago. The helper loops receiveUpPool + withdraw 30 times to harvest full-period pending rewards, compounds via stakePendingRewards(), and withdraws everything.", "lesson": "Lock and reward timing must be computed internally from trusted state — never from hooks the caller can override.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "WECO", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The WECO staking contract paid a bonus on every deposit; after a 25M WECO deposit the attacker measures that a 1-ether deposit nets more than 1 ether, then loops 1-ether deposits until the staking contract's balance is drained.", "lesson": "If a deposit function credits more than the deposited amount per call, looping it drains the contract — verify net flows per call, not just totals.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "XAI", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker buys XAI with a flash-loaned WBNB, burns the entire supply minus 4,596 tokens via the public burn(), syncs the pair, and sells the remaining XAI at the deflated-supply price for more WBNB than borrowed.", "lesson": "Same as HCT — an unrestricted public burn on a small-cap token is a reserve-shrinking price pump.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "The Standard bot (router drain)", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The router's 0xf6ebebbb function swapped the router's own token balances through caller-supplied pools; the attacker invoked it four times with the router's full USDC, USDT, WBTC and WETH balances, each call routing the swap output to himself through the Curve pools.", "lesson": "Any function that swaps \"the contract's balance\" through caller-chosen pools on anyone's call is a drain — restrict it to authorized callers.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "GROK", "date": "2023-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 30 WETH and 63.4M GROK flash-loaned, the attacker alternates swaps and self-transfers of GROK across two Uni V3 pools; GROK's fee/reflection logic credits him on self-transfers while taxing the swaps asymmetrically, so each cycle nets tokens until ~26 ETH of profit is realized and the loans are repaid.", "lesson": "Asymmetric buy/sell taxes plus self-transfer side effects are an exploit primitive — model the token's full transfer mechanics before trusting any pool math.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BCT", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "In a preparation transaction the attacker registers five tool contracts as an inviter chain for the BCT referral system; during the attack he flash-swaps WBNB and drives buy() calls through the tools, collecting referral bonuses on each purchase, while looping transfer-to-pair + skim on the BCT pair to inflate his token balance. The accumulated BCT is then swapped for BUSD and the flash loan repaid.", "lesson": "Referral systems that credit an attacker-controlled inviter chain on every buy are bonus printers — cap referral depth and payouts per address.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "BEARNDAO (Alpaca Bvaults)", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swaps 10,000 WBNB, buys ALPACA to pump its price, and calls the permissionless BvaultsStrategy.convertDustToEarned() — which converts the strategy's dust at the just-inflated live price — before selling the ALPACA back and cashing out in BUSD.", "lesson": "Permissionless \"convert dust\" functions priced at live pool rates are sandwichable — protect them with access control or TWAP pricing.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Bob", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With a 100 WBNB DODO flash loan the attacker adds liquidity to the Bob pair and runs nine cycles: buy Bob with all WBNB, transfer Bob into the pair (credited on transfer), skim, swap WBNB out at the inflated reserves, and remove liquidity — each cycle growing his WBNB before the loan is repaid.", "lesson": "The transfer-credit/skim cycle, again — audit the token's transfer mechanics before trusting any pair's reserves.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "CCV", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Two unverified proxy contracts exposed public functions (0x369baafe, 0xb7da6a49) that operated on their own token balances; the attacker flash-loaned 100K BUSD and called both so the proxies converted their CCV/BUSD holdings to his benefit, then sold CCV back to BUSD and repaid.", "lesson": "Functions that buy or sell using the contract's own balance, callable by anyone, are treasury conversion faucets.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Channels Finance", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After a first transaction manipulated the cCLP market's totalSupply, the attacker flash-loans BUSD/BTCB, adds 115% liquidity to the BTCB/BUSD pair, and donates the LP plus CAKE into the cCLP market to inflate its exchange rate. He then enters all markets, borrows the entire cash of seven markets (WBNB, BUSD, USDT, USDC, DAI, ETH, BTC), and uses redeemUnderlying's rounding error to pull back almost all underlying while burning almost no cTokens.", "lesson": "Compound-fork markets with donatable underlying and redeemUnderlying rounding let one position borrow everything and redeem it back — seed virtual liquidity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Channels", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The smaller variant of the same attack: flash-loan liquidity, mint BUSD/BTCB LP, donate the LP and CAKE to the channels1 market, accrue interest, enter markets, borrow all cUSDC and cBUSD cash, then redeemUnderlying(balance-1) to exit with almost everything despite burning a dust amount of cTokens.", "lesson": "Same root cause as ChannelsFinance — donate-inflate-borrow-redeem; check every Compound-fork market for empty-pool exchange-rate manipulation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "DominoTT", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 0.5 WBNB, buys DominoTT, and through a gasless forwarder executes a meta-transaction calling DominoTT.multicall(burn(1,970,000e18, pair)) — the burn destroys the pair's token balance. After syncing, he sells his remaining DominoTT at the deflated-supply price for more WBNB than borrowed.", "lesson": "Burn functions that can target an AMM pair's balance, reachable via meta-tx, are price pumps — restrict burn sources and beneficiaries.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Elephant Money", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Funded by four chained Pancake V3 BUSD flash loans, the attacker swaps BUSD to WBNB, calls the unprotected Elephant.sweep() — whose buy/burn mechanics raise the WBNB price — and then swaps WBNB back to BUSD at the elevated price.", "lesson": "Unprotected sweep/buyback functions are sandwich targets, exactly like CurveBurner — gate them or add slippage bounds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Floor Protocol", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The ERC1967 proxy's extMulticall executed attacker-supplied target+calldata pairs; because the victim had approved Floor's contracts to move their Pudgy Penguins, the attacker built a multicall of safeTransferFrom(victim→attacker) for every token the victim held and drained the whole collection.", "lesson": "Multicall functions that execute arbitrary targets against protocol-held approvals drain every user who ever approved — validate targets and the executing identity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "GoodCompound (GoodDollar)", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans COMP and WETH, mints 450 cETH, borrows ~15K COMP, and in the sushi callback calls claimComp() on the GoodDollar staking contract followed by collectInterest() with an attacker-supplied market list and forceAndWaiverRewards=true — harvesting the protocol's COMP rewards accrued to the staking contract. The borrowed COMP is then repaid after swapping.", "lesson": "Fund managers must validate that the staking contracts passed to collectInterest are the registered ones — attacker-supplied lists print accrued rewards.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "GoodDollar (GDX)", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker's fake staking contract calls the flawed collectInterest([self], true) — no input validation — which invokes its collectUBIInterest callback; that callback re-enters GDX.buy() using the contract's cDAI to mint GoodDollar at a favorable rate. After two deposit cycles and a sell, the attacker repays the cDAI borrow and returns the WETH flash loan with ~2M profit.", "lesson": "collectInterest must whitelist staking contracts and GDX.buy must be nonReentrant — the callback path let one contract print and sell GoodDollar repeatedly.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "HNet", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Identical structure to DominoTT: flash-loan 0.1 WBNB, buy HNet, execute a forwarder meta-transaction calling multicall(burn(1,970,000e18, pair)) to destroy the pair's HNet balance, sync, and sell at the deflated-supply price.", "lesson": "The same attacker ran the same burn-the-pair script on two tokens — audit burn/multicall/forwarder combos as a unit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "HYPR", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The HYPR L1 ChugSplash proxy's initialize() was never locked, so the attacker called it to install himself as the messenger; finalizeERC20Withdrawal() then accepted him as the trusted bridge messenger and released the proxy's 2.57M HYPR to him.", "lesson": "Bridge finalization functions trust their messenger implicitly — an uninitialized messenger slot on an L1 token proxy is an instant full-drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "KEST", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With a 200 WBNB Radiant flash loan the attacker adds liquidity to the KEST pair and loops nine times: buy KEST, transfer it into the pair, skim, swap WBNB out at the inflated reserves, remove liquidity, and sell — accumulating WBNB before repaying.", "lesson": "The transfer-credit/skim loop once more — treat reserve ratios on such tokens as attacker-controlled by default.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "MAMO", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 19 WBNB and buys 95M MAMO through an unverified presale BuyToken contract at its stale price, then sells the MAMO through the Pancake pair at the higher market price, converting the proceeds back to WBNB to repay the loan.", "lesson": "Presale pricing must track the live market — a stale sale price turns the sale into a cheap-source-to-dump arbitrage (same as SUT).", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "NFTTrader", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker opens swap intents offering a worthless Uni V3 position NFT against five of the victim's CloneX NFTs; in closeSwapIntent() the NFT-receive callback re-enters editCounterPart() and rewrites the swap's counterpart to the victim, so the trade settles with the attacker receiving the victim's NFTs.", "lesson": "Counterpart or state edits reachable during swap execution are reentrancy hazards — lock the swap lifecycle end-to-end.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "PHIL", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "PHIL's public simpleToken() empties the token contract's holdings to the caller; the attacker invoked it once and then swapped the received PHIL for WBNB through the PHIL/WBNB pool.", "lesson": "Public \"simplify/withdraw-all\" maintenance functions on token contracts are backdoors — check every public function for balance-moving side effects.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Pine Protocol", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans the old lending pool's entire WETH balance from the protocol's Gnosis safe and uses it to repay his ~4 WETH debt in the new pool, freeing his PPG NFT collateral; the borrowed funds are then returned to the old pool unchanged, so his debt was extinguished with the protocol's own money.", "lesson": "Lending pools must validate repayment sources and borrower identity — flash-loaning a vault's full balance to its own debtor self-liquidates the debt.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "TIME (Chrono.tech)", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Through the gasless forwarder, the attacker executes a validly signed meta-transaction calling TIME.multicall(burn(62,227e18, TIME_WETH)) — the burn destroys the pair's TIME balance. Syncing and selling his TIME at the deflated-supply price nets ~84 ETH.", "lesson": "Same burn-the-pair primitive as DominoTT/HNet, now on Ethereum — audit burn+multicall+forwarder combinations for pair-balance reachability.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Telcoin", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The CloneableProxy's initialize(_logic, data) was callable without an initialized lock, so the attacker re-initialized it with his own implementation and delegatecalled transferTELFromCloneableProxy(), which transferred the proxy's entire TEL balance to him.", "lesson": "The uninitialized/re-initializable proxy pattern closes out the year — initializers must be one-shot and locked at deployment.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Transit Finance", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Transit's exactInputV3Swap trusted the pool addresses passed in its params; the attacker supplied his own fake pool whose swap() returned amounts that made the router owe him its entire 43.8K USDT balance. The router paid out without validating the pool or the returned amounts.", "lesson": "Routers must validate pool addresses and cap amounts reported by pool callbacks — caller-supplied pools are a direct drain vector.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified staking 0x431abb", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified staking contract credited rewards from the MetaWin buy() and bindParent() calls without sane scaling; the attacker staked modest amounts twice (via a helper contract) and later claimed outsized rewards during a flash-swap-funded claim, draining ~500K of value from the FCN/BUSDT pair.", "lesson": "Unverified staking contracts whose reward math scales off buy/bind events are faucets — model reward accrual against stake size before interacting.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "bZx (Ooki iToken)", "date": "2023-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2023-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After a prior transaction burned all iYFI shares and emptied the pool, the attacker flash-loans YFI, mints 5 wei of YFI into iYFI (5 wei of shares), and donates the flash-loaned YFI to the pool — exploding the exchange rate. Against the inflated 5-wei \"collateral\" he borrows the entire ETH and WBTC pools, and rounding in withdrawCollateral/burn returns both the collateral and the donated YFI to him.", "lesson": "Empty-pool donation inflation is still live in bZx-style lending — share price needs virtual liquidity and collateral checks must not trust dust shares.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2023-h2.md"}, {"protocol": "Barley Finance (wBARL wrapper)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (flash-callback bonding at stale rate)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "wBARL.flash() lends the wrapper's entire BARL reserve and, critically, allows the attacker to bond() the flashed BARL inside the callback at the pre-flash exchange rate. The PoC loops 20×: approve 10 DAI (the small flash fee), flash the whole BARL balance, and in the callback bond every BARL received back into wBARL — minting wBARL cheaper than it is worth. The attacker then debonds the whole position at 100% and swaps the BARL to WETH via Uniswap V3, netting ~$130K.", "lesson": "A token wrapper's flash function must not let the flash recipient mint/bond at the pre-flash rate inside the callback — audit flash+bond/debond cycles as a pair, not in isolation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Bmi Zapper (Bmi)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (missing account==msg.sender check → arbitrary aggregator call)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The zapper's zapToBMI() has no check that the `account` whose funds are moved is msg.sender, and it executes attacker-supplied `_aggregator`/`_aggregatorData` as a raw call. The PoC passes the victim as account and calldata for `USDC.transferFrom(victim, attacker, victimBalance)` as aggregator data; because the victim had approved USDC to the zapper, the zapper executes the transferFrom itself and 114,000 USDC flows to the attacker.", "lesson": "Any function that spends a user's allowance on their behalf must enforce `account == msg.sender` AND never forward raw user-supplied calldata to an arbitrary address.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Citadel Finance (CIT staking)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "oracle manipulation (redemption priced off spot WETH", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker stakes 2,653 CIT at a fixed rate, then flashloans 4,500 WETH and dumps it through the Camelot WETH/USDC pair, crashing the pair price. CitadelRedeem.redeem() — flagged \"flawed\" in the PoC — computes the CIT redemption value from the current state of that same WETH/USDC pair, so the attacker redeems his stake for far more USDC than it is worth, swaps USDC back to WETH, and repays the flashloan, repeating the cycle.", "lesson": "Redemption/mint pricing must never be derived from a spot pair the attacker can swing with one trade — use TWAP, or cap and sanity-check the redemption against the true collateral value.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "DAO Soulmate (BUI redemption)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (no access control on redeem)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SoulMate contract's redeem(shares, receiver) is fully permissionless. The PoC simply calls redeem with the contract's entire BUI balance as shares and the attacker as receiver, converting the protocol's own treasury-backed shares into USDC/DAI/MATIC/AAVE/ENS/ZRX/UNI paid to the attacker (~$319K). The PoC comment is literally \"No access control\".", "lesson": "Redemption functions that can cash out the contract's own holdings are a direct drain if not restricted — always trace who can pass arbitrary shares and who receives the proceeds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Freedom (FREEB)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (buyToken ignores caller-supplied expected payment vs real price)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 500 WBNB from DODO and swaps WBNB→FREE on PancakeSwap to pump the FREE/WBNB pair. He then calls FREEB.buyToken() with an attacker-chosen expectedPaymentAmount of only 5 FREE — the contract never verifies the payment against the actual pool price, so it sells the listing's tokens at the manipulated rate; the attacker sells back and repays the flashloan, keeping the spread (~74 WBNB).", "lesson": "Buy/sell functions that accept the expected payment amount as a caller parameter without checking it against the true market price are exploitable by a simple price pump.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Gamma Strategies (Uniswap V3 LP vaults)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "price manipulation (flashloan-driven deposit", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 3M USDT from the WETH/USDT UniV3 pool and 2M USDC from Balancer, then runs 15 rounds of: swap in the Algebra pool to skew the USDT/USDC ratio, deposit a tiny amount into the Gamma uniproxy vault, withdraw with a price-limit swap at the manipulated rate, and re-deposit. Each deposit/withdraw cycle between the manipulated Algebra pool and the USDT/USDC UniV3 pool drains more value than it locks; the loop extracts ~$6.3M of ETH-equivalent from the vault.", "lesson": "Vault deposit/withdraw flows that let the caller supply swap price limits against a manipulable pool can be milked in a loop — cap per-call amounts and compute vault shares off TWAP rather than current pool state.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "LiquidX (LQDX) zap", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (deposit lacks account==msg.sender check; allowance spent for attacker)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Per the PoC note, the zap's deposit() never checks that `account` is msg.sender, and it swaps/adds liquidity using `account`'s tokens. The attacker calls zap.deposit(victim, WETH, ...) with amounts set to the victim's full WETH allowance on the zap, so the victim's approval is spent buying LQDX and adding WETH/LQDX liquidity for the attacker.", "lesson": "Zaps and routers must spend allowances only for the caller; if a function lets you name the account whose approval is consumed, it is an allowance-drain waiting to happen.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MIC token (swapManual / LP fee claim)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (repeatable LP-fee claim via swapAndSendLPFee)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 1,700 BUSDT, swaps into MIC and BNB, and adds MIC/WBNB liquidity. MIC.swapManual() triggers the vulnerable swapAndSendLPFee, which distributes accumulated swap fees to current LP holders without settling the claim; by transferring the LP tokens through a chain of 10 freshly-deployed helper contracts and calling swapManual() each time, the attacker collects the fee pool repeatedly, then removes liquidity and repays the flashloan (~$500K).", "lesson": "Fee-distribution functions must clear or decrement the claimable amount per LP holder — a public trigger that pays \"whoever holds the LP right now\" can be claimed repeatedly by rotating the LP tokens.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Abracadabra Money (Cauldron V4 / MIM)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (borrow elastic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Inside a 300k MIM DegenBox flashloan, the attacker transfers 240k MIM to the cauldron, calls repayForAll() to clear the total borrow elastic, then repays 15 existing users' borrowParts individually — double-subtracting repays and crushing the cauldron's elastic/base ratio. With the accounting corrupted, a helper adds 100 wei of collateral and loops 90× borrow(1 wei)/repay(1 wei) (each round rounds interest accrual to zero), after which the attacker borrows the cauldron's entire MIM balance and withdraws it (~$6.5M).", "lesson": "Repay paths that let a caller repay both \"everyone\" and individual users without idempotent elastic accounting let an attacker crush the borrow ratio and mint/buy debt at dust prices.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "NBL Game (NFT staking)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Optimism", "class": "reentrancy (onERC721Received hook in withdrawNft)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans the staking contract's entire NBL balance, deposits NFT #737 and all the NBL, then calls withdrawNft(0) — which has no reentrancy protection. When the NFT is transferred back, the attacker's onERC721Received hook re-enters: it transfers the NFT back into the stake, withdraws it again and re-deposits it, duplicating the staking state. The attacker then swaps the double-counted NBL rewards to USDT/WETH and repays the flashloan (~$180K).", "lesson": "Any withdraw that sends an NFT or token to the caller before updating state is a reentrancy point — NFT staking contracts need CEI and reentrancy locks on the 721 callback path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Orbit Chain (Orbit Bridge)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "bridge validation flaw (withdraw accepted forged signatures", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Ethereum Orbit vault's withdraw() failed to properly validate that the validator signatures authorized this chain, vault and depositId. The PoC replays the exact attack: attacker-chosen bytes32s (sha256 of hub+vault chain data), a real Orbit-side tx hash, amount 2,308.79 WBTC, depositId 8735, and 7 fake v/r/s signatures — which the vault accepted, releasing ~$81M of WBTC/ETH/stablecoins.", "lesson": "Bridge release functions must validate every element — source chain, destination vault, depositId uniqueness, and validator threshold — one missing check on the withdrawal path is an $81M-class hole.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Peapods Finance (ppPP wrapper)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (flash-callback bonding at stale rate)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same wrapper pattern as Barley: ppPP.flash() lends the wrapper's whole PEAS reserve and lets the recipient bond() the flashed tokens inside the callback at the pre-flash rate. Looping 20× with 10 DAI per round mints ppPP cheaply; the attacker debonds the accumulated position at 100% and swaps PEAS→WETH (~$1K in this replay).", "lesson": "The same flash+bond-at-stale-rate flaw repeats across wrapper tokens — grep new wrappers for flash functions that permit mint/bond during the callback.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Radiant Capital (new USDC market)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "rounding (rayDiv precision loss after liquidityIndex inflation)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deposits 2M USDC into Radiant's freshly-launched USDC market, then performs 151 nested Aave-style flashloans of 2M USDC, each inflating the market's liquidityIndex; he borrows ~90.7 WETH against it. A helper contract then alternates deposit(amount)/deposit(amount<<1)/withdraw in a loop, exploiting a rounding flaw in rayDiv so each deposit/withdraw cycle siphons a little more USDC from the pool, until the pool is drained (~$4.5M).", "lesson": "Lending-market share math must be re-audited under attacker-inflated index values — a rounding loss that is harmless at normal indices becomes a siphon when the index is pushed to an extreme.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Shell (0xa898 MEV bot)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (unprotected function on MEV robot)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Two \"robot\" contracts held SHELL/BUSD and exposed an unprotected function (selector 0x5f90d725) that moves tokens to caller-supplied addresses. The PoC loops raw calls to each robot passing the victims' full BUSD balances and the attacker as receiver until both bots are emptied, then swaps SHELL→BUSD (~1,000 BUSD).", "lesson": "MEV bots routinely leave transfer-capable functions public — a raw-call fuzz of a bot's selectors with victim/attacker arguments is a cheap bounty pattern.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Socket / Bungee Gateway (route 406)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (route executes arbitrary user-supplied swapExtraData)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The newly-added route 406's performAction() executed the caller-supplied swapExtraData bytes without validating the caller's ownership of the funds. The PoC crafts swapExtraData = `USDC.transferFrom(victim, attacker, victim's full balance)` and calls gateway.executeRoute(406, ...); the gateway holds standing approvals from users, so the route's execution drains each victim's USDC (~$3.3M total).", "lesson": "Bridge/swap routes that execute arbitrary calldata supplied by the user must check that the user actually owns the from-token — otherwise standing approvals turn the route into a mass transferFrom.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Wise Lending (Pendle PT pool; rounding)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "rounding + share-price donation (incorrect health-factor check)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deposits 1 wei-scale amounts and transfers pool tokens to the WiseLending contract to donate to the pool, then loops depositExactAmount(pseudoTotalPool*2-1)/withdrawExactAmount — a rounding-in-favor-of-the-protocol cycle that inflates the share price with the attacker's own donations. A second position then deposits the remaining pool tokens and borrows the maximum wstETH allowed because the health check runs against the manipulated pool state (with a mocked-fresh oracle), walking away with borrowed funds (~$464K incident).", "lesson": "Share-price donation attacks require the protocol's borrow-health check to value collateral at true market price — when collateral value comes from attacker-inflatable share accounting, borrowing caps fail.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Wise Lending (share-price inflation, full flow)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "rounding (2x-1 deposit donation) + bad-debt health check", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After seeding the pool at 2 wei underlying / 1 share, the attacker loops depositExactAmount(2x-1)/withdrawExactAmount(1) to donate collateral while minting 1 share each round, pushing the share price to 36 ETH. Six helper positions then deposit 1 share each, borrow huge amounts of wstETH/WETH/LP tokens, and withdraw 1 wei of collateral — the \"incorrect health factor check\" lets positions enter bad debt, and the attacker finally withdraws all donated value via his inflated shares.", "lesson": "Rounding that favors the protocol on deposit is an attacker donation channel; combined with a health check that ignores real collateral value it becomes a full bank run.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "XSIJ (BSC token)", "date": "2024-01", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (direct pair transfers corrupt pool accounting)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 100k BUSD from DODO and swaps it for XSIJ, pumping the token's price; then, while the Pancake pair holds >1,800 XSIJ, he transfers 1 wei of XSIJ directly to the pair in a loop, exploiting the token's fee/reflection behavior (removePoolAmount) to desynchronize the pair's real balance from its reserves. He then sells his whole XSIJ stack back to BUSD at the inflated rate and repays the flashloan (~$51K).", "lesson": "Fee-on-transfer/reflection tokens let attackers gift tokens directly to pairs to break reserve sync — check pool balanceOf vs reserves and the token's special transfer paths.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "ADC (game / MainPool)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (unrestricted calcStepIncome with caller-chosen values)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The helper contract buys a 3 ETH ticket and joins the game with 15 ETH, then calls mainpool.calcStepIncome(529, 36e18, 100) — the \"vulnerability\" line — a function that credits the player's staticIncome from caller-supplied parameters with no authorization or validation. withdraw() then pays out the inflated income, netting ~20 ETH.", "lesson": "Any income/reward calculation that accepts caller-chosen amounts without checking them against real game state is a free-withdrawal button.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Affine DeFi (LidoLevV3 vault)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (vault used as flashloan recipient executes caller-controlled ops)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker calls Balancer.flashLoan with the LidoLevV3 vault itself as the recipient and userData encoding operation (1, attacker) and then (2, attacker). The vault's receiveFlashLoan executes the caller-supplied action codes without restricting who can drive them, crediting the attacker with 33 aEthwstETH (the vault's leverage position) for zero deposit.", "lesson": "Vault contracts whose flashloan callback executes opcodes chosen by the borrower turn the vault into an open treasury — restrict the callback to trusted initiators.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Babyloogn airdrop", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (unrestricted claim loop)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The airdrop contract's claim function (selector 0xfbe81135) is callable by anyone with no per-user cap. The attacker loops the call with (1, 0) while the airdrop still holds ≥285 BABYLOOGN, draining its token balance, then swaps everything to WBNB (~2.24 WBNB).", "lesson": "Airdrops need per-address claim state and limits — a loopable, uncapped claim is a drain loop for the first bot to find it.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Blueberry Protocol (lending)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "pricing flaw (borrow limits not enforced for newly-added markets)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The whitehat supplies only 1 WETH of collateral (bWETH.mint with a Balancer-flashloaned 1 WETH) and, in the same callback, borrows ~8,616 OHM, ~913 USDC and ~0.69 WBTC, then swaps OHM→WETH and repays the flashloan. The comptroller's account-liquidity math failed to constrain borrowing in the freshly-listed markets, so ~1 WETH of collateral unlocks a six-figure loan basket ($1.4M).", "lesson": "Verify the comptroller actually prices and caps every newly-added market — a market that slips past the collateral/borrow math turns 1 unit of collateral into unlimited credit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Burns Defi", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "BSC", "class": "price manipulation (burnToHolder rewards priced off manipulable spot pair)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After a 250k BUSDT DODO flashloan, the attacker pushes BUSDT through the BUSDT/WBNB pair to inject WBNB into the Burns/WBNB pair, pumping the Burns price. burnToHolder() computes the payout via getAmountsOut() on that same Burns/WBNB pair, so burning tokens pays out at the manipulated rate; the attacker repeats with two tranches, calls receiveRewards(), swaps back, and repays (~$67K).", "lesson": "Burn-reward and redeem functions that value tokens with the live spot pair can be front-run by the very swap that funds the burn.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Compound (cUNI price feed)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "oracle (stale", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans USDC, deposits it as cUSDC collateral, and borrows the maximum UNI the comptroller permits — computed from the UniswapAnchoredView price feed for cUNI, which mispriced UNI. He immediately dumps the borrowed UNI through UNI→WETH→USDC pools to repay the flashloan, leaving an under-collateralized position (~$439K of bad debt).", "lesson": "Lending borrow caps are only as good as the underlying price feed — when the feed lags or misprices, the borrow ceiling becomes the attacker's exit liquidity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "DN404 (official token proxy)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (unprotected init())", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The DN404 distribution proxy's init() has no access control and no initialized guard. The PoC calls init(WETH, 1, 1e18) to re-initialize the contract with attacker-chosen parameters, then calls withdraw(FLIX, victim's full FLIX balance, attacker), draining the token balance the proxy held (~200K).", "lesson": "Every proxy initializer needs an initializer modifier — an unprotected init() on a contract holding user funds is an instant re-init + withdraw.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "DeezNutz (ERC-404)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "rounding (self-transfer balance inflation in ERC-404 conversion)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 2,000 WETH and swaps it all to DeezNutz, then loops 5 self-transfers; the 404 token's ERC20↔NFT conversion rounds in the sender's favor, inflating his balance each hop. He gifts 5% of the tokens to the pair to pass the k check and swaps the inflated DeezNutz stack back to WETH, repaying 2,001 WETH and keeping the rest (~$170K).", "lesson": "ERC-404 conversions are a rounding surface — fuzz self-transfers and boundary amounts to check that the ERC20 balance after an NFT mint/burn cycle never exceeds what was paid.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "DualPools (Venus + DualPools comptrollers)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "BSC", "class": "donation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans BUSD and WBNB from DODO pools, supplies them to Venus as vBUSD/vWBNB and borrows 11,500 LINK; he then mints just 2 wei of DualPools dLINK and donates the 11,500 LINK directly to the dLINK contract. Because the DualPools cToken values collateral off the contract's total token balance, the donation makes 2 wei of dLINK look like 11,500 LINK of collateral; he borrows WBNB/BTCB/ETH/ADA/BUSD against it, redeems his donated LINK back, and repays everything else (~$42K).", "lesson": "cToken exchange-rate math must use accounting-tracked totals, never raw balanceOf — a direct donation instantly inflates any lender that reads token balances for collateral value.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "EGGX (NFT claim)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (reward claim gated on transient balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans the EGGX/WETH UniV3 pool's entire EGGX reserve and, while holding it, calls EGGXCliam.check() for 36 staked NFT IDs — the claim function pays out based on the caller's current EGGX balance. After claiming, he repays the pool (balance + fee) and swaps the claimed EGGX to WETH (~2 ETH).", "lesson": "Reward claims that measure the caller's balance at claim time can be fed by a flashloan — require the balance to be held (or the position locked) for a real duration.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "GAIN token pair", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (reserve desync via direct transfers + skim", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swaps 100k GAIN from the Uniswap V2 pair, then re-syncs the pair's reserves downward via direct 100/188-wei transfers plus skim()/sync() calls. He then gifts 130e12 GAIN into the pair and calls swap() to pull out virtually all of its WETH at the corrupted ratio, walking away with ~18 ETH.", "lesson": "Pairs whose reserves can be re-synced to an attacker-chosen snapshot let the attacker gift dust and buy the other side cheap — check every skim/sync/direct-transfer path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "\"Game\" auction (makeBid)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "reentrancy (ETH refund before state update + broken minimum-bid check)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "newBidEtherMin() has a logic error that makes the minimum bid trivially low, and makeBid() refunds the previous bidder via an ETH transfer before settling state. The attacker's receive() re-enters makeBid 110 times with bad minimum bids, collecting the refund each round from the 0.6 ETH seed (~20 ETH).", "lesson": "Auctions that refund outbid ETH before updating state are reentrant even without a token callback — the plain ETH receive() is the hook.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MINER (BSC)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (fee", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After a 10 WBNB DODO flashloan, the attacker buys 10e12 MINER and loops 50×: transfer his whole MINER balance directly into the Pancake pair and call skim() — the token's transfer mechanics keep inflating the pair's real balance beyond its recorded reserves. The final pair.swap() then pays out ~3.5 WBNB at the stale ratio, and the flashloan is repaid.", "lesson": "Tokens whose transfers mutate balances (burn/reflect) break pair accounting — test every pair token for reserve drift after direct transfers.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Miner (Ethereum UniV3)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (fee-burning token vs UniV3 balance accounting)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker opens a huge swap on the MINER/WETH UniV3 pool and, inside the swap callback, bounces 499,999,999,999,999,999 wei of MINER between himself and the pool 2,000 times; each transfer burns a share of the token, shrinking the pool's real MINER balance while its accounting stays put. The swap then settles at the price limit, draining ~140 ETH of WETH.", "lesson": "UniV3 pools assume token balances only change via swaps — a fee/burn-on-transfer token let an attacker reprice the pool with balance-only churn; flag any pool whose token burns on transfer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Pandora (ERC-404 integer underflow)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "math error (integer underflow in transfer accounting)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Pandora's transferFrom underflows in its ERC-404 accounting (the PoC's stated REASON: integer underflow). The attacker bounces the pair's entire PANDORA balance out to the token contract and back with a sync() in between, inflating the pair's recorded PANDORA reserve, then swaps the inflated amount out of the V2 pair for WETH (~$17K).", "lesson": "In 404/reflection tokens, re-check every subtraction in transfer/transferFrom against the true balance — one underflow converts a pair's reserves into free output.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Particle Trade (lien / NFT lending)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (crafted Lien via fake onERC721Received)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker calls offerBid() with his own contract as the \"collection\", then feeds the exchange's onERC721Received a synthetic NFT transfer whose data encodes an attacker-crafted Lien (lender=0, price=0, rate=0, loanStartTime=0). The exchange credits the attacker's account balance for the fake lien/liquidation path, and withdrawAccountBalance() pays out ~50 ETH.", "lesson": "NFT-lending contracts that trust the data attached to onERC721Received must validate lender, price and collection before crediting any balance — callback data is fully attacker-controlled.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Rugged Art (NFT staking proxy)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (targetedPurchase accepts attacker router commands at 1 wei)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans the RUGGED/WETH pool's RUGGED reserve and, in the callback, calls proxy.claimReward() plus targetedPurchase() for 20 staked NFT IDs with attacker-supplied UniversalRouter commands, buying them for 1 wei. He then unstakes the underlying RUGGED, repays the flashloan and swaps the proceeds (~5 ETH).", "lesson": "Purchase functions that accept caller-supplied router commands must pin the payment amount themselves — letting the caller choose the swap path is a 1-wei checkout.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "SMOOFS staking (MOOVE rewards)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Polygon", "class": "reentrancy (onERC721Received during withdraw)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker stakes Smoofs NFT #2062, then calls Withdraw with forceWithTax. The withdrawal sends the NFT back to the staker before settling rewards; inside onERC721Received the attacker loops 9×: transfer the NFT back into the staking contract and withdraw again, re-claiming the MOOVE reward allocation each round with no lock.", "lesson": "Staking withdrawals that hand the NFT back before zeroing reward state are reentrant — the 721 receive hook is the perfect place to loop the claim.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Seneca (Chamber performOperations)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (arbitrary external call action in chamber)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Chamber's performOperations supports an OPERATION_CALL action that executes arbitrary target+calldata from the caller. The PoC submits data = (PendlePrincipalToken, transferFrom(victim, attacker, victim's full balance)) and the chamber executes it in its own context, spending victims' existing token approvals on the chamber (~$6M).", "lesson": "A generic \"execute arbitrary call\" opcode inside an asset-holding contract is an approval-drain primitive — require the action to be authenticated or whitelisted to known functions.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Swarm Markets (XTOKEN mint)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (public mint on wrapper token)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The XTOKEN wrapper exposes a public mint(address, amount) with no restriction. The attacker mints himself xTokens equal to the wrapper's full DAI and USDC backing, then calls unwrap() on the wrapper to convert the freshly-minted xTokens into the real underlying DAI/USDC (~7,729 total).", "lesson": "Wrapper tokens are only as sound as their mint — a public mint turns the wrapper's entire backing into attacker credit at the unwrap desk.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Zoomer (vulnerable contract 0x72c4cff6)", "date": "2024-02", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (unchecked amount in swap", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 200 WETH and cycles it 5×: each round a fresh helper swaps ~199.9 WETH to Zoomer, then calls the vulnerable Zoomer contract function (selector 0x72c4cff6) passing an attacker-chosen ~30.27M Zoomer amount with only 0.02 ETH; the function pays out based on the caller-supplied amount without taking the tokens at true value, and the helper returns the Zoomer and ETH to the parent. Net ~14 ETH after repaying the flashloan.", "lesson": "Any payout/swap function that trusts a caller-passed token amount against a tiny attached payment is a money printer — diff the amount against the actual balance delta taken.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "ApolloX (ALP redeem / VUN)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public _swap executes caller-controlled aggregator data)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The ApolloX VUN contract exposes a public _swap() that runs arbitrary aggregator calldata using the contract's own holdings. The attacker passes 1inch unoswapTo calldata that routes the VUN contract's entire ALP_APO balance to himself, then approves and redeems the stolen ALP_APO for USDT (~$10K).", "lesson": "Internal swap helpers must never be externally callable with caller-chosen aggregator data — a public _swap on a token-holding contract is a withdrawal gateway.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "ARK token (autoBurn)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (public pair-burn desyncs reserves)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "ARK's autoBurnLiquidityPairTokens() burns the Pancake pair's ARK holdings and is callable by anyone. The attacker loops it ~10,000 times until the pair's real ARK balance is slashed to dust while its recorded reserves stay, then transfers WBNB + his ARK into the pair and calls swap() to pull out WBNB priced on the phantom (burned) ARK — 348 BNB.", "lesson": "A public burn that touches pair balances breaks the reserve invariant the pair prices against — any token function that mutates pair holdings without sync() is a repricing vector.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "BBT (registry takeover)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (unrestricted setRegistry → unlimited mint)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "BBT's mint() consults a registry contract to authorize mints, but setRegistry() has no access control. The attacker swaps the registry for his own contract that returns the attacker for every lookup, then mints 1e22 BBT and dumps them through two Uniswap swaps (~5 ETH).", "lesson": "When mint authorization delegates to a registry, the registry setter must be as protected as the mint itself — otherwise it is an infinite-mint key.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Binemon (marketing sweep)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public sweepTokenForMarketing → front-run)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "BIN's sweepTokenForMarketing() is public and swaps the token contract's marketing holdings into the pair. The attacker triggers it in a loop until the contract's BIN is gone, buys BIN before the sweep's demand lands, waits for other buyers (simulated 10 WBNB buy-in), and sells into the pumped price (~0.2 BNB).", "lesson": "Marketing buyback functions anyone can trigger are front-runnable — the caller can position himself ahead of a forced buy and sell into it.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "CurioDAO (CGT governance)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "governance (vote + lift → unrestricted spell minting DAI", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker locks 20 CGT in the DSChief-style chief, votes for his own contract, and calls lift() to take the governance hat. He then plots and execs a Spell that calls vat.suck() to mint ~1B DAI from the backing VAT, exits it through the DAI join, and mints ~1e12 CGT — the 998B CGT were dumped to market.", "lesson": "Governance quorums measured against a tiny locked supply are capturable for pocket change — check that the hat/spell path requires real economic stake, not just vote math.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "ETHFIN (buyback trigger)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (attacker-controlled buyback trigger + pumped exit)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker dusts 1-wei transfers to a run of fresh addresses until N_holders() reaches NextBuybackMemberCount(), arming the buyback. He then flashloans WBNB to pump the EFToken pairs and calls doBuyback(), which buys back tokens at the inflated price with the token contract's funds — with his own tokens positioned as the seller — and exits with ~2.13 BNB.", "lesson": "Buybacks gated on holder counts or other attacker-influenceable state can be armed and aimed by the attacker — verify the trigger and the price source together.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "GHT (transferFrom accounting)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (fee mis-accounting inflates pair balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "GHT's transferFrom mis-accounts its fee, so bouncing the pair's entire GHT balance out to the token contract and back (with a sync() in between) inflates the pair's real balance above its recorded reserves. The attacker computes the phantom surplus with the standard 997/1000 formula and swaps it out for WETH (~$57K).", "lesson": "Tokens whose transferFrom changes balances asymmetrically break every pair they're listed on — test a full withdraw/deposit round-trip against the reserve delta.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "IT token (mint-to-pool reflection)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (reflection mint-to-pool + direct USDT donations)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The IT token's reflection logic mints IT into the pair whenever the pair is touched (\"transfer from pool will lead to mint to pool\"). The attacker flashloans 2,000 USDT and loops 9×: a helper donates the USDT directly into the IT/USDT pair, the minted IT re-balances the ratio, and swap() pays out the surplus USDT each round (~$13K).", "lesson": "Reflection/rebasing tokens that mint into the pair on transfer turn direct donations into a swap-out pipeline — model the pair's balance after a transfer, not just the reserves.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Juice (staking emissions)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (broken reward-rate math)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The staking contract's harvest() pays rewards from a broken rate calculation that ignores the actual stake size. The attacker stakes ~0.5 ETH worth of JUICE with a 3e9 period parameter, rolls one block, harvests, and receives ~54 ETH of rewards, which he sells through the JUICE/ETH pair.", "lesson": "Reward-per-stake formulas must be re-derived from the code — a rate that doesn't scale with the locked amount turns a one-block stake into the entire emission.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Lava Lending (USDC/USDCe LP wrapper collateral)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "pricing flaw (over-valued wrapper collateral unlocks whole pool)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deposits WETH, then borrows the USDC/USDCe UniV3-wrapper aToken at 99% of its pool balance; Lava's valuation (calcUSDCLPAmount scales the wrapper's summed reserves) over-counts the collateral, letting him borrow every asset in the pool — USDCe, USDC, USDT, WETH and wstETH — up to each aToken's full balance (~$340K).", "lesson": "When a wrapper aToken's collateral value is derived by summing both sides of its LP reserves, one deposit unlocks the whole market — recompute wrapper collateral from shares × true token value.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MO (Optimism lending)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Optimism", "class": "logic flaw (borrow", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The lending contract's borrow() and redeem() could be paired repeatedly to net the attacker value: he loops borrow(mo_balance, 0) + redeem(i) ~80 times (each round settling the order but leaving him ahead), then borrows the pair's entire MO balance in one final order and swaps it to USDT (~$413K).", "lesson": "Borrow/repay/redeem order accounting must conserve value across every state transition — fuzz borrow→redeem→borrow sequences and check the attacker's net balance change is never positive without interest paid.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "ParaSwap (AugustusV6 swap callback)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (public uniswapV3SwapCallback trusts caller deltas + from)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "AugustusV6's uniswapV3SwapCallback is callable by anyone and trusts caller-supplied deltas plus a `from` address embedded in the data. The attacker invokes it directly with data naming a victim who had approved OPSEC to the router; the callback transfers the victim's OPSEC and pays the attacker the claimed WETH amount (a whitehat front-ran the real drain, ~$24K).", "lesson": "Swap callbacks must be callable only by the pool, and the payer address must come from the pool's validated state — never from caller-supplied callback data.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Prisma Finance (MigrateTroveZap)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (migration zap accepts attacker-chosen account)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The MigrateTroveZap performs trove migrations with an attacker-supplied `account` parameter and no verification that the caller owns the trove. The attacker flashloans ~1.44M mkUSD naming a victim's trove so the migration lands the victim's collateral with him, then uses a Balancer-flashloaned wstETH plus a second mkUSD flashloan to open and close his own trove, walking away with ~1,281 wstETH (~$11M).", "lesson": "Migration/withdraw helpers that take the account as a parameter are trove-takeover primitives — the owner must be derived from msg.sender or a verified signature, never from calldata.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "SSS (Blast)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Blast", "class": "logic flaw (self-transfer doubles balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SSS's transfer logic doubles the recipient's balance on self-transfers. The attacker loops SSS.transfer(self, balance) until his holdings cover the pool's WETH side, burns the excess to stay under maxAmountPerTx, then feeds the pool in per-tx-capped chunks and swaps out ~29.5 ETH (~$4.8M).", "lesson": "A token that inflates balances on self-transfer gives free tokens every loop iteration — fuzz transfer(to == from) and assert the balance delta equals the amount.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "TGBS (burn-block token)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (scheduled pair burn timed against holders)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "TGBS burns a share of the pair's tokens when _burnBlock() matches the current block. The attacker flashloans the DPP's whole WBNB balance to buy TGBS, then self-transfers 1 wei in a loop until the burn-block condition no longer holds, and sells his TGBS back to WBNB after the scheduled burn has slashed the pair's supply (~$150K).", "lesson": "Tokens with scheduled burns of pair holdings create predictable repricing windows — model the pair's balance around the burn block when auditing supply-shock mechanics.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unizen Trade Aggregator (VRA)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (aggregator executes arbitrary caller-supplied calls)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Unizen's TradeAggregator executes an arbitrary list of {target, data} calls supplied by the caller. The attacker submits a call to the VRA token encoding transferFrom(tokenHolder, TradeAggregator, holder's full balance); because holders had max-approvals on the aggregator, the aggregator executes the transferFrom and moves the victim's VRA to the attacker (~$2M).", "lesson": "Trade aggregators that run arbitrary user-supplied calls must simulate each call and confirm the caller owns the from-token — otherwise standing approvals turn the aggregator into a mass drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unizen Trade Aggregator (DMTR, raw tx replay)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (same arbitrary-call flaw, unverified contract)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same root cause as the VRA PoC: the unverified TradeAggregator proxy executes raw attacker calldata (selector 0x1ef29a02 with Info + Call[]), and the PoC replays the exact attack tx where the Call is DMTR.transferFrom(victim, aggregator, victim's balance), draining a victim's max-approved DMTR (~$2M total incident).", "lesson": "When the aggregator is unverified, reconstruct the ABI from calldata — the pattern \"Info + array of arbitrary calls\" is the drain signature; check for per-call ownership validation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "WOO Fi (WooPPV2 internal oracle)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "oracle manipulation (swap-driven internal price oracle)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans the UniV3 pool's USDCe and the TraderJoe LBT pair's whole WOO balance, deposits USDCe into Silo and borrows all its WOO. Small USDCe→WOO swaps through WooPPV2 push WOO Fi's internal Wooracle price up, after which he sells the borrowed WOO back in one large WOO→USDCe swap at the inflated price, repeating until the pool is drained (~$8M).", "lesson": "An oracle updated by the same swaps it prices is a flashloan target — any DEX with a spot-driven internal oracle needs a price-slippage bound or a volume-weighted update.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "ZongZi (burnToHolder)", "date": "2024-03", "chain": "BSC", "class": "price manipulation (burn payout priced off spot pair)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker alternates WBNB→ZongZi and ZongZi→WBNB swaps to pump the ZongZi/WBNB pair, then calls ZZF.burnToHolder() with the amount computed from the manipulated getAmountsIn — the burn pays out WBNB at the inflated price. receiveRewards() adds another payout and the leftover tokens are sold back (~$223K).", "lesson": "burnToHolder/receiveRewards pairs that price payouts on the live pair are the Burns-Defi pattern again — audit burn payout math against the price the attacker can set with one swap.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "ATM (BSC token, sandwich)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (pair balance inflation + skim harvest)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The ATM token contract periodically runs token→WBNB→USDT conversions that inflate the WBNB/ATM pair's real WBNB balance above its recorded reserves. The attacker flashloans WBNB, buys ATM, then loops direct ATM transfers into the pair plus skim() — harvesting the pair's excess WBNB (\"pair_lost\" per round, 7 WBNB per iteration) — and finally sells ATM back and repays (~$182K).", "lesson": "When a token contract performs its own pair conversions, the pair's real balance can outrun its reserves — any skim-able surplus the attacker can trigger repeatedly is a payout loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "BNBX (staking contract 0x389a)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (function pulls named user's approved tokens)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The BNBX contract's function (selector 0x11834d4c) takes a victim address and spends that user's BNBX allowance with no caller verification. The PoC loops it over 10 victims (amount capped by each allowance), then sells the collected BNBX for WBNB (~5 ETH).", "lesson": "A function that accepts a user address and pulls their allowance is a drain list — check every such entry point for an owner check before amounts are moved.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "BigBangSwap (reward sale)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "pricing flaw (sellRewardToken pays above market rate)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The reward proxy's sellRewardToken() buys back BGG at a rate that ignores the market price. The attacker flashloans 50 BUSD and loops 70×: a fresh contract swaps 15 BUSD to BGG and immediately sells the BGG via sellRewardToken() for more BUSD than it cost, returning the profit to the parent (~5K BUSD).", "lesson": "Reward-token buyback functions with a fixed or stale payout rate are an arb loop against the reward pool — compare the payout rate to the spot price.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Chainge Finance (minter proxy)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (swap executes arbitrary transferFrom data)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Chainge's minter-proxy swap() executes the caller-supplied data as a token operation without verifying the from-owner. The attacker loops 12 tokens (USDT, SOL, AVAX, BABYDOGE, ...) passing transferFrom(victim, attacker, victim's full balance) — his contract even fakes allowance/balance responses to the proxy's checks — and drains each victim's approved holdings (~$200K).", "lesson": "Cross-chain/aggregation swap paths that forward arbitrary calldata must enforce the owner == caller invariant inside the token op, not in a check the attacker's own contract can fake.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "FIL314 (hourBurn)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public burn of contract reserves)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "FIL314's hourBurn() is public and burns the token contract's own reserve each call. The attacker buys tokens for 0.05 BNB through the contract's buy fallback, loops hourBurn() 6,000× to deflate the supply and inflate the buyback price, then sells his tokens back to the contract 10× at the new rate (~14 BNB).", "lesson": "Supply-deflation functions that anyone can spam turn the buyback desk into a price pump the caller alone controls.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "GFA (reward claim)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (unvalidated reward claim parameters)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The GFA reward contract's claim (selector 0x5f7938f1) credits the caller a huge GFA amount from caller-supplied parameters (400M tokens, 40M, 12222) with no validation of eligibility. The attacker claims after a small BUSD→GFA buy, then sells the credited GFA back to BUSD (~$14K).", "lesson": "Reward claims that accept the reward amount (or its components) as arguments are free mints — the amount must be derived from on-chain locked state.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "GROKD (staking pool params)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public updatePool with caller-supplied PoolInfo)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The GROKD staking contract's updatePool() is callable by anyone with a caller-supplied PoolInfo struct. The attacker deposits LP tokens, rewrites pool 0 with rewardPerBlock = 48M GROKD and an endBlock 100M blocks away, rolls one block, calls update() and reward(), and sells the minted rewards for BNB (~150 BNB).", "lesson": "Pool parameters that determine emission rates must be owner-set only — a public setter turns the farm's whole reward budget into attacker income.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Hackathon (double-credit transfer)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (sender==recipient==pair double-credits balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Hackathon token's transfer credits the recipient twice when sender == recipient == pair. The attacker flashloans 200K BUSD and loops 10×: swap BUSD→Hackathon, transfer the whole balance directly to the pair (double-credit inflates the pair's real balance beyond reserves), skim the phantom surplus, and sell the remaining Hackathon back to BUSD (~$20K).", "lesson": "Transfer implementations that special-case address equality are a bug magnet — the pair-address branch must credit exactly once, or skim() becomes a printer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Hedgey Finance (campaign claim/cancel)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (claim", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans USDC and calls createLockedCampaign with an empty merkle root and zeroed claimLockup parameters (start/cliff/period/periods = 0), then immediately cancelCampaign(). Hedgey's cancel refunds the locked amount while the claim path stays live, so the attacker keeps both the refund and claim rights — in this replay he then transferFrom()s Hedgey's remaining USDC balance through the approval the flawed flow left behind (real incident ~$48M).", "lesson": "Campaign refunds must be mutually exclusive with claims — an empty merkle root plus a refund path that doesn't burn claim rights is a double-spend of the entire escrow.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Hoppy Frog (ERC token quirk)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (transfer-to-contract inflates balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Hoppy token's transfer-to-its-own-contract path inflates the sender's balance (burn/reflection quirk). The attacker flashloans the pool's entire Hoppy reserve, swaps chunks to WETH, self-transfers 2.07e23 Hoppy to inflate his stack, and only needs a smaller WETH→Hoppy swap to repay the pool, keeping the difference (~0.3 ETH).", "lesson": "Self/contract transfers that mutate balances asymmetrically are a hidden mint — test every token for balance drift on transfer(to == from).", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MARS (tax reflections to pair)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (reflection-inflated pair pricing)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MARS reflects part of each transfer tax to the pair, so every swap grows the pair's MARS balance beyond its reserves. The attacker flashloans 350 WBNB and buys MARS in 1,000-ether chunks through fresh receiver contracts (each chunk triggering reflections), then sells in chunks at the reflection-inflated price, repaying the flashloan with >$100K left.", "lesson": "Reflection-to-pair mechanics give every trader a discount that compounds with trade count — chunked swap loops against such pairs are the fastest signal.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "NGFS (reserve sync setter)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public proxy-sync + reserve re-sync)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The NGFS token lets anyone call setProxySync() and then reserveMultiSync(), which re-syncs the Pancake pair's reserves using the pair's live balance and credits the caller the NGFS. The attacker registers himself as the sync proxy, re-syncs the pair, and dumps the credited NGFS for USDT (~$190K).", "lesson": "Any token function that can re-sync a pair's reserves must be owner-gated — a public sync setter gives the caller a price-rewrite primitive.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "OpenLeverage (liquidation/payoff)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (self-liquidation collects insurance payoff)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker creates a minimal xOLE lock to qualify, opens a leveraged long on market 24 through the OPBorrowingDelegator sized to the borrow limit, liquidates his own position, and then calls payoffTrade() — the insurance-backed payoff path never validated that the liquidated position was legitimate, paying out ~$234K.", "lesson": "Liquidations that pay out of an insurance pool must require a real underwater position — when the attacker can both open and liquidate instantly, the insurance pool is the exit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Pike Finance (UUPS proxy)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (unguarded initialize + upgradeToAndCall)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Pike Finance's UUPS proxy left initialize() unguarded, so the attacker re-initializes it with himself as owner (and his own helper addresses), then calls upgradeToAndCall() pointing the proxy at his own implementation and executing withdraw(this) — draining the proxy's ETH (~$1.4M).", "lesson": "UUPS proxies need an initializer guard AND an upgrade authorizer derived from storage — an unprotected initialize is a full proxy takeover.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Rico (BankDiamond flash)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "access-control (flash executes arbitrary calldata)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Rico's BankDiamond exposes a flash() that executes arbitrary caller-supplied calldata in the bank's context. The attacker passes token.transfer(attacker, bank's full balance) for USDC/ARB/LINK/wstETH/WETH/LP and transferFrom(owner, attacker, ...) for three owners with standing approvals, then swaps everything to USDC (~36K).", "lesson": "Flash functions that run arbitrary calldata convert every user approval into a withdrawal — restrict flash to whitelisted operations and never execute raw callbacks in the asset contract's context.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "SATX (pair sandwich via flash-swap)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (skim", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker buys SATX with 1e12 WBNB and adds liquidity, then flash-swaps 60 WBNB from the WBNB/CAKE pair. In the callback he swaps half the WBNB/SATX pair's token balance out, re-gifts SATX and re-syncs (skim/sync), sells the surplus at the manipulated ratio, and repays — draining the SATX pair (incident reported as ~$999M of SATX value destroyed).", "lesson": "Flash-swap callbacks that touch a second pair with skim/sync give the attacker two levers — verify k-invariant accounting across both pools before and after each callback step.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "SquidSwap (fixed-rate conversion)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "pricing flaw (swapTokens", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SquidSwap's swapTokens() and sellSwappedTokens() convert between SQUID_1 and SQUID_2 at internal rates that ignore live pair prices. The attacker flashloans 10K WBNB, buys SQUID_1, routes it through swapTokens(), then calls sellSwappedTokens(0) up to 8,000 times per round (4 rounds), harvesting the mispriced conversions (~$87K).", "lesson": "Converter contracts with fixed internal rates are one price-feed update away from being an infinite arb — compare their implied rate to the live pool every audit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Sumer Money (sdrETH repay reentrancy)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "Base", "class": "reentrancy (ETH refund before borrow-state update)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The helper mints USDC into sdrUSDC and borrows the sdrETH pool's full ETH balance, then calls repayBorrowBehalf with 1 wei of excess; the contract refunds the wei via ETH transfer before decrementing totalBorrows, and the receive() hook re-enters. Inside the re-entry the exchange rate is inflated (cash up, borrows not yet down), letting the attacker borrow the entire cbETH and USDC markets, redeem 150 ETH, and claim NFT rewards (~$350K).", "lesson": "Repay functions that refund excess ETH before updating borrow state are reentrant — the 1-wei refund is the attacker's re-entry ticket into the whole market.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "UPS (transfer quirk + reserve desync)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (pair transfer doesn't credit pair + skim loop)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The UPS token's transfer to the pair doesn't credit the pair's balance (reflection quirk), so gifting UPS directly inflates the pair's USDT-side position relative to recorded reserves. The attacker flashloans 3.5M USDT, loops UPS transfers plus skim() to harvest the excess, then swaps out USDT at the stale ratio (~$28K).", "lesson": "Tokens whose transfers don't move balances as expected break pair math — a skim loop over a desynced pair is the same signature as UPS/MINER/ATM: gift, skim, swap.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified contract 0x00C409", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (caller-supplied pool", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified 0x00C409 contract's function (0xba381f8f) prices a swap from caller-supplied data. The attacker seeds it with 4,704 WETH and invokes the function with his own contract as the price source — his contract mocks getReserves()/calcOutGivenIn() to return 1 — so the swap settles against a fake reserve snapshot and pays out ~18 WETH of profit.", "lesson": "Even unverified contracts can be mapped: if a call accepts attacker-supplied pool/price inputs, test whether mocked pricing responses change the payout — that is the signature of a trusted-external-pricing flaw.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "WSM (presale buyWithBNB vs spot)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "price manipulation (presale fixed rate ignores crashed spot)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 5M WSM from the 1% pool and sells it into the 0.3% pool, crashing WSM's spot price. He then sends all the BNB to the presale proxy's buyWithBNB(2,770,000, false), which sells WSM at the fixed presale rate — far more tokens than the spot price implies — and buys back WSM with the remaining BNB to repay the flashloan, keeping the surplus (≈18K of WSM value).", "lesson": "Presale buybacks with a fixed rate are a one-way door against spot — any flashloan that can crash the spot price before a fixed-rate purchase converts the presale into a discount.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "XBridge (permissionless token listing)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (anyone can listToken with fake chains)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "XBridge's listToken() was permissionless: for 0.15 ETH the attacker registers STC with arbitrary chain IDs (85,936 / 95,838). He then calls withdrawTokens(STC, attacker, xbridge's full STC balance) and the bridge releases its entire STC holding (~$1.6M).", "lesson": "Bridge token listings must be admin-gated and chain-validated — a permissionless listToken plus an unvalidated withdraw is a full treasury drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "YIEDL (SportVault redeem)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (redeem with 0 shares executes attacker aggregator calls)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "YIEDL's SportVault redeem() executes caller-supplied aggregator calldata (dataList) even with zero shares to redeem. The attacker passes 1inch unoswapTo calls that route the vault's own USDC/BTCB/BETH balances to his address and loops redeem(0, ...) 20×, draining the vault.", "lesson": "Redeem paths that run user-supplied aggregator calldata must gate execution on the shares actually burned — zero shares with full swap rights is a vault drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Yield Protocol (strategy mint/burn)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "logic flaw (donation + mint", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 400K USDC and mints pool tokens via mintDivested; he then transfers pool tokens into the strategy vault (donation), mints strategy tokens, and burns them in a sequence that double-counts the donated amount — each mint→burn round returns more pool tokens than it consumed, and the final burnDivested pays out more USDC than was deposited (~181K).", "lesson": "Strategy share math must be invariant under donations — when mint/burn of the strategy token doesn't conserve the pool-token balance, the vault's own donations become withdrawable profit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Z123 (liquidity-burning victim router)", "date": "2024-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (swaps burn pool liquidity, distorting ratios)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim router's Z123 swaps burn liquidity from the pool each call, progressively distorting the token ratios. The attacker flashloans 18M BUSD to buy Z123 and pump the pool, then drives the victim router's Z123→BUSD swap 79× — each swap burns more liquidity and improves his exit rate — selling at the increasingly favorable price (~$135K).", "lesson": "Routers whose swaps mutate pool liquidity (burn/deflection) create ratio drift that compounds per trade — count the liquidity delta of a single swap; if it's not zero, repeated swaps are profitable.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "pNetwork Burner (PNT convertAndBurn)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "price manipulation (conversion priced off pumped spot pool)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker pumps PNT with a simulated 70 WETH flashloan, then calls the Burner's convertAndBurn() — the conversion prices PNT against the spot pool he just moved, so his PNT is converted into WBTC/USDT at the inflated rate. He sells the proceeds back to WETH and repays, netting ~1.7 ETH.", "lesson": "Burn/convert functions that price a token on the pool the attacker can pump with one trade are the same oracle flaw as mints — check the conversion rate source.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "EXcommunity (Boy/Girl token price)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (token getPrice distortion via direct transfers)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 400K BUSDT, buys Boy through 10 helper contracts (3 BNB each), and distorts the token's getPrice() by transferring Boy directly into the Boy contract and gifting tokens into the Girl pair; he then loops 290 zero-amount transferFrom calls on the pair and skim()s the surplus, selling back for ~33 BNB.", "lesson": "Tokens whose price is a function of contract/pair balances can be repriced with direct gifts — trace getPrice() back to its inputs and check whether transfers move them.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Galaxy Fox (GFOX merkle root)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (public setMerkleRoot)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The GFOX claim contract's setMerkleRoot() is callable by anyone. The attacker sets the root to keccak256(attacker, 1.78e27) — a leaf of his own construction — then calls claim() with an empty proof for that amount and receives ~330K of GFOX.", "lesson": "The merkle root is the authorization layer of an airdrop — a public setter makes the whole drop a direct mint; gate it and consider a timelock.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "GPU (self-transfer doubling)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (self-transfer doubles balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The GPU token doubles the sender's balance on self-transfers. The attacker flash-swaps 22,600 BUSD from the BUSD/WBNB pair, buys GPU, loops 87 self-transfers to multiply his stack, sells everything back to BUSD, and repays the flash-swap (~$32K).", "lesson": "balanceOf(to) must equal balanceOf(to) + amount after transfer(to==from) — a token that fails that identity is a free mint via a loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "LiquidityTokens (Vow/Vusd staking rewards)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (reward distribution ignores LP hold time)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 19M BUSD to build Vow/Vusd LP, then stakes through a helper into the vulnerable staking contracts; the reward contract distributes TLN without checking how long the LP was held, so immediate stake→claim cycles pay out. He pumps Vow with the flashloan, locks the TLN in the Tlnswap, sells Vusd/Vow back to BUSD and repays (~200K).", "lesson": "Reward contracts must accrue rewards per block held, not pay on deposit — an instant stake-and-claim loop is the signature of missing hold-time accounting.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MetaDragon (transform mint)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "infinite-mint (transfer to self", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MetaDragon's transfer() executes transform(value) — a mint — whenever the recipient is the token contract itself or the NFT contract. The attacker loops transfer(tokenContract, i) for 40 token IDs, minting tokens with every call, then dumps them for WBNB (~$180K).", "lesson": "Any mint path reachable from transfer() with a caller-chosen recipient is an infinite mint — the to==address(this) branch must never credit value to the sender.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MixedSwapRouter (public swap callback)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "access-control (callback trusts caller-supplied payer", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The MixedSwapRouter's algebraSwapCallback() is public and trusts the caller-supplied payer and amounts. The attacker registers his own contract as the \"pool\" in a swap; when the router calls it, it invokes the callback with payer = victim and a huge WINR amount, so the router transferFrom()s the victim's WINR to the attacker (~10K WINR).", "lesson": "Swap callbacks must derive payer and amounts from the pool that called them — a callback that accepts a payer parameter is a theft primitive.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Normie (tax-driven pair inflation + skim)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "Base", "class": "logic flaw (token buyback tax inflates pair balance, skim harvest)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Each NORMIE transfer into the Sushi pair triggers the token contract's auto-buyback tax, inflating the pair's real balance beyond its reserves. The attacker flashloans NORMIE, sells 80%, then loops 50× (transfer NORMIE to the pair + skim() to harvest the surplus), finishing with a final skim and repay — ~$490K in the real incident.", "lesson": "Token taxes that route funds back into the pair create a skim-able surplus on every transfer — model pair balance vs reserves after one tax-triggering transfer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "OSN (dividend distribution)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (dividends paid to LP holders without hold-time", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The OSN dividend contract pays rewards to current LP holders with no hold-time or EOA requirement. The attacker flashloans 500K USDT to buy OSN and add liquidity, then rotates the LP through 100 fresh helper contracts — each one adds/removes liquidity and collects the dividend — repeating the claim 100× before swapping back (~$109K).", "lesson": "Dividends that pay \"whoever holds the LP right now\" are multi-claimable — require per-address accrual since deposit, or block contracts.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Predy Finance (pair registration + take)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "access-control (unrestricted pair registration with attacker price feed)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Predy lets anyone register a pair with an attacker-supplied price feed (getSqrtPrice returns a fixed 40e9) and whitelist disabled. The attacker registers such a pair, opens a zero-size trade to become the locker, and inside the trade callback calls take() for the pool's entire WETH and USDC — supplying them back only to bypass checkSafe/finalizeLock — then withdraws everything as pair owner (~$464K).", "lesson": "Pair registration must validate the price feed and pool addresses — when the attacker can supply both, the take() in the trade callback drains the pool.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "RedKeys Coin (game RNG)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (predictable on-chain RNG)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "RedKeys' game RNG hashes block.timestamp, prevrandao, coinbase, gaslimit and block.number — all values the attacker can compute in the same block. The PoC reproduces randomNumber() locally, predicts each outcome, and plays 50 rounds at 2× ratio always betting the winning side (~$12K).", "lesson": "Any RNG whose inputs are block context is deterministic to the miner/attacker — games need commit-reveal or off-chain randomness, not a hash of the block header.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "SATURN (sell-limit reset)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (per-block sell cap bypassed via direct pair transfer)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SATURN caps each seller via everyTimeSellLimitAmount(), resetting the cap each block. The attacker flashloans 3,300 WBNB to buy SATURN up to the cap, rolls one block to reset it, then gifts SATURN directly into the pair and calls pair.swap() for WBNB at the inflated ratio (~15 BNB).", "lesson": "Sell limits enforced only inside the token's own sell path are bypassed by direct pair transfers — the cap must be checked against the pair's balance movement, not the token's sell function.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "SCROLL (UniversalRouter sweep)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (permissionless SWEEP on router)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Uniswap UniversalRouter held SCROLL tokens from the token's trading tax, and its SWEEP command (0x05) accepted an arbitrary recipient with no sender check. The attacker sweeps the router's SCROLL, drives a pair swap, and sweeps the rest to himself, converting ~76 ETH.", "lesson": "Routers that accumulate fee tokens are sweep targets — verify the SWEEP/PERMIT commands enforce the recipient against msg.sender.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Sonne Finance (timelock + donation)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "Optimism", "class": "governance (executing queued proposals) + donation attack", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker executes five governance proposals that had been queued in the timelock (anyone can execute after the delay): they add the soVELO market and set its collateral factor with no borrow cap. He then mints soVELO with dust VELO, donates VELO directly to the soVELO contract to inflate its exchange rate, borrows USDC against the donated \"collateral\", and redeems his VELO back (~$20M).", "lesson": "Queued-but-unexecuted governance proposals are live attack surface — audit every queued call as if it executes now, and never let a new market ship with CF > 0 and no borrow cap.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "TCH (burn signature malleability)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (signature v-byte malleability → replayed burns)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "TCH's burnToken() verifies an ECDSA signature but accepts tampered v bytes (0x1c→0x01, 0x1b→0x00), so the attacker re-malleates 34 genuine user burn signatures from past txs and replays them — each burns the pair's TCH and shrinks supply. Burning the pair's holdings pumps the TCH price, and the attacker sells back at the inflated rate (~$18K).", "lesson": "Signature validation must enforce v ∈ {27,28} and use nonces strictly — a malleable v turns every historical signed burn into a reusable supply weapon.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "TGC (staking reward rate)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (reward payout far exceeds stake)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The TGC staking contract pays USDT rewards after a hold period with a broken rate. The attacker swaps 100 USDT to TGC, stakes it (0x836aefb0), warps 5 hours, and claims (0xfd5a466f) — receiving ~29.8K USDT for a 100-USDT stake — funding the exit through a pair flash-swap (~$32K).", "lesson": "Re-derive the reward formula from stake size to payout — a rate that doesn't scale with the deposit is an emission dump the first staker collects.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "TSURU (wrapper onERC1155Received)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "Base", "class": "access-control (public onERC1155Received mints wrapper tokens)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The TSURU wrapper's onERC1155Received is public and mints wrapper tokens without verifying that an ERC1155 was actually transferred or that the caller is the 1155 contract. The attacker calls it directly with (0, self, id 0, 418), mints the corresponding wrapper tokens, and swaps them for ETH on UniV3 (~140K).", "lesson": "Receive hooks must mint only in response to a real transfer from the verified token contract — a public hook with no checks is a direct mint.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Tradeonorion (atomic swap redeem)", "date": "2024-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "validation flaw (redeemAtomic ignores signature", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Tradeonorion's redeemAtomic() releases assets from an order struct without verifying the embedded signature or the secret preimage. The attacker forges RedeemOrders (sender = victim Alice, receiver = attacker, arbitrary secretHash) to redeem Alice's deposited BUSDT/ORN repeatedly, then flashloans 4M BUSDT, deposits it, and \"redeems\" forged orders for 196M ORN, 401M BUSDT, 49.9M ORN, ~8 BNB and 6.2M XRP (~$645K).", "lesson": "Atomic-swap redeem must verify the order signature against the sender and require the secret preimage — an order struct treated as authoritative input is a free-withdrawal form.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "APEMAGA (family burn)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (public family() burns pair holdings)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "APEMAGA's family() function applies the token's burn mechanics to the named \"family\" account. The attacker buys APEMAGA with 0.1 ETH, calls family(pair) three times to burn the Uniswap pair's APEMAGA holdings, then calls sync() so the pair re-anchors at the reduced balance, and sells his tokens at the new, higher price (~9 ETH).", "lesson": "Token functions that can burn or mutate an arbitrary account's balance are pair-repricing weapons — check whether any public function can target the pair address.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Bazaar (vault exitPool)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "Blast", "class": "access-control (exitPool accepts attacker-chosen holder)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Bazaar's vault exitPool() takes a target holder ID without verifying ownership of the position. The attacker exits a victim's LP position with an ExitPoolRequest asking for 850M ETH out (bounded only by the actual pool), receiving ~392 ETH (~$1.4M).", "lesson": "Position-exit functions that accept a holder/account parameter are takeover primitives — the position must be derived from msg.sender, never from calldata.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "CRB2 (transfer-to-contract inflation)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (transfers to token contract inflate balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The CRB token's transfer-to-its-own-contract path inflates the sender's balance. The attacker flashloans 50K BUSD, cycles buy/sell 70×, then loops thousands of CRB transfers into the token contract (100 + 250 + 3,000 rounds), growing his stack, and sells the surplus, repaying the flashloan (~$15K).", "lesson": "Same transfer-to-contract inflation family as GPU/Hoppy — fuzz every token for balance drift when to == address(this), it is a free mint loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Dyson Money (vault redeem)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (share", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker mints Dyson share tokens with only ~901 USDC and ~901 USDT, deposits into the StableV1 strategy, harvests, and then — after donating his LP back to the AMM and burning — redeems 15,000 USDT and 18,000 USDC, roughly 36× his deposit, because the vault's redeem math skewed in his favor (~52 BNB).", "lesson": "Vault redeem amounts must be a function of shares × tracked assets — when donations to the underlying AMM shift the rate in the attacker's favor, redeem becomes the exit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "INcufi (referral reward farming)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (referral commissions paid per stake", "loss": "", "mechanics": "INcufi pays referral commissions on each stake/withdraw cycle with no hold-time requirement. The attacker registers himself as his own referrer, loops 100 stakes of 10K BUSD (warping 100s each), withdraws each order, and collects the AKITA commissions credited to his two referrer contracts, then swaps them for ~$59.6K of BUSD.", "lesson": "Referral rewards must accrue over real staked time — a self-referral stake/withdraw loop is the classic commission farm when payout isn't time-gated.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "JokInTheBox (repeatable unstake)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (unstake pays bonus without consuming the stake)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "JokInTheBox's unstake(0) pays out the lock-period bonus but never invalidates or decrements the stake. The attacker stakes with lock period 1, warps 3 days, and loops unstake(0) until the call reverts, collecting the bonus each iteration, then sells the tokens (~9.2 ETH).", "lesson": "Unstake/claim functions must consume exactly what they pay — a claim that leaves state intact is a while-loop payout.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MineSTM (fixed-rate sell)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "pricing flaw (sell() pays at internal rate ignoring pair)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The MineSTM contract's sell() pays BUSDT at an internal rate that ignores the live pair price. The attacker flashloans 50K BUSDT, syncs and buys STM on the Pancake pair, then calls updateAllowance() and sell(81)/sell(7) — converting STM to BUSDT at the internal rate for more than the pair price (~$13.8K).", "lesson": "Token-to-stable sell desks with fixed internal rates are arb terminals — compare their implied rate against the pair before and after one buy.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "NCD (missing onlyOwner on reward helpers)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public reward functions on helper contract)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "NCD's reward helper contract shipped without its onlyOwner modifiers (the PoC comments them out). The attacker deploys 100 copies of the helper: each preStartTimeRewards() skims the pair's NCD and starts the reward clock, and after a day each ack() claims; a second helper type drains the accrued USDC (~$6.4K).", "lesson": "Grep every deployed helper for functions that were clearly meant to be owner-only — missing modifiers on reward/skim functions convert them into public faucets.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "SteamSwap (MineSTM sell-rate, large)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "pricing flaw (same fixed-rate sell contract at scale)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same MineSTM sell-rate flaw at larger size: the attacker flashloans ~500K BUSD, buys STM on the synced Cake pair, calls updateAllowance(), and sells four chunks to the MineSTM contract at its internal rate, repaying the flashloan with ~$91K left.", "lesson": "When one PoC finds a fixed-rate sell desk, re-run it against every pair and size — the same contract drained multiple pools on different days.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "UwU Lend (sUSDE oracle overvaluation)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "oracle (misvalued sUSDE collateral → whole-market borrow)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "UwU's oracle overvalued sUSDE relative to its true exchange rate. The attacker deposits WETH and runs a borrow-loop: each iteration borrows as much sUSDE as the oracle-derived cap allows and re-deposits it as collateral, so the cap never binds and the entire sUSDE market drains into his hands; he then withdraws his collateral to the liquidation threshold, leaving a self-liquidating position he keeps the proceeds from (~$19.3M).", "lesson": "An oracle price above true collateral value is a borrow-limit nullifier — cross-check every yield-bearing collateral (sUSDE-style wrappers) against its live exchange rate, not its peg.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "UwU Lend (second drain with uSUSDE position)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "oracle (inflated uSUSDE position unlocks all markets)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Follow-up to the first UwU exploit: the attacker holds 60M uSUSDE whose value the protocol over-counts. He flashloans WETH, deposits it, borrows every market's full balance (WETH, CRV, crvUSD, DAI, USDT, FRAX, LUSD), and then withdraws all his WETH collateral — the health check still passes because the uSUSDE position carries the account (~$3.7M).", "lesson": "A single misvalued collateral token keeps paying out until the market is paused — after any oracle incident, re-audit every position holding the affected asset before unpausing.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Velocore (repeatable velocore__execute)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "Linea", "class": "logic flaw (same execute replayed inflates credit)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Velocore's velocore__execute could be replayed in one transaction without netting the pool's balance accounting. The attacker calls it 3× with identical tokens/amounts, each execution crediting him more USDC than it debits, and then drains the pool through four SwapFacet operations (~$6.88M).", "lesson": "State-changing pool operations must be idempotent per transaction — when the same call can be replayed for additional credit, the accounting isn't settled per execution.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "WIFCOIN (ETH) staking", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (claimEarned doesn't consume earnings)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The WIF staking contract's claimEarned(stakingId, burnRate) pays out accrued earnings without zeroing them. The attacker stakes 0.3 ETH worth of WIF, then loops claimEarned(3, 10) until the call reverts, collecting the same earnings each iteration, and sells everything (~3.4 ETH).", "lesson": "Claim functions must decrement the earned balance before paying — a claim that leaves earnings intact is a free loop for every holder.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "WILL (order settlement)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (self-settled expired orders at manipulated price)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The WILL trading contract lets anyone place a sell order with zero margin and settle expired positions after 20 seconds. The attacker places a 71K USDT sell order, buys WILL with 88K USDT to push the settlement price, then calls updateExpiredOrders() and settleExpiredPositions() to settle his own order for more than it was worth (~$52.8K).", "lesson": "Settlement functions that price expired orders on the spot market can be aimed by the order placer — check whether the settler and the order owner are allowed to be the same party.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "YYS (sell desk + pair donation)", "date": "2024-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "pricing flaw (internal sell rate vs donated pair)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The YYS sell contract pays BUSDT at an internal rate ignoring the pair. The attacker flashloans 4.75M BUSDT, donates it directly into the pair and swaps out YYS cheaply, then loops the sell contract's sell() in chunks to convert YYS back to BUSDT at the internal (higher) rate, repaying the flashloan (~$28K).", "lesson": "Sell desks plus donatable pairs compound — when the buy side is a manipulable pair and the sell side is a fixed-rate desk, the spread is the attacker's.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "DeFiPlaza (multi-token LP pricing)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (LP mint", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans from Aave, performs 8 swaps to skew the DeFiPlaza multi-token pool's balances, then calls addMultiple() with 88M-per-token caps (only his actual balances are pulled) and immediately removeLiquidity() for ETH — the LP share pricing on the skewed pool pays out more ETH than was put in. He closes with a chain of 1-wei dust swaps and an Aave supply to settle (~$200K).", "lesson": "LP add/remove flows that price shares off the pool's live balances can be cycled after one skewing swap — verify the add/remove round-trip is lossless at extreme balances.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Dough Finance (ConnectorDeleverageParaswap)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (deleverage connector executes arbitrary swapData)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Dough Finance's ConnectorDeleverageParaswap.flashloanReq() executes attacker-supplied swapData as raw calls without validation. The PoC supplies two calls: a deleverage-swap call and USDC.transferFrom(onBehalfOf, attacker, 596.7e18) — the victim's standing approval of the connector lets the second call drain them while the attacker repays only a dust Aave position (~$1.81M).", "lesson": "Connectors that execute raw user-supplied swapData must simulate and whitelist each call — a single transferFrom in the data turns every approval into a withdrawal.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "GAX (unprotected withdraw)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public function pays out contract's BUSD)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The GAX contract's function (selector 0x6c99d7c8) transfers a caller-specified amount of BUSD from the contract with no authorization or recipient restriction. The PoC passes the contract's full BUSD balance and drains ~50K BUSD in one call.", "lesson": "Any function that moves contract-held funds must derive both amount and recipient from state, not calldata — a one-call full-balance drain is the signature of a missing access check.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "LW (transferFrom the token contract)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (transferFrom pulls from the token contract itself)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The LW token contract holds its own treasury tokens, and its transferFrom allows pulling directly from the token contract's balance. The PoC calls Lw.transferFrom(address(Lw), attacker, 1e30) to grab the treasury, then loops 9,999 swaps of 800M LW to BUSDT on the Pancake pair (~$7K).", "lesson": "transferFrom must reject or properly account for pulls from the token contract's own address — a treasury balance that is transferFrom-able is a giveaway.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "LiFi (depositToGasZipERC20 swapData)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (diamond executes arbitrary callData)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "LiFi's depositToGasZipERC20 executed the caller-supplied SwapData.callData without verifying the sending asset. The attacker passes his own fake token as sendingAssetId (its balanceOf/allowance are mocked) and callData = USDT.transferFrom(victim, attacker, 2.27M); the diamond executes the transferFrom against the victim's standing approval (~$10M).", "lesson": "Bridge/swap diamonds must verify the from-asset is real and owned by the caller before executing embedded callData — an arbitrary-call path plus user approvals is a mass drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MEV bot 0xdd7c (public swap callback)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (bot's uniswapV3SwapCallback callable by anyone)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The 0xdd7c MEV bot exposes a public uniswapV3SwapCallback (0xfa461e33). The attacker invokes it directly with a negative delta and attacker-crafted path/amount data, making the bot's own logic pay WETH, USDT and USDC to the attacker (~$18K).", "lesson": "MEV bot callbacks that don't verify the pool caller are open treasuries — fuzz each bot's selectors with attacker-controlled deltas and watch the token flows.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MRP / WMRP (314 wrapper)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (self-transfer inflation + fallback re-deposit loop)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The WMRP \"314\" wrapper mints MRP on BNB deposits and inflates balances on self-transfers. The attacker bounces 43/58 ETH through the wrapper (its refund fallback re-deposits 58 ETH automatically), transfers MRP to himself until his balance exceeds 6,000 MRP, then loops 20 self-transfers to multiply it (~17 BNB).", "lesson": "314-style wrappers that mint on deposit AND inflate on self-transfer are double printers — trace the balance delta of every deposit/refund/self-transfer path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Minterest (lendRUSDY rounding)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "Mantle", "class": "rounding (exchange-rate manipulation via lend during flashloan)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker enables musdy as collateral and loops 24×: flashloan the pool's entire usdy, wrap it to musd inside the callback, and lend it as RUSDY — each cycle shrinking the wrapped amount by ~3.8e14 so the lend rounds in his favor and inflates his exchange rate. After the loop he redeems ~4.27e24 usdy (a multiple of his input) and borrows 223 WETH + 204 mETH (~427 ETH).", "lesson": "Lend/wrap exchange-rate math must be exact under repeated flashloan-driven cycles — a rounding loss that compounds per iteration is a full-market drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "SBT SmartBank (loan math)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (Loan_Get pays more than deposited collateral)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SmartBank's Loan_Get() pays out USDT from a broken collateral calculation. The attacker flashloans 1.95M BUSD, deposits 950K into the bank, calls _Start() + Buy_SBT() + Loan_Get(1,966,930) to take a ~1.97M USDT \"loan\", and repays the flashloan with the proceeds (~56K BUSD profit).", "lesson": "Loan payouts must be bounded by the deposited collateral at a healthy LTV — when Loan_Get exceeds the deposit, the bank is paying out of other users' funds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Spectra Finance (router arbitrary commands)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (router executes attacker command with victim transferFrom)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Spectra's router exposes a UniversalRouter-style execute (0x3593564c) that runs caller-supplied commands. The attacker submits command 0x12 with data encoding asdCRV.transferFrom(victim, attacker, victim's full balance); the router executes it in its own context against the victim's standing approval (~$73K).", "lesson": "Routers that re-execute arbitrary user commands must enforce the payer — the transferFrom inside the command data is the exact ParaSwap/LiFi pattern, and it always targets standing approvals.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified contract 0x452E25 (public swap callback)", "date": "2024-07", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (public uniswapV3SwapCallback pays caller)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified 0x452E25 contract exposes a public uniswapV3SwapCallback that pays out WETH based on caller-supplied deltas. The attacker calls it with 27.3 ETH of amount0/amount1 deltas and data (true, WETH) and receives ~27 ETH directly.", "lesson": "Even for unverified contracts, a public uniswapV3SwapCallback is a red flag — check whether the payer address and amounts are validated against a real pool.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "ParaSwap Aave RepayAdapter — AAVE_Repay_Adapter.sol", "date": "2024-08", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (uncleaned tokenTransferProxy allowance + crafted swap callees)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The RepayAdapter's swapAndRepay never zeroes the fromToken allowance it grants to the ParaSwap tokenTransferProxy. The attacker supplies LIDOWST/WBTC as Aave collateral and borrows USDT, then runs one priming swapAndRepay — whose crafted simpleSwap calldata points the \"callees\" at his own contract — leaving the proxy with a huge LIDOWST allowance; a second swapAndRepay passes exchangeData = LIDOWST.transfer(attacker, adapter's full balance) plus his own withdraw() that repays the USDT debt from his pocket, so the adapter's entire LIDOWST balance moves to him while he withdraws his Aave collateral back (~56K).", "lesson": "Swap-and-repay adapters must zero the transfer-proxy allowance after every swap and validate the exchange callees — leftover approvals plus caller-chosen callees is a direct token-theft path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "COCO (BSC)", "date": "2024-08", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (direct BUSD injection then swap at stale ratio)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The PoC pranks the vulnerable address and transferFrom()s 2,212 BUSD straight into the CAKE LP, then immediately calls pair.swap() to take out ~3.64e24 COCO priced at the pre-injection ratio (~280 BNB). The vulnerable contract is unverified, so the exact accounting flaw is inferred from the flow: a balance injection without reserve sync funds the swap-out.", "lesson": "A one-line PoC (inject tokens into the pair, swap out the other side) is the fingerprint of reserve-desync — check every pair for balance changes that bypass the swap path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Convergence (CVG reward distributor)", "date": "2024-08", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (distributor trusts claim-contract return value)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Convergence's reward distributor calls each staking-position service's claimCvgCvxMultiple() and pays out whatever amount it returns. The attacker registers his own contract as a claim contract; its claimCvgCvxMultiple returns uint256.max - currentSupply, and the distributor mints that CVG to the attacker (~$200K).", "lesson": "Distributors must whitelist the claim contracts they call and cap payouts server-side — the return value of an untrusted position service is attacker input.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "iVest DAO (skim/sync re-anchor)", "date": "2024-08", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (pump, gift to pair, skim+sync, swap out)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 1,200 WBNB and pumps iVest with 30 consecutive buys, then gifts iVest into the pair three times with skim(address(0)) + sync() to re-anchor the reserves, and finally swaps a calculated 30.8e9 iVest back for WBNB at the manipulated ratio (~338 WBNB).", "lesson": "skim/sync sequences that re-anchor reserves to an attacker-chosen snapshot convert gifted tokens into exit liquidity — trace the reserve delta around every sync.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "NovaXM2E (stake value sandwich)", "date": "2024-08", "chain": "BSC", "class": "price manipulation (stake snapshots USDT value, withdraw prices at spot)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The staking contract converts the staked token amount to a USDT value at stake time and converts that USDT value back to tokens at withdraw time using the live price. The attacker pumps NovaXM2E with a 500K USDT flash-swap, stakes half at the peak price, dumps the rest to crash the price, then withdraws — receiving far more tokens than he staked — and sells them (~$25K).", "lesson": "Stakes that value the deposit in a quote currency at entry and re-price at exit are sandwichable — the entry/exit price pair must be the same source, ideally TWAP.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "OMPx (purchase/buyBack spread)", "date": "2024-08", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "pricing flaw (buyBack pays more than purchase cost)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The OMPx contract's purchase() accepts a caller-set maxPrice cap and buyBack() pays out at its internal price. The attacker flashloans 100 WETH and loops 7×: purchase the contract's entire OMPX balance at maxPrice 1e13 (cap ineffective), then buyBack the same tokens at minPrice 1 — each round's buyback returns more ETH than the purchase cost, netting ~4.37 ETH after repaying the Balancer flashloan.", "lesson": "Purchase and buyback functions must be inverse at the same price — when the two price formulas differ, the spread is a free round-trip loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "VOW (vscTokenManager conversion)", "date": "2024-08", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (stale VOW→vUSD conversion rate)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swaps the VOW/WETH pair's entire VOW reserve and routes it through the vscTokenManager, which converts VOW to vUSD at a stale rate; he sells the minted vUSD back into the vUSD/VOW pair for more VOW, repays the flash-swap with a slice, and repeats the same flow against the VOW/USDT pair — netting ~$1M in ETH and USDT.", "lesson": "Token-manager conversion rates that don't track the live market let one flash-swap mint the whole backing — audit the conversion formula against the spot pairs it feeds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Yodl Router (transferFee)", "date": "2024-08", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (transferFee accepts caller-chosen from", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Yodl Router's transferFee() moves USDC from a caller-named `from` to a caller-named `to` with no authorization — only the victims' standing approvals gate the call. The PoC drains 4 victims with transferFee(amount, 10,000 bps, USDC, victim, attacker) (~5K).", "lesson": "Any function whose signature includes both `from` and `to` parameters is an allowance-drain candidate — the payer must be derived from msg.sender or a verified permit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Zenterest (stale price + donation)", "date": "2024-08", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "oracle (out-of-date WHITE price; donation-inflated collateral)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With WHITE's price feed out of date, the attacker transfers 2,000 MPH directly into the zenMPH contract and mints 21,200 MPH as collateral, then borrows the zenWHITE market's entire WHITE balance against the mispriced position, repaying the 85 ETH flashloan with the borrowed WHITE (~$21K).", "lesson": "A stale price feed plus a donatable cToken is a double vulnerability — re-check exchange-rate math and feed freshness together, since each one weakens the other's check.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified contract 0x8de7 (public grantRole)", "date": "2024-08", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public grantRole → adminWithdraw)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified 0x8de7 contract exposes a public grantRole(). The attacker grants himself the default admin role (bytes32(0)) and then calls adminWithdraw() with the header contract, DAI, and his own address to pull ~10.4K DAI (~$10K).", "lesson": "grantRole with no access control is an instant admin-takeover button — check who can call role-granting functions and whether DEFAULT_ADMIN_ROLE is ever handable.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "AIRBTC (airdrop swap)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public swap pays out contract's token balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The AIRBTC contract's swap function (selector 0x008ea502) pays out tokens based on the contract's own balance with no authorization — the attacker passes the contract's full PandaToken balance with the \"AIR\" tag and an amount of 96, receives the tokens in his contract, and swaps them to BUSD via PancakeSwap (~$6.8K).", "lesson": "Swap/airdrop functions that pay out from the contract's balance with caller-supplied parameters are drains — check who can call and how the amount is derived.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Bankroll Network (buyFor self-loop)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (dividend accounting broken by buyFor to contract)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Bankroll's buyFor() lets the caller name the customer. The attacker flashloans 16K WBNB, buys tokens for himself, then loops 2,810× buyFor(bankRollContract, its own WBNB balance) — each call credits tokens to the contract without spending attacker funds and inflates his dividend share — then sells his tokens and withdraws the dividends (~404 WBNB).", "lesson": "Buy functions that accept a customer parameter must exclude the contract itself and recompute dividends against actual flows — a self-buy loop is a dividend printer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Bedrock (uniBTC vault mint)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "rounding (vault mint math) + access-control", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Bedrock's uniBTC vault mint() (impl L2417-2420) lacks access control and its mint math lets the attacker mint more uniBTC than the ETH deposited. The attacker flashloans 30.8 ETH, mints uniBTC with it, swaps uniBTC→WBTC→WETH through the 0.05% UniV3 pools, repays the Balancer flashloan and keeps the excess WETH (~$1.7M).", "lesson": "Vault mint functions must be permissioned and exact — a public mint with favorable rounding converts every flashloan into a free mint/swap arb.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Caterpillar Coin (CUT)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (LP donation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swaps 4.5M BUSD and loops 10 fresh contracts: each buys CUT, adds BUSD/CUT liquidity, sells the leftover CUT, then transfers its LP tokens to the pair contract itself and burns them — distorting the pair's LP accounting — before selling CUT again at the skewed ratio and returning the BUSD to the parent (~$1.4M).", "lesson": "LP tokens sent to the pair contract or burned change the pair's implied price — trace LP balance movement in every token flow; a burn loop is a repricing loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "DOGGO (balance-threshold token)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (token-contract balance threshold manipulated around swap)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "DOGGO's transfer behavior changes around a token-contract balance threshold (~4.207e18). The attacker flashloans the UniV3 pool's DOGGO, sells a computed chunk to WETH, tops the token contract's own balance to just below the threshold, sells the remainder, and buys back the exact amount to repay — netting the spread (~$7K).", "lesson": "Tokens with mechanics keyed to their own contract balance are a price-lever — model the behavior exactly at the threshold before assuming a swap is fair.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "HANA (balance-threshold token)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (same threshold mechanic at 1e17+1)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same family as DOGGO: HANA's behavior changes around a token-contract balance threshold of 100000000000000001. The attacker flashloans the UniV3 pool's HANA, sells a chunk to WETH, refills the token contract's own balance to the threshold, sells the rest, and repays the flash (~$283 in this replay).", "lesson": "When you find one threshold-gated token exploit, sweep sibling tokens by the same deployer — the mechanic is usually copy-pasted.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "INUMI (marketing wallet takeover)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (public setMarketingWallet + rescueEth)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The INUMI token contract's setMarketingWallet() is public, and rescueEth() sends the contract's entire ETH balance to the marketing wallet. The attacker sets the wallet to his own address and calls rescueEth(), receiving ~$11K of ETH.", "lesson": "rescue/withdraw functions that pay a configurable wallet are only as safe as the setter — a public setter plus a rescue function is a complete drain pair.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MARA (unvalidated payout amounts)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (function pays WBNB per caller-supplied entries)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The MARA contract's function (selector 0x5fc985ea) pays out WBNB per caller-supplied amount entries without validation. The attacker funds an 11-WBNB DODO flashloan, calls the function with two 10-WBNB entries (addresses pointing at the Pancake pair), swaps the proceeds, and repays (~8.8 WBNB).", "lesson": "Payout functions whose amount arrays come straight from calldata are free withdrawals — every entry must be checked against an on-chain balance or entitlement.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "OTSea (repeatable claim)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (claim doesn't consume positions; overlapping index batches)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "OTSea's staking claim() doesn't mark positions as claimed, so the same indexes can be claimed repeatedly. The attacker runs 46 claim/withdraw batches with overlapping index ranges (each batch re-using earlier indexes plus a new one), collecting the rewards multiple times, then swaps the OTSea tokens to ETH (~$26K).", "lesson": "Claims must consume or time-gate exactly what they pay — overlapping index batches are the manual version of a while-loop claim.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "OnyxDAO (oETH rounding + fake liquidation markets)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "rounding + access-control (liquidation accepts attacker-supplied market contracts)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Two flaws combine: (1) oETH's dust mint (x wei) / redeemUnderlying(y) rounds in the attacker's favor, and looping it 54× inflates his exchange rate; (2) the NFTLiquidation contract's liquidateWithSingleRepay() trusted caller-supplied oTokenRepay/oTokenCollateral addresses — the attacker's fake repay token's transfer() then liquidates his own oVUSD borrow against oETH and redeems the collateral, netting >$3.8M across VUSD/XCN/DAI/WBTC/USDT.", "lesson": "Liquidation entry points must resolve markets from registered state, never from calldata — pairing that with a mint/redeem rounding loop turns the whole protocol into exit liquidity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "PLN (zero-amount transferFrom triggers deflation)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (burn", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The PLN token executes its burn/reflection logic even on zero-amount transferFrom calls. The attacker buys PLN with WETH, then calls PLN.transferFrom(victim, dead, 0) — a zero-value transfer that still triggers the deflation on the victim's (and effectively the pair's) holdings — repricing the token, and sells his PLN back to WETH (~$400K).", "lesson": "Zero-amount transfers must be no-ops — if any token logic fires on amount == 0, the attacker can trigger supply shocks without spending anything.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Penpie (permissionless withdrawMarket)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (deposit helper withdrawable by anyone)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Penpie's PendleMarketDepositHelper.withdrawMarket() lacked access control, letting the attacker withdraw the helper's staked Pendle LP of the agETH and rswETH markets. Funded by a Balancer flashloan of both assets, he harvests the markets' rewards (batchHarvestMarketRewards + multiclaim) and redeems the LP into underlying tokens via the Pendle router, repaying the flashloan with part of the proceeds.", "lesson": "Deposit helpers that let anyone withdraw staked positions turn every user's stake into the caller's harvest — withdraw must check the deposit owner.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Pesto the Baby King Penguin", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (balance-threshold token mechanic)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Same DOGGO/HANA family: Pesto's transfer behavior changes around a token-contract balance threshold of 4.2e18. The attacker flashloans the UniV3 pool's Pesto, sells a chunk to WETH, refills the token contract's own balance to the threshold, sells the remainder, and buys back the exact flash amount (~$1.4K).", "lesson": "Threshold-gated tokenomics are an exploit pattern in themselves — the third copy of the same mechanic should be your first grep target.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Pythia (reward reset on share transfer)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (claimable rewards re-credited to fresh share holders)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Pythia's staking contract credits rewards to whoever holds the staking shares without per-share accrual tracking. The attacker stakes 0.5 ETH of PYTHIA, then loops 30×: transfer the shares to a fresh helper, claim the credited rewards there, and re-stake the payout — compounding until he exits with ~21 ETH.", "lesson": "Staking rewards must accrue per share-epoch, not per address — a share transfer that resets claimable rewards is a compounding loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Shezmu (public collateral mint)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (collateral token mint lacks access control)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Per the PoC's own note, the Shezmu Vault's collateral token mint() has no access control — anyone can mint any amount. The attacker mints type(uint128).max - 1 collateral tokens, deposits them via addCollateral(), and borrows ~1e26 ShezUSD against them (~$4.9M).", "lesson": "Collateral tokens are the trust root of the vault — a permissionless mint makes the borrow function a stablecoin printer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "WXETA (public init + mint-to-pair)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (unguarded initialize + mint targets the pair)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The WXETA diamond's initialize() is unguarded and its mint() accepts an arbitrary recipient. The attacker re-initializes with max values, mints 1e15 tokens directly to the Pancake pair, then calls pair.swap() to take the pair's BUSD and sells the WXETA for BNB (~$110K).", "lesson": "An initializer without a guard plus a mint without recipient restrictions is a two-step money printer — either check alone would have stopped the drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified contract 0x03f9 (public swap callback)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (public uniswapV3SwapCallback pays caller)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified contract exposes a public uniswapV3SwapCallback that pays WETH based on caller-supplied deltas. The attacker calls it with positive/negative deltas and data (WETH, attacker, 10000), receives the WETH, and withdraws it (~$1.7K).", "lesson": "A public swap callback on an unverified contract is still readable — the deltas + data decide the payout, and an unvalidated callback is an open WETH faucet.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified contract 0x16d0 (multiCallWithRevert) — unverified_16d0.sol", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (arbitrary token calls via multicall)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified 0x16d0 contract's multiCallWithRevert() executes arbitrary calls against a token address. The attacker passes TetherToken with transferFrom(victim, attacker, victim's full balance), spending the victim's standing USDT approval on the contract (~$329).", "lesson": "Multicall facades that execute arbitrary token calldata convert approvals into withdrawals — the token and calldata must both be whitelisted.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified contract 0x5697 (victim approval capture)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (exploit obtained victim's max WETH approval)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The real attack induced the victim (0x7c243e…) to grant a max WETH approval to the attacker — through the unverified contract's logic — after which the attacker simply transferFrom()s ~5.05 WETH. The PoC replays the approval + transfer with a prank (~$12K).", "lesson": "When an incident's traces show a fresh max-approval from a victim, the bug is upstream in whatever function earned that approval — trace it, don't just replay it.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified contract 0x766a (pancakeV3SwapCallback)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (public callback pays WBNB)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified contract's pancakeV3SwapCallback is public and pays out WBNB for caller-supplied deltas. The attacker calls it with a large negative amount0 and a positive amount1 (51156128500000 × 3600) plus data (attacker, contract), withdraws the received WBNB and forwards the ETH (~$100).", "lesson": "Same MEV-bot callback pattern as 0xdd7c and 0x452E25 — a public callback with caller-controlled deltas is the most replicated low-hanging drain of 2024.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified contract 0xa89f (uniswapV3SwapCallback)", "date": "2024-09", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (public callback pays WETH)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The unverified 0xb309 contract's uniswapV3SwapCallback is public; the attacker calls it with 0.36 ETH of positive delta and a negative counter-delta plus packed data, and the callback transfers WETH to the caller (~$1.5K).", "lesson": "The public-callback drain appeared at least five times in 2024 alone — grep every candidate's dispatcher for uniswapV3SwapCallback/pancakeV3SwapCallback and check the caller validation.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "AIZPT (self-sell token)", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (transfer-to-contract = sell paid from contract's own BNB)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The AIZPT token processes transfers to the token contract itself as sells, paying BNB from its own balance. The attacker flashloans 8,000 BNB, buys AIZPT with them, then loops 199 self-sell transfers (3.84M AIZPT each) — each draining more of the contract's BNB — and repays the flashloan (~34.88 BNB).", "lesson": "Tokens that treat transfer(to == token) as a sell funded by the contract's own balance are self-draining ATMs — one buy plus a transfer loop empties the treasury.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "BUBAI (ORAAI transferFrom the pair)", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (transferFrom pulls the pair's whole balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The ORAAI token's transferFrom lets the attacker pull the Uniswap pair's entire ORAAI balance (minus 100) directly into his wallet. He then calls pair.sync() to re-anchor the reserves and sells the tokens for ETH (~$131K).", "lesson": "transferFrom against the pair address must be impossible without the pair's consent — a token that lets anyone pull pair funds turns sync+sell into a full drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Unknown Compound fork on Base — CompoundFork_exploit.sol", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "Base", "class": "oracle manipulation (uSUI price feed follows manipulable UniV3 spot)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The fork's price oracle for uSUI tracks a UniV3 spot pool. The attacker Morpho-flashloans 800 WETH, mints a small cWETH position, borrows the fork's entire uSUI market, then swaps 500 WETH→uSUI to pump uSUI in the oracle; with uSUI valued at the pumped price, his 50-uSUI deposit counts as huge collateral, so he borrows the entire cWETH market before swapping back and repaying (~$1M).", "lesson": "A fork is only as safe as its price feeds — when the oracle for a listed asset is a spot pool, one swap converts dust collateral into the whole market's borrow capacity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Erc20transfer (whitehat rescue)", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (public erc20TransferFrom with caller-supplied from", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The contract at 0x43dc… exposes a public erc20TransferFrom() that executes a transferFrom with caller-supplied token, from, to and amount. The PoC replays the whitehat's rescue call — moving a victim's approved USDC to safety before the attacker's drain — demonstrating that the function lets anyone move any user's approved funds (~$14.7K at stake).", "lesson": "A function named erc20TransferFrom with all four parameters caller-supplied is an allowance drain by construction — the `from` must always be derived from msg.sender or a verified permit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "FIRE (transfer-to-pair inflation)", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (_transfer bug inflates pair balance on direct transfer)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "FIRE's _transfer() (L274-279) inflates the pair's FIRE balance when tokens are transferred directly to it. The attacker Aave-flashloans ETH and loops fresh 20-ETH contracts (the token rejects contract buyers, so one contract per buy): each buys FIRE, transfers the pair's balance to the pair again to trigger the inflation, and swaps out WETH for the inflated delta (~8.45 ETH).", "lesson": "A transfer function that doesn't conserve balances when the recipient is the pair gives every buyer a mint — check pair balance before/after one direct transfer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "HYDT (MintV2 priced off WBNB/USDT pair)", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "BSC", "class": "oracle manipulation (initialMint uses spot pair price)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "HYDT's MintV2 mints tokens using the WBNB/USDT pair price. The attacker flashloans 11M USDT, swaps it to WBNB to pump the pair, then calls initialMint() with 11 BNB — minting HYDT at the manipulated price — sells the HYDT back to USDT, and repays (~5.8K USDT).", "lesson": "Mint functions that read a spot pair for their rate are one pump away from a free mint — the mint price must come from TWAP or a committed rate.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Lava Lending (cUSDC collateral vs LP borrow)", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "pricing flaw (cUSDC collateral over-valued against LP borrow)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deposits his cUSDC position into Lava's LendingPool and borrows 430.6 of the WETH/USDC LP wrapper token against it — the pool's valuation of the cUSDC collateral (or of the LP) lets the borrow exceed the collateral's true value — and he immediately withdraws the LP (~$130K).", "lesson": "When a lending pool accepts yield-bearing receipts as collateral, the receipt's value must be computed through its own exchange rate, not face value — otherwise the deposit unlocks more than it's worth.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Morpho Blue (PAXG market oracle)", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "oracle (PAXG collateral mispriced → over-borrow)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans PAXG and supplies it as collateral in a Morpho Blue PAXG/USDC market whose oracle pricing lets him borrow more USDC than the collateral's true value. He swaps USDC→PAXG to repay the flashloan and keeps the difference (~$230K).", "lesson": "Even in permissionless-market architectures the oracle is the invariant — a PAXG feed that reads a manipulable source makes the whole isolated market a free loan.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "P719 (self-sell + burn-inflated price)", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (sell-to-contract pays from contract funds at burn-inflated price)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "P719 processes transfers to the token contract as sells, paying BNB from its own balance at a price that its own burn/fee logic wrongly inflates. The attacker flashloans 4,000 BNB, buys through 47 helper contracts to pump the internal price, consolidates the tokens, then sells via 100 fresh helpers at the inflated price — draining the contract's BNB (~547 BNB).", "lesson": "Sell-to-contract mechanics combined with burn-driven price inflation are a compounding drain — audit the sell price formula against the burn's effect on supply.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "SASHA (token mechanic credit)", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (gift-to-pair triggers token supply mechanic)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SASHA token's mechanics credit the sender far more tokens than paid (a 0.07 ETH buy plus a 1e18 SASHA gift to the V2 pair yields a ~9.9e22 SASHA position). The attacker then dumps the credited stack on the UniV3 1% pool for ~249 ETH.", "lesson": "Tokens whose transfer/reflection logic mints on pair interactions turn a dust purchase into a whale position — compare the balance delta of every transfer path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "VISTA (flashloan burn + free-balance sell)", "date": "2024-10", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (token flashloan burns balance without freeze check)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "VISTA's flashLoan burns the borrowed amount from the token contract without checking the token's frozen state, and getFreeBalance() exposes the unburned remainder. The attacker flashloans 1,500 USDT to buy BUSD and stake it in the presale for VISTA, then flashloans his VISTA and — inside the callback — sells the exposed \"free balance\" through the sale contract at its fixed rate, repaying the outer flashloan (~29K USDT).", "lesson": "Token-level flashloans that burn balances are only safe if the burn/freeze accounting is airtight — an exposed free balance sold at a fixed rate is a direct drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "AK1111 (public lzReceive mint)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (nonblockingLzReceive1 mints for anyone)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Per the PoC comment, AK1111's nonblockingLzReceive1() — the LayerZero-style receive entry — lacks access control and mints tokens for free. The attacker calls it with the pair's balance as the amount, minting that many AK1111 to himself, then swaps them for BUSD (~$31.5K).", "lesson": "Cross-chain receive hooks must verify the lz endpoint and message — a public receive that mints is a free mint for whoever calls it first.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Chi Protocol (Balancer flashloan recipient)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (protocol contract used as flashloan recipient)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The PoC replays the Chi exploit: Balancer.flashLoan is invoked with the Chi protocol contract (0x931b…) as the recipient and 25,000 WETH; the recipient's receiveFlashLoan runs its fee/swap logic with attacker-controlled inputs, letting the attacker extract value from the flash-loaned WETH (~16.3K).", "lesson": "A flashloan callback that lets the borrower pick the recipient contract is a vector — the recipient's receiveFlashLoan must authenticate the initiator and validate its inputs.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "CoW (public swap callback)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (public uniswapV3SwapCallback pays WETH)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The CoW-related contract (0xA58c…) exposes a public uniswapV3SwapCallback that pays WETH from caller-supplied deltas. The attacker calls it with a large negative amount0 and crafted data (amount, contract, WETH, attacker), receives the WETH, and unwraps it (~59K).", "lesson": "The public-callback drain pattern repeated again on a major infra contract — check every deployed callback for a pool-address guard before assuming it's internal.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "DeltaPrime (fake reward pair + crafted swap)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "access-control (claimReward trusts attacker-supplied pair)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker opens his own SmartLoan via the permissionless factory, flashloans all of Balancer's WETH and deposits it as collateral, then calls swapDebtParaSwap with crafted ParaSwap calldata (callee = WETH.withdraw) to borrow ETH, and finally claimReward() on a fake pair contract whose claim() re-enters — the loan contract never validates the pair, so the attacker converts the loan's collateral and borrowed ETH to himself (~$4.75M).", "lesson": "Reward-claim entry points that accept arbitrary pair contracts are reentrancy doors — validate the pair against a registry and lock the loan during claims.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "MFT (transfer burns pool tokens)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (sell path burns the pair's tokens)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MFT's transfer() burns tokens from the Pancake pair whenever a sell touches it. The attacker loops 5 transferFroms moving the pair's MFT balance back into the pair (each burning a slice), skims the excess, then buys MFT with flashloaned BUSD to push the pair's real balance below its reserves and swaps the difference out for BUSD at the stale ratio (~$33.7K).", "lesson": "Tokens whose sell logic burns pool holdings shrink the pair's real balance every trade — measure pair balance vs reserves after one sell and you've found the drift.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "0x MainnetSettler (crafted action bytes)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (execute() parses attacker-chosen lengths into transferFrom)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The 0x Settler's execute() accepted attacker-crafted action bytes: an order call (0x38c9c147) with attacker-chosen length/offset fields (160/100) packed ahead of a raw transferFrom(victim, attacker, 3.08e23 HOLD). The Settler executed the packed actions without validating the lengths, spending the victim's approved HOLD tokens (~$66K).", "lesson": "Calldata-parsing entry points are a fuzz target — lengths/offsets must be bounds-checked or an attacker packs a transferFrom of someone else's approved funds into the same byte string.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Matez (stake amount truncation)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "BSC", "class": "math error (2^128 stake truncates to 0 tokens, full credit recorded)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Matez's stake() truncates the amount in an integer conversion: staking 2^128 transfers 0 tokens but records the full amount as staked. The attacker stakes 2^128 with 25 self-referrals and claims MATEZ rewards for the phantom stake, repeating the cycle for more free tokens (~80K).", "lesson": "Every cast between integer widths in stake/deposit paths must reject overflow — a truncating conversion that still credits the full amount is a zero-cost stake.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "NFTG (caller-supplied reward duration)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (staking rewards computed from caller-supplied time)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The NFTG contract's staking function (0x85d07203) accepts a caller-supplied duration (here 21,250 × 1e13 × 3,600 seconds) and pays rewards computed from it. The attacker flashloans BUSD, loops 11 calls with increasing USDT transfers, collects the time-inflated rewards, and repays (~$10K).", "lesson": "Reward functions that accept the staking time as a parameter are time machines — duration must be derived from on-chain state, never calldata.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Polter Finance (spookyToken collateral) — PolterFinance_exploit.sol", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "Fantom", "class": "oracle manipulation (manipulable collateral priced by its own pools)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Polter's lending pool accepted spookyToken as collateral with a manipulable valuation. The attacker flashloans the V3 pool's entire spookyToken balance, deposits 1e18 as collateral via the V2 flash-swap path, and borrows every market's entire liquidity — WFTM, MIM, sFTMX, axlUSDC, wBTC, wETH, USDCe and wSOL — repaying the flash-swap from the proceeds (~$7M).", "lesson": "Collateral whose price is derived from the pools the attacker can swing with the same flashloan is free credit — 1 wei of collateral unlocks the whole lending pool.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "RPP (buy/sell reflection arb)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (token mechanics make each buy", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The RPP token's transfer/reflection mechanics make buy/sell round-trips net positive. The attacker flashloans 1.2M BUSD and loops 1,450 buys of ~1e23 RPP (paying from the flashloan), then sells back repeatedly until hitting the balance floor, repaying the flashloan with ~$14.1K left.", "lesson": "Reflection tokens that return more on each cycle turn a flashloan into a grind — compare the balance after one buy→sell round-trip; if it's positive, so is the loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "VRug (MEV bot callback overpayment)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (bot's uniswapV2Call overpays on flash-swap)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The PoC replays an exploit against the MEV bot contract (0x0000E0Ca…): the attacker seeds the WETH pair, calls pair.swap() to trigger the bot's uniswapV2Call, and the bot's flawed callback settles more than required, letting the attacker pocket the difference (~8.4K).", "lesson": "MEV bots with overpaying callbacks are free money when triggered deliberately — replay each bot's callback with seeded pairs and measure what it returns.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "X319 (public claimEther)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (claimEther accepts arbitrary receiver and amount)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The X319 contract's claimEther(receiver, amount) is callable by anyone with an arbitrary amount. The attacker claims 20.85 BNB to his own address in one call (~12.9K).", "lesson": "A claim function with both receiver and amount in the signature is a drain by design — the amount must be derived from the caller's verified balance.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "ERC-1967 proxy (crafted orders)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (order function accepts attacker-chosen size", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The proxy's order function (0x9b3e9b92) lets the attacker craft orders with arbitrary size fields. He places two orders — one creating and one filling with the proxy's full USDT balance as the payout — and the proxy settles them, transferring its USDT to him, which he swaps to BNB (~$8.5K).", "lesson": "Order-matching functions must verify that the filled amounts correspond to real escrowed balances — crafted size fields turn the escrow into the payout.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "vETH (factory convert mis-accounting)", "date": "2024-11", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "logic flaw (convert credits far more than the input)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The vETH factory's convert function (0x6c0472da) mis-accounts the BIF input. The attacker flashloans ~32.5K WETH from Balancer, buys BIF, and calls the factory with a 300-ETH BIF amount — the call credits him ~6.38e24 BIF, far more than supplied — which he sells back before repaying the flashloan (~447K).", "lesson": "Convert/mint functions must credit exactly the supplied amount — a fixed-parameter convert that pays a multiple of the input is a printer for anyone with flashloan capital.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "BTC24H (public lock claim + router swap)", "date": "2024-12", "chain": "Polygon", "class": "access-control (claim() on the lock is public)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The BTC24H lock contract's claim() is public, so the attacker claims its full locked BTC24H balance. He then transfers the tokens to the Uniswap UniversalRouter and executes two V3_SWAP_EXACT_IN commands (BTC24H→USDT at 1%, then a larger batch BTC24H→WBTC), receiving ~4,953 USDT and 0.76 WBTC (~$85.7K).", "lesson": "A public claim on a lock/vault contract converts the locked balance into an attacker's swap input — the claim must verify the beneficiary and the vesting schedule.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Bizness (splitLock reentrancy)", "date": "2024-12", "chain": "Base", "class": "reentrancy (ETH refund before split state update)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Bizness's splitLock() sends an ETH refund before finalizing the original lock's state. The attacker's receive() re-enters and withdraws the original lock entirely, after which the split completes and mints a new lock holding the same amount — the locked value is double-spent (~15.7K).", "lesson": "Split/merge functions that transfer ETH before committing state are reentrant against the original position — commit the split, then refund.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Clober DEX (rebalancer burn hook)", "date": "2024-12", "chain": "Base", "class": "reentrancy (user-supplied burn hook re-enters burn)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Clober's rebalancer executes user-supplied hooks during burn(). The attacker opens a book with his own fake token, mints LP with Morpho-flashloaned WETH, and burns — his burnHook re-enters burn() a second time, so the same LP is redeemed twice and the pool pays out double the WETH (~$501K).", "lesson": "Hooks executed mid-burn are reentrancy doors by design — lock the rebalancer around hook calls or drop user hooks from the burn path entirely.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "JHY (dividend LP accounting bug)", "date": "2024-12", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (swap sets attacker's LP balance to the pool total; removals never decrement)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The JHY dividend contract's bug sets the caller's recorded LP balance to the pool's LP total whenever a swap touches it. The attacker flashloans 25K BUSD, swaps to JHY to trigger the bug, adds real liquidity, then calls removeLiquidity 4× — each removal is honored against the phantom LP balance, which never decrements — and sells the extracted JHY back to BUSD (~11K).", "lesson": "LP-balance tracking that reassigns balances on unrelated actions is a repeatable-withdraw primitive — check every state write in the dividend/distribution contract for writes to user LP balances.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "LABUBU (self-transfer inflation)", "date": "2024-12", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (self-transfers multiply balance)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "LABUBU inflates its balance on self-transfers. The attacker flashloans the PancakeV3 pool's entire LABUBU, loops 30 self-transfers to multiply his stack, repays the flashloan plus fee, and sells the surplus through VOVO to WBNB (~17.4 BNB).", "lesson": "The self-transfer mint pattern kept appearing all year — assert balanceOf(to==from) conserves balance in every new token's fuzz suite.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Moonhacker (vault redeem driver)", "date": "2024-12", "chain": "Optimism", "class": "logic flaw (executeOperation REDEEM uses attacker-supplied amounts)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Moonhacker vault's executeOperation lets the caller drive a REDEEM operation with attacker-supplied amounts. The attacker Aave-flashloans USDC, funds the vault so it can repay, and calls executeOperation to repay-and-redeem the vault's full mUSDC position — each redeem returns more USDC than the repay consumed, and the 4× loop plus returnFunds() moves the vault's balance to the attacker (~$318.9K).", "lesson": "Vault operation drivers that accept amounts/params from the caller are governance-less governance — the operation type and amounts must be bound to the vault's own state.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "Pledge (public swapTokenU)", "date": "2024-12", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (swapTokenU swaps the contract's own tokens to caller-chosen target)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Pledge's swapTokenU(amount, target) is public: the attacker passes the contract's entire MFT balance as the amount and himself as the target, and the contract swaps its own holdings to him (~15K).", "lesson": "A swap function whose amount and target are both caller-supplied is a treasury drain — derive both from the caller's verified balance.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "SlurpyCoin (transfer-triggered buyback)", "date": "2024-12", "chain": "BSC", "class": "logic flaw (transfer to contract triggers BuyOrSell; 1-wei self-transfer triggers buyback)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SLURPY's transfer to the token contract triggers its BuyOrSell auto-buyback, and 1-wei self-transfers trigger swapEthForTokens. The attacker flashloans 40 WBNB and loops buy/trigger cycles — each triggered buyback lifts the price — then buys via 8 helpers and sells everything back at the pumped price, repaying the flashloan (~3K).", "lesson": "Tokens with transfer-triggered buybacks give the sender a free price lever — model the token contract's own swap activity for every transfer path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2024.md"}, {"protocol": "98Token", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The token's swap contract (0xB040D8...) exposes `swapTokensForTokens(path, amountIn, amountOutMin, to)` with no access control, and it funds the swap from its own held token balance rather than from tokens the caller sent in. The attacker simply calls it with path [98Token, USDT], amountIn = the contract's entire 98Token balance, amountOutMin 0 and himself as recipient, converting all of the contract's tokens into USDT paid to himself (~28k USDT loss).", "lesson": "When a helper/swap contract's public swap entrypoint uses the contract's own balance as the input (or trusts a caller-supplied router), it is a self-custody drain — always check who funds the swap and who receives the output.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "AST Token", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 30M BUSD from a Pancake V3 pool, swaps it all to AST with the output routed to a proxy address (pumping the AST price), then donates 1 BUSD + N AST directly to the BUSD/AST pair (bypassing the router's ratio checks) and calls the pair's `skim()`. The team's forked pair has a buggy skim that transfers the excess balance out TWICE, so the attacker recovers 2× the AST he donated while leaving only 1 wei behind; he sells the AST for BUSD and repays the flash loan (~65k USD profit).", "lesson": "Token teams that fork an AMM pair and touch skim/sync/transfer logic must be diffed against the canonical Pancake/Uniswap implementation — a skim that moves the excess twice is an instant double-withdrawal.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "HORS", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The vulnerable contract held the WBNB/HORS Cake-LP tokens and exposed a function (selector 0xf78283c7) that accepts a caller-supplied router address and invokes `addLiquidity(...)` on it without validating the address or the caller. The attacker passed his own contract as the \"router\"; when the victim contract called back into it, the fake `addLiquidity` executed `transferFrom(victim, attacker, entire LP balance)`, draining all LP tokens. The attacker then removed liquidity through the real router for WBNB and repaid a 0.1 WBNB Pancake flash loan (14.8 WBNB stolen).", "lesson": "Any function that takes a router/strategy/target address and later calls it must validate both the caller and the address — an unvalidated callee parameter is an arbitrary token-pull primitive against the contract's own balance.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "IPC Token", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The IPC token's \"price pump\" mechanic burns part of the pair's token balance on every sell. The attacker takes two DODO flash loans of USDT and loops 16 times: each iteration flash-swaps IPC out of the Pancake pair via `pair.swap(1, values[1], this, data)` (repaying USDT+1 inside `pancakeCall`, which also bypasses a timelock check with the 1-USDT sync swap), then immediately sells the whole IPC balance back to USDT through the router. Each sell's pair-burn distorts the pool, so the sell price drifts in the attacker's favor every round and he pockets the difference, leaving real buyers holding an inflated token (~590k USDT lost total).", "lesson": "Deflationary \"burn-on-sell\" mechanics mutate the pair balance outside the constant-product math — the resulting price drift can be arbitraged in a loop with flash-swaps, so audit fee-on-transfer/burn tokens for self-reinforcing buy/sell cycles.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "IdolsNFT", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Idols NFT contract credits stETH rewards to both the sender and receiver on every NFT transfer, but the claimed rewards are never subtracted from the global `allocatedStethRewards` pool, and self-transfers are allowed. The attacker deploys a contract whose constructor loops `safeTransferFrom(this, this, tokenId)` up to 2000 times — each self-transfer mints rewards to the same contract twice without losing the NFT — then forwards the accumulated stETH and the NFT back to the attacker and selfdestructs. Repeated across 15 contracts, this drained 97 stETH.", "lesson": "Reward logic keyed on transfers must settle reward debt on BOTH sides of the transfer and disallow self-transfers — a transfer event that credits rewards without decrementing a pool is an infinite-claim loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "JPulsePot", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 4,300 WBNB from a Pancake V3 pool and swaps it all into LINK, pumping the LINK price, then calls the unprotected `swapProfitFees()` on the JPulsePot contract so the pot converts its accumulated fees at the now-distorted price in the attacker's favor. He then sells all LINK back to WBNB at the pumped rate and repays the flash loan, keeping the difference (~21.5k profit).", "lesson": "Fee-conversion functions like `swapProfitFees()` must not be callable by anyone — a public \"swap the protocol's fees\" button is a ready-made pump-and-dump target for flash-loan sandwiches.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "LAURA Token", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The LAURA token exposes `removeLiquidityWhenKIncreases()` which burns liquidity from the LAURA/WETH pair on the caller's behalf whenever the pair's k has grown. The attacker flash-loans 30,000 WETH from the Balancer vault, swaps a magic amount into LAURA (pushing the pair's k up), adds liquidity through the router, then calls `removeLiquidityWhenKIncreases()` so the removal drains the pair's WETH to him; he sells the leftover LAURA, repays the Balancer loan and nets ~12.34 ETH (~$41.2K).", "lesson": "Token contracts that wrap AMM interactions (add/remove liquidity for the holder) need strict authorization and invariant checks — a public `removeLiquidity*` function lets an attacker engineer the pool state and then drain the pair.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "LPMine", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "LPMine computed rewards from the pair's raw token balance and failed to update the claim checkpoint/time correctly. The attacker DODO-flash-loans USDT, swaps half into ZF and stakes via `partakeAddLp(tokenId 2, ...)`, warps time forward 2 hours, then takes a second flash loan and loops `extractReward(1)` ~2000 times inside the callback — each call pays rewards again because the contract neither zeroes the pending reward nor advances the timestamp. He then re-syncs the pair with a 1-wei ZF transfer, skims the manipulated excess and sells ZF/WTO for USDT to repay (~24k USDT loss).", "lesson": "Staking contracts must update `lastClaimTime`/reward debt atomically inside the claim itself — rewards computed from a raw pair balance with no per-user checkpoint are claimable an unbounded number of times.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Mosca", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Mosca \"program\" contract pays out on `exitProgram()` more than what `join()` deposits, because the fiat-mode credit/payout math is broken. The attacker joins with 30 USDC, flash-loans 1,000 USDC, buys in fiat mode, exits once, then loops `join(30) → exitProgram()` 20 more times — each round returns more USDC than it took in — and repays the flash loan with a ~19k profit.", "lesson": "Join/exit-style yield \"programs\" must guarantee payout ≤ deposit + real accrued yield; if `exit` is unconditionally profitable relative to `join`, the whole contract is a drain loop.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Mosca (2nd contract)", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "A second Mosca contract has the same broken fiat accounting: `withdrawFiat()` converts internal fiat credits to USDC/BUSD at a rate far above what `join()` deposits. The attacker DODO-flash-loans 7,000 BUSD, loops `join(1,000 BUSD, fiat=1)` seven times, then calls `withdrawFiat(..., fiat=1)` and `withdrawFiat(..., fiat=0)` with huge amounts — cashing out ~44k in stablecoins for 7k deposited — and repays the BUSD loan, keeping the USDC profit (~37.6k loss).", "lesson": "Multi-currency join/withdraw functions must reconcile the deposit currency against the withdrawal currency at a fair, checkable rate — a skewed internal fiat rate turns deposit→withdraw into free money.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Odos Limit Order Router", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Odos Limit Order Router's `isValidSigImpl` honors ERC-6492-style wrapped signatures and, with `allowSideEffects=true`, executes the embedded call. The attacker crafts a \"signature\" that is really `abi.encode(USDC, transfer(attacker, fullBalance), 0x01)` plus the 0x6492... detection suffix and passes it to `isValidSigImpl(address(0x04), 0, signature, true)`. The router decodes the wrapper and performs the embedded USDC transfer from its own balance to the attacker, draining all ~50k USDC it held.", "lesson": "A signature validator that supports ERC-6492/allowSideEffects and executes arbitrary embedded calldata turns every caller into an execution proxy — any such validator holding tokens is a self-custody wallet, so treat its calldata path as untrusted code execution.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Paribus", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Paribus lets a \"PNFT delegator\" mint a claim on an NFT position that is already deposited as collateral, double-counting it. The attacker Aave-flash-loans ~3.09M USDT, swaps part to PBX on Camelot, mints a Camelot V3 NFT position (PBX/USDT), approves it to the delegator, calls `enterNFTMarkets` and then `PNFTTokenDelegator.mint(tokenId)` to collateralize the same position twice. With the inflated borrowing power he borrows pETH, pARB, pWBTC and pUSDT from the Paribus markets, swaps PBX/WBTC back to USDT, repays Aave and keeps the borrowed assets (~86k total).", "lesson": "In NFT-collateral lending, verify that a position token can only be registered/minted into the market once — delegator wrappers that mint a claim on an already-deposited tokenId double the borrowing power.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "RoulettePotV2", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 4,203 WBNB from a Pancake V3 pool and directly flash-swaps ~17.5k LINK out of the Pancake pair, then calls the public `finishRound()` and `swapProfitFees()` on RoulettePotV2 so the pot converts its profit fees at the attacker-distorted price. He returns the LINK to the pair, pulls WBNB back out, and repays the flash loan, keeping the profit (~28k).", "lesson": "Roulette/game contracts with public `finishRound()`/`swapProfitFees()` combine a state-changing trigger with an unguarded DEX swap — anyone can sandwich them with a flash loan, so these functions must be keeper-only and price-checked.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "UniLend V2", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker pledges 200 USDC to the UniLend V2 pool and moves the position NFT (id 115) to an attack contract. He flash-loans 60M USDC and then 5.7 wstETH from Morpho Blue; in the callback he unwraps to stETH, calls `lend(pool, -60M USDC)` and `lend(pool, stETH)` (mixed positive/negative lends), then `borrow()`s the pool's ENTIRE stETH balance. A miscalculated health factor after the negative-lend/redeem sequence lets this tiny position borrow all liquidity; he redeems and repays Morpho, walking away with 60 stETH.", "lesson": "Lending cores that accept signed (negative = redeem) amounts must recompute the health factor after every mixed lend/redeem step — a miscalculated health factor turns one small position into a full-pool drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "SORRA Staking — sorraStaking.sol", "date": "2025-01", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-01", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SORRA's staking contract miscomputes rewards so that repeated small withdraws each pay the full pending reward. The attacker deposits 122,868 SOR with tier 0, warps 14 days forward, then calls `withdraw(1)` 800 times in a loop — each call pays out rewards again because the checkpoint/reward state is not correctly updated — and finally sells the reclaimed SOR for ETH in 7 chunks, netting 4.8 + 2.4 + 0.8 ETH.", "lesson": "Every withdraw must zero the user's pending reward and advance their checkpoint in the same transaction — a reward calculation that survives the withdraw call makes the contract an 800× ATM.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Bybit (cold wallet)", "date": "2025-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-02", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Bybit cold wallet is a Gnosis Safe proxy whose slot 0 stores the masterCopy. The attacker socially engineered the Safe UI so the signers approved an `execTransaction` that DELEGATECALLs a \"Trojan\" contract calling `transfer(backdoor, 0)`; the Trojan's `transfer` simply writes the backdoor contract's address into slot 0, replacing the Safe implementation. Once masterCopy points to the attacker's Backdoor contract, `sweepETH`/`sweepERC20` are callable directly on the wallet address, draining 401,346 ETH plus mETH, cmETH and stETH (~$1.5B).", "lesson": "A single delegatecall executed by a multisig can overwrite the wallet's own implementation slot — signers (and the UI they sign through) must verify the actual calldata and delegatecall targets, because UI-level compromise plus one bad delegatecall converts a Safe into an attacker-controlled wallet.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Four.Meme", "date": "2025-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-02", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Four.Meme migrates a meme token's liquidity to Pancake V3 when market cap reaches 24 BNB, but it reuses an already-created pool without validating its price. The attacker pre-created the Pancake pool with `sqrtPriceX96` set ~368 trillion times the normal value; when the platform later called `addLiquidity`, its BNB liquidity landed in that distorted pool. The attacker, holding 1,603 tokens bought for 0.0001 BNB during the platform-only phase, then swapped them into 23.426 WBNB at the corrupted price (~186k total; ~20 meme tokens hit the same way).", "lesson": "Launchpad liquidity migration must create and initialize the pool itself (or verify the existing pool's sqrtPrice) — if it trusts a pre-existing pool, an attacker pre-creates one at an absurd price and converts dust into the entire migrated liquidity.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Hegic", "date": "2025-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-02", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deposited a trivial 0.0025 WBTC into the Hegic WBTC ATM puts pool, then called `withdrawWithoutHedge(2)` in a loop — 100 times in one tx and 331 times in another. Each call pays out on stale per-user state because the withdrawal path never updates the user's deposited/hedged balance, so the same tiny position is repeatedly cashed out, draining the pool (~104M value per header).", "lesson": "Option/insurance-pool withdrawals must decrement the user's deposited balance atomically — a `withdrawWithoutHedge` that reads pre-withdrawal state is an infinite-withdraw ATM for anyone with one dust deposit.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Peapods Finance", "date": "2025-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-02", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swaps 9.42e21 pOHM out of the Uniswap V2 pair, converts pOHM→PEAS through the V3 router, and calls `TokenRewards.depositFromPairedLpToken(0, 999)` with a slippage override that disables sanity checks — the rewards contract credits the deposit without verifying the token or the amount actually transferred. He then swaps PEAS back to pOHM, repays the pair, and keeps the pOHM profit (~$3,500).", "lesson": "Reward contracts that accept \"paired LP token\" deposits must verify the token identity and the real transferred amount — an override/slippage flag plus a missing check turns a flash-swap into free farming rewards.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Step Hero NFTs", "date": "2025-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-02", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 1,000 WBNB and unwraps it, calls the undocumented listing function (selector 0xded4de3a) on the StepHero contract to register his own asset/order with himself as beneficiary, then has a second contract buy his own asset via `buyAsset{value: 1000 ether}(81122, 1, ...)` — the 1,000 BNB round-trips back to him. The contract's 3 BNB refund to his contract re-triggers `claimReferral(address(0))` through the `receive()` hook, so every refund claims referral rewards again, draining the referral pool until the flash loan is repaid with 137.9 BNB profit left over.", "lesson": "NFT-game contracts that send BNB refunds to user contracts and expose a claim function are reentrancy candidates — a `receive()` that re-enters `claimReferral` turns each refund into another reward claim, so audit every value transfer into a caller-controlled address.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Venus (zkSync)", "date": "2025-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-02", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker Aave-flash-loans 2,100 WETH and mints vWETH as collateral, then repeatedly borrows wUSDM from Venus and re-lends it through a helper contract that mints vUSDM. He then donates the underlying USDM straight into the wUSDM vault, inflating the vault's assets and the wUSDM/vUSDM exchange rate. With the distorted rate he self-liquidates his own vUSDM position 34 times — each liquidation repays wUSDM and seizes vWETH collateral valued at the inflated exchange rate — redeems the seized WETH each round, and finally repays the Aave loan with ~86.7 WETH profit.", "lesson": "Vault/wrapper shares must never be priced from a raw token balance anyone can inflate with a direct donation — donation attacks distort exchange rates and turn self-liquidation into a collateral-extraction loop in lending markets.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified 0xDE91… (slot game)", "date": "2025-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-02", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker sends 0.6 BNB into the victim's `unlockSlot(3)`, then triggers the unknown `0x2dad6442(3)`; the contract's payout path returns ~3e15×3600 wei (~10.8 BNB) to the caller — far more than the deposit — and a fallback hook re-triggers the same selector on any incoming refund to compound the drain (6,700 USD total loss).", "lesson": "For unverified BSC game/staking contracts, trace every value transfer on each state-changing call — \"unlock/slot\" mechanics with a fixed payout formula can pay a multiplier to the wrong side of the ledger.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified 0xD4F1…", "date": "2025-02", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-02", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim contract's `initialize()` has no initializer guard and no access control, and it was never called by the team. The attacker simply calls `initialize()` to become the contract's owner, then calls `withdrawFees(address(0), 23.007 BNB)` to send the accumulated fees to himself (15.2k USD).", "lesson": "Always check `initialize()` for initializer modifiers and ownership checks — an unprotected, un-called initializer is a free ownership takeover followed by `withdrawFees`.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Alkimiya (Silica Pools)", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker Morpho-flash-loans 10 WBTC and deposits only ~0.56 WBTC into the Silica pool, calling `collateralizedMint` with an `index` field pointing at his own contract (a fake index token whose `shares()`, `balance()` and `transferFrom` return attacker-chosen values) and a `shares` argument of `type(uint128).max + 2` that triggers an unsafe `uint128(shares)` cast. The truncated/overflowed share accounting lets the minted position be redeemed for far more than was deposited; after transferring the position, `startPool`, `endPool` and `redeemShort` pay out ~1.14 WBTC (~95.5k) for the dust deposit.", "lesson": "Derivative mints must validate the index/payout token identity and every numeric cast width — an unsafe `uint128` cast combined with a caller-supplied fake index turns a dust deposit into an oversized redemption.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "BBX Token", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "BBX burns part of the liquidity pool's token supply on a timer (`lastBurnTime` + `lastBurnGapTime`, burnRate 300) to pump its price. The attacker buys BBX with 0.05 BNB, then loops 500 zero-value self-transfers — each transfer advances the token's burn clock so the pair-burn fires at the moment of his choosing — and finally sells the whole BBX balance for BUSD, netting 11,902 BUSD.", "lesson": "Time-gated burn mechanics that key off transfer activity can be driven by zero-value self-transfers — an attacker who can schedule the pair-burn schedules his own exit pump, so audit whether burn timing depends on anything the attacker can nudge for free.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "DCF Token", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "DCF's `transfer` has a hardcoded special case: when `to` is the Pancake BUSD-DCF pair, it destroys about half of the transferred DCF from the PAIR's balance, inflating the DCF and DCT prices. The attacker recursively flash-loans BUSD from 9 Pancake V3 pools and recursively swaps across 6 V2 pairs, buys 80.4M BUSD of DCF and 29.9M BUSD of DCT, then transfers 82.7 DCF to the pair to fire the burn, and sells everything back at the pumped price — 442,028 BUSD profit.", "lesson": "Grep token `_transfer` for address comparisons against the LP pair — any mint/burn side effect targeting the pair's balance is a self-pump backdoor an attacker can trigger and dump against.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "DUCKVADER", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`DUCKVADER.buyTokens(0)` credits tokens without requiring any payment. The attacker deploys 200 tiny helper contracts that each call `buyTokens(0)` and transfer the freshly minted tokens to the attack contract, then sells the accumulated balance for WETH via the Uniswap router (~5 ETH profit).", "lesson": "Token \"buy\" entrypoints must require an actual payment and enforce the minted amount against it — a `buyTokens(0)` that still mints is a faucet; fuzz buy/mint functions with zero and dust amounts.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "H2O Token", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "H2O's transfer branches on a pseudo-random value derived from `block.timestamp`, the pair address and the previous blockhash, taxing or burning differently depending on the outcome. The attacker flash-loans 100k BUSD and buys H2O, then loops 50× `transfer(pair, all H2O)` + `pair.skim(this)` to siphon the pair's H2O while the favorable branch of the random check holds (he warps/retries until it fires, which is why two attack txs reverted before the profitable one), and finally sells everything back for BUSD (~22.5k profit).", "lesson": "", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Leverage SIR (vault)", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SIR vault's `uniswapV3SwapCallback` authenticates the caller against a whitelist kept in transient storage slot 1, which is written during `mint` from caller-supplied VaultParameters. The attacker deploys two fake ERC20s, creates a Uniswap V3 pool for them, then calls `vault.initialize`/`vault.mint` with his fake token params so the vault's own mint writes attacker-chosen data into slot 1. He then uses the ImmutableCreate2Factory to deploy a contract whose ADDRESS equals the value now in slot 1, so that contract passes the callback auth check and drains all USDC, WBTC and WETH from the vault (~353.8k).", "lesson": "Callback authentication must be bound to a trusted, immutable pool address — whitelists stored in transient/slot state that a mint path can write are forgeable, and CREATE2 vanity addressing turns any attacker-writable auth slot into a full bypass.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "1inch Fusion v1 Settlement — OneInchFusionV1SettlementHack.sol_exp.sol", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Settlement contract's `_settleOrder` appends a resolver/fee suffix to copied calldata using unchecked Yul arithmetic (`interactionLength + suffixLength` and the offset computation). The attacker crafts nested orders whose interaction length/offset fields are near-2^256 values; the additions overflow so the suffix is written early — into the middle of the copied payload — and the interaction length is zeroed, rewriting the final fill order's maker/taker amounts and resolver. The corrupted order is then \"filled\" against the TrustedVolumes victim: taking 1M USDC while paying only ~1 USDT (4.5M total stolen).", "lesson": "Assembly-level calldata/memory copying must check every length and offset addition for overflow — with crafted length fields an attacker rewrites the settlement payload mid-execution and fills orders at self-chosen prices.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Pump-style tokens (TAGAIFUN/GROK/PEPE/TEST)", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "These tokens' `buyToken(expectAmount, sellsman, slippage, receiver)` lets the buyer route freshly bought tokens to an arbitrary receiver, including the LP pair. The attacker flash-loans 100 WBNB and, for each of four tokens: buys 0.001 BNB worth directly INTO the pair (inflating its token balance beyond reserves), deposits 1 WBNB directly and calls `pair.mint(this)` to mint LP from the unbalanced surplus, then buys 20 BNB worth for himself and sells all tokens back for WBNB via the router — netting ~11.29 BNB across the four tokens.", "lesson": "Token buy functions with a caller-chosen receiver enable donation attacks — when tokens can be minted straight into the pair and the pair's `mint` credits LP from unbalanced balances, an attacker mints LP for free; restrict buy receivers and verify reserve accounting.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "SBR Token", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker swaps a dust 4e-15 ETH into SBR (receiving ~54.8e9 wei), calls `pair.skim(pair)` to re-baseline, transfers 1 wei of SBR directly to the pair — triggering the token's transfer-to-pair side effect that destroys part of the pair's SBR balance — then `sync()`s and sells his remaining SBR at the resulting skewed price for ~8.5 ETH.", "lesson": "Same hunt as DCF: a 1-wei transfer to the pair followed by sync and a sell is the signature of a pair-burn token — diff `_transfer` for pair-address special cases that mutate the pair's balance and make the pool self-pumping.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "SamPrisonman Token", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Mirror of the SBR pattern: the attacker calls an undocumented token function (selector 0x4f49cd31), buys a dust amount of SamPrisonman with 4e-15 ETH, runs `skim` → `transfer(pair, 1 wei)` → `sync` to fire the token's burn-on-transfer-to-pair behavior, then sells his whole balance for ETH — extracting ~14k USD from the distorted pool.", "lesson": "The skim/1-wei-transfer/sync/sell sequence is a fingerprint worth automating in a detector — it flags tokens whose transfer mutates the pair's balance, the single most common self-pump backdoor in 2025 token hacks.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "YziAI Token", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "YziAI's `transferFrom` contains a planted backdoor: when `msg.sender == manager` and `amount == 1199002345`, it mints `supply * 10000` to itself, self-approves the router, and swaps `balanceOf(to) * 1000` tokens for ETH to the manager. The PoC simply calls `transferFrom(CAKE_LP, CAKE_LP, 1199002345)` as the manager and pockets ~376 BNB from the mint-and-dump.", "lesson": "Magic-number comparisons in `transferFrom`/`_transfer` that branch into mint-and-sell code are rugpull backdoors — grep for hardcoded amount constants next to `_mint` calls and for manager/owner branches in token transfer paths.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "wKeyDAO", "date": "2025-03", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-03", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The wKeyDAO \"sell\" contract's `buy()` prices wKeyDAO below the Pancake market rate. The attacker DODO-flash-loans 1,200 BUSD and loops 5× (67× was possible): call `buy()` to acquire wKeyDAO cheaply, then dump it on Pancake for BUSD, repaying the loan with a profit (~767 USD).", "lesson": "Fixed-price sale contracts that don't reference a live oracle are standing arbitrage — if `buy()` price < DEX price, a buy→dump loop drains it, so check for any public purchase function whose rate is decoupled from the market.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "AIRWA Token", "date": "2025-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-04", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The AIRWA token exposes `setBurnRate(uint256)` with no ownership check, and its transfer burns part of the LP pair's token balance when the recipient is the pair. The attacker buys AIRWA with 0.1 BNB, calls `setBurnRate(980)`, fires a zero-value `transfer(CAKE_LP, 0)` to burn ~98% of the pair's AIRWA balance, resets `setBurnRate(0)`, and sells his remaining tokens at the inflated price — 56.73 BNB out.", "lesson": "Burn/tax parameters must be owner-gated: a public `setBurnRate` plus a pair-targeted burn lets anyone set a 98% burn, fire it once on the pool, reset it, and dump at the pumped price.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "BTNFT", "date": "2025-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-04", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The BTNFT contract's `transferFrom` does not enforce token approvals, so the attacker sweeps every NFT (ids 1..totalSupply) from their owners to the BTNFT contract itself (tx1). Holding the full NFT set entitles the holder to BTT rewards, which the attacker claims and then sells in 50 chunks through the project router for 19,025 BUSD (tx2).", "lesson": "ERC721 `transferFrom` must check owner/approval on every path — an approval-less transferFrom lets an attacker sweep the whole collection and capture the staking/reward stream attached to it.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Impermax V3", "date": "2025-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-04", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker Morpho-flash-loans WETH and USDC, and opens a Uniswap V3 position through the TokenizedUniswapV3Position manager while pushing the pool price with swaps capped at an absurd `sqrtPriceLimitX96` — distorting the position's reported value. He collateralizes the position in Impermax, mints, then borrows the Borrowable contract's ENTIRE WETH balance against it (borrow(255, ...)), runs `reinvest` and `restructureBadDebt(255)` to reclassify his own borrow, redeems the position, and walks away with ~300k.", "lesson": "Lending against NFT LP positions must value them with an independent, manipulation-resistant oracle and a safety margin — one pool-price swing from a single swap let the attacker borrow the borrowable's whole reserve.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Laundromat (mixer)", "date": "2025-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-04", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker deploys his contract at a precomputed address (0x2E95…) and re-submits the SAME deposit commitment four times, then drives the withdrawal flow himself: `withdrawStart` with five crafted signature scalars, five `withdrawStep()` calls, and `withdrawFinal()`, self-destructing the received ETH to the attacker (~$1.5K). The flow shows the mixer's withdraw verification can be walked through with replayed/crafted inputs rather than a fresh secret-note proof.", "lesson": "Mixer deposits must bind one commitment to one spendable note via a global nullifier set — repeated identical deposits plus a full withdraw cycle are the fingerprint of a commitment-reuse double-spend.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Life Protocol", "date": "2025-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-04", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Life Protocol contract's internal `buy()` and `sell()` are not inverse-consistent: buying then immediately selling the same amount of life tokens returns more BUSD than the buy cost. The attacker DODO-flash-loans 110k BUSD and loops 53× `buy(1000e18)` then 53× `sell(1000e18)`, harvesting the negative spread each cycle and repaying the loan with 15,114 BUSD left.", "lesson": "Any contract exposing paired buy/sell functions must be checked for spread symmetry — if buy(1k) followed by sell(1k) nets a profit, the negative spread is a free-money loop for flash-loaners.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "R0AR Token", "date": "2025-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-04", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The R0AR token's emergency-withdraw/vesting math (constants T0, BIGC, DEN, K, OFF) reduces to an identity: once `block.timestamp >= T0`, the condition `((timestamp*rate*K - OFF*rate)/(rate*K)) == timestamp - T0` always holds, so the lock is bypassable on arrival. The attacker calls the withdraw path, moving 100M R0AR tokens and ~26.77 LP tokens to himself (~777k USD).", "lesson": "Vesting and emergency-withdraw conditions must be real constraints, not arithmetic identities — simplify the unlock math by hand; if it cancels out to `timestamp >= T0`, the whole reserve is one call away.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified 0x6077… (Base)", "date": "2025-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-04", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim contract exposes `uniswapV3SwapCallback` publicly and, when called, transfers tokens to the recipient encoded in the caller-supplied data without checking that msg.sender is a real Uniswap pool. The attacker calls it twice — once with a delta encoding 22.51 WETH and once with 27.26M USDC — and the contract pays both amounts to the attacker address from its own balance (~$62.3K).", "lesson": "`uniswapV3SwapCallback` must hard-require `msg.sender == knownPool` and decode the payer from trusted data — a callback that pays whoever is named in the data is a public withdrawal button for the contract's whole balance.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "YB Token", "date": "2025-04", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-04", "loss": "", "mechanics": "YB charges a 0.25% fee-on-transfer, so direct transfers into the pair leave `balance > reserve`. The attacker flash-loans 19,200 BUSD and loops 66×: push BUSD straight into the pair, then `pair.swap` out YB using a custom `getAmount0ToReachK` that exploits the balance/reserve gap to pull slightly more YB than k-accounting allows (received via child contracts). He then reverses the cycle — pushing YB in and pulling BUSD with `getAmount1ToReachK` — and repays the flash loan with 15,261 BUSD profit.", "lesson": "Fee-on-transfer tokens break the pair invariant `balance == reserve + fee` — attackers can arithmetically extract the gap on every cycle, so audit any direct-transfer + direct-pair.swap combination against token fee behavior.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Cork Protocol", "date": "2025-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-05", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Cork's configurable module system lets a user initialize a module with a caller-chosen module contract, and the protocol then prices ct/ds minting and redemption using that module's `rate()`. The attacker passes his own contract as the module — one that answers `rate()` with 0 — deposits WstETH via `depositPsm`, issues a new ds, and in the Uniswap v4-style `unlockCallback` drives `CorkHook.beforeSwap` and `PoolManager.settleFor` with crafted SwapParams so his swaps settle at the attacker-controlled rate; he then reclaims the Ra (WstETH) with `returnRaWithCtDs`, draining ~12M USD.", "lesson": "Module/rate-provider addresses in configurable protocols must be whitelisted — if a user can supply the \"module\" that answers the exchange-rate query, the protocol's pricing is whatever the attacker returns, and redemption becomes free money.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "IRYSAI Token", "date": "2025-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-05", "loss": "", "mechanics": "IRYSAI's `setTaxWallet` is callable without restriction; the attacker points the tax wallet at his own contract, which then executes `transferFrom(pair, taxWallet, 99.99% of the pair's IRYSAI)` — pulling almost the entire pair balance because the tax wallet is fee-exempt — syncs the pair and swaps the tokens for BNB (69.6K USD), the classic backdoor/rugpull pattern.", "lesson": "Grep token contracts for `setTaxWallet`/fee-recipient setters without ownership checks and check whether that wallet can `transferFrom` the LP pair — a wallet that can pull the pair and a public setter is a pre-planted rugpull.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "KRC Token", "date": "2025-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-05", "loss": "", "mechanics": "KRC's transfer has a side effect that credits the LP pair with more tokens than were sent. The attacker flash-loans ~248k USDT (DODO) and 100k USDT (Pancake V3), buys KRC with two large swaps, then runs 17 cycles of `transfer(pair, X)` + `pair.skim(this)` — each cycle the pair's balance overshoots the cached reserves, and skim sweeps the surplus to the attacker, desynchronizing balances from reserves while accumulating KRC. One final direct `pair.swap` converts the accumulated KRC into 355k USDT; he repays both loans with ~7k profit.", "lesson": "The transfer+skim loop is the universal extractor for tokens whose transfer mutates the pair's balance — diff `_transfer` for pair-address branches and, if found, expect exactly this 17-cycle desync pattern.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "MBU Token", "date": "2025-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-05", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The MBU proxy's `deposit()` credits 30,000,000 MBU for a 0.001 WBNB deposit — the minted amount is not derived from any sane price/rate. The attacker deposits 0.001 WBNB, receives 30M MBU, and swaps them for ~2.16M BUSD on Pancake in one tx.", "lesson": "Fuzz deposit/mint ratios with dust inputs — any deposit function that returns a fixed or rate-decoupled token amount for dust collateral is a faucet the protocol itself is funding.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Nalakuvara LotteryTicket50", "date": "2025-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-05", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 2.93M USDC and pumps the Nalakuvara token price by donating 2.8M USDC into the V2 pair and pulling Nalakuvara out, then calls `LotteryTicketSwap50.transferToken(130M)` to convert tokens into lottery tickets cheaply, restores the pair, and loops 130× `DestructionOfLotteryTickets(20M)` — each destruction refunds USDC worth more than the tickets cost at the manipulated rate — repaying the loan with 105,470 USDC profit.", "lesson": "Ticket/rebate systems whose destruction refunds a token amount priced from a manipulable pool are loop-drainable — if refund(ticket) > cost(ticket) under any pool state, a flash-loaner repeats it until the pot is empty.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "RICE (BentoBox-like vault)", "date": "2025-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-05", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The vault-like contract's `registerProtocol()` is public, and `setMasterContractApproval(user, masterContract, true, v=0, r=0, s=0)` accepts a zeroed EIP-712 signature. The attacker registers himself as a protocol, \"approves\" his master contract for a real user's account with the empty signature, then calls `withdraw(RICE, from=victim, to=attacker, balance, balance)` to pull the victim's entire RICE deposit, swapping it to USDT→WETH for ~34.5 WETH.", "lesson": "Master-contract approval flows must reject zero signatures and gate protocol registration — a v/r/s=0 signature check that passes turns every user's vault balance into an attacker-withdrawable balance.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Unwarp", "date": "2025-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-05", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Unwarp contract's `unwrapWETH(amount, to)` is callable by anyone and unwraps the contract's own WETH to an arbitrary recipient. The attacker Balancer-flash-loans ~100.85 WETH, sends it to the contract, then calls `unwrapWETH(104.83e18, attacker)` — pulling out ~4 WETH more than he sent, straight from the contract's pre-existing balance — re-wraps and repays the loan (~9k USDT profit).", "lesson": "Helper functions that unwrap/withdraw a contract's own token balance must check the caller and cap the amount — a public `unwrapWETH(amount, to)` with a free amount parameter is a direct drain of whatever the contract holds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Usual Money (USD0++)", "date": "2025-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-05", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker creates a brand-new Uniswap V3 USD0/sUSDS pool at an extreme `sqrtPriceX96` of his choosing and mints a tiny position there, then Morpho-flash-loans USD0Plus and deposits it through the VaultRouter, whose ParaSwap-encoded swap path routes through the manipulated pool — so the vault's share/sync pricing is computed at the attacker-set price. He then unwinds: decrease/collect the V3 position, arb the Curve USD0↔USD0Plus pool in both directions to pocket the mispricing, and exits to WETH (~43k profit).", "lesson": "Sync/deposit vaults that price shares through arbitrary swap paths are vulnerable to self-created pools — an attacker who can choose the pool a vault's pricing swap routes through sets the price, so vaults must price from robust oracles, not swap-path spot prices.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "YDT Token", "date": "2025-05", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-05", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The YDT token exposes selector 0xec22f4c7 with a (from, to, amount, module) signature; the attacker calls it with (pair, attacker, pair's entire YDT balance minus 1000e6, taxModule) — moving the pair's whole YDT balance to himself — then calls the pair's 0xfff6cae9 (sync) and sells a tenth of the haul for USDT.", "lesson": "Unverified tokens deserve a selector-level access audit — any four-arg (from, to, amount, module) function callable by outsiders that can move the pair's balance is a standing drain, and a following sync() call confirms the attacker is trading the stolen supply.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "AAVE Boost", "date": "2025-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-06", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The AaveBoost contract's `proxyDeposit(asset, user, amount)` accrues boost rewards on every call without checking that anything was actually deposited. The attacker loops `proxyDeposit(AAVE, self, uint128(0))` up to 163 times with a zero amount, accumulating rewards as if he had deposited each round, then withdraws from the Aave pool and transfers the inflated AAVE balance out (~14.8k USD).", "lesson": "Deposit/boost entrypoints must treat zero-amount calls as no-ops — a per-call accrual that ignores `amount == 0` is a free-reward loop, so fuzz reward functions with zero-value deposits.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Bankroll Network", "date": "2025-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-06", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The dividend contract credits a buyer with rewards drawn from the donation pool, and donations are not excluded from the donor's own dividend basis. The attacker flash-swaps 2,000 WBNB, calls `donatePool(1000 WBNB)`, then `buy(240)` (crediting himself dividends from the pool he just fed), `sell(myTokens())`, tops up ~94 WBNB and calls `withdraw()` — extracting the pot plus his own donation, and repaying the pair (24.5 WBNB profit).", "lesson": "Dividend pools must exclude the donor from the reward basis of their own donation — donate→buy→sell→withdraw is a circular claim that returns the donation as dividends plus the pool's existing pot.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Bankroll Stack", "date": "2025-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-06", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker Pancake-V3-flash-loans 28,300 BUSD, calls `buy()` with the whole amount — which credits him dividends from the existing pot — then immediately `sell(myTokens())` and `withdraw()`. Because dividends are credited on buy and not clawed back on sell, the buy→sell→withdraw sequence drains the dividend pot for a ~5k profit.", "lesson": "Reward credits must be tied to actual holding time — if buying credits dividends and selling doesn't net them out, buy/sell/withdraw in one block is a pot-draining loop; trace the dividend state through every exit path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Gangster Finance", "date": "2025-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-06", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The vault's `donate()` feeds the reward pool without excluding the donor. The attacker flash-swaps 1.02 BTCB, donates 1 BTCB to the vault, then `depositTo` a small amount, `resolve(myTokens())` and `harvest()` — harvesting his own donation back plus the accrued pot — and repays the flash swap with a 16.5k profit.", "lesson": "Same family as Bankroll: `donate → deposit → resolve → harvest` must net the donation out of the donor's rewards — any vault where your own donation inflates your own harvest is a flash-loan target.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Gradient Market Maker Pool", "date": "2025-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-06", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Gradient pool prices a token deposit at the token's current swap rate. The attacker Morpho-flash-loans 3 WETH, buys 1,000 GRAY for ≤1 WETH to pump the GRAY price, then `provideLiquidity{0.632 ETH}(gray, 950e18, 0)` — the pool values his GRAY at the pumped rate and mints him shares worth far more ETH — and immediately `withdrawLiquidity(gray, 10000 shares)`, taking out more ETH than he put in (~5k profit).", "lesson": "Liquidity pools that value token deposits at a manipulable DEX price are pump-deposit-withdraw targets — deposit shares must be priced from a manipulation-resistant oracle or the pool's own invariant, not the spot price of the token being deposited.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "MetaPool", "date": "2025-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-06", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MetaPool's `depositETH()` mints mpETH shares for the receiver, but the public `mint(shares, receiver)` is not offset against the deposit — the attacker deposits 107 ETH (getting ~97 mpETH), then calls `mint(amount, self)` again with the same amount, double-crediting his mpETH (~194 mpETH for 107 ETH). He sells the mpETH back for ETH through the mpETH/ETH pool and Uniswap, repays the 200 WETH Balancer flash loan, and keeps ~9 ETH plus leftover mpETH (the full protocol exploit reached ~27M).", "lesson": "When `deposit` and `mint` are both public and deposit doesn't decrement the mintable amount, the same deposit can be minted twice — check that share minting is internal or that deposit paths consume the share entitlement.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Resupply Finance", "date": "2025-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-06", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The sCRVUSD (Savings crvUSD) exchange rate is fed by the crvUSD controller, and the Resupply vault prices sCRVUSD collateral from it. The attacker flash-loans 4,000 USDC, swaps to crvUSD, DONATES 2,000 crvUSD straight to the controller and mints 1 wei of sCRVUSD — inflating the sCRVUSD rate — then deposits that 1 wei as collateral and borrows 10,000,000 reUSD. He swaps the reUSD to crvUSD, redeems ~9,339 sCRVUSD, and swaps back to USDC, repaying the loan with ~9.6M profit.", "lesson": "Collateral priced from a rate that a donation can move is one donation away from insolvency — 2,000 crvUSD to the controller turned 1 wei of sCRVUSD into backing for a 10M borrow; price collateral only from donation-proof oracles.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Stead", "date": "2025-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-06", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim contract 0xf9ff… exposes selector 0x16fb27ce with no access control; calling it with (1,1,1) transfers the contract's entire STEAD balance to the caller (~14.5k USD), a leftover debug/owner function pattern.", "lesson": "Enumerate every selector on unverified contracts and try minimal arguments — an unprotected function that drains the balance when called with (1,1,1) is a common leftover-test-function bug, and it costs nothing to check each one.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified 0x8490… helper", "date": "2025-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-06", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker Pancake-V3-flash-loans 9,350 units of the proxy token, swaps them for the project Token through the SmartRouter, then invokes selector 0x5ff02eae on the token's helper contract (0x8490…) — a state trigger that distorts the token's price/rebate — and swaps the Token back to the proxy token at the improved rate, repaying the loan with ~48.3k profit.", "lesson": "Token \"helper\" contracts with no-arg callable functions are price-altering buttons — a swap → trigger → swap-back sandwich around such a function means the trigger moves the price in the caller's favor; audit what each no-arg selector does to token state.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified 0xB5CB… (printMoney)", "date": "2025-06", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-06", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim contract's 0x94655f2b takes crafted calldata encoding a token, an amount, a swap route, and a recipient, and pays out that amount of the named token to the named recipient with no authorization check. The attacker calls it 7 times — once per asset (WBNB, ETH, USDT, BTCB, USDC, and two proxy tokens) — moving ~2M USD total to himself; the PoC comment calls it \"the printMoney\".", "lesson": "Payout/route functions on unverified contracts must check msg.sender — a multi-asset \"pay token X to address Y\" function callable by anyone is a faucet; when calldata embeds both the asset and the recipient, treat it as a standing drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "FPC Token", "date": "2025-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "FPC burns part of the LP pair's token balance whenever FPC is transferred to the pool, inflating the price for anyone holding FPC. The attacker Pancake-V3-flash-loans 23.02M USDT, buys FPC via a direct pair swap (repaying USDT in `pancakeCall`), moves 247,441 FPC to a helper contract, and sells it back through the router at the pair-burned, inflated price — 4.7M USDT profit before repaying the loan.", "lesson": "The PoC comment says it directly: \"FPC burns tokens on transfers to the pool — attacker can sell at an inflated price\" — any token whose `_transfer` mutates the pair's balance when `to == pair` is a self-pump the holder can trigger at will.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Multicall victim", "date": "2025-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim contract exposes a multicall-style function (0xc9586258) taking an array of {target, callData, value, allowFailure} and executes each call with msg.sender = victim — with no permission check. A user had approved the victim contract for their USDC, so the attacker submits a single Call targeting USDC with `transferFrom(approvedUser, attacker, fullBalance)`; the multicall executes it as the victim, spending the standing approval (~10k USDT).", "lesson": "Any multicall/execute entrypoint runs with the contract's own msg.sender — if the contract holds users' token approvals, a public multicall is a standing allowance-drain; audit who can call it and which approvals the contract holds.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "RANT Token", "date": "2025-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 2,813 BUSD, pulls 96,605 RANT out of the Cake pair via a direct swap (repaying in `pancakeCall`), then transfers 10,733 RANT to the RANT token contract itself — the token treats receiving its own tokens as a burn request and pays out BNB to the sender. He wraps the payout, repays the flash loan, and walks away with ~311 BNB.", "lesson": "Tokens with a burn-to-redeem path triggered by receiving their own tokens create a self-serve payout — trace what a token does when `to == address(token)`; if it pays out value, a flash-loaner can fund the burn and pocket the redemption.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "SWAPP Staking", "date": "2025-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SWAPP's staking deposit credits the user's balance without actually transferring the underlying cUSDC in. The attacker reads the staking contract's own cUSDC balance and calls `deposit(cUsdc, thatBalance, 0)` — crediting himself the contract's entire holdings without paying — then `emergencyWithdraw(cUsdc)` pays the credited amount out, draining the contract (~32.2k USD).", "lesson": "`deposit` must `transferFrom` the funds BEFORE crediting the user — a deposit path that credits the caller's balance without pulling tokens, combined with emergencyWithdraw, lets anyone withdraw the contract's own holdings.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Stepp2p", "date": "2025-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The P2P marketplace refunds the sale amount on both `cancelSaleOrder` and `modifySaleOrder` without invalidating the order between calls. The attacker flash-loans 50k BUSD, funds a `createSaleOrder(amount)`, then calls `cancelSaleOrder(saleId)` and `modifySaleOrder(saleId, amount, false)` on the SAME order — each call refunds the full amount, so he receives 2× what he put in, and repays the loan with ~43k profit.", "lesson": "Order state machines must mark an order dead on the first refund — cancel+modify (or any two lifecycle calls) both paying out is a double-spend; test every state-transition pair on marketplace orders for dual refund paths.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "SuperRare", "date": "2025-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The RARE token claim/staking proxy exposes `updateMerkleRoot(bytes32)` with no access control. The attacker installs his OWN Merkle root, then calls `claim(entireBalance, emptyProof)` — his claim validates against the attacker-chosen root — and drains all 11.9e24 wei of RARE held by the staking contract (~730k USD).", "lesson": "Merkle-root setters are ownership functions — a public `updateMerkleRoot` lets the attacker install his own root and claim the whole pot with an empty proof; every airdrop/claim contract must gate the root-setter.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "VDS Token", "date": "2025-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The VDS token's deposit gives VDS for AVD at one rate, while sending VDS back to the token contract (burn) returns AVD at a 1:1 ratio. Because the two rates are not consistent, the attacker Moolah-flash-loans 20k BUSD, swaps to AVD, deposits for VDS, then transfers VDS to the token contract to redeem MORE AVD than he deposited, and sells the AVD back for BUSD (~13k profit).", "lesson": "Deposit and burn-redeem legs of a token must be inverse-consistent — if deposit AVD→VDS and burn VDS→AVD use different ratios, the loop is profitable for whoever borrows the entry capital.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "WETC Token", "date": "2025-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans 1M BUSD and manipulates the BUSD/WETC pair: a small direct swap, then large direct WETC transfers into the pair followed by `skim(otherAddress)` and `sync()` — twice — desynchronizing the cached reserves from real balances and distorting the price. A final direct `pair.swap` extracts 351,495 BUSD at the corrupted rate, and the flash loan is repaid with ~101k profit.", "lesson": "transfer → skim → sync → swap is the canonical reserve-manipulation fingerprint on V2 pairs — pools whose prices feed anything must never be readable as raw balances an attacker can desync in one block.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "GMX (V1, Arbitrum)", "date": "2025-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker opens two leveraged ETH longs through the order book and closes them via the keeper, and inside the position-close callback (`gmxPositionCallback`/fallback) he opens a huge BTC SHORT and immediately routes its decrease through the position router — the open/close churn moves GMX's `globalShortAveragePrice`. With that accounting skewed he mints and stakes GLP, then loops `mintAndStakeGlp(FRAX)` + `increasePosition` + `decreasePosition` and redeems each vault asset with `unstakeAndRedeemGlp` sized to `poolAmounts − reservedAmounts` at `getMaxPrice` — harvesting the phantom surplus from WETH, WBTC, USDC, USDE, LINK, UNI, USDT, FRAX and DAI.", "lesson": "Perp protocols must not let positions opened and closed inside keeper/callback flows skew global average-price accounting — a churned short moved GMX's short-average price, and the GLP redeem loop then converted that skew into real withdrawals across every pool asset.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified 0x54cd proxy", "date": "2025-07", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-07", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker first calls the proxy's unguarded function `0x03b79c24(address(this))` to register his contract in the proxy's payment path, then executes a Uniswap V3 `swap` with an absurd `sqrtPriceLimitX96` (1461446703485210103287273052203988822378723970341) so the weETH→WETH swap pays out ~114.53 WETH funded by the proxy's balance; his callback only supplies the weETH. He unwraps the WETH and forwards the ETH to himself (~285.7k USD).", "lesson": "Unverified proxies with address-arg hook functions can wire the caller into the protocol's payout path — one registration call plus a swap at an extreme price limit turned the proxy's WETH into the attacker's balance.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Unverified 0x8d2E… (Base)", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim's `uniswapV3SwapCallback` is callable by anyone and transfers the amount named in `amount0Delta` to whatever address is encoded in the data. The attacker simply calls `uniswapV3SwapCallback(entireBalance, 0, abi.encode(USDC, attacker))` and the contract sends its whole USDC balance to him (~40k USDC).", "lesson": "Swap callbacks must hard-require `msg.sender == knownPool` and derive the payer from trusted state — a callback that pays the address encoded in caller data is a public withdrawal button (same pattern as 0x6077 in April).", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Victim 0xf340 (VRF)", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim's `initVRF(attacker, LINK)` is public and stores the attacker's contract as the fee/fulfillment recipient. The attacker then calls the payout function 0x607d60e6(0) 80 times, each call transferring LINK from the victim to the stored address, and swaps the drained LINK for ETH (~4k USD).", "lesson": "VRF/fee-recipient initializers must be owner-only — a public `initVRF` that persists the payout address converts the contract's LINK balance into a faucet for whoever initializes first.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "ABCC App", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The ABCC App lets a depositor call `addFixedDay(1_000_000_000)` to extend the \"fixed days\" of his own deposit after depositing, and `claimDDDD()` pays out scaled by the stored term. The attacker flash-loans 12,500 BUSD, deposits just 125, boosts the fixed days to a billion, claims DDDD tokens worth ~10,062 BUSD, and swaps them back to BUSD.", "lesson": "Claim math must not depend on term parameters the depositor can set or extend post-deposit — an `addFixedDay` that inflates maturity is a payout multiplier, so trace claim amounts against every user-settable time field.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Bebop DEX", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Bebop's Jam settlement `settle(order, signature, interactions, ...)` executes the supplied interactions without verifying the order's signature or constraining the calls. The attacker passes an empty signature and two interactions encoding `USDC.transferFrom(user1, attacker, 20,134.5 USDC)` and `transferFrom(user2, attacker, 1,000,000)`; because those users had approved the settlement contract, the arbitrary interactions run with the contract's own msg.sender and drain the standing allowances (~21k USD).", "lesson": "Settlement contracts that execute arbitrary interaction calldata must verify the order signature AND bind interactions to the order — an accepted empty signature turns every user approval to the settler into a public allowance-drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "EverValueCoin", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The EverValue orderbook's `addNewOrder` is public and its matching logic lets the caller both place and fill in one transaction. The attacker flash-loans 12 WBTC, places a BUY order for 60,000 EVA at a price of his choosing (15000), then sells his EVA through the swap routers — the fill executes against his own order at that attacker-set price, so his EVA converts at the inflated rate (~100k USD).", "lesson": "Orderbooks must prevent self-matching and cap price deviation from a real oracle — a public `addNewOrder` that can be filled in the same tx lets the attacker set his own execution price, effectively printing value from thin EVA.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Grizzifi", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Grizzifi's `_incrementUplineTeamCount()` counts `totalInvested` — including already-withdrawn funds — instead of active investment when computing referral tiers. The attacker chains 30 fresh contracts, each investing 10 BUSD with the previous contract as referrer (building a deep upline), then each calls `collectRefBonus()`; the phantom team counts pay full upline referral bonuses on 10-BUSD stakes (~61k USD drained).", "lesson": "Referral/bonus tiers must be computed from ACTIVE invested amounts — counting cumulative (including withdrawn) totals lets an attacker chain accounts and harvest the whole upline bonus pyramid.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Hexotic", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Hexotic's `take(id)` lets a caller seize locked HEX positions at a stale/fixed price. The attacker flash-swaps 0.037 WETH for HEX from the V3 pool and calls `take()` on two known position ids — taking the positions for less than their underlying HEX value, which he keeps after repaying the WETH (~$500, a small-scale demonstration of the pricing flaw).", "lesson": "\"Take\"/auction functions must price from a live, manipulation-resistant source — two `take()` calls at stale prices let the caller capture positions below intrinsic value; check how position price is computed and whether it can be moved or is outdated.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Multicall3 + Xera Token", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "A victim contract had approved the Multicall3 contract to spend its Xera tokens. The attacker submits `aggregate3([transferFrom(victim, cakeLP, 27,900 Xera)])`; because the Xera token's `_msgSender()` implementation misidentifies the caller in the multicall context, the transferFrom executes as if the VICTIM had initiated it, spending the victim's approval. The attacker then flash-swaps the LP pair for WBNB (~17k USD).", "lesson": "Tokens that override `_msgSender()` to return anything but msg.sender break allowance security under multicall/delegatecall — an approval to Multicall3 becomes spendable by anyone when the token trusts the wrong sender, so audit _msgSender overrides on tokens holding real approvals.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "PDZ Token + TbBuild", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "TbBuild's `burnToHolder` computes the BNB reward for burning PDZ using `router.getAmountsOut` at call time. The attacker flash-swaps 10 WBNB, buys PDZ to pump the pool, burns PDZ via `burnToHolder` — which now prices the reward at the pumped rate — calls `receiveRewards` for the inflated BNB, sells the remaining PDZ, and repays the flash loan (~3.3 BNB profit).", "lesson": "Burn-to-reward functions that size payouts from a router's live `getAmountsOut` are pump-and-claim targets — buy → burn → receiveRewards in one tx extracts the manipulated delta, so reward math must use TWAP or an oracle.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Size Credit", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Size Credit's `leverageUpWithSwap` executes the swap path from caller-supplied `SwapParams` without validating the encoded calls. The attacker crafts `GenericRoute` data (flipping one length byte 0x80→0x60) so the leverage contract executes `transferFrom(victim, attacker, fullBalance)` on wstUSR — the victim had approved the leverage contract, and the injected call runs with the contract's own msg.sender — draining the victim's wstUSR (~19.7k USD).", "lesson": "Leverage/swap functions accepting generic route data must whitelist or validate the calls they execute — arbitrary calldata runs with the contract's own approvals, so every user allowance to the leverage contract is a standing drain (same family as the Bebop/0x Settler hacks).", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "WXC Token", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker Moolah-flash-loans 49.15 WBNB and performs a direct `Cake_LP.swap` that pulls a massive WXC amount out against just 1 wei of WBNB (repaying the flash loan inside `pancakeCall` via crafted data), then immediately sells the WXC back through the Pancake router. Because the direct swap and the router sell price against differently synced accounting, the WXC sells for ~37.5 WBNB more than the 1-wei purchase cost.", "lesson": "A direct `pair.swap` with a 1-wei counter-amount followed by a router sell is the fingerprint of exploiting desynced pair accounting — always check whether a token/pair lets the direct-swap price diverge from the router price.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "YuliAI", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim contract sells YuliAI for USDT priced at the YULIAI/USDT V3 pool's live spot price. The attacker flash-loans 200k USDT, buys YuliAI to pump the pool, then calls the victim's `sellToken` 40× — the victim keeps buying his YuliAI at the pumped spot — and finally dumps the remainder back to USDT, repaying the loan with ~78k profit.", "lesson": "Contracts that BUY tokens at a DEX spot price are sandwich targets — pump the pool, sell to the contract repeatedly, dump the rest; buy-side pricing must use TWAP/median oracles just like sell-side.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "0x Settler + Coinbase fee account", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "A Coinbase fee account had approved multiple ERC-20 tokens to the 0x mainnet Settler, which lets anyone execute arbitrary actions. The attacker calls `execute()` with a single action encoding `transferFrom(coinbaseFeeAccount, attacker, full ANDY balance)`; the settler runs the action with its own msg.sender, spending the standing approval and draining ~300k USD.", "lesson": "Approvals granted to swapper/settler contracts that execute arbitrary user-supplied actions are standing drains — this hack required no contract flaw, just one misplaced allowance, so monitoring hot-wallet approvals to execution-style contracts is itself a hunt surface.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "D3XAI proxy", "date": "2025-08", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-08", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The D3XAI proxy's `exchange()` lets traders buy low and sell high because its pricing does not track the live Pancake pool. The attacker flash-loans 20M USDT, buys D3XAT cheaply through the proxy (2 rounds), pumps the Pancake price with ~6.18M USDT of buys (27 rounds), then sells the proxy-bought tokens back through `exchange()` 30× at the favorable stale spread, while dumping the Pancake-bought tokens on Pancake — netting 190 BNB.", "lesson": "Any exchange/swap function that prices from a fixed or stale rate instead of the live DEX is a two-way arb: buy cheap via the proxy, pump the DEX, sell back to the proxy — audit exchange() pricing against the current pool state.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Kame (aggregation router)", "date": "2025-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Kame's aggregation router `swap()` executes caller-supplied `executeParams` against the caller-supplied `executor` address. The attacker crafts SwapParams with `executor = USDC token`, `executeParams = transferFrom(targetUser, attacker, targetUser's full USDC balance)` and a zero amount — the router runs the transferFrom with its own msg.sender, spending the target user's standing approval to the router (~18.2k USD).", "lesson": "Router swap functions that accept an executor + executeParams pair must whitelist the executor — crafted params execute arbitrary transferFrom against any user who ever approved the router (same family as Bebop and the 0x Settler hacks).", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "NGP Token", "date": "2025-09", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-09", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The NGP token's `_update()` calls the pair's `sync()` during transfers. The attacker flash-loans 211M USDT and swaps USDT→NGP with the output routed to the DEAD address, slashing the pair's NGP balance; his own NGP sale then triggers `sync()`, re-baselining reserves to the reduced balance and inflating the NGP price. Selling his prepared 1.35M NGP at the post-sync price nets ~2M USDT.", "lesson": "Tokens whose transfer/`_update` hooks call `pair.sync()` let an attacker re-baseline reserves after burning or donating into the pair mid-swap — a sync() inside token transfer logic is a price-manipulation backdoor, so grep token code for pair.sync calls.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Abracadabra Cauldrons (MIM/Spell)", "date": "2025-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The Spell v3 `cook()` accepts a REPAY action whose amount is attacker-chosen; for each of six cauldrons the attacker checks `borrowLimit` vs the cauldron's MIM balance in the BentoBox, then cooks `[REPAY, NO_OP]` with a value equal to the cauldron's whole MIM balance — the unvalidated repay path moves that MIM to the attacker instead of applying a real debt payment. He withdraws the MIM from the BentoBox, swaps MIM→3CRV→USDT→WETH and exits with ~1.7M.", "lesson": "Multi-action cook/batch functions must validate every action against the user's REAL debt and collateral — an unvalidated REPAY amount let one attacker drain the MIM held by six cauldrons, so fuzz each action code with max/zero/negative values.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Sharwa Finance", "date": "2025-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker creates a margin account, provides USDC through the supply-token pool and `provideERC20`, opens a WBTC long via `increaseLongPosition`, then WITHDRAWS his entire pool deposit — leaving the position open against collateral that no longer backs it. In a second tx he flash-loans WBTC, swaps it to USDC, closes the long with `decreaseLongPosition`, and swaps back — the close pays out as if the position had real PnL, netting ~146k USD.", "lesson": "Margin protocols must re-check collateral when LP funds are withdrawn — an open position whose backing was pulled out can still be \"closed\" for profit, so trace position PnL against current (not initial) locked collateral and block LP withdrawals that undercut open positions.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "TokenHolder (BorrowerOperations)", "date": "2025-10", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-10", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`BorrowerOperations.sell()` accepts caller-supplied `tokenHolder`, `inchRouter` and `whitelistedDex` addresses and executes the `sellingCode` through them. The attacker passes his own contract for all three, implements `loans(0)` to return a fake Loan struct and a no-op `privilegedLoan(WBNB, 20 ether)`, and calls `sell(0, privilegedLoan(WBNB, 20e18), ...)` — the protocol executes the \"privileged loan\" path against his fake record and releases 20 WBNB from its reserves.", "lesson": "Sell/repay entrypoints that take tokenHolder, router and DEX addresses as parameters must validate them against trusted registries — passing your own contract for every external callee and serving a fake loans() record turns the protocol into a 20-WBNB dispenser.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Balancer V2 ComposableStablePool", "date": "2025-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The November 2025 Balancer V2 exploit uses the ComposableStablePool's rate-provider scaling factors: the attacker computes a `trickAmt` just below the scaling-factor precision threshold (e.g. `10000 / ((scalingFactor - 1e18) * 10000 / 1e18)`), then runs a three-phase `batchSwap(GIVEN_OUT)` — phase 1 extracts BPT in shrinking steps, phase 2 loops 25–30 rounds of two-step given-out swaps (a big step plus the trick amount) whose amount-in rounds DOWN so the pool pays out while charging nothing, and phase 3 drains the pool's BPT supply — across the osETH/wETH and wstETH/wETH pools for ~120M USD.", "lesson": "Stable-pool swap math with non-1e18 rate/scaling factors must round against the attacker — a given-out amount sized just under the scaling-factor granularity moves balances while its input rounds to zero, so fuzz swap amounts around `scalingFactor − 1e18` boundaries on every rate-provider pool.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "DRL Vault V3", "date": "2025-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker Morpho-flash-loans 13.98M USDC and swaps USDC→WETH through the DexRouter to move the pool price, then calls the vault's `swapToWETH(100,000 USDC)` — the vault converts at the now-distorted price with no min-return check, receiving too little WETH. He then swaps WETH back to USDC at the reverted price, repays the flash loan and keeps the ~100k USDT difference.", "lesson": "Vault `swapTo*` functions must enforce a min-return/slippage bound — a vault that swaps USDC→WETH without one is a standing sandwich target: move the price, let the vault eat the loss, revert the price.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Moonwell", "date": "2025-11", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-11", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loans wrsETH from the wstETH/wrsETH pool, mints mwrsETH cTokens, enters the market, and borrows ~20,592 mwstETH against it — far more than the wrsETH collateral is worth, because Moonwell valued the wrapper collateral with a stale/misaligned exchange rate. He swaps the borrowed wstETH to WETH, buys wrsETH back with WETH to repay the flash loan, and finally dumps the leftover wrsETH for WETH (~1M USD).", "lesson": "cToken/collateral markets must price wrapper assets with the CURRENT underlying exchange rate — a stale wrsETH rate converted a modest collateral position into a 1M over-borrow; cross-check each collateral's price feed against the live wrapper/underlying rate.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "Yearn yETH", "date": "2025-12", "chain": "Chain", "class": "Class: 2025-12", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The yETH pool tracks virtual balances (`vb_prod_sum`) for 8 assets, one of which (OETH) is rebasing, and `update_rates` is public. The attacker runs a choreographed sequence: a large removal, four add/remove cycles with precise amounts, single-asset rate updates, an `OETH.rebase()` mid-flow, more add/remove cycles with empty `remove_liquidity(0)` calls between rate updates — each step skewing the virtual-balance math — and finally `remove_liquidity(entire supply)`, extracting ~9M USD more than his share.", "lesson": "LP pools containing rebasing assets must recompute virtual balances atomically and gate rate updates — attacker-driven `update_rates` plus a rebase between add/remove legs let him inflate his redeemable share to the whole pool, so audit vb_prod/sum invariants after every rate change and rebase.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2025.md"}, {"protocol": "MT Token", "date": "2026-01", "chain": "BSC", "class": "fee-logic (unbounded fee split)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "MT's `transactionFee()` splits `transactFeeValue` across an unbounded list of percentages without enforcing `sum(shares) <= 100`, so a transfer can debit the sender for far more than `amount`. AMM pairs become unintended fee targets. Attacker flashloans USDT, buys MT, seeds MT into the MT/USDT pair, then `skim()` + `sync()` to lock manipulated reserves and swaps a small MT amount to drain ~36,995 USDT from the pair.", "lesson": "When auditing transfer-fee tokens, prove that fee percentages are bounded and sum to ≤100%, and that contracts (esp. AMM pairs) cannot be overcharged into a reserve desync.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "PRXVT", "date": "2026-01", "chain": "Base", "class": "reward-accounting logic flaw", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Attacker stakes 400,000 PRXVT, then loops (until a gas threshold) transferring the whole stPRXVT balance into a freshly CREATE2-deployed helper which calls `earned()` + `claimReward()` and returns the shares. Because the staking contract's reward debt is not carried per-holder correctly, each fresh address can claim the accrued rewards again, repeatedly harvesting ~32.8 ETH worth of PRXVT.", "lesson": "Check that staking rewards track per-account reward debt updated on every transfer/claim; transferring shares to a fresh address must never reset claimable rewards.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "SynapLogic", "date": "2026-01", "chain": "Base", "class": "input-validation (bulk refund accounting)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The presale's `buy()`/`swap()` accept arrays of `recipients`, `rates`, and `refundFlags` and pay each entry a `rate`% refund out of the contract's balance, while `msg.value` (or the swap input) is received only once. The attacker passes dozens of entries all flagged for a 10% refund, so total refunds vastly exceed the single payment and drain the sale contract of ~27.6 ETH and ~3,450 USDC.", "lesson": "Any bulk/array parameter path that distributes value must verify `sum(distributed) <= received` in the same units before any transfer.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "Truebit", "date": "2026-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "arithmetic-overflow in bonding-curve pricing", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The TRU pool's `getPurchasePrice` uses a quadratic formula; the PoC reverse-solves the buy amount `A` that drives the formula to the uint256 maximum (via `maxPrice/(100*R)` + sqrt), where the computed price overflows/loses precision and the purchase is effectively mispriced. The attacker loops buy → sell of the same TRU amount, each iteration netting ETH from the pool, until ~8,540 ETH is drained.", "lesson": "Bonding-curve and AMM pricing formulas need explicit overflow/precision bounds — test buy/sell symmetry at extreme amounts and add revert guards near max values.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "Futureswap", "date": "2026-01", "chain": "Arbitrum", "class": "fee unit-mismatch (logic flaw)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The victim contract computes a token-unit fee (`abs(delta) * feeRateWad / 1e18`) and forwards it into `FeeManager.addFee()`, where the receiving system interprets the value as feeBasisPoints. The attacker seeds positions with tiny/big deltas, runs `updateFunding()`, then a main `changePosition(-68 WETH, 496,500 USDC)` and a drain `changePosition(0, -894,992 USDC, 0)` to collect ~394,742 USDC.e plus WETH from victim inventory.", "lesson": "When cross-contract calls pass numeric values, verify the units/scaling each side expects — a wad-vs-bps mismatch turns fees into free withdrawals.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "Makina", "date": "2026-01", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "oracle manipulation (pool-balance pricing)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Makina's `Machine.updateTotalAum()` prices DUSD from Curve pool balances via `accountForPosition` commands (`balanceOf` reads). With a 280M USDC flashloan the attacker pumps DUSD and MIM in their Curve pools, calls the accounting/update functions to make the protocol see DUSD as expensive, then swaps the cheaply-bought DUSD (and LP) back to USDC at the inflated valuation, netting ~5.1M USDC. Repeated twice in one tx.", "lesson": "Never price protocol assets from raw AMM `balanceOf` values that a flashloan can move in one block; use TWAPs or manipulation-resistant oracles.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "LAXO Token", "date": "2026-02", "chain": "BSC", "class": "fee-on-transfer + reserve desync", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 350 BUSD from Pancake V3, buys LAXO via the fee-on-transfer router path, adds and then removes liquidity, then raw-transfers their entire LAXO balance directly into the BUSD pair so its real balance diverges from stored reserves, and executes a low-level `pair.swap()` to extract BUSD at an inflated rate (~137K USD profit). The flawed fee accounting on LAXO transfers is what makes the pair's balances/reserves exploitable.", "lesson": "Fee-on-transfer tokens interacting with V2 pairs should be fuzzed for balance-vs-reserve drift; raw token donations into a pair followed by a direct `swap()` is a red flag worth testing on every new pair.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "Moonwell", "date": "2026-02", "chain": "Base", "class": "oracle manipulation → bad debt", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With a WETH flashloan from an Aerodrome CL pool, the attacker calls `liquidateBorrow(victim, amount, mcbETH)` to repay a victim's WETH debt and seize cbETH collateral, redeems mcbETH for cbETH, swaps cbETH→WETH, and repays — profiting from the liquidation discount. The incident's root cause is the Moonwell Chainlink cbETH oracle on Base mispricing collateral, allowing underwater positions to be liquidated in a way that left ~1.78M in protocol bad debt.", "lesson": "Collateral pricing feeds derived from thin/exchange-rate markets must be cross-checked and deviation-bounded, or liquidations become a free money printer for the first caller.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "Alkemi Earn", "date": "2026-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "self-liquidation logic flaw", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloans 51 WETH, supplies 50 ETH as aweth collateral, borrows 39.5 aweth against it, then calls `liquidateBorrow{value: amount}(self, aweth, aweth, amount)` paying exactly their own borrow balance and finally `withdraw(aweth, max)` — the borrow/liquidate/withdraw accounting lets a borrower capture the liquidation value of their own position, netting 43.45 ETH from the protocol.", "lesson": "Liquidation must be zero-sum for self-liquidations — enforce borrower≠liquidator or deduct seized collateral from the borrower's balance before paying out.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "Curve LlamaLend / crvUSD", "date": "2026-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access-control (public vault mint) + liquidation gaming", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Using nested Morpho flashloans (USDC then WETH) the attacker creates a crvUSD loan, deposits into the Yearn scrvUSD vault, freely calls `sDOLA.mint()` to mint shares out of thin air, and uses a second contract to iterate `crvUSD_Controller.users_to_liquidate()` and `liquidate(user, 0)` on every liquidatable position. Redeeming the inflated shares and pocketing liquidation proceeds nets ~$240K before repaying.", "lesson": "ERC4626-style vaults must restrict minting to the deposit path and liquidations must respect collateral math — a public mint plus a permissionless liquidate-all loop is an open drain.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "EST / BNBDeposit", "date": "2026-03", "chain": "BSC", "class": "accounting desync (skim loop) + EIP-7702 EOA delegation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Using EIP-7702 delegation (needed because BNBDeposit only lets EOAs claim), the attacker flashloans 20,000 WBNB, repeatedly deposits into BNBDeposit, swaps WBNB→EST, sends 1 EST directly to BNBDeposit to desync its internal accounting, then runs a 100-iteration loop of transferring EST into the pair and `pair.skim(bnbDeposit)` to keep inflating the deposit contract's balance. The desynced accounting credits disproportionate LP/withdrawable value, and the attacker swaps EST back for a ~150.2 WBNB profit.", "lesson": "Deposit contracts that derive user value from raw token balances must be audited for direct-donation and skim() desync paths; also test EIP-7702 delegation against EOA-only gating.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "Venus (THE market)", "date": "2026-03", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (stolen approvals) + donation-inflation + borrowBehalf", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Six EOAs had pre-approved the future attack-contract address, letting the attacker `transferFrom` their THE into vTHE — inflating the market exchange rate. The attacker then uses Venus `borrowBehalf(victim, ...)` to take USDC, CAKE and WBNB loans on the victim's behalf (cash to attacker, debt to victim), mints vUSDC, enters the market, borrows THE and donates it back into vTHE to further inflate collateral value for another borrowBehalf round.", "lesson": "Track allowance grants to undeployed/predicted addresses as a hunting signal, and flag `borrowBehalf`-style functions where collateral valuation can be pushed by direct donations.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "MONA LisaVault", "date": "2026-04", "chain": "BSC", "class": "business-logic abuse (self-referral) + insider LP drain", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Each 220 USDT node purchase pays 70 USDT to the L1 referrer and 50 USDT to L2. The attacker deploys 25 fresh `NodeBuyer` proxy contracts (bypassing the 1-node-per-address cap) that register the attack contract as L1 and an attacker-controlled L2, recouping 120/220 USDT per node. Stage 1 (insider component) has the vault owner remove their 100%-owned MONA/USDT LP, routing ~66,450 USDT to the exploit contract; total profit ≈60,950 USDT.", "lesson": "Referral rewards must be self-referral-proof (no cycles, no same-wallet tiers) and per-address caps must be bypass-proof; check LP ownership concentration for insider-drain paths.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "Saturn Protocol (sUSDat)", "date": "2026-04", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "arithmetic underflow (DoS) + tolerance-bound extraction", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SAT-001: after `transferInRewards()` and `burnQueuedShares()` the invariant `strcBalance >= getUnvestedAmount()` breaks, so `convertFromStrc()` underflows (`strcBalance - unvested`) and panics (0x11), freezing all withdrawals for up to 30 days with no admin escape hatch. SAT-002: `_validateConversion()` applies toleranceBps=2000 (20%) independently to both price and amount, so the PROCESSOR role can extract up to 1-(0.8/1.2) = 33.33% per `convertFromUsdat()` call. TVL at risk ~$35.7M (disclosure, unpatched).", "lesson": "Audit vesting/redemption arithmetic for state sequences that break `balance >= unvested` invariants, and never stack independent tolerance windows on both sides of a conversion.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "DxSale", "date": "2026-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (owner backdoor)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The DxSale Legacy Liquidity Locker's owner-only `DXLOCKERLP(address,uint256)` backdoor drains any locked LP position without respecting individual timelocks. Ownership was secretly relayed through 89 wallets over 269 days to the attacker, who used an EIP-7702 delegated drainer contract to batch-empty all 1,400+ locked LP positions for ~$7.3M in BNB.", "lesson": "An owner-only function that moves user-locked funds IS the vulnerability; verify lockers enforce timelocks inside the token transfer path, not just at the UI, and watch ownership-transfer chains.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "Joe Agent", "date": "2026-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "reentrancy (CEI violation)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`removeLiquidityViaContract(liquidity,...)` sends BNB to the caller before zeroing `lpInfo[user].lpAmount`. The attacker parks one ~437 LP position, then its `receive()` re-enters with the same liquidity value 25 times — every re-entry passes the stale `liquidity <= lpInfo[user].lpAmount` check and burns a fresh slice of the pool's shared LP, draining 62.5 BNB + ~1.19M JOE.", "lesson": "State updates that gate asset accounting (lpAmount, shares, allowances) must happen before any external call; reentrancy via `receive()` on BNB/ETH payouts is the classic missed path.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "LegendaryMoneyMonNft", "date": "2026-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (ecrecover zero-address signature bypass)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`cliamRewred()` verifies a claim via `recoverSigner(hash, sig) == admin`, but the admin was set to address(0) and `ecrecover` returns address(0) for malformed signatures without the contract checking it. A forged 65-byte signature (r=0, s=0, v=27) therefore verifies, and the attacker drains the contract's entire MON balance, swapping it for ~85.5K USDT.", "lesson": "`ecrecover` returns address(0) on failure — always require the recovered signer != address(0), and treat an admin set to zero as an immediate drain condition.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "SKP", "date": "2026-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (owner backdoor) + oracle manipulation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The SKP token has an owner-only `ownerBurnLiquidityPairTokens(amount)` that burns SKP sitting directly inside the SKP/USDT pair. The owner burns ~95% of the pair's SKP, then `pair.sync()` re-bases reserves, leaving USDT intact and spiking the on-chain SKP price ~20x. The owner then supplies the over-valued SKP as collateral on Venus/Lista DAO and borrows BTCB + USDT (~$212K).", "lesson": "Any owner function able to move/burn tokens inside an LP pair is a price-oracle weapon against lending markets that price by spot reserves.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "SKP (exit-scam framing) — SKP_exp2.sol", "date": "2026-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "malicious token hook (engineered rug)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "SKP's `_transfer()` fires `_runSpecialPairFlow()` when the pair sends SKP to a whitelisted buyer, redistributing an unbounded amount of treasury SKP to the single owner-set WL_ADDRESS; the trigger (`balanceOf(pair) - reserve`) is flash-loan inflatable. The attacker (same tx as SKP_exp) buys with ~205M flashloaned USDT routed to WL, triggering a ~9.7B free-SKP treasury dump, then sells the free SKP back to drain ~205M USDT from the pair — an operator-engineered rug (WL set 6 days prior, unverified source, throwaway token pattern).", "lesson": "Unverified token source + owner-set whitelist + pair-triggered mint/redistribution hook is the rug-pull fingerprint; treat \"hacks\" of fresh tokens as operator exfiltration until proven otherwise.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "WUSD / GLOVE", "date": "2026-05", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "Sybil incentive-abuse (no claim ledger)", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`WUSD.wrap()`'s `_englove()` mints ~2 GLOVE to any caller whose current GLOVE balance is under 2e18 — eligibility keys only on the live balance, with no per-address claim ledger, cooldown, or identity binding. The attacker flashloans USDT, spins up 80 fresh helper contracts that each wrap 100k WUSD (advancing the global epoch), pumps 101 extra wrap/unwrap cycles to complete vesting, then unwraps and dumps the free GLOVE into thin Uniswap V3 GLO/USDC and GLO/USDT pools, draining the LPs.", "lesson": "Reward/incentive systems must track per-address claims and vesting in storage — any reward conditioned only on current balance is Sybil-farmable by fresh addresses.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "YSDAO", "date": "2026-05", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (permissionless sync) + tax-bypass logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "YSDAO's transfer protection detects add/remove-liquidity by comparing pair balances to reserves. The attacker buys YSDAO while having the pair pay out 1 wei USDT so `_isRemoveLiquidity()` reads true and buy taxes/cooldown are skipped; then calls the unprotected `Staking.sync()` which donates all staking-held USDT into the pair and resyncs reserves, raising the apparent YSDAO price; then transfers 1 USDT into the pair before selling so the sell takes the `_isAddLiquidity()` (untaxed) branch. Profit ~19.5K USDT.", "lesson": "Tax/branch logic driven by pair-balance-vs-reserve comparisons is gameable with 1-wei donations — verify staking `sync()`/donation entry points are access-controlled and that fee branches don't depend on attacker-influenced balance deltas.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "ATM", "date": "2026-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "Sybil bypass of anti-whale + auto-dump logic flaw", "loss": "", "mechanics": "ATM's `_transfer()` sell branch auto-sells 20% of the sell size from the contract's own reserves at `amountOutMin=0` whenever its balance exceeds `swapAtAmount`, leaking value to marketing/dividend wallets. All anti-whale guards (MAX_HOLDER, coldTime, tOwnedU profit fee) key off fresh per-address state, so the attacker spreads 2.85M ATM across 30 CREATE2 farmer clones (each under the cap, zero cooldown, zero cost-basis), pumps the price via a burn-on-buy ratchet, then dumps all 30 clones to extract ~243,543 USDT (net ~89K) from the pair.", "lesson": "Per-address rate limits with fresh-address defaults of zero are not limits — any Sybil-able guard must key off global/totalized state, and contract self-balance auto-sells are a leak worth modeling in every trade.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "BY Token", "date": "2026-06", "chain": "BSC", "class": "access-control (permissionless burn) + price manipulation", "loss": "", "mechanics": "`triggerAutoBurn()` is permissionless once trading is enabled. The attacker corner-buys BY via the router (output routed to the router itself), donates WBNB to the pair and `sync()`s to cross the trading-enable BNB threshold, buys BY for themselves, then calls `triggerAutoBurn()` to burn BY out of the pair — crashing the BY reserve and spiking the price. Five 1-wei sells plus a final dump of the remaining BY drain ~146.6 WBNB (~$87.4K) at the manipulated price.", "lesson": "Token burns that hit LP-pair holdings must be restricted and delayed, not permissionless — a burn plus a sync is a one-transaction price pump against spot-priced exits.", "aftermath": "", "type": "incident", "location": "database/2026.md"}, {"protocol": "Eleven Finance", "date": "2021-06-23", "chain": "BSC (and Polygon)", "class": "bridge", "loss": "$4.5M", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-swapped the vault's underlying tokens (pegged BTC/ETH/USDT) from PancakeSwap, minted them into nrvBTC/nrvETH/nrvFUSDT and deposited into the MasterMind vault via the intermediate vault, then called `emergencyBurn()` to pull out an amount equal to the vault's full pre-attack balance (i.e., his own deposit counted again), followed by a normal withdrawal of his original deposit. Total ~$4.5M: 30.75 BTCB (~$1.05M), 286 ETH (~$561K), 2.241M BUSD from the NRV 3Pool LP and 0.647M BUSD from the NRV-BUSD LP. He moved 2,293 ETH of proceeds out over the Nerve bridge to 0xdb2d590aCe7cAe51DF1fB3312738038Ec032Bf33.", "lesson": "Emergency/burn paths that bypass internal accounting must still reconcile balance-vs-share bookkeeping — hunt for any \"withdraw without accounting\" function in vault wrappers. When auditing an aggregator, prove that a deposit followed by burn + regular withdrawal cannot extract more than the depositor's own share.", "aftermath": "Peckshield blamed a \"dumb logic issue\" — shockingly simple for $4.5M. The article entered the leaderboard at position #27.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "1inch (Fusion v1 resolvers)", "date": "2025-03-05", "chain": "Ethereum; calldata corruption / integer underflow in the deprecated `_settleOrder` / `resolveOrders` (0x1944799f) path of 1inch's obsolete Fusion v1 resolver contracts.", "class": "overflow", "loss": "~$5M (most returned; ~$450K kept)", "mechanics": "The attacker (Tornado-Cash-funded deployer 0xa7264a43...) created normal-looking orders swapping pennies for millions, padded calldata with null bytes, set interactionLength to -512 so memory pointers underflowed and pointed at attacker-controlled data, and added a fake suffix \"interaction\" so the victim resolver (TrustedVolumes 0xb02f39e3...) got called as the resolution target. This drained 2.4M USDC + 1,276 WETH (~$5M) from resolver partners, including the 1inch Settlement V1 contract. After an on-chain \"Can I have bounty?\" negotiation with TrustedVolumes, he returned most funds and kept ~$450K (~10%).", "lesson": "Deprecated/legacy contract entry points that are still deployed and funded are live attack surface — retire or kill them, and re-audit after any language-level rewrite (Solidity→Yul) because audits of the pre-refactor source do not cover the deployed bytecode. Hunt for unconstrained user-supplied integers used as memory offsets/lengths in settlement or calldata-decoding code.", "aftermath": "1inch's first post said \"no end-user funds at risk\" and omitted the $5M; SlowMist exposed the real numbers the next day, and Decurity's Omar Ganiev published a candid postmortem confessing the firm's own missed tracking. The attacker accidentally sent half the haul to the 1inch settlement contract and had to race shoucccc and others to grab it back. 1inch launched a bug bounty program after the fact.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "8ight Finance", "date": "2021-12-08", "chain": "Harmony; Olympus-DAO fork treasury drained via \"compromised keys\" (claimed multi-sig was never in place).", "class": "logic", "loss": "$1.75M", "mechanics": "Three transactions drained the treasury: 490,170 DAI, then 378,417 DAI + 10,843 LP tokens, then 12,362 USDT (~$1.75M total). 868,587 DAI was consolidated from account xxca1d to cc541 and then sent to Tornado Cash.", "lesson": "Verify a protocol's claimed multi-sig/treasury custody on-chain before assessing risk — \"compromised key\" stories are usually missing or ceremonial multisigs. Key storage OPSEC (no keys in cloud docs/chats) is part of the attack surface you can audit from public artifacts.", "aftermath": "Many users accused the developer of an inside job, noting the team still used Facebook-era OPSEC; rekt's verdict: \"maybe hacked, maybe rugged, definitely rekt.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "LNDFi", "date": "2025-05-09", "chain": "Sonic; access-control backdoor in an Aave V3 fork (\"Admin Privileges\"", "class": "logic", "loss": "$1.18M", "mechanics": "On March 29, 2025 (41 days before the drain), in a 45-second window the deployer 0xc0454e29... granted himself the Pool Admin role and deployed the backdoored token contracts, then went dormant. At 02:29 UTC on May 9 the attacker called `transferUnderlyingTo` repeatedly to empty every pool: $476K USDC, 153.7 ETH ($389K), 373,594 wS ($202K), 189K staked Sonic ($105K) and 4.51 scETH ($11.5K) — $1,183,500 total — bridging out to Ethereum and BSC within ten minutes; admin rights were only revoked at 09:19 AM.", "lesson": "Diff any lending-protocol fork against the upstream reference implementation — a widened access modifier like `onlyPool || isPoolAdmin` is an instant critical. Admin/withdrawal privileges must be multisig + timelocked, and dormant-but-deployed backdoor modifications (weeks of staging) are exactly what on-chain monitoring should flag.", "aftermath": "The team blamed an \"undercover DPRK IT worker\"; ZachXBT confirmed the DPRK attribution but was not formally engaged. Tiancheng Mai's unofficial postmortem exposed the backdoor that the official one glossed over, and the 41-day staging period remains unexplained.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Lucky Star Currency / FSL", "date": "2023-10-09", "chain": "BSC; insider rug pulls via a privileged withdraw function and a pre-minted token dump.", "class": "drain", "loss": "$2.79M combined (two rugs)", "mechanics": "In the early hours of Monday (UTC) the LSC deployer (0x9ef72ee6...) withdrew 1.65M LSC from AwardCentre and 1.45M LSC from NFTMerge, swapped the ~3M LSC for $1.1M BSC-USD and forwarded it to 0x23f8c805... On Tuesday afternoon the FSL deployer (0x281b8cb2...) transferred 97M of his 100M pre-mint to 0x7249b903..., which dumped them for $1.68M BSC-USD, swapped to 7,952.2 BNB and deposited into Tornado Cash.", "lesson": "A privileged `withdrawToken`/sweep function on a token-holding contract and a deployer-held pre-mint of ~100% supply are the two highest-yield rug indicators to check on any new BSC project. Unverified contracts and <24h launch-to-dump timelines should kill the thesis immediately.", "aftermath": "The LSC Telegram played the rug off as a hack and floated a relaunch. Written while SBF's trial dominated the timeline; rekt noted two more BSC rugs the same week (Islamic Coin $150K, Marble X $160K).", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Aave", "date": "2026-03-10", "chain": "Ethereum; liquidation storm caused by a protocol-level oracle configuration error (CAPO wstETH oracle)", "class": "Price", "loss": "$27.78M liquidated (10,938 wstETH; no bad debt)", "mechanics": "At 11:46 UTC on March 10 Chaos Labs computed the update off-chain; AgentHub (BGD Labs) executed it one block later via Chainlink Automation with no human review. Aave priced wstETH 2.85% below market, pushing 34 leveraged E-Mode (wstETH/WETH) positions past their liquidation line. Automated liquidators swept 10,938 wstETH ($27.78M), capturing ~512 ETH: ~116 ETH standard bonuses, ~13 ETH fees, and ~382 ETH pure arbitrage (buying at 1.1939, selling at 1.2285). Recoveries (~154.92 ETH) plus a TokenLogic AIP for a 512.19 ETH reimbursement (net ~357.56 ETH to the DAO) followed.", "lesson": "Safety-layer parameters that must move in lockstep (snapshot ratio vs timestamp vs growth cap) are a configuration invariant — a rate limiter that blocks legitimate correction is as dangerous as the manipulation it prevents. Audit stale-parameter drift on oracles/risk modules and simulate \"cap clamped below market\" scenarios; near-misses that fail to fire are pre-exploit warnings.", "aftermath": "LTV Protocol spotted the anomaly before Aave's postmortem; the same mismatched update had almost fired a month earlier when the agent wasn't yet connected. It was the third act in a pattern (Moonwell's ~$1.78M cbETH bad debt, YieldBlox's $10M VWAP pool) of safety layers themselves mispricing, and came days after ACI and BGD Labs both exited the Aave DAO.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Abracadabra (I)", "date": "2024-01-30", "chain": "Ethereum; MIM cauldrons drained via debt-share rounding + ERC-4626-style first-depositor share inflation on BentoBox.", "class": "rounding", "loss": "$6.5M", "mechanics": "The attacker flashloaned MIM via Degenbox, deposited MIM to BentoBox with the recipient set to BentoBox itself (donation), called `repayForAll()` to repay other users' liabilities (then manually repaid remaining borrowers to drive borrow elastic to zero), then looped borrow/repay to inflate the share price, added collateral, and borrowed a large MIM amount against the inflated shares. He drained the yvCrv3Crypto and magicAPE cauldrons for $6.5M, split across two accumulation addresses ($4.2M and $2.2M), and dumping the MIM for ETH depegged the stablecoin to ~$0.95.", "lesson": "Debt-share accounting (`part`/elastic) must be rounding-safe under borrow/repay loops, and vaults must defend against donation-based share-price inflation (dead shares / virtual shares). When auditing any fork of a lending vault, test first-depositor and single-user edge cases explicitly.", "aftermath": "BlockSec and PeckShield raised the alarm within the hour; the team promised DAO-treasury buyback-and-burn of MIM to restore the peg and reached out to the attacker on-chain to negotiate.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Abracadabra (II)", "date": "2025-03-25", "chain": "Arbitrum; liquidation loophole in the GMX-linked gmCauldrons (failed GMX deposit + self-liquidation = phantom collateral).", "class": "logic", "loss": "$13M (6,260 ETH)", "mechanics": "(1) Deposit into GMX but force the deposit to fail so tokens sit in OrderAgent; (2) borrow and push the position into liquidation; (3) self-liquidate — the contract wipes the position but forgets to scrub the order; (4) borrow again against the \"ghost\" collateral the system still sees. The attacker (0xAF9e33Aa...) drained 6,260 ETH (~$13M) from cauldron 0x625Fe795..., bridged Arbitrum→Ethereum and split it across three wallets. Abracadabra paused borrowing and offered a 20% bounty, unanswered.", "lesson": "Liquidation paths must invalidate every in-flight order/deposit in every integrated external system — cross-protocol state (GMX OrderAgent) is part of the invariant surface, not just the lending contract. Audit the failed-deposit + liquidation + re-borrow interaction explicitly; Guardian Audits owned the miss and added invariant testing in response.", "aftermath": "Vladimir S (\"Officer's Notes\") flagged it first, PeckShield confirmed; GMX distanced itself (\"no issues with GMX contracts\"). The team's \"Path Forward\" promised a 6.5M MIM buyback, while its treasury is ~50% MIM/SPELL — cumulative Abracadabra losses now ~$20M.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Abracadabra (III)", "date": "2025-10-04", "chain": "Ethereum; logic bug in the deprecated CauldronV4 `cook()` multi-action flow — a status-flag reset bypassing the solvency check.", "class": "logic", "loss": "$1.8M (1,793,755 MIM minted against zero collateral)", "mechanics": "The attacker called `cook()` with actions `[5, 0]`: action 5 queued a massive borrow and set the solvency flag, action 0 immediately reset it, and the check never ran — 1,793,755 MIM borrowed against zero collateral across six deprecated mainnet cauldrons (last audited November 2023). About half was swapped to ETH: 51 ETH to Tornado Cash, then the rest (344 ETH) laundered through 3+ dozen mixer transactions. The DAO treasury bought back the dumped MIM.", "lesson": "Multi-action \"cook\"/multicall patterns must accumulate status flags (monotonic OR), never overwrite them — hunt for any action that can reset a security flag set by an earlier action. \"Deprecated\" contracts that still hold value and remain callable are production contracts: they need kill switches and re-audits, not labels.", "aftermath": "William Li spotted it first, BlockSec Phalcon confirmed. Synnax (an Abracadabra fork with the same code) paused its contracts four days earlier, and PeckShield deleted the Synnax audit report 18 hours before the exploit and refunded the client — documented by William Li. Third exploit in under two years; cumulative ~$21M.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Acala Network", "date": "2022-08-14", "chain": "Polkadot (Acala parachain); infinite mint via a misconfigured liquidity-pool incentive — `incentives.claim_rewards()` on the new iBTC/aUSD pool minted aUSD exponentially (1.3B total).", "class": "misconfig", "loss": "~$1.3B erroneously minted (~$1.6M escaped)", "mechanics": "One user's wallet minted ~1.2B aUSD from the bug (some users triggered it accidentally); a handful of others monetized it by bridging aUSD to Moonbeam, swapping to DOT (sent to Polkadot) and iBTC (sent to Interlay), nearly draining the iBTC pool. About $1.6M in \"good value\" and $4.6M of bad-debt aUSD left the chain before Polkadot governance disabled transfers; the accidental minter cooperated to return and burn his excess, and aUSD held above $0.90 after reactivation.", "lesson": "Reward/incentive payout functions must be bounded by their configured rate and unit-tested against pool config changes — a misconfigured pool reward rate is an unbacked-mint primitive. Treat \"pool went live today\" config as a security event: check reward math on bootstrapped pools before trading starts.", "aftermath": "Acala called it \"a misconfiguration of the iBTC/aUSD liquidity pool\"; on-chain tracing of recipient addresses was underway and a community governance decision was pending on how to handle the wrongly minted aUSD. The 1.2B-mint wallet was contrasted with the accidental user who cooperated (\"this was a fault of the system, not mine\").", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Aevo / Ribbon Finance", "date": "2025-12-12", "chain": "Ethereum; legacy Ribbon (Opyn Gamma-based) options vaults — a Dec 6 oracle-config upgrade exposed the price-feed proxies", "class": "oracle", "loss": "$2.7M", "mechanics": "A staged multi-wallet crew (Mastermind/Specialist/Engineer/Bagman/\"Fall Guy\") whitelisted collateral and deployed a disguised oracle to build a compliance history, then in one transaction hammered the Proxy Admin (0x9D7b...) to swap the oracle to a malicious implementation that cranked the AAVE price \"to infinity\" and locked the expiry timestamp to Dec 12, 2025. The attacker created \"Frankenstein\" oTokens (wstETH-collateralized, AAVE-underlying, stETH call with a 3,800 USDC 8-decimal strike) — e.g. burning 225 oTokens returned ~22.46 WETH per cycle — and the primary drain burned oTokens for 846 WETH + 178K USDC (~$2.6M). Proceeds were distributed in precise 100.1 ETH batches for Tornado Cash.", "lesson": "Any upgrade touching oracle/proxy access must be diffed against the original invariants (Opyn's collateral-matching was silently dropped), and proxy-admin authorization checking `tx.origin` or a permission flag instead of `msg.sender` is an instant critical. Legacy \"zombie\" vaults with live TVL need decommissioning kill switches, not maintenance patches.", "aftermath": "Specter flagged the exploit before Aevo announced it (19 hours later). Aevo decommissioned all Ribbon vaults and deleted its compensation tweets after proposing a 19% haircut partly subsidized by \"zombie\" depositors dormant 2-4 years; the 2021 OpenZeppelin audit offered no protection post-upgrade.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "AFX Trade", "date": "2026-07-22", "chain": "Arbitrum; validator compromise of the AFX bridge's off-chain signing system (no smart-contract bug).", "class": "logic", "loss": "$24.15M USDC", "mechanics": "An operational address created the withdrawal, it cleared the dispute window, and a batched finalize released $24.15M USDC to the loot wallet (0x2f2974fa...). The funds moved like routine traffic: six transfers through the shared BridgingKit CCTP router (burn on Arbitrum, mint on Ethereum to the same recipient), then eight UniswapX Dutch-auction fills (solver \"Rizzolver\") swapped them to 12,467.44 ETH, consolidated into 0x627654B2..., then fragmented across ~24 addresses in a day. AFX's 30%-keep bounty went unanswered.", "lesson": "Bridge withdrawal authorization is key management: hot validator keys controlling a large quorum are the vulnerability, and audits of contract logic (Zellic documented zero test coverage and unresolved acknowledgments) say nothing about the signers. Require cold-storage/multi-domain custody, per-withdrawal caps, and dispute windows that react to validator-compromise indicators.", "aftermath": "Blockaid flagged it at 21:30 UTC with the exact figure before AFX spoke; Arbitrum's Steven Goldfeder drew the boundary that the native bridge was not involved. zeroShadow/SEAL attribute it to UNC4899 (TraderTraitor, DPRK) via a gas-funding pattern matching the KelpDAO exploit; Ostium lost $23.75M on the same network a week earlier.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Agave DAO & Hundred Finance", "date": "2022-03-15", "chain": "Gnosis (xDai); reentrancy through the xDAI token's `callAfterTransfer()` hook", "class": "reentrancy", "loss": "$11.7M combined (2,116 ETH + 2,363 ETH)", "mechanics": "Using flash-loan collateral, the attacker nested borrow calls inside one another via the token's post-transfer callback so each borrow executed against the not-yet-updated debt state; repeating this drained 2,116 ETH ($5.5M) from Agave (11:25:40 UTC) and 2,363 ETH ($6.2M) from Hundred Finance (11:28:40 UTC), three minutes apart. Both hauls were bridged to the attacker's ETH address and sent to Tornado Cash within hours.", "lesson": "Forking a battle-tested protocol does not inherit its security properties — re-verify every environment-specific token behavior (chain-native tokens with hooks like `callAfterTransfer`) when porting code across chains. Reentrancy guards/CEI and strict collateral whitelists are the two mitigations the originals had and the forks dropped.", "aftermath": "Same vector as CREAM Finance's $18.8M loss the previous August; AGVE plunged >20% while HND barely moved. Daniel Von Fange and Mudit Gupta credited for the analysis; entered the leaderboard at #35.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Akropolis", "date": "2020-11-12", "chain": "Ethereum; reentrancy through a malicious ERC20 used as the deposit token", "class": "logic", "loss": "$2M DAI", "mechanics": "The attacker created a fake token whose transfer re-enters deposit: deposit faketoken → callback fires → deposit 25K DAI inside the callback → credited 25K DAI from the delta, and also credited for the original deposit → withdraw 50K DAI. Batches of ~$50K were executed over ~8 hours before the main hit; ~$2M of gains were sent to a holding address.", "lesson": "Never derive deposits from before/after balance deltas when the deposited token is attacker-controlled — use share-based accounting with CEI and reentrancy guards, and whitelist deposit tokens. Treat \"accepts arbitrary ERC20 as deposit token\" as a reentrancy search surface.", "aftermath": "The contracts had been audited by Smartdec and Certik; Akropolis admins initially claimed they were just doing \"fixes\" before the $2M loss came out — the lie cost them the community's trust.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Alchemix", "date": "2021-06-16", "chain": "Ethereum; logic bug in the newly launched alETH (ETH-collateral) product — borrowers were assigned no debt and could withdraw their locked collateral while keeping the minted alETH.", "class": "logic", "loss": "~2,700 ETH (~$6.5M) unbacked alETH", "mechanics": "Users discovered they could mint alETH against deposited ETH and simultaneously withdraw the ETH that was supposed to stay locked as collateral — a self-serve \"reverse rugpull\" with no attacker needed. The team paused new alETH mints within hours, but the alETH pool was left undercollateralized by ~2,700 ETH (~$6.5M), which the treasury had to backstop via a public voluntary-return appeal.", "lesson": "Every new collateral type deployment must re-run the debt-accounting invariant tests (mint ⇒ debt = collateral value) and monitor the first blocks for zero-debt positions — a copied \"transmuter\" that skips debt assignment is a free-withdrawal bug. Pause switches on new products are cheap insurance.", "aftermath": "Co-founder Scoopy Trooples told rekt the team had ~$4M non-ALCX treasury and needed >25% voluntary returns to stay solvent; one user re-invested the misappropriated funds straight back into the protocol.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "AlexLab (I)", "date": "2024-05-14", "chain": "BNB network; XLink bridge (ALEX/Stacks) drained after a phishing-compromised deployer key performed four malicious proxy upgrades.", "class": "bridge", "loss": "$4.3M", "mechanics": "The deployer address executed four malicious upgrades; within an hour the attacker (0x27055aE4...) withdrew ~13.7M STX (~$4.3M) across two transactions, sending ~3M STX to centralized exchanges before freezes could land. The team recovered aBTC, sUSDT, xBTC, xUSD, ALEX, atALEX and other assets, and offered a 10% bounty with a 48-hour deadline; some STX slipped through CEX freezes.", "lesson": "The proxy-admin/deployer key is the highest-value key in the system — put upgrades behind a multisig with timelock and monitor for any implementation change. Audits (CoinFabrik) and bug bounties (Immunefi) don't protect against the upgrade path, so threat-model the admin surface as first-class.", "aftermath": "Certik Alert caught the suspicious transaction May 14, but AlexLab confirmed only the next day. ImmuneBytes/Chain Aegis linked the attacker to the Mars Defi 412 price-manipulation attack ($100K, April 16); the team floated burning unrecovered STX and reissuing tokens to victims.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "AlexLab (II)", "date": "2025-06-06", "chain": "Stacks; vault permission hijack through the permissionless self-listing flow — a malicious \"token\" with a fake transfer function drained the amm-vault.", "class": "access control", "loss": "~$16.2M (official tally $8.37M)", "mechanics": "The attacker deployed \"ssl-labubu-672d3\" (a token with a draining transfer function), created a Labubu/STX pool to get vault permissions auto-granted, flipped the farming flag, then executed one `swap-x-for-y` call — the vault-as-sender context let the fake transfer sweep 8,403,867 STX ($5.54M), 50.74 aBTC ($5.43M), 12.76 sBTC ($1.35M), 119.4M ALEX ($2.12M) and 1.75M sUSDT ($1.74M) out of vault SP102V8P0...amm-vault-v2-01 in a single transaction.", "lesson": "Permissionless listing flows must never auto-grant vault-level permissions to user-supplied token contracts, and a vault must never call attacker-controlled code with an elevated (`as-contract`) context. Audits of the upcoming product (DAMM) don't cover the live system — audit what is deployed and in production, and check every path where user tokens get \"approved\" privileges.", "aftermath": "Reubs BTC raised the alarm within minutes; researcher Nolan (Exvul) debunked the Stacks-blame framing. Official damage claims ($8.37M) were roughly half the on-chain total ($16.18M), and compensation was promised from the foundation treasury — the second AlexLab exploit in 13 months with a completely different vector.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Alpha Finance (Alpha Homora)", "date": "2021-02-13", "chain": "Ethereum; debt-share rounding + permissionless `resolveReserve` + custom \"evil spell\" against HomoraBankv2's unannounced", "class": "rounding", "loss": "~$37.5M", "mechanics": "The attacker deployed an \"evil spell\", borrowed 1,000 sUSD as the first (sole) borrower, then repaid all but 1 mini-sUSD so he held 1 debt share; calling `resolveReserve` accrued ~19,709,787,742,196 units of debt while totalShare stayed 1; he then looped borrows of (totalDebt − 1) — each rounding to 0 new borrow share, so the protocol treated them as debt-free — doubling 16 + 10 + 10 times, seeded with Aave flashloans (1.8M then ~10M USDC swapped to sUSD and deposited to Cream's Iron Bank for liquidity). The haul: 13,244.63 WETH + 3.6M USDC + 5.6M USDT + 4.26M DAI; stablecoins were wrapped into aTokens/a3Crv to evade freezes, and 1K ETH was sent to each of Cream's and Alpha's deployer addresses as a false flag.", "lesson": "Borrow share must never round to zero — enforce a minimum borrow or round shares up, and fuzz the first/sole-borrower edge case. Any permissionless function that mutates totalDebt without touching shares (like `resolveReserve`) is a debt-inflation primitive; also treat \"deployed but not announced\" pools as live attack surface.", "aftermath": "The exploit required insider knowledge (the unannounced pool), so Alpha Finance named a \"prime suspect\"; SBF withdrew $400M FTT from Cream and 3AC sent $3M ALPHA to Binance amid the panic. Andre Cronje called it \"9 transactions, 4 different manipulations\", Banteg: \"The setup is absolutely insane.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "AlphaPo", "date": "2023-07-22", "chain": "Multi-chain CEX/payments-processor hot-wallet drain (alphapo.eth) — attributed to the Lazarus Group's distinct on-chain fingerprint", "class": "logic", "loss": "$60M (ETH, TRON, BTC)", "mechanics": "In the early hours (UTC) of Saturday, the alphapo.eth hot wallet was drained of 2,464 ETH ($4.6M) plus 6M+ USDT and assorted tokens on Ethereum; assets were swapped to ETH, consolidated into a secondary account and dispersed, while TRON-side funds were routed to CEXs. ZachXBT first reported $23M, then traced a further $37M, totaling $60M across ETH/TRON/BTC.", "lesson": "Hot-wallet operations are the attack surface to audit on any payments/CEX-adjacent service — hunt for large single-key wallets, missing withdrawal limits and no cold-wallet tiering, and watch for Lazarus fingerprint patterns (swap-to-ETH, consolidation, dispersion) when triaging any wallet-drain report.", "aftermath": "MistTrack linked the wallets to addresses from the Atomic Wallet drain (also Lazarus) and a possible Coinspaid attack; Hypedrop, a customer, suspended deposits. rekt noted a $60M hack barely moved the news cycle anymore.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Ankr & Helio", "date": "2022-12-02", "chain": "BSC; compromised Ankr deployer key → malicious upgrade of the aBNBc token with a public free-mint; cascaded into Helio Money through a stale oracle.", "class": "logic", "loss": "$5M (Ankr) + ~$19M (Helio Money, via depegged aBNBc collateral)", "mechanics": "The compromised deployer funded the exploiter (0xf3a465c9...) and published/upgraded the malicious implementation; the attacker minted directly to himself and drained PancakeSwap's aBNB pools — limited to ~$5M only by available liquidity — while copycats joined the public mint. A second actor bought the depegged aBNBc cheap and, before Helio's oracle updated, collateralized it to borrow 16M HAY (~$15.5M profit; another user ~$3.5M), depegging HAY to $0.62. Binance froze ~$3M of the proceeds.", "lesson": "Deployer/owner keys of yield-bearing receipt tokens are mint authorities — upgrades must be timelocked and multisig, and audit findings on privileged roles (\"Confirmed\" risk) must be remediated, not acknowledged. Oracle staleness turns one protocol's depegged token into the next protocol's free collateral: check downstream protocols' oracle latency for exactly this contagion.", "aftermath": "CZ's summary: developer key hacked → contract upgraded to a malicious one → Binance paused withdrawals and froze ~$3M. Helio promised user compensation; underlying staked BNB was unaffected and aBNBc was reissued via snapshot.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "AscendEX", "date": "2021-12-11", "chain": "CEX hot wallet drained across three chains (Ethereum $60M", "class": "logic", "loss": "$77.7M", "mechanics": "Draining began just before 8 PM UTC on December 11 from the exchange's hot wallets on Ethereum, BSC and Polygon, with the stolen funds consolidated into attacker addresses on each chain. The BSC loot included Bemil Coin, which dumped 98% post-incident, cutting the BSC-side value by ~$6.5M. Withdrawals were suspended and full reimbursement was promised.", "lesson": "When a CEX incident spans multiple chains simultaneously, it points to a single custody/ops compromise — triage accordingly rather than hunting for per-chain bugs. Track whether a breached exchange actually makes users whole before tokens it must buy back lose value (the Bemil dump shrank the recoverable value by $6.5M).", "aftermath": "Came the same week as BitMart's ~$196M loss; AscendEX deleted incriminating tweets and promised refunds. As one anon noted, the loss exceeded their entire Series B raise.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Atlantis Loans", "date": "2023-06-07", "chain": "BSC; governance takeover of an abandoned-but-live lending protocol", "class": "governance", "loss": "~$1M, rising to $2.5M a week later", "mechanics": "An earlier attempt (April 12) failed to pass; with the project dead, proposal #52 (published June 7) passed unnoticed. The attacker (0xEADe071F..., originally Binance-funded) upgraded the token contracts and drained ~$1M from users who still had approvals to Atlantis contracts; within a week further drains brought the total to $2.5M (per PeckShield).", "lesson": "Abandoned-but-live protocols are governance-attack targets — the lesson cuts both ways: as a hunter, audit dead projects for takeoverable governance (low quorum, cheap token) and always check old approvals; as a defender, a shutdown must actually revoke powers, not just stop maintenance. Governance needs execution delays and emergency vetoes even in sunset mode.", "aftermath": "rekt listed the pattern: Tornado Cash's governance hijack, Beanstalk's $181M flash-loan governance attack (no execution delay), and Swerve's failed attempt. Numen Cyber published the full proposal-breakdown thread and the list of contracts users should revoke.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-01.md"}, {"protocol": "Badger DAO — December 2, 2021 — $120M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; front-end compromise (Cloudflare) injecting malicious approval transactions into legitimate user flows.", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The malicious approvals were first inserted about 12 days before the drain; over 500 addresses eventually approved the hacker's address. Starting 00:00:23 UTC on 2 Dec 2021, the attacker used those approvals to pull tokens, including a single drain of ~900 byvWBTC worth over $50M (the victim had granted the unlimited allowance ~6 hours earlier). Most stolen assets were vault deposit tokens, which were cashed out and the underlying BTC bridged back to the Bitcoin network. Badger paused its contracts ~2h20m after the first malicious tx (using an \"unusual\" pausability feature in `transferFrom()`), which stopped further losses.", "lesson": "Front-end trust is transitive — scope all approvals (finite amounts, revocable) and treat every `increaseAllowance`/`approve` call that appears in a dapp's signed payloads as a potential drain; hunt for suspicious approval targets in front-end/API layers and monitor DNS/Cloudflare configuration drift.", "aftermath": "#4 on the leaderboard; detected when a user flagged the suspicious `increaseAllowance()` approval in Discord. Peckshield traced the first approvals back nearly two weeks; rekt's refrain: \"infinite approval means unlimited trust\".", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-02.md"}, {"protocol": "Beanstalk — April 18, 2022 — $181M ($76M kept)", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; governance attack — flash-loaned voting power executing an emergency proposal instantly.", "class": "governance", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker pre-staged two governance proposals ~1 day earlier (a 1-day delay applied to all $BEAN governance actions): BIP-18 stealing all protocol assets and BIP-19 donating $250k to the Ukraine fund. Funded from Tornado via the Synapse bridge, they flash-loaned 350M DAI, 500M USDC, 150M USDT from Aave, 32.4M BEAN from Uniswap v2 and 11.6M LUSD from SushiSwap, added the stablecoins as Curve pool liquidity to farm voting power, then voted for and emergency-committed BIP-18 — draining 36,084,584 BEAN, 874,663,982 BEAN3CRV-f, 60,562,844 BEANLUSD-f and 0.54 UNI-V2 WETH/BEAN. After removing liquidity and repaying the flash loans, profit was 24,830 WETH (~$76M), laundered via Tornado Cash.", "lesson": "Governance weight must be un-flash-loanable (locked/vote-escrowed tokens with decay) and every governance action needs a timelock — audit `emergency*` paths for instant execution and for init/upgrade proposals that can move all treasury funds.", "aftermath": "#5 on the leaderboard (rekt records the $181M damage, not the kept profit); traced step-by-step by Peckshield, Igor Igamberdiev and Kelvin Fichter. The anon founder Publius doxxed as a group of three afterward to deflect insider-job suspicion; Omniscia noted the attack fell outside their audit scope.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-02.md"}, {"protocol": "bEarnFi — May 16, 2021 — $18M", "date": "", "chain": "Binance Smart Chain; unit/denomination mismatch between vault and strategy withdrawal logic", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loaned 7,804,239 BUSD from CREAM and deposited it into BvaultsBank, which routed to BvaultsStrategy and the Alpaca vault, minting 7,598,066 ibBUSD (farmed via Alpaca FairLaunch). Withdrawing the full 7,804,239 amount was interpreted by the strategy as 7,804,239 ibBUSD = 8,016,006 BUSD of value. Repeating the deposit/withdraw cycle kept inflating the user's credit until the pool was drained; the attacker repaid the flash loan with 7,806,580 BUSD and kept the profit.", "lesson": "Cross-contract calls must use consistent asset denominations — an interest-bearing share token (ibToken) passed through an interface that expects the underlying is a classic credit-inflation bug; hunt every bank/strategy boundary for missing exchange-rate conversion.", "aftermath": "Weekend attack (10:36 AM UTC, May 16, 2021); analysis from Peckshield and bEarn. Rekt's meta-lesson: BSC's copy-paste code boom makes forked protocols easy prey — \"time is the most valuable audit of all\".", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-02.md"}, {"protocol": "Belt Finance — May 29, 2021 — $6.3M", "date": "", "chain": "Binance Smart Chain; flash-loan manipulation of strategy share valuation (incorrect share pricing in bVenusBUSD/bEllipsisBUSD vaults).", "class": "price", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker took 8 flash loans totaling $385M BUSD from PancakeSwap; deposited 10M BUSD into the bEllipsisBUSD strategy and 187M BUSD into bVenusBUSD (both undersubscribed). They swapped 190M BUSD → 169M USDT through Ellipsis to skew the pool, withdrew more BUSD than deposited from the now-oversubscribed bVenusBUSD strategy, then swapped 169M USDT → 189M BUSD; steps 4–7 were repeated 7 times. Flash loans were repaid and the profit withdrawn.", "lesson": "Share valuation derived from spot AMM balances is manipulable — require time-weighted/oracle-resistant pricing; the deposit-cheap/withdraw-rich \"undersubscribed vs oversubscribed strategy\" loop is a recognizable BSC farm-exploit signature to hunt for in vault code.", "aftermath": "Analyzed by FrankResearcher and Mudit__Gupta; rekt stresses the similarity to the $25M Harvest Finance hack and Belt's collaboration announcement two days earlier. EPS LP/stakers also profited from the incident.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-02.md"}, {"protocol": "Bondly Finance — July 15, 2021 — $5.9M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; infinite mint via a compromised (or insider-controlled) mint address.", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker minted 373M BONDLY and dumped them 100,000 at a time, crashing the BONDLY price by ~80%. ~$4.8M DAI was sent to Tornado Cash; ~$1.1M in DAI and BONDLY remained on the associated wallet (0xc433d50dd0614c81ee314289ec82aa63710d25e8) at the time of writing.", "lesson": "Any mint function behind a single EOA key is a rug/compromise vector — hunt for uncapped mints, minter roles without multisig/timelock, and large mints immediately followed by small repeated dumps to AMMs and Tornado.", "aftermath": "Team's announcement tweet claimed the wallet \"was compromised by an unknown party\"; rekt draws a parallel to the Paid Network rug. Bondly had ~47k Twitter followers, Binance investment and celebrity partnerships (Logan Paul; Jake Paul was hit by the Chainswap exploit days earlier) — first entry on the leaderboard at #28.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-02.md"}, {"protocol": "ChainSwap — July 13, 2021 — $4.4M (second hack of the month; first was $800k)", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum + BSC — cross-chain bridge; unauthorized minting via per-token proxy Factory contracts (ETH side)", "class": "bridge", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker called the receive/mint function of each token's Factory contract, used a new address as the auth signature each tx, paid 0.005 ETH, set `to` to a chosen address and repeated — the attacker's wallet shows a long series of 0.005 ETH dust transactions, each one minting fresh tokens. On BSC, 20 tokens were hit; e.g. WilderWorld saw 40 txs × 500,000 WILD = 20M WILD, sold for ~650 WBNB (~$200k) via PancakeSwap, depleting the WILD/WBNB pool. Total ~$4.4M was accumulated, with 456 ETH (~$935k) bridged back and sold via 1inch in 5 transactions.", "lesson": "Bridge mint functions must not accept caller-supplied \"signatures\"/addresses as auth — enumerate every mint path of a bridge and check it is gated by real signatures or a fixed minter set. Cross-chain bridges are both the target and the exit route: track dust-fee mint calls and cross-chain sell-side swaps when hunting.", "aftermath": "The attacker's wallet was funded from Tornado Cash yet converted a bounty into freezable USDT, leading rekt to speculate about an insider or false-flag; ChainSwap arranged a full compensation plan but kept its tarnished reputation. Exploit breakdown credited to @cmichelio.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "CoinDCX — July 18–19, 2025 — $44.3M", "date": "", "chain": "Centralized exchange (hot-wallet drain) — ETH + Solana; no smart-contract flaw", "class": "drain", "loss": "", "mechanics": "July 16 — 1 ETH left Tornado Cash, passed through FixedFloat, Polygon, and deBridge to Solana. July 18 21:07 UTC — a single USDT test transaction; 22:09–22:14 UTC — a five-minute systematic drain of $2M, $7M, $10M, $10M, $5M, $5M and a final $5M (≈$28.3M SOL + $15.78M ETH), split across two Solana addresses, then bridged to Ethereum (0xEF0c5b9E…D2 holding ~$44.3M). CoinDCX stayed silent for ~17 hours and only disclosed at 14:48 UTC on July 19 — seven minutes after ZachXBT's public Telegram post at 14:41 UTC.", "lesson": "CEX \"all funds safe / no customer funds impacted\" claims are falsifiable on-chain — hunt untagged exchange hot wallets through counterparty and flow clustering, and treat Tornado→FixedFloat→deBridge pre-staging as a laundering signature. Multi-day rehearsal (test tx → timed batch drains) is a professional-actor signature worth logging for every CEX incident.", "aftermath": "ZachXBT forced the disclosure (mirroring WazirX's $235M silence in July 2024); some sources linked the prep to Lazarus Group; CoinDCX offered up to a 25% recovery bounty while claiming $538M in reserves absorbed the hit.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "Compound — Sep 29 – Oct 4, 2021 — ~$147M COMP (\"bank error\", $80M first wave + $68.8M second)", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum — buggy COMP distribution in the updated Comptroller (Proposal 62)", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After the Sep 29 Comptroller update, ~$80M of excess COMP was wrongly distributed. The Reservoir had not been drained for ~2 months and held 200k+ COMP (~$68M); any user could call `drip()` to refill the Comptroller, and 3.5 days later the Reservoir was drained and another $68.8M flowed into the vulnerable vault. Banteg's tally: $80M initial + $22M already claimed + $45M at risk = $147M total.", "lesson": "For every rewards/refill mechanism, ask \"who can trigger accrual, and what happens if the distribution logic is buggy?\" — permissionless drip/accrue functions turn a one-time bug into a repeatable drain. When a protocol bug is found, model the full vault lifecycle (reserves already accrued) rather than only the first loss.", "aftermath": "Founder Robert Leshner threatened doxxed recipients with the IRS (\"return 90%, keep 10% as white-hat\"), a threat that damaged Compound's reputation more than the loss; contrasted with Alchemix's polite appeal, which recovered 55% of its $6.5M mistaken payout.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "Cream Finance (1st) — August 30, 2021 — $18.8M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum — Cream lending market; flash-loan collateralized borrow plus reentrancy through the AMP token's ERC77/ERC1820 pre-transfer hook.", "class": "reentrancy", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Contract A flash-loaned 500 WETH as collateral to mint 24.17k crETH, borrowed 19.48M AMP, then reentered `borrow()` inside the AMP transfer to take another 355 ETH before the position was updated. Contract B, funded with half (9.74M) of A's AMP, liquidated part of A's loan to redeem 187 WETH back to A; A repaid the flash loan with the reentrancy ETH and kept a profit of 41 ETH + 9.74M AMP per cycle. The process was repeated across 17 transactions, netting ~6k ETH (~$18.8M) plus 418.3M AMP tokens.", "lesson": "When a lending protocol lists a NEW collateral token, the token contract itself is part of the attack surface — audit ERC777/ERC77-style pre/post transfer hooks for reentrancy before listing. Always require `nonReentrant` on borrow/lend (as @muditgupta noted, that alone would have stopped it).", "aftermath": "Cream's first direct attack (earlier involvement was the Alpha Finance incident); stolen ETH remained parked at 0xce1f4b4f…6ede at time of writing; analysis credited to PeckShield.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "BurgerSwap — May 28, 2021 — $7.2M", "date": "", "chain": "BSC — flash-swap + attacker-created fake token pair + reentrancy in the AMM's swap before reserves update.", "class": "reentrancy", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flash-swap 6k WBNB from PancakeSwap, swap most to 92k BURGER on BurgerSwap, then create a pair of 100 fake (non-standard BEP-20) tokens + 45k BURGER. Swap the 100 fake tokens for 4.4k WBNB through the new pair, then reenter and swap the 45k BURGER for another 4.4k WBNB before reserves updated — 8.8k WBNB total — swap back 493 WBNB to 108.7k BURGER and repay the flash swap. Across ~14 \"servings\": 4.4k WBNB ($1.6M), 1.4M USDT ($1.4M), 432k BURGER ($3.2M), 142k xBURGER ($1M), 95k ROCKS and more.", "lesson": "When auditing a forked AMM, diff it against the original — a deleted invariant check (`x*y>=k`) or missing reentrancy guard is the highest-value finding. Also check that pairs accept non-standard tokens whose accounting can be manipulated during the same block.", "aftermath": "Fourth BSC copycat hack of the week (~3am UTC+8), broken down publicly by @FrankResearcher; BurgerSwap promised a detailed compensation plan; rekt's piece repeatedly asks whether the missing check was negligence or something \"seedier\".", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "Conic Finance — July 21, 2023 — $4.2M (two attacks: $3.3M ETH omnipool + ~$934k crvUSD omnipool)", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum — (1st) read-only reentrancy price manipulation via CurveLPOracleV2; (2nd) sandwich-style exchange against loosely bounded imbalanced Curve pools.", "class": "", "loss": "", "mechanics": "", "lesson": "Read-only reentrancy in price oracles is now a standard vault kill-shot — audit every oracle/view contract the vault reads, including NEW versions deployed after the last audit, and verify a reentrancy guard actually triggers for the addresses the pool really uses (ETH vs WETH assumptions silently break guards). For omnipools: tighten imbalance bounds so single-pool price skew can't be converted into a withdraw surplus.", "aftermath": "Conic was one of the most anticipated Curve-Wars projects (touted as the next CVX/Yearn); CNC fell from ~$6 to $1.72 and TVL to under a third of its pre-hack level; the first frontrunner address had been labeled \"LadyPepe Token Exploiter\" by BlockSec.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "Cover Protocol — December 28, 2020 — $9.4M taken, $3.2M recovered (~$6.2M net); COVER supply inflated 48 quadrillion %", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum — infinite mint via the Blacksmith shield-mining reward accounting (storage/memory bug) after a new Balancer pool was added.", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Dec 28 04:09 UTC, the team multisig added a new Balancer pool to Blacksmith for the new coverage expirations. At 08:08 an attacker deposited 1,326,880 BPT and withdrew 1,326,878.99 BPT while claiming ~703.64 COVER. Grap Finance repeated the trick at the extreme: deposit 15,255.55 BPT, withdraw all but 1 wei, wait for the last other user to withdraw, redeposit, then claim — the reward math on the 1-wei balance minted the 40.8-quintillion COVER. Grap sold via 1inch for 4,350 ETH (~$4.4M) and returned it to the team as a \"white hat\"; six addresses total exploited the loophole before it was closed.", "lesson": "Reward-claim arithmetic must be fuzz-tested at extreme balances — a single user holding ~all pool liquidity (1 wei dust) is the classic trigger for minting bugs. When a pool/reward contract is added by multisig, re-audit the reward rebasing it introduces.", "aftermath": "COVER fell 40x in 4 hours while Grap's GRAP token pumped 40x (24h volume $236 → $5.46M); the protocol's fourth token iteration (SAFE → SAFE2 → COVER → new snapshot token) was planned to refund users; rekt floated the \"insider job gone wrong\" theory after MXC listed GRAP the same day.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "Cream Finance (2nd) — October 27, 2021 — ~$130M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum — flash-loan leverage loop across two addresses", "class": "Price", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Address A flash-loaned 500M DAI from MakerDAO → Curve yPool yDAI → mint yUSD → deposit into Yearn's yUSD strategy, then used the yUSDVault shares as CREAM collateral to mint ~$500M crYUSD. Address B flash-loaned $2B ETH from AAVE as collateral to borrow ~$500M yUSD, deposited it for more crYUSD; A and B looped deposit/borrow with B passing ~$500M yUSDVault to A each round until A held ~$1.5B crYUSD + $500M yUSDVault. Redeeming ~$500M yUSDVault shrank the vault's totalSupply to $8M; depositing ~$8M yUSD doubled the share price, so CREAM valued A's collateral at $3B — $2B of ETH repaid B's AAVE loan, ~$500M yUSD repaid A's DAI loan, and the remaining ~$1B of crYUSD borrowing capacity drained CREAM's ~$130M lending assets.", "lesson": "Never price a yield-bearing vault share as live balance ÷ totalSupply — deposit/redeem is a two-way manipulation primitive that can double or halve the ratio in one block; use immutable or time-averaged oracle prices for LP/vault-share collateral, or disallow them entirely in lending markets.", "aftermath": "The exploit tx input data carried the message \"gÃTµ Baave lucky, iron bank lucky, cream not. ydev : incest bad, dont do\" — Mudit Gupta read it as experienced DeFi devs with a grudge in the Yearn ecosystem; Cream's deployer begged \"you win. we're rekt… 10% bounty\"; the attacker offloaded via renBridge and $40M+ of single-sided CRETH2 liquidity.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "Crypto.com — January 17, 2022 — $33.7M (~4.8k ETH ≈ $15M + 444 BTC ≈ $18.7M)", "date": "", "chain": "Centralized exchange — user-wallet drain bypassing 2FA and the withdrawal whitelist (ETH side) plus a large BTC hot-wallet sweep; no contract flaw.", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Jan 17 04:44 UTC Crypto.com tweeted \"All funds are safe\"; CertiK counted ~4,836 ETH drained from ~282 user wallets into Tornado Cash. ErgoBTC's follow-up found the payout wallet bc1q7cyrf…pemf making an abnormally large withdrawal that was consolidated into 67.75 BTC outputs, then serialized 24–25 BTC deposits into a BTC tumbler used by Lazarus Group and Darkside ransomware; 173 BTC sat unmixed at bc1qk8wlw…jsq. Together the two legs total ~$33.7M, and Crypto.com never acknowledged a loss at the time.", "lesson": "CEX hot/payout wallets can be clustered and monitored on-chain, so \"all funds safe\" claims are testable — track tumbler deposit patterns (24–25 BTC chunks) as a laundering fingerprint. 2FA-bypassing wallet drains are an infrastructure/API compromise signature, distinct from contract exploits; don't file them as smart-contract findings.", "aftermath": "CEO Kris Marszalek kept posting \"all funds safe\" while users reported losses; rekt's article was updated after ErgoBTC's investigation doubled the figure; a Deloitte SOC2 audit didn't stop it — the exchange quietly refunded affected users later.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "Curve Finance (frontend) — August 9, 2022 — ~$575k (340 ETH)", "date": "", "chain": "Web2 DNS hijack of curve.fi — mirrored malicious frontend harvesting token approvals; on-chain leg drained via FixedFloat", "class": "hack", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Users who approved on the fake UI had ~340 ETH (~$575k) taken by 0x50f9202e…f331. The attacker deposited 292 ETH to FixedFloat (112 ETH frozen), 20 ETH to Binance, and 27.7 ETH to Tornado Cash. curve.exchange (the alternate UI) was unaffected and the team directed users there while the mirrored site was taken down, though some nameservers stayed stale.", "lesson": "Treat the frontend as part of the target: audit DNS/registrar configs, nameserver change controls, and whether the dapp is served via IPFS/ENS. Approval-harvesting via hijacked UIs converts years of smart-contract hardening into a $575k siphon, so check for malicious approval contracts and stale nameservers in any Web3 recon.", "aftermath": "", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "dYdX v3 — November 17, 2023 — $9M from the v3 insurance fund", "date": "", "chain": "Perpetuals market manipulation — one actor built outsized YFI long open interest on dYdX", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The actor previously attempted the same play on the SUSHI market two weeks earlier. After the YFI spot crash (~$200M market cap wiped), dYdX's liquidations on the YFI market were filled with $9M from the insurance fund (leaving $13.5M); no user funds were affected. dYdX added extra margin requirements for lower-liquidity tokens in response.", "lesson": "Watch open-interest concentration in low-liquidity perp markets — one actor holding most of a market's OI is a manipulation red flag, and the liquidation insurance fund is the real payout target. For perp venues, the hunting checklist is: per-market margin requirements, OI caps, and whether insurance-fund accounting can be forced into covering a coordinated spot dump.", "aftermath": "Founder Antonio Juliano characterized it as an intentional, well-capitalized market manipulation targeting the insurance pool; the team refused to negotiate with the exploiter and offered bounties for identifying them — critics flagged the unilateral risk-parameter change as skirting decentralized decision-making.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-03.md"}, {"protocol": "EasyFi", "date": "2021-04-20", "chain": "Polygon — compromised founder machine / leaked admin mnemonic keys (no contract vulnerability)", "class": "logic", "loss": "~$59M ($6M stables + ~$53M EASY)", "mechanics": "the hacker did not touch the code — they took the mnemonic controlling EasyFi's admin key on Polygon. With that one key they drained ~$6M in stablecoins plus ~2.98M EASY (~$53M, roughly 30% of total supply) via a plain `transfer` call that had no timelock. The token only fell ~20% because the thief could not easily dump: EASY's 24h volume was only ~$10M and the attacker now holds ~30% of supply. The protocol effectively became centralized in a criminal's hands overnight.", "lesson": "(1) Audit admin-key topology as a finding in itself: any single hot key that can drain treasury liquidity with no timelock is a Critical waiting to happen — require multisig + hardware wallet + timelock for anything holding other people's funds. (2) \"No code bug\" hacks still kill protocols: check OPSEC posture (key custody, machine isolation) in pre-audit recon.", "aftermath": "founder AnkittGaur said the compromised machine was used \"solely for official transfers\"; team published an open letter offering the hacker a $1M \"clean payout\" bounty; rekt noted the OPSEC was \"poor even just for an individual\". First big Layer-2 hack.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-04.md"}, {"protocol": "Euler Finance", "date": "2023-03-14", "chain": "Ethereum — flash-loan + leveraged self-liquidation via `donateToReserves` missing a health check", "class": "logic", "loss": "~$197M (86k ETH derivatives, 849 WBTC, 34M USDC, 8.9M DAI)", "mechanics": "attacker used two contracts: one built a huge, underwater leveraged position with flash-loaned funds, then donated eTokens to reserves, making the position unliquidatable while leaving the collateral claim inflated; the second contract acted as liquidator, seizing the inflated eToken collateral at a discount and withdrawing the underlying. Losses totaled ~$197M (stETH/rETH ~$134.6M, 849 WBTC ~$18.6M, 34M USDC, 8.9M DAI); Euler TVL fell $264M → $10M. Some funds were routed toward Tornado Cash via a pass-through address.", "lesson": "(1) Every state-changing path that moves collateral or debt — even a \"donation\" — must re-run the account-health invariant; donative/exempt paths are prime hunting ground in lending protocols. (2) Trace cross-protocol blast radius: integrated vaults (Angle $17M, Balancer $11.9M, Temple $5M) all lost through one missed health check.", "aftermath": "Peckshield raised the alarm; auditor Omniscia (1 of 6 auditors) published the post-mortem and audit-insurance protocol Sherlock paid a $4.5M claim for missing the bug; Euler reached out to the attacker on-chain via tx input data but funds had already started moving to Tornado.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-04.md"}, {"protocol": "Fei Protocol", "date": "2021-04-07", "chain": "Ethereum — economic-mechanism failure: \"direct incentives\" sell-penalty model + pre-swap launch mechanics collapsed the peg (no attacker)", "class": "logic", "loss": "peg break / TRIBE -30% (design failure, not an exploit)", "mechanics": "the Token Genesis Event offered up-to-50% mint discounts but the discount was voided once contributions exceeded $250M — they raised $1.3B in ETH, which went irreversibly into Protocol Controlled Value; ~17k addresses participated. PCV was used to build a Uniswap FEI/ETH pool that peaked at $2.6B in liquidity. The peg broke quickly under pre-swap selling pressure; because ~98% of TRIBE liquidity sat in the TRIBE-FEI pair, holders selling TRIBE for ETH were forced through the penalty or paid heavy slippage. Rewards shutdown followed the incentive-calculation bug despite Consensys Diligence + OpenZeppelin audits.", "lesson": "(1) Economic/mechanism audits must simulate both sides of every trade — penalties that burn on below-peg transfers tax the counterparty too and can kill demand exactly when the peg needs it most. (2) Check conditional launch discounts and pre-swap/auto-convert options: a threshold-crossing that retroactively voids a promised discount converts hype into a de facto rug.", "aftermath": "backed by a16z, Framework and Coinbase yet widely called \"an overfunded disaster\"; Julien Bouteloup (rekt founder) posted a public \"blacklist\" of FEI holders to exclude from future airdrops; Robert Leshner publicly bid \"$0.70 USDC\" for unlimited FEI.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-04.md"}, {"protocol": "Force DAO", "date": "2021-04-04", "chain": "Ethereum — whitehat \"rescue\" mint via unchecked `transferFrom` return value in the FORCE/xFORCE vault", "class": "logic", "loss": "4,112 FORCE drained & returned; -90% price crash", "mechanics": "a whitehat found the bug and minted 347,432,986 xFORCE, withdrew 4,112 FORCE with it and sold part via 1inch, then alerted the team's Discord at 08:50 UTC (\"removed all of them for the safety of the token\"). The community dumped before the warning landed — FORCE fell 90% in minutes — and copycat hackers with \"poor OPSEC\" then arrived to feed on the leftovers. The whitehat ultimately returned 14,833 FORCE, keeping Force DAO off the leaderboard.", "lesson": "(1) Never trust ERC20 return values — legacy tokens like MiniMe return false instead of reverting; wrap every transfer/transferFrom in SafeERC20 and treat \"false\" as failure, especially inside mint/burn vaults. (2) The return-value bug class repeats across years (BAT-style); when reviewing vaults, check the exact token standard used, not just the function names.", "aftermath": "samczsun's one-liner — \"lmao the xforce vault didn't check the return value on transferFrom\"; rekt dubbed it \"the smallest airdrop followed by the easiest hack\"; whitehat returned the money and the team narrowly avoided the leaderboard.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-04.md"}, {"protocol": "Fortress Protocol", "date": "2022-05-09", "chain": "BSC — governance takeover (malicious proposal) + publicly callable price oracle manipulation", "class": "oracle", "loss": "$3M", "mechanics": "the attacker funded the attack with ETH sourced from Tornado Cash on mainnet, swapped it for large FTS holdings to both reach governance quorum and back collateral. After the proposal passed, they manipulated the FTS oracle price through the public submit() and deposited just 100 FTS (~$4.5 pre-hack) as collateral against the 0.7 factor — enough to drain every asset on the platform (~$3M). Afterwards they deposited 1,048 ETH (~$2.6M) and 400k DAI into Tornado Cash.", "lesson": "(1) A price feed with any externally callable setter — public submit(), or one controlled by a single EOA — inside a lending platform is an instant full-drain primitive; require multi-source/Chainlink-style validation and access control. (2) Monitor governance parameters: an absurd collateral factor passing quorum and living 3 days unchallenged is a governance alerting failure — set time-locks plus parameter sanity bounds, and alert on suspicious proposals.", "aftermath": "attack decoded by BlockSec and Certik; Fortress is the lending arm of JetFuel Finance; both auditors (Hash0x, EtherAuthority) missed the oracle bug; the site listed ChainLink among \"collaboraters\" while actually using a manipulable self-reported feed.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-04.md"}, {"protocol": "Furucombo", "date": "2021-03-01", "chain": "Ethereum — \"evil contract\" exploit: nested delegatecall chain + storage-slot poisoning", "class": "logic", "loss": "~$14M", "mechanics": "first the attacker used a malicious contract to make the FC proxy delegatecall Aave V2's proxy `initialize()`, writing the attacker's implementation address into the FC proxy's slot. Then fund-stealing txs were sent to the FC proxy with the Aave LendingPool proxy as \"handler\": the nested delegatecalls resolved to the hacker's code, which ran in the FC proxy's context and transferred away every token users had approved (~$14M across many wallets: 3.9k stETH, 2.4M USDC, 649k USDT, 257k DAI, aTokens/cTokens, 142M BAO, 38.6k PERP and more). Cream Finance's treasury was also drained as a \"borrower\".", "lesson": "(1) When a proxy delegatecalls a proxy, audit every storage slot the callee reads — an implementation-address slot that shares layout with the caller's storage lets an attacker rewrite the code that runs. Restrict handler functions/parameters and don't let handlers self-modify implementation slots. (2) User-side: infinite approvals turn any single compromised router contract into full-wallet theft — scoped approvals + periodic revocation (Debank/Etherscan revoke tools) is a hunting-relevant hygiene check for integrated apps.", "aftermath": "Kurt Barry traced the nested delegatecalls via ethtx.info; rekt interviewed victim Limzero, who repaid loans at health factor 1.15 and revoked all permissions 90 minutes after the theft; auditor Haechi later disputed scope, noting the exploited Aave-via-proxy scenario was never in their audit scope.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-04.md"}, {"protocol": "Harvest Finance — Oct 26, 2020 — ~$33.8M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; flash-loan-driven price manipulation of a Curve pool feeding a yield-vault deposit/withdraw oracle.", "class": "price", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With a $50M USDT flash loan the attacker repeatedly swapped 11.4M USDC→USDT on Curve Y to push USDT up, deposited 60.6M USDT into the fUSDT vault, swapped back to depress the price, then withdrew 61.1M USDT (~$0.5M profit per loop) because shares redeemed against the manipulated index. ~4 loops fit per 10M-gas block; the attacker ran 32 loops in ~7 minutes, then converted to renBTC and exited via Tornado Cash. Drains stopped at $25M of an available $400M; the pie split roughly $24M hacker / $6M Uniswap LPs / $2.5M Harvest devs / $1M Curve LPs.", "lesson": "Any vault that prices deposits/withdrawals off a spot AMM it itself touches is flash-loan-arbitrageable — check every \"price index\" path for swap-induced pump/dump asymmetry. Tighten slippage AND raise the arbitrage/deviation tolerance on share accounting, not just one.", "aftermath": "REKT framed it as a \"skilled farmer\", and even published a whistleblower note suggesting the 3% slippage looked planted. Harvest (audited by PeckShield/Haechi/CertiK 3 weeks earlier) called it an \"arbitrage economic attack\"; attacker refunded ~$2.48M to the deployer, redistributed pro-rata.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "HECO Bridge / HTX — Nov 22, 2023 — ~$99M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum (HECO Chain's ETH bridge) + HTX hot wallets; compromised operator/hot-wallet private keys.", "class": "bridge", "loss": "", "mechanics": "From 09:59 UTC the compromised operator (0x3d655889…) withdrew bridge funds (0xa929022c…) to the attacker 0xfc146d1c…, forwarding to an accumulation wallet: 42M USDT, >10k ETH ($19M), 489 HBTC ($18.8M), 347M SHIB ($2.8M), plus UNI/USDC/LINK/TUSD → $86.6M per PeckShield. At 10:41 UTC two compromised HTX hot wallets sent 1,240 ETH, 7.3M USDT, 1.78M USDC, 62.2K LINK ($12.5M) to two attacker addresses. Total ≈$99M, 12 days after Poloniex lost $126M and weeks after HTX's $8M drain (recovered).", "lesson": "When hunting CEX/bridge programs, trace operator and hot-wallet keys as an attack surface: one reused credential across affiliated entities lets an attacker \"rinse and repeat\" after the first breach. Follow post-breach fund-flows to accumulation wallets to link multi-incident campaigns.", "aftermath": "REKT highlighted all three Sun-linked hacks (Poloniex, HTX, HECO) as suspected Lazarus Group work with the same hallmarks — $233M lost across 4 incidents in 3 months. Sun promised full compensation of HTX hot-wallet losses within two hours.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Hedera — Mar 10, 2023 — ~$515k stolen / ~$12M ecosystem damage", "date": "", "chain": "Hedera Hashgraph; exploit of a flaw in the network's own Smart Contract Service code", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker targeted LP accounts on Pangolin, HeliSwap and other HTS DEXs; initially believed to affect only Hashport-wrapped tokens, it turned out ALL HTS tokens were at risk. The attacker burned bridged/wrapped tokens and removed LP positions; funds were bridged back to Ethereum via Hashport until it was deactivated, after which CEX cash-outs were used. Direct haul ≈$515k (Pangolin $120k, HeliSwap $2k; ~$60k HBAR + $280k HTS stables left on Hedera, $175k ETH on Ethereum), while TVL fell from $36.8M to $24.6M.", "lesson": "Chain-level smart-contract-service bugs make every DEX on the chain vulnerable at once — when auditing protocol contracts that wrap chain-native token services (HTS, Move objects, etc.), treat the platform token-service implementation itself as in-scope attack surface. A network pause is an operational kill-switch finding, not a fix.", "aftermath": "Hedera's governing council (Boeing, Dell, Ubisoft) turned off network proxies, taking mainnet fully offline — a first-of-kind response that REKT flagged as damaging DeFi legitimacy claims. HTS had been audited by FP Complete in 2021; the bug shipped in a smart-contract service update.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Hedgey Finance — Apr 19, 2024 — $44.7M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum + Arbitrum; flash-loan-funded abuse of the Token Claim contract's user-supplied parameters (not a reentrancy", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With a $1.3M USDC Balancer flash loan, the attacker crafted a createLockedCampaign call whose manipulated claimLockup parameter tricked the vulnerable contract (0xbc452fdc… on both chains) into approving USDC to the attack contract, then spent that approval to pull USDC. Run as two separate transactions per chain to dodge front-run bots. Ethereum lost $2.1M (USDC, NOBL, MASA) and Arbitrum lost $42.6M in BONUS tokens ≈ $44.7M total; funds parked at attacker-held addresses.", "lesson": "For every \"create campaign / claim / vest\" function, model the authorization flow explicitly — approval targets must be derived from validated campaign state, never from raw calldata parameters. Audit all multi-parameter token infra functions with a property test: no user input may redirect the approval recipient.", "aftermath": "Consensys Diligence had audited the Token Lockup and Vesting Plans in Jun-Jul 2023. Hedgey's team posted an onchain message to the attacker praising the \"white hat\" find (\"well done\") — a response REKT mocked as insulting to users; Cyvers first reported, Hedgey confirmed hours later.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Hope Finance — Feb 20, 2023 — $1.86M", "date": "", "chain": "Arbitrum; insider rug pull engineered through an upgradable SwapHelper parameter pointed at a fake router.", "class": "Governance", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Pre-launch, the team deployed a fake router (txn 0xf188) and pointed SwapHelper at it (txn 0xc9ee, approved by all 3 multisig owners); _swapExactTokenForTokens was set to wallet 0x957D (txn 0x1b47). When GenesisRewardPool.openTrade() borrowed USDC, the pool sent WETH to TradingHelper \"to swap\" — the fake path instead forwarded it (and 477 WETH via swapExactTokensForTokens, with _uSDC deliberately left empty) straight to 0x957D. ~$800k WETH + ~$1M USDC drained at launch; USDC was swapped to a total of 1,095 ETH, bridged to Ethereum via Celer, and deposited into Tornado Cash.", "lesson": "An audit that never reviews the multisig's upgrade path and its current onchain parameters misses the most common rug mechanism — always diff the deployed SwapHelper/router pointers and their timelock/multisig ownership against the audited snapshot. Tomb-fork \"GenesisRewardPool\" clones with pre-launch router swaps are a red flag by themselves.", "aftermath": "The team's own tweet accused a named, KYC'd dev (\"FUCKING SCAMMER… 2 MLN DOLLARS\") whose ID's address is a vacant lot per Streetview — REKT notes fake/bought KYC is trivial and the whole team's multisig approved the rug tx. Audits by Cognitos (passed, two unrelated 'major' flags) and AuditRateTech (report later deleted).", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "HTX (Huobi) — Sep 25, 2023 — $7.9M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; CEX hot-wallet drain (details scant", "class": "drain", "loss": "", "mechanics": "At 10:00 UTC Sunday, 4,999 ETH (~$7.9M) was drained from an HTX hot wallet; HTX moved the remaining 633 ETH (~$1M) to safety over two hours later. Attacker addresses: 0xdb1D7446… and 0x799982b7… — announced by Justin Sun less than 12 hours after Mixin Network admitted a $200M loss.", "lesson": "CEX hunts should track hot-wallet anomaly patterns (partial drains, near-round amounts like 4,999 ETH, slow remaining-fund rescue) as insider/compromise indicators — the same signature shows up in the HECO/HTX Nov 2023 hacks. Cross-platform incident clustering (Mixin, HTX, JPEX within days) is campaign telemetry, not coincidence.", "aftermath": "Sun called the loss \"two weeks' revenue\" against \"$3B held\", offered the hacker an onchain 5% bounty plus a job (7-day deadline, then law enforcement), and claimed the hacker was \"identified\" without proof — the rebrand to \"HTX\" drew immediate FTX comparisons from CZ.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Hundred Finance — Apr 15, 2023 — $7.4M", "date": "", "chain": "Optimism; flash-loan exchange-rate donation attack on an EMPTY", "class": "rounding", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loaned 500 WBTC from Aave, redeemed 0.3 staked WBTC, then with a second contract minted 200 hWBTC with 4 BTC, redeemed the 4 BTC back keeping a dust amount of hWBTC, and donated 200 WBTC to inflate the exchange rate so 2 wei of hWBTC controlled the pool. With ~2 remaining hWBTC the contract borrowed 1,021.91 ETH, repaid old debt with 1 hWBTC, and withdrew its 500.3 WBTC. Funds bridged to Ethereum (~$5.4M parked there, $0.9M left on OP); HND token fell ~50% intraday.", "lesson": "In Compound-style forks, EVERY cToken market — even an empty, UI-unused one — is an attack surface; donation-inflation works whenever exchangeRate reads live token balance and redeem/borrow rounding is sloppy. Inventory all deployed markets and fork-constant/rounding diffs against the original Compound math, not just the ones the UI exposes.", "aftermath": "Hundred's 3rd REKT entry (after the Meter stray hit $3.3M and the xDAI/Agave $6.2M reentrancy) — total $16.9M; the team called it \"a general flaw in the code, not specific to Hundred\" and offered $500k for info after onchain negotiation failed. Audited by WhiteHatDAO in Feb 2022; analysis by Von Fange/Peckshield/Beosin/Numen.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Humanity Protocol — Jun 8, 2026 — 447M $H (team-confirmed; ~$36.4M realized)", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum + BSC; private-key leakage via DPRK-style spear-phishing of a director's laptop", "class": "bridge", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Director Chong Yee Wai opened a fake Bithumb zip on Jun 5 (hncagent.exe loader, Hancom-signed), giving the attacker RDP access; neither Sophos nor Defender flagged it. On Jun 8 the attacker drained 6,045,060 $H straight from the admin hot wallet, then with 3/6 ETH Safe keys transferred ProxyAdmin ownership, upgraded the bridge to a malicious implementation and swept 141,182,632 $H in one tx. On BSC the same playbook (3/5 keys) activated an unlimited mint: team confirmed 3×100M H mints; QuillAudits documented ~1.5B H total across undisclosed mints. ~$36.4M realized through Uniswap DEX-only dumping (never a CEX); $H fell ~90%; bridges halted, BSC contract still attacker-controlled.", "lesson": "For any audited protocol, verify the human layer behind the \"multisig\" — distinct signers per key, no key material ever co-located on one device, rotation history — and check EVERY ProxyAdmin for a timelock plus AdminChanged event monitoring; an upgrade path without a delay window converts one laptop compromise into instant protocol ownership. DPRK intrusions now start from spear-phished workstations: treat signer laptops as part of the attack surface.", "aftermath": "PeckShield called it \"a staged performance… nothing less than a Rug Pull\" (dev, MM-financier and MM keys on the same machine), while ZachXBT demanded disclosure of HK market-maker agreements then walked back to \"MM activity and the compromise were independent\". Specter flagged the drain first; recovery portal re-pegs H 1:1.048 against a snapshot taken 7 minutes before the first attack tx; 88% of the 9M registered Human IDs had never completed biometric verification.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Hyperbridge — Apr 13, 2026 — ~$2.5M (revised; ~$806k direct)", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; forged MMR (Merkle Mountain Range) proof accepted by the bridge's Solidity verifier — proof-verification logic flaw", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker submitted a ChangeAssetAdmin request for the bridged DOT token with leafCount:1, leaf_index:1, proof:[root], leafCount:1 — the computed root equalled the expected root without ever using the attacker's leaf hash. Admin of the DOT token contract (0x8d010bf9…) passed to 0xc513e4f5…, which minted 1B DOT (also ~999B ARGN, plus MANTA/CERE targets) and dumped into shallow pools for 108.2 ETH ≈ $237k. A separate earlier actor used the same door via TokenGateway for 245 ETH (~$570k, split 16.39 ETH × 15 wallets → Tornado Cash). Hyperbridge later revised total losses to $2.5M including incentive pools on ETH/Base/BNB/Arbitrum and users who swapped into artificially cheap DOT.", "lesson": "Proof-verification libraries are where \"trustless bridges\" actually live or die — fuzz every MMR/Merkle verifier for boundary inputs (leaf_index == leafCount, empty proof, proof == root) and enforce proof-to-request binding to kill replay. When auditors explicitly recommend \"specialist review of your custom Solidity libraries\", the next exploit candidate is exactly that library.", "aftermath": "Twelve days earlier Hyperbridge had posted an April Fools joke that Lazarus drained $37M, linking \"Why Hyperbridge Can't Be Hacked\" (deleted after the real hack). SR Labs' June 2024 audit had flagged the custom libraries and named \"exploit a bug in validation scheme\" as a high-value threat; a January 2025 advisory on the ismp-grandpa crate (verifier accepting invalid inputs) was the same bug class. The day after, Polkadot SDK's pallet_mmr disclosed the same-class bug (could have slashed ~446M DOT via forged BEEFY reports).", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Hypr Network — Dec 13, 2023 — 2.57M HYPR (~$220k)", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; L2 bridge exploit via reinitialization of the OP Stack bridge contract — a known OP monorepo bug shipped because Hypr deployed an unreleased `develop` branch.", "class": "bridge", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Two days after launch, the attacker called the vulnerable reinitialization path on the Hypr bridge and finalized a withdrawal of 2.57M HYPR tokens it didn't legitimately own. Proceeds were dumped for 97 ETH (~$220k), crashing HYPR ~40% intraday; only two addresses had bridged funds at that point, and the team initially told users the bridge was down \"for more tests\" before admitting the exploit. Attacker funded via FixedFloat; ETH sat at 0x5b8d… at writing.", "lesson": "Never ship a fork of a production `develop`/main branch — pin to governance-approved releases only, and check the upstream repo's security advisories/patches against your deployed bytecode at deploy time. Track \"legacy slot clearing\" and initializer-guard patterns in every upgradeable bridge you audit: a reinitialization path that resets guards is a full bypass candidate.", "aftermath": "First code-exploit bridge hack since the BNB Bridge (excluding Shibarium's self-rekt). OP Labs admitted the release/comms process gap and the Optimism Foundation said production builders should only use governance-approved releases; BlockSec published the technical summary and Hypr posted a full post-mortem.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Indexed Finance — Oct 14, 2021 — $16M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; flash-loan-driven manipulation of the index pool's value oracle", "class": "price", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loaned the pool's other assets and used them to buy UNI, whose weight decrease hadn't yet taken effect, collapsing the extrapolated pool value (trades were capped at 1/2 of balance out / 1/3 of balance in, so this was done gradually). After calling updateMinimumBalance with the gamed value, the controller valued the DEFI5 pool at just 29,851 SUSHI (~$300k) despite it holding over $100M of assets. Depositing the now heavily over-weighted SUSHI then minted a massively inflated number of DEFI5/CC10 index tokens, which were cashed out for the pool's real assets — $16M across DEFI5, CC10 and (via its 37.05% exposure) the FFF pool; funds remained parked at the attacker address at writing.", "lesson": "In Balancer-style pools, audit EVERY valuation path that trusts an on-chain token balance or weight state mid-reweight — flash loans can move balances faster than weight updates land, and any single-token-extrapolation formula is a manipulation target. Bound minted index tokens by cross-checking the pool valuation against a second, manipulation-resistant source (time-weighted weights, minimum balances, NAV from multiple tokens).", "aftermath": "The team publicly believed they knew the attacker: a Discord user 'UmbralUpsilon' who, a month before, had asked detailed questions about the oracle parameters under the cover of \"building an arbitrage bot\" — and who'd requested Kovan testnet ETH via Gitter under a dead Twitter account. Dr Laurence Day offered a 10% white-hat bounty and warned of law enforcement; the exploit had sat undiscovered for ten months in production.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Indodax — Sep 10-11, 2024 — ~$25.2M", "date": "", "chain": "Multi-chain CEX breach (Ethereum", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Cyvers flagged suspicious outflows from Indodax wallets across networks; over 150 transactions drained 6.14M USDT, 1,047 ETH ($2.48M), 25 BTC ($1.41M), 2.2M MATIC ($849k), 1.4M ARB ($749.6k), 2M ENA ($465k) and more ≈ $25.22M total. Stolen funds consolidated at ETH ($12.37M), Polygon ($6.8M), Tron ($2.55M), BTC ($1.4M), Optimism ($900k) addresses while the exchange kept >$400M in other wallets. Indodax called it \"complete maintenance\"; co-founder William Sutanto claimed funds \"100% safe\".", "lesson": "When auditing a CEX program scope, model the withdrawal path end-to-end (auth → signing → hot-wallet custody → change-address handling): attackers who control the withdrawal system don't need the keys. Monitor for the Lazarus signature — multi-chain parallel draining, change addresses returning dust to the source, speed and precision — as an early detection heuristic.", "aftermath": "Cyvers' Yosi Hammer said the speed/complexity/pattern \"highly resembles\" Lazarus Group though unconfirmed. REKT's writeup arrived while the exchange was still in \"maintenance\" with the post-mortem pending; Arkham showed wallets still holding $400M+.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Infini — Feb 23-24, 2025 — $49.5M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; rogue-privilege drain — a developer (0xc49b5e5b…) retained a special withdraw role (0x8e0b per QuillAudits) on Infini's vault and executed it after 114 days of waiting; no flash loans", "class": "Access-control", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker funded a fresh wallet with 1 ETH from Tornado Cash, deployed a contract disguised as part of Infini's vault system with admin privileges, then in two transactions withdrew 11.5M and 38M USDC from MEV Capital's Usual USDC vault on Morpho. USDC was swapped to DAI via Maker's Sky to dodge blacklisting, then DAI to 17,696 ETH at ~$2,798, parked at 0xfcc8Ad91… LookOnChain spotted the anomaly first (\"a newly created wallet spent 49.5M $DAI to buy 17,696 $ETH\").", "lesson": "Audit the COMPLETE role graph of every vault — who holds each privileged role today, whether any role outlives its need, and whether revoke/renounce exists and was called. Unverified contracts + lingering single-key withdraw roles is the combination to flag first; also treat \"authority handover\" commits in git history as a hunting lead.", "aftermath": "Christian (Infini founder) confirmed his personal key wasn't leaked and took personal responsibility, offering the attacker 20% + no legal action; 70% of the funds reportedly belonged to big investors he knew. The hack landed right after Bybit's $1.43B loss; QuillAudits noted the attacker used minimal obfuscation, so the trail may still be traceable.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Inverse Finance — Apr 2, 2022 — ~$15.6M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; oracle manipulation — the INV price feed was a SushiSwap TWAP on a thinly-traded pair (short sample window)", "class": "oracle", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker withdrew 901 ETH from Tornado Cash, dusted 241 clean addresses with 1.5 ETH via Disperse, deployed 5 contracts (one real), and swapped 500 ETH → 1.7k INV through SushiSwap's INV-WETH pair, moving the price ~50x on low liquidity. Simultaneously spamming the exploit transaction to land in the next block before arbitrage bots could correct the price, they deposited the 1.7k INV (fair value $644k) as collateral and borrowed $15.6M — 1,588 ETH, 94 WBTC, 4M DOLA, 39.3 YFI — and never repaid.", "lesson": "For lending protocols, map every collateral's oracle to its liquidity depth: TWAPs over thin pairs with short windows are the #1 manipulation target, and the fix is a deeper-liquidity/Chainlink-style feed, not a longer window alone. Watch for the MEV-aware signatures (Disperse-funded decoy addresses, cross-block price pinning, front-runner protection) as evidence of a pro attacker targeting your oracles next.", "aftermath": "Flashbot expert bertcmiller called it \"one of the most MEV aware hacks I've seen\" — the attacker held the oracle price across multiple blocks and blocked arb bots. Inverse announced a move to a Chainlink INV feed once liquidity requirements were met.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Inverse Finance (2) — Jun 16, 2022 — $1.2M attacker / $5.8M total", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; second oracle manipulation in two months", "class": "oracle", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Flash loan of 27,000 WBTC from Aave → deposit 225 WBTC to crv3crypto (5,375 crv3crypto) → 4,906 yvCurve-3Crypto into Inverse as collateral → swap 26,775 WBTC to 75,403,376 USDT to pump the pool's balance-based price → borrow 10,133,949 DOLA (far above the true collateral value) → reverse-swap USDT back to 26,626 WBTC, swap DOLA to 3Crv, redeem 10.1M USDT, buy 451 WBTC, repay the loan. ~1,000 ETH of profit went to Tornado Cash; the DOLA market was left with $10.63M bad debt (over half the protocol's $20M TVL).", "lesson": "Never price a yield-bearing LP token from the raw balances of its underlying pool — balance-based LP pricing is exactly the \"second manipulation\" Inverse suffered; require external or TWAP-based LP valuation. After any oracle incident, sweep EVERY other market in the same protocol for the same pricing pattern, since attackers return to the unfixed siblings.", "aftermath": "Peckshield disclosed the vuln publicly while funds were still at risk and deleted the tweet after being reprimanded. Peckshield later suggested the malicious tx may have been sniped by a front-running bot; Inverse claimed \"no user funds were taken or at risk\" while the attacker address was Tornado-funded two minutes before the exploit.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Ionic Money — Feb 4, 2025 — ~$6.9M", "date": "", "chain": "Mode Network (Ethereum L2); fake-collateral listing via social engineering — attackers impersonated Lombard Finance team members to get a counterfeit LBTC token approved as collateral", "class": "oracle", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Starting from 0.01 ETH, the attacker minted 250 LBTC from the counterfeit contract, supplied it to Ionic and borrowed MBTC, uniBTC, wrsETH, WETH, STONE and everything else across the protocol's vaults. 1,204 ETH (~$3.2M) was bridged to Ethereum and pushed into Tornado Cash; the attacker still holds 38.34 MBTC of loans (~$3.7M) on Mode. Blast radius extended to Ironclad and Layerbank, which held the borrowed MBTC positions.", "lesson": "Collateral-listing workflows need machine-checkable identity: verify the token's deployer/mint authority, compare bytecode with the canonical issuer, and require the issuer's known keys to sign the listing request — social engineering only works where a human decides \"looks legit\". Always simulate worst-case: if this token's mint is unlimited and held by the lister, the market is a free printer.", "aftermath": "ZachXBT noted Ionic is just Midas (twice-hacked in 2023: $660k + $600k) wearing a carnival mask; the team called it \"quite a sophisticated social engineering exploit\" while a Discord member asked what social engineering is. Lombard Finance confirmed \"an unofficial LBTC has been deployed as collateral on Ionic Money on Mode.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "IoTeX (ioTube bridge) — Feb 21, 2026 — $4.4M drained + 821M CIOTX minted", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum bridge (ioTube); single-EOA owner key of TransferValidatorWithPayload compromised → malicious upgrade() → full bridge administration. No code exploit.", "class": "signature", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker called upgrade() on TransferValidatorWithPayload (0xE7eBA1CE…) to install a validation-free implementation, then transferred ownership of TokenSafe and MinterPool. In 189 transactions on a Saturday morning: ~$4.4M of real reserves drained (1.36k USDC, 1.14k USDT, 635 WETH, 6.12 WBTC, 20,159 DAI, 8.72 PAXG, 13.85M IOTX, 45,825 BUSD, 2,835 UNI) and 821M CIOTX + 9.3M CCS minted across 7-10 txs (IoTeX's own 410M figure contradicted on-chain). Assets swapped to ~2,183 ETH, bridged via THORChain to 66.77 BTC parked in four fresh wallets, untouched. IOTX fell 22%; Upbit suspended deposits; chain patched to blacklist 29 attacker addresses.", "lesson": "The bridge upgrade path IS the attack surface: audit who owns upgrade()/validator logic, require multisig + timelock + an emergency freeze for every bridge component, and check whether governance proposals that would decentralize validation (IoTeX's IIP-55) are already drafted but undeployed — those are your findings. Per Chainalysis, private-key compromise drove 88% of Q1-2025 thefts: key-custody review is now a core audit deliverable, not an aside.", "aftermath": "Three loss figures circulated ($4.3M Specter / $8M PeckShield / \"$2M net\" per IoTeX) — all measuring different things; 86% of minted CIOTX claimed frozen. A funding trail connects the attacker EOA to the Feb 2025 Infini $49.5M hack. IoTeX offered a $440k white-hat bounty with 48h deadline and pledged 100% treasury compensation; FBI and Korean DAXA were engaged.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "JayPegs Automart (Sushi MISO) — Sep 17, 2021 — $3.1M (returned)", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; supply-chain / front-end attack on the Sushi MISO auction platform — a contracted developer inserted his own wallet address into the auction contract instead of the intended auctionWallet.", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The JayPegs Automart NFT auction routed proceeds to the developer's wallet (0x3dDD8b6D…), funded through Binance/FTX-linked intermediaries (0xe5f7ae14… ← 0xba6f4f83… ← 0x482c9f85…, previously linked to 0xAK). 864.8 ETH (~$3.1M) was taken; within hours of Sushi CTO Joseph DeLong doxxing the suspect (resume, website, Facebook, email, invoices in a public Google doc) and threatening FBI involvement, 865.094 ETH was returned — slightly more than stolen.", "lesson": "Treat front-end and auction-deploy supply chains as first-class attack surface: verify the payout/auctionWallet address in the deployed bytecode and diff the front-end bundle against the repo before every auction. When tracking insiders, follow the funding graph backwards through CEX off-ramps — KYC'd exchange links are what convert suspicion into attribution.", "aftermath": "DeLong publicly accused @eratos1122 (a previously reputable whitehat with a long GitHub history) and refused to back down; the doxx doc was later made private. REKT framed it as \"a blue-chip rekt by a front-end attack\" and noted Sushi's pattern of close calls.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Jimbo's Protocol — May 28, 2023 — ~$7.5M", "date": "", "chain": "Arbitrum; flash-loan attack on the semi-stablecoin rebalancing mechanism — no slippage control in the JimboController's shift() function.", "class": "price", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker flash-loaned 10k ETH and bought JIMBO to pump its price, deposited part of the overvalued JIMBO into JimboController to trigger a rebalance via shift() (transferring the contract's WETH back into the pool), then sold the remaining JIMBO into the pool — draining all WETH liquidity and crashing the token. Over 4,000 ETH (~$7.5M) was bridged to Ethereum and parked at 0x5f3591e2…; the v2 protocol was only three days old (v1 had already collapsed at launch earlier in the month).", "lesson": "Every rebalance/peg-maintenance function needs explicit price bounds — slippage caps, deviation oracles, and reentrancy/deadline checks — because \"deposit → trigger rebalance → sell\" against the same pool is the canonical flash-loan kill chain. Protocols that relaunch a failed design with MORE complexity (leverage) are repeating the same flaw at higher speed; audit the new feature first.", "aftermath": "The team took ~6 hours to acknowledge the alert; Jimbo offered a 10% onchain bounty with a 4PM-UTC-next-day law-enforcement ultimatum, citing researchers from the Euler/Sentiment recoveries. REKT counted it as the 6th Arbitrum incident of 2023 and quoted UltraXBT's \"sus v1 → v2 with even more complexity\" take.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Kannagi Finance — Jul 29, 2023 — ~$1.1M (of $2.1M TVL)", "date": "", "chain": "zkSync Era; classic rug pull by the team/owner through the yield aggregator's admin withdrawal path.", "class": "rug", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With $2.1M TVL, the deployer used the MainChef owner power to withdraw user funds, rugged ~$1.1M (600 ETH), bridged to Ethereum and deposited into Tornado Cash from 0x95ec03b8…; the project's website and socials were deleted immediately after. TVL went from $2.1M to $0.17.", "lesson": "Read audit \"centralization\" lines as findings, not boilerplate: any function that lets an owner withdraw on behalf of arbitrary users is a rug path regardless of what else the audit says. For every yield-aggregator target, enumerate the admin's \"on behalf of\" capabilities and state them as severity-weighted risks; also treat unverified vault contracts + deleted-audit-report projects as near-certain rugs.", "aftermath": "Kannagi had TWO audits and a (deleted) endorsement giveaway from ecosystem leader SyncSwap — the same pattern as EraLend's $3.4M hack that week. Auditors SolidProof said the vault was outside their scope, pointing at SourceHat (ex-Solidity Finance); REKT asked whether rubber-stamp audits legitimize rugs.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "KelpDAO (rsETH / LayerZero DVN) — Apr 18, 2026 — ~$290M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum + Arbitrum (Unichain→Ethereum OFT path); verification-layer breach — attacker poisoned LayerZero's own DVN infrastructure and forged a bridge packet; no smart-contract flaw in KelpDAO", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "One forged packet released 116,500 rsETH (~18% of supply) from the shared custody adapter in a single tx; the attacker (funded with ~$230 from Tornado Cash ~10h earlier) split it across eight pre-staged wallets, supplied 89,567 rsETH to Aave V3 as collateral (eMode 3), and borrowed ~82,650 WETH + 821 wstETH at ~99% LTV within minutes — plus ~$40M on Compound/Euler before freezes. ~$178M consolidated on Ethereum, $72M on Arbitrum; the Arbitrum Security Council used emergency powers to freeze 30,766 ETH (~$73.6M), and ~75,700 ETH (~$175M) was converted to BTC via THORChain/Umbra/Chainflip within 36h. Blast radius: Aave WETH pools hit 100% utilization, $8.45B left Aave in 48h, ~$13.2B DeFi TVL erased, 30+ protocols paused LayerZero bridges, and Aave faces $123.7M–$230.1M bad debt depending on Kelp's loss socialization.", "lesson": "The bridge security config IS the audit: enumerate every OApp's requiredDVNCount/optionalDVN list on-chain and flag any 1-of-1 setup as critical — BGD Labs flagged exactly this risk for rsETH in Feb 2025 governance and it was disregarded. Infrastructure is now the attack surface: treat DVN/RPC/relayer operators (and their node binaries, failover logic, and signing quorums) as in-scope for bridge programs, and check for the RPC-poisoning signature — nodes that lie only to verifier IPs, DDoS-to-failover, self-deleting implants.", "aftermath": "Preliminary attribution is DPRK's Lazarus/TraderTraitor (2nd nine-figure theft in 18 days after Drift's $285M; $577M in 3 weeks). LayerZero blamed Kelp's 1-of-1 config; Kelp countered it was LayerZero's documented default — ~40% of LayerZero apps (1,111 of ~3,500 OApps) ran 1-of-1, and LayerZero only banned the config AFTER the hack. A \"DeFi United\" recovery effort (Stani 5,000 ETH, EtherFi, Lido, Mantle, Frax et al.) plus Kelp's 40,300 rsETH recovery left a ~89,500 ETH shortfall.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "KiiChain — Aug 22, 2026 — 148.3M KII (~$9.7M nominal; ~$1.6M realized)", "date": "", "chain": "Cosmos EVM chain (KiiChain); exploit of a shared Cosmos EVM-module bug (staking precompile balance write-back)", "class": "overflow", "loss": "", "mechanics": "The attacker precomputed the future address of an EVM contract deployment, converted that address into a delayed-vesting account funded with exactly 2 KII, then deployed a helper contract onto it. The contract inherited vesting status, delegated 2 KII + 1 wei (one wei over its balance), and its mirrored EVM balance underflowed to ~2^256. Follow-up helper calls then debited chosen victim accounts (one drain: 1,023.953 KII; the largest: 42,178,466 KII) crediting the attacker — 148,326,583.15 KII across 18 sequences. The chain halted at block 9,355,723, freezing 54.4% (80.7M KII) in place; the rest was bridged to BNB via Hyperlane, ~95.6% of it sold on PancakeSwap (plus 3M KII to a KuCoin deposit) for ~$1.6M.", "lesson": "Chain-level EVM/state-machine accounting bugs propagate to every app-chain on the stack — when one chain (MANTRA, TAC) reports a native precompile exploit, immediately halt-and-audit sibling chains running the same module version. For hunt purposes: check vesting-account edge cases (delegate amount == balance + 1 wei, precomputed contract addresses converted to vesting accounts) as the exact repro path; watch upstream repos for fixes shipped without coordinated disclosure.", "aftermath": "Third chain hit by the same bug in 3 days (MANTRA Aug 20, TAC earlier Aug 22); Cosmos Labs' public acknowledgment came Aug 24-25, after private mitigation notices, and KiiChain accused the labs of knowing the flaw for months, treating it as low-risk, and shipping the fix without timely private warning. KII fell 83% intraday; KiiChain claimed \"no user funds lost\" after freezing over half the loot.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "KiloEx — Apr 14, 2025 — ~$7.49M", "date": "", "chain": "Multi-chain perp DEX (Base $3.31M", "class": "Access-control", "loss": "", "mechanics": "A Tornado-funded wallet (0x00fac928…) forged the forwarder signature, cranked the ETH price down to $100, opened leveraged longs, then jacked the price to $10,000 and closed for profit — repeating across Base, BNB, opBNB, Taiko and Manta in quick succession for ~$7.49M total. Proceeds were bridged out via zkBridge, deBridge and Meson. KiloEx suspended trading, blacklisted the address, and offered the attacker a 10% \"whitehat bounty\" (keep 10%, return 90%) with silence in response.", "lesson": "For perp DEX audits, walk the ENTIRE price-feed trust chain end to end — keeper auth, forwarder/relayer signature verification, and what data the forwarder is allowed to relay; a single trustless link (MinimalForwarder) voids the oracle regardless of Pyth/Chainlink quality. Check the same chain on EVERY deployment: this exploit replayed 1:1 across five chains because the same hole was shipped everywhere.", "aftermath": "Five audits since June 2023 didn't stop it; the most recent auditor (ScaleBit, Mar 2025) said the root cause fell \"outside the scope\" of their audit. Chaofan Shou flagged the access-control flaw within minutes (\"Anyone can change Kilo's price oracle\"). KiloEx filed a Hong Kong police report and froze positions per pre-hack snapshots.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Kokomo Finance — Mar 26-27, 2023 — ~$4M", "date": "", "chain": "Optimism; rug pull executed through the protocol's own upgrade path — the deployer swapped the cBTC market's implementation for a malicious contract and swept WBTC deposits.", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "After upgrading the implementation, the attacker called selector 0x804edaad to transfer the 7,010 sonne WBTC to 0x5C8d…, which swapped it for 141 WBTC (~$4M), split across four wallets (50/40/32 BTCB ≈ $1.4M/$1.1M/$0.9M, plus 20 WBTC ≈ $0.6M). Website, Twitter, GitHub and Medium were deleted; the protocol was less than a week old and the KOKO token under 36 hours old. ~$2M in other tokens remained stuck in the paused pools.", "lesson": "A \"rug\" is usually an upgradeability finding: audit the deployer's power to swap implementations and the existence of pause/emergency functions BEFORE TVL, and treat token-only audit scopes (audit of the ERC-20, not the protocol) as a no-scope warning. Track protocol age — <1 week old lending markets with pausable, upgradable, unaudited core logic are statistically near-certain rugs.", "aftermath": "REKT called it the largest Optimism incident they'd covered since Wintermute's Gnosis Safe blunder; CertiK and Beosin documented the four-step rug within hours. The deleted-web footprint (site/socials/GitHub gone) is itself the rug fingerprint.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Kronos Research — Nov 18, 2023 — $26M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum + BSC; CEX/market-maker API-key compromise — \"unauthorized access of some of our API keys\" per Kronos", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "From ~17:00 UTC Saturday, Kronos Research accounts on four exchanges drained to attacker 0x2b0502FD… — direct withdrawals from OKX and BTSE, and (because of CEX withdrawal whitelists) indirect flows via Kronos' own address on Binance and Deeocoin. Total ~$26M; Kronos confirmed the figure, said it \"remains in good standing\" and would cover losses internally. Woo X paused markets to protect positions from the liquidity hole its largest LP left.", "lesson": "When scoping CEX/market-maker programs, treat API keys (and their withdrawal-whitelist graph: which destination addresses each key can reach) as a first-class asset — key compromise is a loss event even when the venue's custody is untouched. Map LP-concentration risk: one dominant market maker's downtime is a platform-level liquidity outage, a coupling worth flagging in risk reports.", "aftermath": "REKT connected the dots to FTX/Alameda: Kronos had previously boasted an \"Alameda/FTX/FTT type setup\" while claiming separate entities. Earlier in August 2023, two disgruntled Kronos engineers sabotaged trading code over unpaid bonuses, costing $1.4M — the firm's second incident in months.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "KyberSwap (Elastic) — Nov 22-23, 2023 — $48M+", "date": "", "chain": "Six chains (Arbitrum >$20M", "class": "rounding", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With a flash loan, the attacker drained the lowest-liquidity pools first, then used swaps + position edits to move current prices/ticks to the boundary where the vulnerable check's error flips sign. Multi-step swaps across ticks triggered the double counting and emptied the pools; the same recipe ran identically on all six chains from 0x50275e0b… (execution) with loot parked at 0xc9b826ba… (Scroll was funded but never attacked). TVL collapsed from $71M to under $3M. Funding: Tornado Cash on Ethereum (fanning out to ARB/OP/BASE) and FixedFloat for MATIC/AVAX.", "lesson": "Concentrated-liquidity tick math is a precision minefield — fuzz swap sequences at tick boundaries and empty-liquidity regions, and formally check the liquidity-counting invariant across cross-tick swaps (deltaLiquidity sums) rather than unit-testing happy paths. The attacker's own event-log comments (\"Step 2, finding liquidity required…\") show stepwise invariant probing is how pros find these — emulate it in audits.", "aftermath": "The exploiter left on-chain easter-egg comments and a message (\"Negotiations will start in a few hours when I am fully rested\") and sent 1,000 ETH to an address tied to the Oct 2021 Indexed Finance hacker (Andean Medjedovic). Elastic had been audited by ChainSecurity and a Sherlock contest; Spreek spotted the hack, confirmed by Kyber within the hour, and the month's total theft passed $300M.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "LastPass Users (wallet-draining campaign) — Oct 2023 (ongoing since Dec 2022) — $37M+ (incl. $4.4M in one day)", "date": "", "chain": "Multi-chain", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Since mid-Dec 2022, the attacker has drained hundreds of wallets with minimum losses \"well over $10k\" — victims include crypto-org employees, VCs, DeFi protocol builders and long-dormant (near-decade-old) wallets. Signature pattern: thefts cluster 10am-4pm UTC, favor weekends, tokens are swapped to ETH inside the victim's wallet before withdrawal, staked positions/NFTs are usually missed, and funds exit via non-KYC swap services (FixedFloat, SimpleSwap, SideShift, ChangeNOW, LetsExchange). One day last week produced $4.4M alone; total ≥$37M, traced by ZachXBT and Tayvano.", "lesson": "For bounty programs, \"off-chain custody of user secrets\" is a real attack surface: any integration that encourages storing seeds/keys in browser extensions or cloud password managers inherits their breach history — flag and suggest hardware/enclave storage. Hunt-wise, the campaign's on-chain fingerprint (UTC-hours clustering, weekend bias, in-wallet swap-before-send, CEX-less exits via instant swappers) is a reusable detection heuristic for wallet-drainer campaigns.", "aftermath": "The LastPass connection went public only in Aug 2023, though tracing began in April; LastPass' Aug 2022 statement claimed no customer data access, then the Dec update revealed vault backups were copied and warned of brute-force. REKT's takeaway: \"Improved UX is not worth trusting sensitive info to 3rd parties… When did you last change your seed?\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "LCX — Jan 8, 2022 — $7.94M", "date": "", "chain": "Ethereum; CEX hot-wallet private key exploit — attacker signed directly with the compromised LCX hot wallet (0x4631018f…).", "class": "hack", "loss": "", "mechanics": "From ~22:30 UTC on Jan 8, the attacker drained the LCX hot wallet: 162.68 ETH ($503k), 3,437,783 USDC, 761,237 EURe ($865k), 101,250 SAND ($486k), 1,847 LINK, 17.25M LCX ($2.47M), 669 QNT, 4,819 ENJ, 4.76 MKR — $7.94M total per LCX's incident report, with deposits/withdrawals paused. ~1,891 ETH (~$6M) of proceeds went to Tornado Cash from the hacker wallet 0x16540227…; ~$1M of LCX plus 611k EURe (frozen by issuer Monerium) stayed in the address.", "lesson": "CeFi hunts revolve around key custody, not code: enumerate hot-wallet signing paths, check whether hot wallets hold more than operational float, and whether stablecoin issuers can freeze (Monerium froze 611k EURe — the one recovery win). PeckShield-style monitoring caught the outflow within minutes; exchanges need that telemetry on their own wallets, not just customer accounts.", "aftermath": "REKT's writeup was openly dismissive of CEX hot-wallet hygiene (\"Next time, they can write this article themselves\" + a template) and noted the shrinking USP of CeFi. PeckShield spotted the drain first, before LCX's own announcement.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-05.md"}, {"protocol": "Levana Protocol", "date": "2023-12-29", "chain": "Osmosis (Cosmos) / oracle manipulation — opening high-leverage perps positions at deliberately stale oracle prices", "class": "price", "loss": "~$1.1M (~10% of protocol liquidity)", "mechanics": "The attack began Dec 13 and ramped up on Dec 26, running ~14 days. Stage 1 drained ~4% of LPs over 12 days with tiny per-trade profits (attributed to organic trader PnL at first); stage 2, during Osmosis congestion, drained an additional ~5%. Total loss ~$1.146M: stATOM_USD $241k, ATOM_USD $229k, BTC_USD $190k, ETH_USD $128k, TIA_USD $108k, USDC pools $168k + $82k. The attacker spammed txs so no oracle updates could land, DDoSed the backend scheduled to push updates, tracked the delta between stale oracle data and real market price, then used a multiexecute tx to open a long/short AND push the stale data to market pricing in one atomic action — profiting with certainty. The team noticed only when losses spiked during congestion and paused the protocol.", "lesson": "(1) Every position open must consume a FRESH oracle price — require the update in the same tx or queue positions behind an update; anything else turns \"jammed update channel\" into free money. (2) Audit the failure mode \"oracle updates stop arriving\" (fee-market bugs, bot liveness, congestion, DDoS), not just price manipulation of the oracle itself.", "aftermath": "Audited by FYEO and Peckshield, but congestion-dependent bugs were out of scope; a concurrent DDoS hampered the team's response. Levana plans to make users whole via fees and an LVN airdrop, and to add a tx queuing system requiring a fresh oracle price to open positions.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-06.md"}, {"protocol": "Level Finance", "date": "2023-05-02", "chain": "BSC / repeated referral reward claims — LevelReferralControllerV2 `claimMultiple` allowed the same epoch's referral rewards to be claimed over and over.", "class": "logic", "loss": "~$1.1M (214k LVL → 3,345 BNB)", "mechanics": "The exploiter created many referral accounts, used flash loans to make swaps and inflate their reward tier, then repeatedly called `claimMultiple` for the same epoch, draining 214k LVL (~$1.1M) which they dumped for 3,345 BNB. The LVL price crashed from $8.42 to $2.93 (-65%) before mostly recovering. The attack was first attempted over a week earlier but went unnoticed; definalist's monitoring raised the alarm while the exploit was ongoing, and the Level team confirmed two hours later.", "lesson": "(1) Every reward/rebate claim path must record claimed epochs (or nonce) per user — missing epoch-replay protection on a claim function is an infinite mint. (2) For proxy contracts, audit the DEPLOYED bytecode behind the proxy, not the audited GitHub commit — post-audit upgrades are the most common source of unaudited code (Quantstamp confirmed the vuln post-dated their audit).", "aftermath": "Quantstamp and Obelisk had audited the core contracts without spotting it; Quantstamp later stated the bug was introduced in the April 18 upgrade (proxy 0x977087422C008233615b572fBC3F209Ed300063a, impl 0x9f00…) not present in the audited commit. Losses were contained to the referral programme — Treasury funds and LPs were safe. Exploiter: 0x70319d1c09e1373fc7b10403c852909e5b20a9d5.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-06.md"}, {"protocol": "MonoX", "date": "2021-11-30", "chain": "Polygon + Ethereum; price manipulation of the native MONO token through a pricing bug in the Monoswap contract's `swapTokenForExactToken` path", "class": "price", "loss": "$31.4M", "mechanics": "The attacker looped MONO self-swaps to inflate the token's price to \"sky high\", then used the overvalued MONO to buy out virtually all other deposited assets in MonoX's vCASH virtual-pair pools. ~$19.4M was taken on Polygon first; 17 minutes later the identical vector hit Ethereum for ~$12M more — total $31.4M (5.7M MATIC, 3.9k WETH, 36.1 WBTC, 5.1M DUCK, 4.1k MIM, 3.1k GHST, 1.2k LINK, 274 IMX). The protocol was ~1 month old and had only passed $30M TVL three days earlier.", "lesson": "Every DEX swap/price-update path must enforce tokenIn != tokenOut (or treat self-swaps as no-ops) — grep for missing same-asset guards in `swapForExact`/`updatePrice` functions of novel AMM designs. Both Halborn and Peckshield had audited Monoswap and both missed it: single-sided/virtual-pair pool pricing deserves a dedicated adversarial pass.", "aftermath": "The bug was identified by BlockSec; the team's Telegram/Twitter statement begged the attacker to \"PLEASE reach out to us\". rekt flagged the irony of the \"streamlined swap routing\" sales pitch.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-07.md"}, {"protocol": "Nomad Bridge", "date": "2022-08-02", "chain": "Ethereum bridge (ecosystem damage to Moonbeam", "class": "logic", "loss": "$190M", "mechanics": "After one failed attempt (costing $350k in gas) the original attacker's tx called `process()` directly without proving validity; with 0x00 as trusted root the check passed. Because the tx was public, copycats simply copy-pasted the same calldata from Etherscan and swapped in their own address — a 2.5-hour free-for-all of crowdhacking, MEV bots and self-declared whitehats drained $190M. The top three addresses took $47M, $40M and $8M; the Rari Capital (Arbitrum) exploiter returned for ~$3M in stables, straight into Tornado. The team halted it by removing the Replica contract as owner — three hours after the exploit began.", "lesson": "Treat zero-value initialization of security-critical config (trusted roots, signers, thresholds, roles) as a never-trusted sentinel and assert on it in every upgrade's post-deploy checks. Public exploit calldata converts a 1-of-1 hack into a permissionless crowd drain — bridge pause/kill-switch procedures must be rehearsed to fire in minutes, and upgrades of message-validation logic need regression tests for \"unproven\" inputs.", "aftermath": "The 100th rekt leaderboard entry. Quantstamp's June audit had flagged a similar issue (QSP-19) with the remark \"We believe the Nomad team has misunderstood the issue\"; the team's acknowledgement came three hours late. Moonbeam TVL fell $300M→$135M, EVMOS ~$7M→$3M, Milkomeda $31M→$20M; 🍉🍉🍉.eth took $4M and claimed whitehat.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-07.md"}, {"protocol": "PAID Network", "date": "2021-03-05", "chain": "Ethereum; unlimited mint of PAID tokens and dump into the Uniswap PAID/ETH pair. Suspected insider job — contract ownership was transferred to the attacker's address 30 minutes before the mint.", "class": "access control", "loss": "~$3.1M realized ($27.4M nominal)", "mechanics": "The attacker minted 59,471,745.571 PAID just after 18:00 UTC and dumped them into the Uniswap PAID/ETH pool over the following ~30 minutes, swapping for 2,079.6 WETH (~$3.1M realized) and leaving 594,717,455.71 PAID (~$24.3M nominal) in the account — rekt's \"Total Rugged Amount\" $27.4M. PAID price fell from ~$2.86 to ~$0.32. WARONRUGS had publicly warned about the mint capability over a month earlier.", "lesson": "An uncapped, ownership-gated mint on a project token is the single highest-yield rug primitive — check who holds the mint/owner role, whether it changed hands near the incident, and the ownership-transfer timestamps. Public pre-incident warnings (WARONRUGS, a month before) are forensic gold: check the warning history before attributing anything to a sophisticated external hack.", "aftermath": "An on-chain note congratulated the \"Russian Hackers\" on \"putin in the work\"; CEO Kyle Chasse pleaded private-key compromise while PAID promised to restore all balances with a new contract. Rekt's framing: \"an infinite mint is a magical thing — a golden ticket.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-07.md"}, {"protocol": "PancakeBunny", "date": "2021-05-20", "chain": "BSC; 8-flash-loan price manipulation of PancakeSwap LP pool reserves feeding the `VaultFlipToFlip` reward calculation", "class": "price", "loss": "$45M", "mechanics": "The attacker took 8 flash loans — 1.05M WBNB (WBNB+CAKE), 522.52K WBNB (WBNB+BUSD), 210.16K WBNB (WBNB+ETH), 133.5K WBNB (WBNB+BTCB), 241.02K WBNB (WBNB+SAFEMOON), 98.5K WBNB (WBNB+BELT), 66.29K WBNB (WBNB+DOT) and 2.96M USDT from Fortube. They deposited 2.96M USDT + 7,886 WBNB into WBNB+BUSDT (minting 144.45K LP), swapped 2.32M WBNB→3.83M BUSDT to skew the pool valuation, then called `getReward()` on VaultFlipToFlip to claim 6.97M BUNNY with the inflated LP valuation (dev team got a separate 1.05M BUNNY). Selling the BUNNY dropped its price from $146 to $6 — ~$45M lost; PancakeBunny's TVL fell from a $10B+ peak to ~$1B.", "lesson": "Reward/valuation logic must never read spot DEX reserves inside the same transaction context — require TWAP/oracle pricing for vault reward claims (hunt `getReward`/claim paths that multiply shares by current reserve ratios). Also: the audited code and the deployed code diverged — Haechi's audit predated the exploited contracts; treat post-audit upgrades as unaudited code.", "aftermath": "Haechi's statement: the exploited `helper` function was in new contracts they never audited, though their report had warned about unaudited changeable external contracts and flash-loan weakness. BSC had a brutal day: Venus Protocol and WARONRUGS imploded within hours.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-07.md"}, {"protocol": "Pike Finance", "date": "2024-05-03", "chain": "Ethereum", "class": "access control", "loss": ">$1.9M (two attacks)", "mechanics": "April 26 on Arbitrum: the attacker (0xAdaF1626aEC26A7937aE7d1Fa0664e6E0904C1d0) initialized the target contract (0x7856493B59cdb1685757A6DcCe12425F6a6666a0), took owner permissions and upgraded the spoke to a malicious implementation to siphon funds. Pike's post-exploit patch altered the storage layout (moving the initialized flag), so on April 30 a second attacker (0x19066f7431df29a0910d287c8822936bb7d89e23) repeated the same class of attack across Ethereum, Arbitrum and Optimism, draining over $1.6M in ARB, OP and ETH — caught by Chain Aegis — bringing the combined loss to over $1.9M.", "lesson": "Every upgradeable contract needs an initializer guard (initialized flag + versioning) that provably survives storage-layout changes — verify layout stability across upgrades and that re-initialization is impossible from any caller. Post-incident patches are new attack surface: \"we fixed it\" without killing the initializer (and without re-auditing) is how the second exploit happened.", "aftermath": "Pike had no public audits and no bug bounty (both \"Coming soon\") and still listed testnet contracts; the team offered the April 26 attacker a 20% bounty on-chain while $6.45M presale holders demanded answers. Quill Audits produced the postmortem; rekt: \"Fool me once… fool me twice, shame on me.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-07.md"}, {"protocol": "Poloniex", "date": "2023-11-10", "chain": "CEX hot-wallet drain across three chains (Ethereum $59.2M", "class": "drain", "loss": "$126M (CEX hot wallets)", "mechanics": "Largest single asset losses were 33M USDT (22M on TRON, 11M on ETH), $18.6M native BTC, a further $14M of BTC on TRON and $5M USDC on Ethereum. On Ethereum the loot was dispersed across ~13 labelled hacker addresses which swapped tokens to ETH and held or further dispersed. The attacker's swap strategy had side effects: converting the freezable TRON-side USDT to TRX pumped Justin Sun's TRX bags by 25%, and in the liquidation rush ~$2.6M of Golem's GLM was lost by transferring it directly to the token's contract. Poloniex announced the wallet was \"disabled for maintenance\" ~30 min after the drain began; Sun then promised full reimbursement and a 5% whitehat bounty with a 7-day grace period.", "lesson": "CEX incidents spanning multiple chains simultaneously point to one custody/ops compromise, not per-chain bugs — triage for key theft first. On-chain, the attacker's own \"mistakes\" (pumping a related token 25%, burning $2.6M GLM to the token contract, visible dispersal chains) are triage telemetry: rushed conversion behavior and frozen-asset avoidance (USDT→TRX) mark a live drain in progress.", "aftermath": "Arkham offered a $4,000 bounty for hacker ID via its intel market; rekt flags the possible DPRK/Lazarus link (over $250M lost that year in Lazarus-linked incidents: Atomic Wallet, AlphaPo, Stake, CoinEx). Came weeks after Sun's HTX lost $7.9M (returned within two weeks); attacker ETH 0x0a5984f86200415894821bfefc1c1de036dbf9e7, TRON TKK6d1YALy8HCSoCSWWd1ZJhyC9NPPx4wa, BTC bc1qnpc7u2ha7ct9c458rrqsawylz9e9j6jvkvzttt.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-08.md"}, {"protocol": "Poly Network", "date": "2021-08-10", "chain": "Cross-chain bridge/relay privilege escalation on three chains via the EthCrossChainManager relay contract — no private-key compromise", "class": "logic", "loss": "~$611M (Ethereum + BSC + Polygon)", "mechanics": "The attacker sent cross-chain messages to the Proxy Lock Contracts on Ethereum (0x250e7698...), BSC and Polygon that tricked EthCrossChainManager into calling EthCrossChainData and executing `putCurEpochConPubKeyBytes`, replacing the keeper public keys with attacker-controlled ones — the contract \"hacked itself\" (kelvinfichter's breakdown). With keeper control they drained Ethereum (96.4M USDC, 1,032 WBTC, 673k DAI, 43k UNI, 259.7B SHIB, 14.47 renBTC, 33.4M USDT, 26,109 wETH, 616k FEI), BSC (87.6M USDC, 26,629 BTCB, 6,613 BNB, 299 ETH, 1,023 BUSD) and Polygon (85M USDC). Tether froze all 33M USDT on Ethereum. After an OPSEC slip exposed by @WardBradt (a return tx sent from an address tied to FTX/Binance/OKEx accounts), the attacker announced \"READY TO SURRENDER\" and returned funds; at write time ~$2.6M (ETH), $1.1M (BSC) and $1M (Polygon) had been returned.", "lesson": "Cross-chain relay contracts must whitelist exactly which target contracts and functions a user message can invoke — the relay manager should never hold privileges over its own data/keeper contract (\"separate concerns\"). Never gate privileged calls on a 4-byte selector computed from user input: prefix collisions are trivially grindable, so use explicit target+function allowlists, not sighash matching.", "aftermath": "", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-08.md"}, {"protocol": "Qubit Finance", "date": "2022-01-27", "chain": "BSC lending protocol (PancakeBunny team) — cross-chain collateral mint without backing: QBridge deposit logic flaw", "class": "Bridge", "loss": "$80M (200k BNB)", "mechanics": "At ~21:30 UTC on Jan 27 the attacker called the Ethereum-side QBridge deposit with no ETH locked, and the zero-address transfer silently succeeded. The BSC side credited them 77,162 qXETH (~$185M) of collateral against loans on Qubit, which they used to borrow WETH, BTC-B, USD stablecoins plus CAKE, BUNNY and MDX, swapping everything into 200k BNB (~$80M) that remained sitting in the BSC address. The cross-chain collateral feature had been audited by Theori in Dec 2021.", "lesson": "When adding a new asset/deposit path, the deprecated one must be removed or explicitly reverted — leftover legacy functions stay permanently callable and drift out of any audit scope. Also treat calls to address(0) as a hard failure everywhere (a call to a no-code address returns success in Solidity), and prove on the destination chain that minted credit strictly equals assets locked on the source chain.", "aftermath": "#7 on the leaderboard at the time; the Qubit team (behind repeat offender PancakeBunny) signaled willingness to negotiate beyond their $250k Immunefi max bounty. rekt's grudge was personal: the PancakeBunny hacker had gifted them 100k DAI in May 2021, which they returned with the note \"not to lose it this time\".", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-08.md"}, {"protocol": "Raydium", "date": "2022-12-16", "chain": "Solana AMM; compromised private key of the pool owner/admin account (suspected trojan) — no on-chain exploit", "class": "drain", "loss": "~$4.4M (LP protocol fees)", "mechanics": "The drain was first flagged by the Solana DEX aggregator PRISM (\"a wallet is draining LP Pools from Raydium liquidity pools using admin wallet as a signer without having/burning LP tokens\"), which pulled its own PRISM/USDC liquidity as a warning. Affected pools: SOL-USDC, SOL-USDT, RAY-USDC, RAY-USDT, RAY-SOL, stSOL-USDC, ZBC-USDC, UXP-USDC, whETH-USDC. Raydium's announcement came ~40 minutes later, halting authority on the AMM & farm programs and shipping a patch. Most funds were bridged to Ethereum, swapped to ETH and deposited into Tornado Cash, with 100k SOL (~$1.4M) still at the attacker's Solana address (AgJddDJLt17nHyXDCpyGELxwsZZQPqfUsuwzoiqVGJwD).", "lesson": "An AMM's admin/owner key is a direct fee-drain primitive — inventory every instruction the owner key can call (withdraw_pnl, fee-parameter setters like SyncNeedTake) and require multisig/derived-PDA custody for anything that moves accumulated protocol value. Watch for admin-signer transactions that touch fee accounts without corresponding LP-token accounting: that signature (drain \"without having/burning LP tokens\") is exactly what the aggregator PRISM caught in minutes.", "aftermath": "Analysis by OtterSec; rekt's post-FTX framing notes the eternal ambiguity of \"compromised key\" cases — insider opportunism can't be ruled out. The incident hit during the bear-market fallout from FTX's collapse, which rekt highlights as context for tempted ecosystem devs.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-08.md"}, {"protocol": "Ronin Network — 2022-03-23 (detected 2022-03-29) — ~$624M (173,600 ETH + 25.5M USDC)", "date": "", "chain": "Ronin (Axie Infinity) Ethereum sidechain bridge; validator-threshold compromise — four Sky Mavis validator keys plus a never-revoked Axie DAO delegation", "class": "bridge", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With 5-of-9 approval the attacker authorized two withdrawals from the Ronin Bridge contract: first 173,600 ETH, then 25.5M USDC (swapped to ETH via other addresses and returned to the main wallet). Detection took six days — the alert came from a user who couldn't withdraw 5k ETH. Funds sat at 0x098b716b8aaf21512996dc57eb0615e2383e2f96; 6,250 ETH was peeled off, some moved to FTX and Crypto.com, and the wallet was originally funded from a Binance account (\"KYC'd accounts are easily acquired\").", "lesson": "Bridge security must assume correlated compromise of any single operator group — thresholds should be counted in *distinct operators*, not validators, and any cross-party signing delegation needs an expiry that is enforced on-chain, because \"temporary\" grants never revoke themselves. The six-day detection gap is the other lesson: monitor bridge balances and withdrawals continuously — a $624M outflow with zero alarms is a monitoring failure, not just a key failure.", "aftermath": "New biggest crypto hack ever at the time, dethroning Poly Network; nobody noticed for six days. Sky Mavis moved the threshold to 8-of-9 — but did so ~11 hours *before* officially announcing the incident. The case is rekt's exhibit A that decentralisation is not academic: validator-set centralisation turned one operator's breach into the entire bridge's bank run.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-08.md"}, {"protocol": "Spartan Protocol", "date": "2021-05-02", "chain": "BSC; flash-loan-funded manipulation of the SPARTA/WBNB pool's LP-burn share calculation (10K WBNB flash loan", "class": "logic", "loss": "$30.5M", "mechanics": "With a 10K WBNB flash loan the attacker swapped WBNB→SPARTA five times (1,913 WBNB each → 2,536,613 SPARTA), added that plus 11,853 WBNB to mint 933,350 SPT1-WBNB LP tokens; then ten more swaps (1,674 WBNB each → 2,639,121 SPARTA) and donated all SPARTA plus 21,632 WBNB straight into the pool to inflate its balances. Burning the 933K LP tokens then withdrew 2,538,199 SPARTA + 20,694 WBNB (≈9K WBNB profit on an 11,853 WBNB deposit), and a second add-1,414,010-LP-then-burn cycle netted 2,643,882 SPARTA + 21,555 WBNB; the loop repeated until the pool was drained, then the 100,260 WBNB loan was repaid. About a third of the profit was lost to slippage on exit: final haul 219.5 BTC (~$12.4M) + 3,311 ETH (~$9.7M), with the bulk parked at 0x3b6e… (30.7K WBNB, 4.6M SPARTA, 1.3M BUSD-T, 23.2 BTCB, 924K BUSD).", "lesson": "Liquidity-share (LP burn/withdraw) math must use cached, cumulative pool accounting — never live `balanceOf`-style reads — because donation/swap-inflated balances turn LP redemptions into free withdrawals. When auditing a fork of an ETH blue chip, diff the share-calculation logic against the original line by line; \"optimizations\" introduced during the BSC copy-paste boom are where the holes live.", "aftermath": "$30M extracted for $66 in flash-loan fees (\"excellent ROI\" — rekt); Peckshield's root-cause analysis and Igor Igamberdiev's exfil tracing (1inch → Nerve → Anyswap swaps to BTCB/BETH) are credited. 6th on the leaderboard; rekt wondered aloud how rekt you have to get before CZ steps in.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-09.md"}, {"protocol": "Sturdy Finance", "date": "2023-06-12", "chain": "Ethereum; flash-loan + read-only reentrancy on the SturdyOracle to manipulate the price of collateral token B-stETH-STABLE in a leveraged-yield lending protocol.", "class": "Oracle", "loss": "~$800K (442 ETH)", "mechanics": "Attacker 0x1e8419e7… deployed attack contract 0x0b09c862… (with built-in front-running protection) and flash-loaned funds to distort the oracle's reported value of B-stETH-STABLE collateral, then borrowed against the inflated collateral, leaving the protocol underwater; profit 442 ETH (~$800K) was deposited into Tornado Cash roughly 20 minutes after the funding transaction. Ancilia raised the alarm and the team paused all markets shortly after.", "lesson": "Read-only reentrancy corrupts price quotes without any classic reentrant call — for every view/pricing function, ask whether its inputs (pool balances, reserves, totalSupply) can change between the quote and the borrow; the answer is the attack window. Also treat vulnerability advisories about the pools you integrate (the Feb 2023 Balancer post named the exact pools) as in-scope audit findings, not somebody else's problem.", "aftermath": "Ancilia spotted it first, BlockSec reconstructed the steps; despite three audits (CertiK, Quantstamp, Code4rena) the vulnerable LendingPool version fell outside audit scope — Quantstamp pointed this out in June 2024 and the leaderboard entry was updated. Rekt tied it to the then-hot \"oracle-free lending\" debate.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-09.md"}, {"protocol": "Sonne Finance", "date": "2024-05-14", "chain": "Optimism; Compound v2 fork donation attack on the brand-new VELO market", "class": "governance", "loss": "$20M (WETH, VELO, soVELO, wrapped USDC)", "mechanics": "Sonne deployed the VELO market and created a proposal with a 4-day governance period; after it passed and the timelock expired, the attacker's bot won the race to execute it and, in the same transaction (0x9312ae37…), donated into the soVELO/soUSDC/soWETH markets to inflate exchange rates, then borrowed out ~$20M of WETH, VELO, soVELO and wrapped USDC from ~$60M TVL. Nerv Alert sounded the alarm late Tuesday with an initial $3M estimate before the tally reached $20M; MEV researcher Tony KΞ (fuzzland) front-ran the attacker with just $100 of gas and prevented >$6.5M in additional losses.", "lesson": "A known, warned-about and audit-flagged vulnerability is still exploitable if the governance activation sequence isn't atomic — market creation, collateral-factor setting and donation-defense (virtual/dead shares) must go live as one indivisible operation BEFORE the first user/attacker can touch the new market. For any Compound fork: every \"add new market\" proposal is a countdown; time the activation window and pre-compute the donation math.", "aftermath": "Hundred Finance had publicly warned other Compound forks after its own 2023 donation attack, and Sonne \"turned a deaf ear\"; the team posted the exploit on Discord first and X only hours later, with a post-mortem ~5h after the attack. Users spotted that Mendi Finance is a friendly fork of Sonne — rekt's closer: \"Could they be exploited soon?\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-09.md"}, {"protocol": "Stars Arena", "date": "2023-10-07", "chain": "Avalanche; FriendTech clone hit twice in two days — a broken `getPrice()` drain", "class": "hack", "loss": "~$2.9M (entire TVL; plus ~$2K in the first hack)", "mechanics": "On Saturday the attacker reentered `sellShares()` so the contract sent AVAX equal to a (large) block-height value instead of the owed share proceeds, draining ~$2.9M — the protocol's entire TVL. Hacker address: 0xa2Ebf3FCD757e9BE1E58B643b6B5077D11b4ad7A. The team's response to the first incident had been \"THE EXPLOIT HAS BEEN FIXED. BUT DON'T GET THIS WRONG WE ARE AT WAR.\"", "lesson": "An emergency \"fix\" that upgrades to another unverified contract is not a fix — verify bytecode before and after every upgrade, and specifically audit share-buy/sell functions for reentrancy and for any path where the payout amount can be influenced by calldata or chain state. Treat an unverified, un-audited fast-fork shilled by ecosystem insiders as a risk signal in itself, not as validation.", "aftermath": "Ava Labs CEO Emin Gün Sirer shilled the project for a week, then dismissed the full drain as \"a mere speed bump\" recoverable \"in about 10 days\", doubling down as CT watched; the team claimed \"security is the core driving factor\" while reportedly holding user private keys and being open to DDoS. Analysis credited to 0xlilitch and Beosin; the team floated a relaunch.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-09.md"}, {"protocol": "SushiSwap (RouteProcessor2 \"Yoink\")", "date": "2023-04-09", "chain": "Ethereum + 13 other chains; the 4-day-old RouteProcessor2 router accepted arbitrary route data — a fake UniV3 pool impersonated a liquidity pool to trigger `transferFrom` on any address that had approved the router.", "class": "drain", "loss": "$3.3M (across 14 chains; 0xSifu hit for 1,800 ETH)", "mechanics": "The attacker created a fake UniV3 pool, passed its address in place of a real liquidity pool in a crafted route, and during the callback the router pulled approved tokens from victims' wallets to the attacker (\"yoink\"). Over $3.3M was stolen from users who had approved the days-old contract across 14 chains. DeFi antagonist 0xSifu lost 1,800 ETH; BlockSec and other whitehats raced to revoke/front-run user approvals, and one self-described whitehat who targeted Sifu botched the return — only 100 ETH came back. Example tx 0xea3480f1…; router on ETH: 0x044b75f554b886A065b9567891e45c79542d7357.", "lesson": "Every callback invoked by an untrusted external contract must authenticate its caller against a trusted pool set (factory-derived address check), because in a router a fake pool equals arbitrary `transferFrom` on every historical approver. Newly deployed router contracts are prime hunting ground: map each callback for missing `msg.sender` validation and user-controlled pool addresses, and check which big wallets hold stale approvals.", "aftermath": "Head Chef Jared Grey acknowledged the bug, urged revocations, and promised a full post-mortem after removing the exploited contract; credited analysts Inspex, 0xfoobar and ernestognw. Rekt folded it into Sushi's long drama arc (internal infighting, Grey's SEC subpoena and $500K salary, \"paltry\" bounties) and noted the 4-day-old router's small approval set saved it from becoming \"AMM-ageddon\".", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-09.md"}, {"protocol": "Team Finance", "date": "2022-10-27", "chain": "Ethereum; flawed `migrate()` in Team Finance's Liquidity Lock \"v2→v3 migration\" contract — locked Uniswap V2 LP moved into attacker-controlled V3 pairs at skewed prices", "class": "logic", "loss": "$15.8M (4 projects' locked liquidity)", "mechanics": "The attacker created his own UniV3 pairs with manipulated price curves and called the flawed `migrate()` to move each project's real UniV2 locked liquidity into them, pocketing the leftover refund on each migration. Per-pool losses: CAW $11.5M, FEG $1.9M, TSUKA $1.7M (price cushioned by a secondary UniV3 pool), KNDX $0.7M — ~$15.8M total. Exploiter addresses 0x161cebb8… and 0xba399a25… (stolen funds); attack contract 0xcff07c4e…; attack tx 0xb2e3ea72….", "lesson": "Any migrate/upgrade path that moves LP must whitelist its destination pools (price-verified against the source pair) and enforce sender authorization per lock owner — a check that any lock action can satisfy is no check at all. Security-infrastructure providers are themselves attack surface: an \"anti-rug\" tool with a flawed migration primitive rugs every project it \"protects\" in one exploit.", "aftermath": "The self-proclaimed \"Industry Leader In Project Security & Automation\" lost $15.8M of funds it was safeguarding — rekt: \"Four projects got rugged through their shared anti-rug mechanism.\" #46 on the leaderboard; rekt mocked the claim of protecting $2.5B+ in assets given the liquidity profile of the locked tokens.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-09.md"}, {"protocol": "THORChain", "date": "2021-07-16", "chain": "THORChain's ETH Bifrost (bridge) on Ethereum — deposit-value spoofing via an \"override loop\" reached by wrapping the router with an attacker contract.", "class": "hack", "loss": "$5M", "mechanics": "The exploit was looped to drain the bridge's liquidity across multiple assets: 2,500 ETH, 57,975 SUSHI, 8.7 YFI, 171,912 DODO, 514.5 ALCX, 1,167,216 KYL, 13.3 AAVE — ~$5M total. The code even carried the comment \"// it is important to keep this part outside the above loop\"; the official fix was \"make the over-ride only happen if it specifically is a vaultTransferEvent\". Attacker wallet 0x3a196410a0f5facd08fd7880a4b8551cd085c031, with funds sent through Tornado.", "lesson": "(1) In bridge/router code, transaction-type discrimination must be explicit (calldata signature match, whitelist) — wrapping the router with your own contract must never be able to reach privileged value-transfer overrides; hunt for loops whose branch semantics change which value/deposit field gets read. (2) A code comment marking exactly where logic \"must stay outside the loop\" is a free audit beacon — such comments flag fragile invariants that deserve an adversarial test.", "aftermath": "THORChain's market cap had peaked near $5B two months earlier ($1.2B at write time), so rekt framed it as \"even the old gods cannot escape the exploits\"; Cronje's maxim quoted: \"innovation leads to exploitation\"; the team published a preliminary incident report the same day.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-10.md"}, {"protocol": "Treasure DAO", "date": "2022-03-03", "chain": "Arbitrum; NFT marketplace logic bug — `buyItem` missing a quantity check let listed NFTs be \"bought\" for zero.", "class": "exploit", "loss": "~$1.4M in NFTs", "mechanics": "The attacker called `buyItem()` with quantity 0 on existing listings, paying nothing while receiving the NFTs — draining ~$1.4M worth of NFTs from the largest Arbitrum NFT marketplace (including Smol Brains listings with a ~2.5k MAGIC / >$9k floor). The marketplace was paused shortly after exploitation began; MAGIC dipped about a third before stabilising at ~-10%. On-chain investigators linked an exploiter account to ENS and Binance addresses, and the doxxing threat prompted many NFTs to be returned — ~50 remained stolen when the team promised to make buyers whole.", "lesson": "(1) Every NFT marketplace buy/offer path needs explicit quantity validation — zero-quantity purchases collapse the payment term regardless of price; `require(_quantity > 0)` is the single line that separates a marketplace from a free-mint machine. (2) When fees or payments are computed multiplicatively with quantity, fuzz the zero edge case across buy/bid/accept paths — this bug class recurs wherever quantity is a user input.", "aftermath": "rekt's lede: \"Swiggity swooty, somebody plundered the Treasure DAO booty\"; co-founder John Patten: \"I will personally give up all of my Smols to repair this\"; the MAGIC community's near-instant rally saved the project.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-10.md"}, {"protocol": "Uranium Finance", "date": "2021-04-28", "chain": "BSC; math bug in the Uniswap-v2-forked `UraniumPair` — a broken constant-product check let 1 wei drain 98% of a pair's output token.", "class": "logic", "loss": "~$57M", "mechanics": "The attacker first sent the minimum amount of each token to the pair contracts, then used a low-level `swap()` to drain both reserves: 34k WBNB ($18M), 17.9M BUSD ($17.9M), 1.8k ETH ($4.7M), 80 BTC ($4.3M), 26.5k DOT ($0.8M), 638k ADA ($0.8M), 5.7M USDT ($5.7M) — ~$57.2M, with ≥2,200 ETH quickly mixed through Tornado Cash. The buggy v2 had been live ~10 days; the fix (v2.1, \"the only significant change\") was due to ship the same day the exploit hit, and the contracts repo was deleted from GitHub afterwards.", "lesson": "(1) Fork-diff audits: every numeric deviation from upstream AMM code invalidates the core invariant — diff all constants between fork and source before launch; a 1000→10000 change in two of three places breaks `x*y=k` silently. (2) \"Bug introduced by the team's own edit + repo deleted + patched version due the same day\" is the insider/rug signature — treat exploit timing that coincides with a scheduled fix migration as a red flag, not a coincidence.", "aftermath": "second place on rekt's leaderboard at the time (a \"much more preferable prize than the EASY tokens of our previous article\"); their MasterChef rewards contract had already been exploited earlier that month; credit 0xdeadf4ce and igor igamberdiev; rekt asked openly whether the team added the bug and used the liquidity migration to hide it.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-10.md"}, {"protocol": "Value DeFi", "date": "2020-11-14", "chain": "Ethereum; flash-loan price manipulation of the Curve pool used as a spot oracle", "class": "price", "loss": "$7M ($2M returned)", "mechanics": "At 15:24 — 20 minutes before a scheduled AMA — the attacker manipulated the Curve pool price with a flash loan and withdrew through the Vault-proxy path, draining ~$7M from the vault (TVL had been >$11M earlier in the day). $2M plus a note were returned; the VALUE token fell from $2.73 to $1.87. It followed $FARM and $AKRO as the third flash-loan victim in quick succession.", "lesson": "(1) Never derive deposit/withdrawal value from a Curve spot price — use virtual price / TWAP, and specifically audit WHICH getter each withdrawal math calls, because the \"wrong Curve function\" converts overpriced shares into real funds. (2) Enumerate every withdrawal entry point: a safe \"Bank\" contract means nothing if a second Vault-proxy path reaches the same funds with different checks — map all proxy paths into shared value.", "aftermath": "the team had tweeted security boasts a day before the hack (deleted, but rekt kept the screenshot — \"PRICELESS\"); Stani Kulechov tweeted as the AMA began; rekt's closing essay: flash loans \"are here to teach and humble anyone who rushes this process\".", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-10.md"}, {"protocol": "Warp Finance", "date": "2020-12-18", "chain": "Ethereum; flash-loan price manipulation of Uniswap V2 LP tokens used as lending collateral (flash-swapped $180M from Uniswap", "class": "price", "loss": "$7.8M", "mechanics": "An account funded with just 1 ETH via Tornado Cash took four flashloans (2.9M DAI + 76k WETH from dYdX; 90k/82k/96k WETH from Uniswap pairs), deposited 2.9M DAI + 76k WETH to mint 94,349 WETH-DAI LP tokens as collateral, then swapped 341k WETH for 47.6M DAI on Uniswap to make DAI expensive and pump the LP token price. Against the inflated collateral the attacker borrowed 3.86M DAI + 3.9M USDC (~$7.8M), repaid the flashloans, and exited. Notably the attacker did not profit immediately: the LP collateral stayed locked in an underwater position and most gains were eaten by fees — at write time the funds remained recoverable via liquidation.", "lesson": "Never value LP-token collateral from spot AMM prices — a single large swap in the underlying pair doubles the LP token's recorded value; require TWAP or manipulation-resistant pricing for any collateral whose value is a function of pool composition. Also note the \"deposit LP → pump underlying → borrow against inflated collateral\" flow as a recognizable flash-loan lending signature.", "aftermath": "Attack initiated 22:24 UTC on Dec 17, 2020, traced step-by-step by rekt's OPSEC; pie chart credited to @n2ckchong. Rekt mused that a future \"altruistic attacker\" might use the same technique to ransom funds back to protocols, Jekyll-and-Hyde framing included.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Wasabi Protocol", "date": "2026-04-30", "chain": "Ethereum + Base + Berachain + Blast; private-key compromise of the sole deployer EOA holding ADMIN_ROLE over every upgradeable vault", "class": "governance", "loss": "$5.9M", "mechanics": "Funding arrived from a Tornado-linked address at 01:12 UTC Apr 30; malicious contracts were deployed and gas loaded by 07:46; at 07:49:47 the Ethereum drain emptied eight vaults in one transaction (840.9 WETH ~$1.9M, ~$172K USDC, 400B REKT, 878M PEPE, 10B MOG, etc.), Base followed 2m50s later, the LongPool at 07:59:35. Totals: ~$2M Ethereum, ~$2.5M Base, plus Berachain/Blast → $5.9M across four chains. Tokens were converted to ETH and split across five attacker wallets; four of five moved through Tornado Cash within days. Blockaid flagged the drain live; CertiK followed within minutes.", "lesson": "A single EOA holding ADMIN_ROLE over upgradeable vaults is a standing full-drain primitive — in any audit, enumerate who can upgrade + route assets and demand multisig/timelock or flag it as critical. `strategyDeposit`-style admin asset-routing functions without a destination whitelist convert a key compromise into a direct sweep of every vault.", "aftermath": "Wasabi's first statement came 2+ hours after Blockaid's alert with no figure or mechanism; Virtuals Protocol (50+ AI trading agents on Wasabi) froze margin deposits faster than the team's second post; ZachXBT's question — \"Why did a single EOA seemingly have so much control?\" April 2026 closed as DeFi's worst month (~$635M across 28→29 incidents).", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "WazirX", "date": "2024-07-18", "chain": "CEX Safe multisig compromise (Ethereum hot wallet 0x27fD43BA…); hybrid attack — two private keys compromised", "class": "signature", "loss": "$235M", "mechanics": "Preparation began ≥8 days before (Tornado-funded test transactions Jul 8–10 involving ETH, SHIB and USDT with the multisig). On Jul 18 the attacker upgraded the Safe to their contract and drained: 33M USDT (22M TRON + 11M ETH), $18.6M BTC plus $14M BTC on TRON, $5M USDC, and more, swapping tokens to ETH on the way out. Cyvers alerted during the drain; WazirX confirmed ~30 minutes later and paused withdrawals; the attacker's KYC'd exchange deposit was later identified via an Arkham bounty (ZachXBT). #7 on the leaderboard.", "lesson": "Multisig UX is part of the security model — signers must verify the *decoded transaction contents*, not the wallet's display, and custody co-signers must independently verify payloads (a display/payload mismatch is a known attack class). Failed or anomalous transactions immediately preceding a drain are precursors worth alerting on. \"Not your keys, not your crypto\" applies to exchanges whose Safe is the crown jewel.", "aftermath": "Mudit Gupta's breakdown calls it \"a very methodical and organized attack, pointing towards DPRK as the hacker\" (unconfirmed); rekt notes the pattern of CEX multisig compromises after DMM Bitcoin's $304M in May 2024.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Whale Hunter's Payday", "date": "2024-08-20", "chain": "Ethereum; phishing — a whale signed a malicious transaction handing control of their EOA to the attacker", "class": "logic", "loss": "$55.47M (DAI)", "mechanics": "On Aug 20 ZachXBT noticed 55.47M DAI leave a single wallet; the whale attempted to withdraw their funds but the ownership change had already executed and the transaction failed. The attacker withdrew 55,473,618 DAI directly to 0x5D4b2A02… (main heist tx 0xf70042bf…); by reporting time 27.5M DAI had been swapped for 10,625 ETH. Victim 0xf2B88943…, phishing address 0x0000db5c….", "lesson": "For any wallet using a DSProxy (or similar delegated proxy), signing a single malicious transaction transfers *everything* the proxy controls — monitor whales' proxy-owner changes and failed owner-change withdrawals as live-phishing telemetry. Phishing remains the highest-ROI \"exploit\": CertiK reported ~$498M lost to it in H1 2024 alone.", "aftermath": "Blocksec's Jingyi Guo inferred the phishing vector from the victim's failed DSProxy invocations after the ownership change; rekt's closer — \"there's always a bigger phish out there and it might just be waiting for you to take the bait.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Wintermute (I)", "date": "2022-06-05", "chain": "Optimism; deployment-replay takeover — 20M OP were sent to a Gnosis Safe proxy address that had no contract deployed on Optimism; an attacker replayed the proxy deployment to make themselves owner.", "class": "logic", "loss": "20M OP (~$27.6M)", "mechanics": "20M OP were sent May 27; Wintermute realized the error May 30, but the OP launch went ahead Jun 1 with ~10% of circulating supply unowned. An exploiter (Tornado-funded Jun 1) deployed an adapted ProxyFactory and replayed batched deployments of 162 safes at a time until the matching address was created, hijacking the funds Jun 5. They sold 1M OP for 720 ETH and sent 1M OP to Vitalik's address; 18M OP remained unsold at write time. The Optimism Foundation issued Wintermute a replacement 20M OP.", "lesson": "Always deploy the receiving Safe (or any counterfactual contract) on the target chain before a large distribution, and treat \"confirmed receipt\" as worthless unless the recipient verified *access*. Legacy CREATE-based factories are cross-chain replayable — audit factory nonce/address determinism when contracts or funds are expected at an address that only exists on another chain.", "aftermath": "The exploiter waited 4 days between deploying the contract and hijacking (pointed out by yoav.eth) — rekt wonders why an attacker would leave Wintermute a rescue window; the 20M OP stake (almost 10% of circulating supply) raised governance-takeover concerns.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Wintermute (II)", "date": "2022-09-20", "chain": "Ethereum; private-key compromise of a Profanity-generated vanity-address hot wallet", "class": "hack", "loss": "~$160M ($162.3M at write time)", "mechanics": "The attacker (0xe74b28c2…) used the recovered key to drain ~$162.3M: $118.4M of stables (mostly parked into Curve 3pool to avoid blacklisting — making the attacker the 3rd-largest 3CRV holder at 13% of supply), 671 WBTC (~$13M), 6,928 ETH ($9.4M), and small-caps including 21% of PRIMATE's supply. CEO Evgeny Gaevoy announced the theft ~3 hours later, stating Cefi/OTC were unaffected and the firm remained solvent. In the days after the Profanity disclosure, 0x6AE09A… drained $3.3M from other vanity wallets.", "lesson": "Vanity-address wallets are a weak-key risk class — inventory privileged addresses and check key-generation provenance (Profanity-era addresses are assumed-compromised). When a key is suspected compromised, revoke *all* of its roles and approvals, not just move its funds; Wintermute's missed admin removal is the entire lesson. First major hack after the Tornado Cash sanctions.", "aftermath": "The vanity address was chosen for \"gas savings\" per Gaevoy — an expensive choice; a scammer capitalized on watchers by spoofing a \"WinterMuteInu\" honeypot from the exploiter's address (~166 ETH captured). Rekt flags post-sanction 3pool-as-mixer concerns for Curve users.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "WooFi", "date": "2024-03-05", "chain": "Arbitrum; flash-loan manipulation of WOOFi's sPMM oracle price for WOO", "class": "oracle", "loss": "$8.5M", "mechanics": "The attacker (0x9961190b…) flash-loaned funds, manipulated the sPMM's WOO price, and drained the pool using the swap function 3 times, taking ~$8.5M (attack tx 0x40e1b8c7…, contract 0xD4c633C9…). Spreek broke the news; the team paused the affected pools within 13 minutes. WOOFi sent an on-chain message assuming a whitehat and offering a 10% bounty; their post-mortem confirmed the out-of-range adjustment and the missing WOO fallback. CertiK had audited the swap/oracle contracts in October 2022.", "lesson": "Novel oracle designs (sPMM) must range-bound every price adjustment and have a fallback that covers *every* token, especially the protocol's own token — a native token with a new lending market and thin liquidity is the classic economics for this attack. Multi-chain expansion re-opens per-chain economic assumptions; re-audit the oracle per deployment.", "aftermath": "Part of Arbitrum's early-2024 spree (Radiant Capital, Gamma Strategies, Seneca before it); rekt mocks the \"One DEX to rule all chains\" slogan and the team bragging about its sPMM hours before the exploit.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "WOO X", "date": "2025-07-24", "chain": "CEX hot-wallet drain across five networks (Ethereum", "class": "logic", "loss": "$14M", "mechanics": "Jul 24 ~14:09 UTC WOO X announced a \"contained incident\" while funds were already flowing: the ETH hot wallet 0x63DFE4e3… to 4 attacker addresses, the BTC hot wallet bc1qm4hy… to 5 addresses, 5.03 BTCB on BSC, and ~7M TRX on TRON (traced by Cyvers, who sent rekt the trace). Nine user accounts were cited by the team, but on-chain data shows hot-wallet outflows directly to attackers; the tally climbed from ~$12M to $14M confirmed. Compensation came from the \"company treasury.\"", "lesson": "A single phished employee device = multi-chain hot-wallet drain; CEX audits should treat dev-environment access, signing flows and withdrawal review as the primary attack surface, not the smart contracts. Watch for the \"user accounts were breached, treasury compensates\" narrative pattern that papers over hot-wallet losses.", "aftermath": "Third WOO-ecosystem incident in two years (Kronos Research $26M API-key theft Nov 2023; WooFi $8.5M oracle exploit Mar 2024); the bug bounty program was quietly paused on Bugcrowd two weeks before the breach. Rekt: security theater — \"all flash, zero substance.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Wormhole", "date": "2022-02-02", "chain": "Solana bridge; forged VAA SignatureSet → minted 120k wETH (whETH) on Solana without any backing", "class": "signature", "loss": "$326M (120k wETH)", "mechanics": "The attacker (0x629e7da2…) created an account hours earlier containing a single serialized Secp256k1 instruction, faked the SignatureSet, and minted 120k wETH. 93,750 ETH were bridged back to Ethereum in 3 transactions (still in the hacker's wallet at write time); the remaining ~36k whETH were liquidated on Solana into USDC/SOL. samczsun flagged the issue within minutes; the team first claimed \"down for maintenance\", then promised and restored 1:1 backing within 24 hours. Certus One's on-chain $10M whitehat bounty offer went unanswered.", "lesson": "Bridge mint primitives must verify the *actual guardian signatures and address*, not merely delegate to a precompile — audit the instruction-verification boundary (sysvar vs program-version mismatch) and every path where a previously-created VAA/SignatureSet can be replayed into a fresh mint. A $326M loss from one mis-verified address is the whole lesson.", "aftermath": "Second bridge exploit in less than a week (after Qubit) and second on the leaderboard; rekt notes Vitalik Buterin's cross-chain security warnings being validated, and that the $10M bounty was the biggest ever offered.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "xToken (I)", "date": "2021-05-12", "chain": "Ethereum; flash-loan price manipulation of the SNX spot price used when minting xSNXa (pricing routed via Kyber", "class": "price", "loss": "$24M+ (2.4k ETH, 781k BNT, 407k SNX)", "mechanics": "dYdX flash loan of 61,833 ETH (~$267M): deposited 10k ETH to borrow 564k SNX on Aave and swapped 5.5k ETH → 700k SNX on Sushi; sold 1.2M SNX for 818 ETH on Uniswap v2, crashing SNX; minted 1.2B xSNXa for just 0.12 ETH (the protocol bought SNX at the crushed price via Kyber→Uni v2); swapped 105M xSNX → 414 ETH at the normal internal price; reversed the swaps and repaid Aave/dYdX; then issued xBNTa four times for 0.03 ETH total (3.9B xBNTa) and swapped half for 781k BNT. Everything was sold to ~5.6k ETH via 1inch.", "lesson": "When a protocol mints at market price but values internally at a \"normal\" price, the gap is pure arbitrage — audit every pricing hop (here Kyber→Uniswap) and require manipulation-resistant feeds for mint/valuation. Flash-loan + private-transaction (Flashbots) combinations mean mempool monitoring will miss the attack; rely on on-chain post-hoc monitoring instead.", "aftermath": "The fourth leaderboard entry for auditor Peckshield (\"most rekt auditor\"); rekt notes the shock that a \"blue chip\" with strong partnerships fell, and openly wonders whether the attack came from within.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "xToken (II)", "date": "2021-08-29", "chain": "Ethereum; access-control typo — an erroneous `require()` made xSNXAdmin's `callFunction` publicly callable", "class": "flash", "loss": "~$4.5M (xSNX)", "mechanics": "Flash loan of 25,000 ETH from dYdX; borrowed ~1M SNX from Aave V1+V2; swapped 6.8k ETH → 519k SNX on Bancor (holding ~1.5M SNX); dumped 1.5M SNX on Kyber for ~6.5M USDC, depressing SNX; swapped USDC → sUSD on Curve; transferred ~2M sUSD to xSNXAdmin and called `callFunction` to burn the sUSD debt and swap 614k SNX for 811k sUSD at the artificially depressed price; swapped sUSD → USDC, reversed everything and repaid. ~$4.5M stolen — same token, same flash-loan technique, three months after the first hit.", "lesson": "Privileged functions need exact access-control checks — grep every `require()` for typos and wrong comparison operands (sender vs msg.sender, == vs !=); a debt-management function that swaps at spot prices is a manipulation surface even when access control is *meant* to exist. A protocol hit twice by the same family in one quarter needs a whole-family re-audit, not a patch.", "aftermath": "The team compensated users again and phased out xSNX, admitting it was \"by far our most complex product\" — rekt notes the reputation damage of two leaderboard entries and the implicit confession of complexity risk.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Yearn (I)", "date": "2021-02-04", "chain": "Ethereum; nine flash loans funding an arbitrage loop against the yDAI v1 vault during a migration window in which the withdrawal fee had been turned off.", "class": "price", "loss": "$11M vault loss ($2.7M attacker profit)", "mechanics": "Flash loans of 116k ETH (dYdX) + 99k ETH (Aave v2); borrowed 134M USDC + 129M DAI on Compound; added 134M USDC + 36M DAI to the 3CRV pool and withdrew 165M USDT; then repeated five times: deposit 93M DAI (less each time) into the yDAI vault, add 165M USDT to 3CRV, withdraw 92M DAI from the vault, withdraw 165M USDT from 3CRV; the final round withdrew 39M DAI + 134M USDC. Each cycle left the attacker with more 3CRV, later swapped to stables. The $11M loss split: $2.7M attacker profit, $3.5M to Curve LPs, $3.5M to Curve stakers, $1.4M in Aave v2 fees.", "lesson": "Migration \"fee-off\" windows convert a vault into a free arbitrage machine if its pricing pool can be skewed — audit migration states and treat zero-fee + skewed-pool combinations as exploitable by construction. Vault share prices derived from spot AMM balances are manipulable with deposits/withdrawals alone (no fancy calls needed).", "aftermath": "The Yearn post-mortem did not mention flash loans; Igor Igamberdiev published the detailed flow. Tether froze 1.7M USDT of the loot. Rekt debunked the Cronje-vs-Bouteloup feud theory — the attacker's account was funded hours before the criticism was made.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Yearn (II)", "date": "2023-04-13", "chain": "Ethereum; misconfiguration in the immutable iearn yUSDT contract — the Fulcrum *USDC* address had been used instead of Fulcrum USDT since launch — exploited to mint 1.2 quadrillion yUSDT for 10k USDT.", "class": "price", "loss": "$10M+ (yUSDT iearn vault)", "mechanics": "Using the misconfigured basket, the attacker minted ~1.2 quadrillion yUSDT with just 10k USDT and swapped them for other stables totaling $11.4M (BlockSec's breakdown). The attacker was Tornado-funded and re-deposited 1,000 ETH for laundering; the three exploiter addresses held ~$1.5M + $1.5M + 7.4M DAI at write time. Team member storming0x acknowledged the attack before official comms; current vaults (~$450M TVL) were unaffected.", "lesson": "Yield-basket configuration (strategy/token addresses) is security-critical state — a wrong address in a basket is a mint primitive; hunt for copy/paste token-address errors in legacy contracts. Immutable legacy contracts still hold TVL years after deprecation and cannot be patched — they need monitoring, exit paths, or explicit wind-down, not neglect.", "aftermath": "CertiK's February 2020 audit of iearn covered only yDAI; 1,156 days passed before anyone noticed. Rekt ties it to \"test in prod\" culture and notes Sushi was hit the same week.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Yearn (III)", "date": "2025-11-30", "chain": "Ethereum; arithmetic underflow in the Newton-Raphson solver of Yearn's forgotten yETH stableswap — a corrupted invariant plus a dust deposit minted 2.35×10⁵⁶ yETH and drained the pool in one atomic transaction.", "class": "rounding", "loss": "$9M (yETH stableswap)", "mechanics": "21:11 UTC Nov 30, a single transaction: ~$8M drained from the yETH stableswap pool and ~$0.9M from the Curve yETH-WETH pool; ~1,000 ETH (~$3M) went into Tornado Cash as ten 100-ETH deposits in the same block; helper contracts self-destructed. Seed funds arrived via Railgun 30 minutes before. Yearn's war room (SEAL911 + ChainSecurity) and Plume/Dinero recovered $2.33M (857.49 pxETH); the primary attacker address still held ~$3.84M.", "lesson": "Abandoned-but-live contracts with novel math are the highest-ROI hunt targets of 2025–26 — no maintainer, no monitoring, full value. Specifically audit Newton-Raphson solvers and invariant math for underflow when zero-amount operations and rate updates can corrupt Σ/Π state; \"legacy code isn't technical debt, it's a loaded gun.\"", "aftermath": "Togbe spotted unusual Tornado/LST movements first; Peckshield alerted; Banteg's post-mortem confirmed the unchecked underflow. Rekt's irony: a casual researcher found what established processes didn't — \"which legacy products need eyes on them?\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Yearn (IV)", "date": "2025-12-16", "chain": "Ethereum; a virtual carbon copy of the April 2023 yUSDT exploit on the 2", "class": "misconfig", "loss": "~$293K (yTUSD)", "mechanics": "With a 30M USDC Morpho flash loan the attacker donated blind assets, broke the share accounting, minted an astronomical quantity of yTUSD shares, and dumped them into the Curve yPool for yDAI/yUSDC — draining the pool and collapsing the yPool LP price. Profit ~$293K, swapped to ~103 ETH parked at 0x0F214a04… (no Tornado, no bridges). Downstream collateral damage: STABLEx, which prices collateral off the Curve yPool, faced theoretical insolvency; ~214k sUSD remain permanently stuck in the vault because the old code doesn't recognize sUSD as a valid asset.", "lesson": "The \"strategy tracks the wrong asset\" misconfiguration is a repeatable, copy-pasteable exploit class — inventory every legacy vault's strategy-address configuration. Donation attacks against broken share accounting are the standard archaeology toolkit. Immutable = unguarded: attackers are now systematically excavating five-year-old bytecode — treat locked liquidity in ancient contracts as hacker bounties, not savings.", "aftermath": "William Li caught it live (\"Another Yearn V1 vault attack is underway!\"); Yearn's response was an archeological disclaimer (\"deployed over 2,100 days ago\"). Rekt frames a season of \"relic hunting\": Abracadabra's deprecated cauldrons ($1.8M), Balancer's rounding error ($128M), and Yearn's own yETH ($9M) two weeks earlier.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "YieldBlox", "date": "2026-02-22", "chain": "Stellar (Blend V2 lending); oracle manipulation — one 100x trade in a dead USTRY/USDC SDEX market poisoned the Reflector VWAP oracle", "class": "oracle", "loss": "$10.97M", "mechanics": "The attacker's wallet was created Feb 14 (56.32 XLM); on Feb 21 23:35 a burner account placed a sell offer of 1.2185 USTRY at 107 USDC (100x); at 00:10:21 Feb 22 a trigger account bought 0.05 USTRY against it — a ~50-cent trade that became the oracle's price at 00:15/00:20. Depositing ~153k USTRY (real value ~$160k, oracle value up to $15.99M), the attacker borrowed 1,000,196 USDC + 61,249,278 XLM in two transactions. Funds were swapped to USDC and bridged via Allbridge → Base → Ethereum (Across/Relay); Tier 1 Validators froze ~48M XLM (~80%); a 72-hour 90%-return bounty from the Security Council was ignored, and funds exited via Tornado.", "lesson": "Collateral listings need market-quality gates — minimum depth, multi-maker, volume floors — because a formally verified health factor is worthless if the oracle input is one 50-cent trade; audit the whole oracle→adapter→pool pipeline for median/deviation/staleness checks. No audit can cover \"should we list this asset?\" — that's governance, and governance must ask whether the collateral's market can be trusted.", "aftermath": "Blend V2 ran a $125k Code4rena + first-ever Rust/Soroban Certora formal verification contest (Feb 2025) — every proof held; the exploit lived between the code and the world (same architecture as Makina Finance a month earlier). Script3 compensated depositors; gas wallets were funded by flagged phishing addresses and one EVM wallet carries a Binance KYC trail.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "ZeroLend — 2025-02-23 (disclosed late 2025) — ~$371K (3.92 LBTC)", "date": "", "chain": "Base; collateral-manipulation borrow — PT-LBTC (a Pendle principal token) supplied as collateral with gameable oracle parameters", "class": "oracle", "loss": "", "mechanics": "Feb 23, 2025, the attacker (0x218C572b…, funded via Across) deposited PT-LBTC as collateral and borrowed 3.923 LBTC in three transactions within 45 minutes (0.9532, 1.4769, 1.4929); swapped the LBTC through Aerodrome and bridged the profit out via Across — arrived with 38 ETH, left with 163.65 ETH (~$125k net). The debt token still accrues interest on-chain; the worthless PT-LBTC collateral sits untouched. ZeroLend never disclosed: Discord blamed \"high utilization\" and \"maintenance\" until torakapa's tweet in late 2025.", "lesson": "After a public precedent (Ionic's $8.8M, 18 days earlier), listing the same asset class with gameable oracle parameters is a governance failure — audit new collateral listings against recent incidents in the same asset family. Hunt \"silent insolvency\": zombie markets where supply works but withdrawals return \"high utilization\" forever — and check for automated siphoning bots draining whatever liquidity appears.", "aftermath": "A Gnosis Safe (created two months after the exploit) with Gelato automation has extracted $100k+ from the broken pool since — \"someone built an automated extraction operation on top of a pool ZeroLend left broken and open.\" ZERO token -100%, OKX delisting, GitHub flatlined since Sept 2025, while the founder's LinkedIn still claims \"$200M+ under management.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "ZKasino", "date": "2024-04-20", "chain": "Ethereum; exit-scam rugpull — 10", "class": "logic", "loss": "$33M rug", "mechanics": "The bridge page (later deleted) had stated funds would be \"returned and can be bridged back\" once the chain was live; instead the ETH was swapped into ZKAS and staked on Lido. Red flags had been piling up since December 2023: ZachXBT publicly flagged the founder and team (unpaid Pancakesbrah, a $200K giveaway never awarded, an unpaid bet, gore-video marketing, unreimbursed phishing victims); IDOs on ApeTerminal and AIT Protocol were abruptly canceled; Telegram mods gaslit investors (\"People always FUD\"). The team then went dark, banning critics.", "lesson": "Exit-scam signatures compound: canceled listings + unpaid contractors + false tech claims + a founder with failed-project history + investor vesting imposed on user funds — when a \"chain\" is a 2-minute Arbitrum Nitro deployment and deposits aren't 1:1 redeemable, treat the raise as a rug target. \"If ZachXBT is calling something a scam months in advance, run to the exits.\"", "aftermath": "Founder Ildar \"Derivatives Ape\" Elham came from the failed ZigZagExchange/Syncus projects (allegedly misusing ZigZag's $15M raise to build ZKasino); Series A investors ($26M at a $350M valuation — MEXC, Big Brain Holdings, Trading Axe, Pentoshi, Sisyphus) had the very vesting terms later imposed on users; Big Brain Holdings declined its token distribution and distanced itself.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "zkLend", "date": "2025-02-11", "chain": "Starknet; rounding-error/precision manipulation of the `lending_accumulator` — flash-loan loops inflated it to 4.069297906051644020", "class": "rounding", "loss": "$9.57M", "mechanics": "The attacker's custom contract ran flash-loan transactions that squeezed `lending_accumulator` from 1.0 to 851.0 in a single transaction, landing on the magic value 4.069297906051644020, then used the rounding to mint free tokens and drained 2,200+ ETH plus USDC, STRK and USDT — $9.57M total. 1,801 ETH were bridged to Ethereum (0x645c7783…); 706.4 WETH sent to Railgun were *bounced back* by its exclusion policy. A 10% whitehat offer with a Feb 14 deadline went unanswered; the post-mortem arrived via an unverified Google Drive link and claimed FBI/HSI involvement.", "lesson": "Lending-accumulator precision is an exploit surface — model deposit/withdraw rounding at extreme accumulator values and empty-market states; \"empty market + donations + withdrawal rounding\" is a known killer combination across lending forks. Audits certify code as written, not arithmetic under adversarial precision — add property tests for share/exchange-rate rounding.", "aftermath": "SlowMist linked the attacker on-chain to the EraLend reentrancy exploit (July 2023) via two shared L1 addresses — a repeat operator (zkLend's post-mortem also names OnyxDAO, Yei, Channels, Starlay, though those claims lack evidence). Vitalik praised Railgun's rejection as \"privacy pools done right.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Zoth", "date": "2025-03-21", "chain": "Ethereum; admin-key compromise → proxy upgrade of `USD0PPSubVaultUpgradeable` to a malicious implementation → 8.85M USD0++ withdrawn", "class": "drain", "loss": "$8.4M", "mechanics": "Mar 21 the attacker (0x3b33c5Cd…) upgraded the vault proxy and, within minutes of the upgrade, withdrew 8.85M USD0++ tokens ($8.4M), converted to DAI, and moved them to 0x7b0cd0D8… (attack tx 0x33bf669d…). John Doe caught it live; SlowMist and Cyvers confirmed; asset issuers locked down 73% of Zoth's TVL right after, limiting further damage. Zoth + Securr offered a $500k/10% bounty; Crystal Blockchain took over the investigation. The attacker had stalked the protocol for weeks, funding wallets and deploying contracts in multiple failed attempts.", "lesson": "Whoever holds upgrade rights on a vault proxy holds a full-drain primitive — audit admin/upgrade custody (multisig? timelock?) on every proxy, and treat a second exploit within weeks as evidence the first post-mortem's fixes never landed: verify remediation, not announcements. Admin-key compromise on an RWA protocol is indistinguishable from insider action.", "aftermath": "Rekt: \"No contract audit in existence could have saved Zoth from its $8.4 million digital execution\" — the humans holding the keys were the problem; \"protocols should start auditing the people who work for them.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Zunami (I)", "date": "2023-08-13", "chain": "Ethereum; price manipulation of Zunami's zETH and UZD Curve pools via flash-loan swaps (e.g. SDT on Sushiswap)", "class": "price", "loss": "$2.1M (1,184 ETH)", "mechanics": "Using flash loans the attacker executed large token swaps (SDT) to create slippage, manipulated the LP prices, and drained the two pools for 1,184 ETH (~$2.1M), deposited straight into Tornado Cash. Attacker 0x5f4c21c9…; exploit txs 0x2aec4fdb… (zETH) and 0x0788ba22… (UZD). Peckshield raised the alarm (deliberately withholding tx hashes after the BlockSec/Vyper-debacle backlash); BlockSec published the step-by-step. The collateral itself remained secure per the team.", "lesson": "Any valuation function that reads raw pool balances (`totalHoldings`) is donation/slippage-manipulable — hunt for it in yield aggregators and stake-DAO strategies. Curve-ecosystem contagion in summer 2023 (Conic, JPEG'd, Alchemix, Curve itself, then Zunami) shows that when one ecosystem protocol falls, every adjacent protocol should be re-audited immediately.", "aftermath": "The attacked MimCurveStakeDAO strategy was added after Ackee Blockchain's audit of an earlier version (the article was edited to remove the Ackee reference); the team's first statement claimed \"collateral remain secure\" mid-drain. Rekt credits the post-Vyper era's new alerting standards and the SEAL 911 hotline as the (imperfect) community response.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "Zunami (II)", "date": "2025-05-14", "chain": "Ethereum; admin-key compromise (or insider job) — a single call to `withdrawStuckToken()` drained the UsdtCrvUsdStakeDaoCurve strategy of 296", "class": "exploit", "loss": "~$500K", "mechanics": "On May 14 the Zunami deployer wallet (0xe9b2B067…) granted admin role to 0x05137041…; seven minutes later that address called `withdrawStuckToken()` on the UsdtCrvUsdStakeDaoCurve strategy and transferred 296,456 LP tokens — the collateral for zunUSD and zunETH (~$500K) — to itself, then into Tornado Cash. Fourth Zunami incident after the Jan 2023 $49K sandwich, the Feb 2023 $260K flashloan series, and the Aug 2023 $2.1M price manipulation.", "lesson": "Emergency withdraw functions are the most dangerous permission in any contract — they need multisig + timelock + per-asset limits, and any protocol where one key can `withdrawStuckToken` the whole vault is a standing drain. When an \"admin key compromise\" hits a protocol showing every abandonment symptom (no commits, gauge rewards zeroed, domain expired), treat insider exit as the leading hypothesis. First Discord response \"rekt lmfao\" is the tell.", "aftermath": "CEO Kirill Kozlov surfaced two weeks later with \"compromised deployer or malicious intent by the key holder\"; CTO Mikhail Zelenin blamed Russian border police cloning his laptop and denied owning a Ferrari; even a team moderator threatened to file a Thai police report (deadline passed unanswered). Vladimir S. flagged the compromise; PeckShield confirmed; rekt: \"Emergency functions make excellent getaway vehicles.\"", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-11.md"}, {"protocol": "bZx", "date": "2021-11-05", "chain": "Polygon + BSC protocol deployments (stolen BZRX later bridged to Ethereum and posted as collateral to borrow other assets); private-key compromise via spear-phishing — no smart-contract flaw.", "class": "logic", "loss": "~$55M", "mechanics": "The attacker used the compromised key to seize the contracts and drain their BZRX, then updated the contract code to extract tokens from any wallet that had granted approvals to the affected contracts. To sidestep the liquidity problem of dumping a huge BZRX stack, the stolen BZRX was sent to Ethereum and used as collateral to borrow a variety of other assets. SlowMist's running total reached ~$55M; bZx asked Circle to freeze the stolen USDC (USDT held on Binance was frozen quickly) and offered the hacker to \"chat and reach an agreement\".", "lesson": "(1) Production admin keys must never live on a personal device/EOA — any single key controlling protocol deployments is a full-drain primitive; enforce hardware wallets, multisigs and key separation for everyone with admin roles. (2) A compromised admin key converts instantly into a mass-drain of every user with standing token approvals — treat admin-key hygiene and approval-revocation UX as first-class audit findings, and treat any contract upgrade right after a key theft as a red flag.", "aftermath": "", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-12.md"}, {"protocol": "bZx", "date": "2021-11-06", "chain": "Polygon + BSC (exit/collateral leg on Ethereum); phishing-compromised developer personal wallet (mnemonic) that controlled the protocol's Polygon/BSC deployments — no smart-contract flaw.", "class": "logic", "loss": "~$55M", "mechanics": "With the compromised keys the attacker drained BZRX from the protocol contracts, then updated the contract code to extract tokens from any wallet that had granted token approvals to the affected contracts (approval-sweep upgrade). To get around the liquidity problem of dumping a huge BZRX bag, the attacker bridged stolen BZRX to Ethereum and used it as collateral to borrow a variety of other assets — lower profit per token, but liquid. SlowMist's running total reached ~$55M at time of writing; ~10 hours after the initial announcement, bZx confirmed the phishing vector while stressing \"the code itself hadn't been compromised\".", "lesson": "(1) Protocol admin rights must never sit in a single personal EOA — hardware-custodied multisig key separation for every chain deployment, with personal machines treated as outside the trust boundary. (2) Phishing a dev's personal machine is a full protocol-kill vector: team-member OPSEC is part of the attack surface, and after any admin-key incident users must be pushed to revoke token approvals, because the follow-up upgrade can sweep every existing allowance.", "aftermath": "bZx's fourth major incident (\"4th time's the charm\") — after the Feb 2020 flash-loan exploits ($298k + $645k, the first flash-loan attacks in DeFi history) and a Sept 2020 $8M incident later returned. Community replies were \"exasperation rather than shock\" (\"What is this like 4th time?\", \"Y'all ngmi\"); bZx asked Circle to freeze USDC and confirmed Binance froze funds/USDT, and offered to \"chat and reach an agreement\" with the hacker. Debut entry on the leaderboard, straight into the top 10.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-14.md"}, {"protocol": "bZx — Nov 6, 2021 — ~$55M", "date": "", "chain": "Polygon + BSC (then Ethereum for cashing out); private-key compromise of a developer EOA via a spear-phishing email — no smart-contract exploit", "class": "logic", "loss": "", "mechanics": "With the compromised key the attacker took control of the Polygon/BSC contracts and drained BZRX, then updated the contract code to extract tokens from every wallet that had granted approvals to the affected contracts (so the attack expanded from the treasury to individual users). To avoid dumping illiquid BZRX, the attacker sent stolen BZRX to Ethereum as collateral and borrowed other assets instead. Slowmist kept a running total of ~$55M lost; bZx asked Circle to freeze stolen USDC, while USDT and funds on Binance were frozen quickly. The team published a preliminary post-mortem with the hacker's addresses across Polygon, BSC and Ethereum, and offered to \"chat and reach an agreement\".", "lesson": "(1) No high-TVL protocol's deployments/upgrade rights may sit behind a single dev EOA — admin keys need multisig + hardware wallets + rotation, because spear-phishing a developer is the cheapest high-yield attack in Web3; (2) after any admin-key compromise, standing user token approvals become a second wave of theft — auditors/hunters should check whether a compromised protocol can patch contracts to sweep approvals, and whether a pause/kill-switch exists that works even with admin keys lost.", "aftermath": "rekt's debut entry for bzx.network went straight into the leaderboard top 10; it was bZx's 4th incident — the Feb 2020 pair ($298k and $645k) were literally the first flash-loan-based exploits, and Sep 2020's $8M was taken and later returned. Community replies ranged from exasperation (\"What is this like 4th time?\") to \"Y'all ngmi\"; rekt's closer — human error should never have been able to lead to such an enormous loss, but \"it takes an especially callous and greedy hacker\" to also drain individual users' wallets.", "type": "analysis", "location": "analyses/volume-16.md"}];
